97e510e0-551d-422d-b315-0a3d5e97c830	74d0d3ca-7e39-442f-b93d-e5b2f31efa5c	Difference of 2 squares	Sure! The difference of two squares is a useful algebraic identity that states:\n\na \n2\n −b \n2\n =(a+b)(a−b)\nThis means that when you have a square number subtracted from another square number, you can factor it into a product of two binomials.\n\nHere’s how to understand it:\n\nSteps to Factor using the Difference of Two Squares:\nIdentify the squares: Check if both terms can be written as a square. For example, x \n2\n −9 can be identified as x \n2\n −3 \n2\n .\nApply the formula: Use the formula a \n2\n −b \n2\n =(a+b)(a−b). In our example, this becomes:\n\nx \n2\n −3 \n2\n =(x+3)(x−3)\nExamples:\nExample 1: x \n2\n −16\n\nIdentify that 16=4 \n2\n .\nFactor: x \n2\n −4 \n2\n =(x+4)(x−4).\nExample 2: 9y \n2\n −25\n\nRecognize that 9y \n2\n =(3y) \n2\n  and 25=5 \n2\n .\nFactor: 9y \n2\n −25=(3y+5)(3y−5).\nVisual Interpretation:\nYou can think of it geometrically; if you have a large square (area a \n2\n ) and you remove a smaller square (area b \n2\n ), the remaining area can be rearranged into a rectangle of dimensions (a+b) and (a−b).\n\n	Mathematics	{"important exam"}	2026-03-11 08:17:15.550861	2026-03-11 08:17:15.550861
1fdfa09e-c27e-43fb-8962-f6ed58908b5e	74d0d3ca-7e39-442f-b93d-e5b2f31efa5c	Characteristics Biology 1 	Biology Essentials: Life, Classification, and Diversity\nThe 7 Characteristics of Life\nAll living organisms share fundamental characteristics that distinguish them from non-living matter. These are:\n\nGrowth :\nThis is one of the 7 characteristics of life.\nGrowth is a permanent increase in size and mass\nAll organisms start small and grow larger \nGrowth happens by cell division and increase in cell size\nReproduction:\n\nThis is the ability to reproduce new organisms\nNew organisms are of the same species \nCan be sexual or asexual\nEnsures survial of species\nNutrition:\n\nHow organisms obtain food\nFood provides energy and materials for growth \nPlants make food by photosynthesis which is considered as autotrophic \nAnimals eat plants or other animals known as hetrotrophic\nRespiration :\n\nRespiration releases energy from food in the cells \nEnergy is needed for movement,growth and repair\nCan occur with or without oxygen \nRespiration is different from breathing \nExcretion:\n\nThis is the removal of watse products\n\nWatse is produced during metabolsim\n\nWaste can be harmful if not removed\n\nMovement:\n\nIs the abiltiy to change position\n\n Animals move from place to place\n\nPlants move part slowly\n\nSensitivity:\n\nThis detecting and responding to stimuli \n\nStimuli inculdes light sound and touch \n\nHelps organisms respond to their enviroment \n\nBiological Classification System\nThe biological classification system, also known as taxonomy, is the scientific method of naming, defining, and classifying groups of biological organisms based on shared characteristics. It arranges organisms in a hierarchical structure.\n\nClassification is putting organisms into groups based on similar cahracterists\n\nBinomial Naming System\nThe binomial naming system, developed by Carl Linnaeus, provides a standardized way to name species. Each species is given a unique two-part scientific name.\n\nThe first part of the name is the Genus, which is always capitalized.\nThe second part of the name is the Species (or specific epithet), which is always written in lowercase.\nThe entire scientific name is typically italicized.\nExample: The scientific name for humans is Homo sapiens.\n\nDichtomas key\n\nThis is a series of choices between descriptions to guide you to the organisms name\n\nKingdoms of Living Organisms\nHistorically, living organisms have been broadly classified into several kingdoms. The most commonly recognized kingdoms are:\n\nAnimalia: This kingdom includes multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that are heterotrophic, meaning they obtain nutrients by consuming other organisms. They are typically motile meaning the will move independently on point in their life\nKey Characteristics\n\nMulticellar[bodies conntain many cells]\n\ncells have a nucleus but no cell wall or chlorplasts\n\nfeed on organic substances made by others livng organisn, either as herbivores, carnivores or omnivores[ meaning they are hetroptropic]\n\nPlantae: This kingdom comprises multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that are autotrophic, meaning they produce their own food, typically through photosynthesis. All plants get their green colour due to a pigment called Chlorophyll. Which absorbs sunlight energy to make sugars throguh photosynthesis\nKey characteristcs:\n\nmulitcellar\n\ncells ahvea nucleus, cell walls made of cellouse abd often contain chlroplatss\n\nfeed by photosynthesis\n\nmay have roots, stems and leaves [ but some don't have these organisms\n\nFungi: This kingdom consists primarily of multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that obtain nutrients by absorption. They play crucial roles as decomposers.\nKey Characteriscs:\n\nusually multicellar\n\nhave nuclei\n\nhave cell walls not made of cellulose\n\ndont have chlorophyll fee by saprophytic or parasitic nutrition\n\nProtista: This diverse group includes mostly unicellular, eukaryotic organisms. It's a broad category for organisms that don't fit neatly into the other eukaryotic kingdoms.\nKey characteristcs\n\nmulticellular or unicellualar\n\ncells have a nucleus \n\ncell may or may not have a cekk wall and chkroplasts\n\nsome feed by photosynthesis and others feed on organic substance made by other organisms\n\nMonera: This kingdom, now often divided into Bacteria and Archaea, encompasses all unicellular prokaryotic organisms. Prokaryotes are characterized by the absence of a nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles.\nKey characteritcs \n\noften unicelluar\n\nhave no nulceus\n\nhave cell walls not made of cellouse \n\nhave no mitochondria\n\nVertebrates\nThese are animals with a backbone\n\nMajor Vertebrate Groups:\nFish: Apart from mudskippers all fish live in water\nCharacteristics\n\nThey are vertabrates with scaly skin \n\nthey have gills \n\nthey have fins \n\ntheir eggs have no shells and are laid in water\n\nAmphibians: Amphibians have a life cycle that often includes aquatic larval stages (like tadpoles) and terrestrial adult stages. They possess moist skin and typically undergo metamorphosis.\nCharacteristcs\n\nthey are vertabrates with moise skin with no scales\n\nthey eggs have no shells and are laid in water larva( tadpoles) live in water\n\nadults often live on land \n\nlarva have gills adults have lungs\n\nReptiles: Reptiles are characterized by their dry, scaly skin, which helps prevent water loss. Most reptiles lay amniotic eggs on land and are ectothermic (cold-blooded), meaning they rely on external sources of heat to regulate their body temperature.\nCharacteristics\n\nthey lay hard shelled eggs \n\nthey are vertabrates with scaly skin\n\nBirds: Birds are endothermic (warm-blooded) vertebrates distinguished by the presence of feathers, beaks, and wings. They lay hard-shelled eggs and are adapted for flight, though some species are flightless.\nCharacteristics\n\nvertabrates with feathers\nlay eggs with hard shells \nendothermic(meaning they are warm blooded)\nhave a beak\n\nMammals: Mammals are endothermic vertebrates that possess hair or fur, mammary glands for producing milk to nourish their young, and typically give birth to live young.\nCharacteristics \n\nThey have hair on skin\nTheir young develop in uterus attached to the mothers placenta \nThe femals have mammary glands which produce milk \nThey have different kinds of teeth (incisors,canines,premolars and molars)\nThe have a pinna (earflop) on the outside of their body \nThey have sweat glands in skin\nThey have a diaphragh \nArthropods\n\nArthropods form the largest phylum in the animal kingdom. They are invertebrates characterized by a hard exoskeleton, a segmented body, and jointed appendages.\n\nCharacteristics of all Arthpods\n\nThey have serval pairs of legs \n\nThey have an exoskelton \n\nMajor Arthropod Groups:\nInsects: This is the most diverse group of arthropods. They typically have three distinct body segments: a head, a thorax, and an abdomen. Insects possess six legs attached to the thorax.\nCharacteristics\nThey are arthopods with 3 pairs of legs \n\nThey havue 2 pairs of wings one or both may be vestigal \n\nThey breath through tubes called tracheae\n\nTheir body is serparted into 3 parts head thorax and abdomen \n\nOne pair of antennae\n\nArachnids: This group includes spiders, scorpions, mites, and ticks. Arachnids generally have eight legs and two main body segments: the cephalothorax (a fused head and thorax) and the abdomen.\nCharacteristics\n\n4 pairs of jointed legs \n\nThey have no antennae \n\nTheir body is divided into to parts- chephalothorax and abdomen\n\nCrustaceans: Primarily aquatic arthropods, crustaceans are characterized by having two pairs of antennae. Examples include crabs, lobsters, shrimp, and barnacles.\nCharacteristcs\n\n4 or more pairs of jointed legs \n\n2 pairs of antennae\n\nMyriapods: This group includes arthropods with elongated bodies and numerous legs. Centipedes have one pair of legs per body segment (except for a few segments near the head and tail), while millipedes have two pairs of legs per body segment.\nCharacteristcs\n\nTheir body consists of many similar segments \n\nEach of their body segments have jointed legs\n\nThey have one pair antennae\n\nFerns \n\nPlants with leaves called fronds, stems and roots \n\nThey cant reproduce flowers\n\nThey reproduce spores produce underside of fronds \n\nFlowering plants\n\nPlants that produce flowers \n\nThey are plants with roots stems and leaves \n\nTheir seeds are produced inside an ovary in the flowers\n\nDivided into 2 main groups : monocotyledons and dicotyledons\n\n🌱 Monocotyledons (monocots):\n\nHave one cotyledon in the seed\n\nRoots grow out of directly to from the stem \n\nLeaves with parallel veins (like grass)\n\nFlower parts in multiples of 3\n\nHave vascular bundle arranged randomly\n\nExample: corn, rice, tulips\n\n🌿 Dicotyledons (dicots):\n\nHave two cotyledons\n\nLeaves with branching veins\n\nFlower parts in multiples of 4 or 5\n\nVascualar bundles arranged in a ring \n\nThey usually have a main coat with side roots\n\nExample: beans, sunflowers, roses\n\n Viruses are tiny invaders — not living cells, but they can infect them.\n\nThey:\n\n🔹 Can’t reproduce on their own or do anything other than exist until they get in a host cell \n\n🔹 Have genetic material (DNA or RNA) in a protein coat\n\n🔹 Cause diseases like influenza, colds, COVID-19\n\nThey do not display and of the 7 characteristics of life\n\nAnimal and Plant Cells \n\nCell structure \n\nAll organinsms contain cells large organisms contain millions. They are multicellar.\n\nSome organisms are unicellular eg Bacteria and yeast\n\nAll cells are made of existing cells\n\nNew cells are formed when a fully grown cell divides (in other words the process call mitosis-one cell splits into 2 indentical cells)\n\n🔍 Plant cells have:\n\n✅ Cell wall (rigid, gives shape)\n\n✅ Chloroplasts (for photosynthesis)\n\n✅ Large central vacuole (stores water)\n\n🔍 Animal cells have:\n\n✅ Cell membrane (flexible)\n\n❌ No cell wall\n\n❌ No chloroplasts\n\n✅ Small vacuoles (if any)\n\nBoth have: nucleus, cytoplasm, mitochondria, ribosomes\n\nCytoplasm \n\nClear jelly nearly all water about 70% contains many substances disolved in it espesically protiens. Different metabolic reactions take place in the cytoplasm\n\nVacuoles \n\nSpace in the cell surrounded by membrane containing a solution\n\nA solution of sugars and other substances call cell sap \n\nFull vacuole presses outwards to help keep it in shape\n\nAnimal cells have a much smaller membrane bound spaces call vesicles which may contain food or water \\\n\nVesicles\n\n🔍 Vesicles are tiny, membrane-bound sacs that transport materials inside cells.\n\n🔹 Move stuff like proteins, lipids, or waste\n\n🔹 Can carry cargo between organelles (e.g., from ER to Golgi)\n\n🔹 Some release materials outside (exocytosis)\n\nThink of them as cellular delivery trucks 🚚\n\nChloroplasts\n\nFound in most green plants\n\ncontain chlorphyll the green pigment \n\nchrophyll absorbs sunligh for photosynthesis \n\noften contain starch grains produced duting photosynthesis \n\nanimal cells dont have chloroplasts or starch grains \n\nsome animal cells contain glycogen granules in the cytoplasm instead\n\n	Biology	{"important exam"}	2026-03-11 17:37:44.875153	2026-03-11 17:37:44.875153
