7e06f3b7-11e5-4b09-9550-6e2606631177	70dfaa3a-aca7-4c13-9d84-8bdf076f012b	Independent and Dependent clause 	Independent clause is a group of words that contain a subject and a verb.\nIndependent sentences express a complete thought. Independent sentences can stand alone. \nFor example:\n1)She loves to read.\n2) The sun is shining bright.\n3)They went to the park.\n4)I enjoy playing soccer on Fridays.\n5)They finished their homework before going to bed.\nDependent clauses\nDependent clause is a group of words that contain a subject that cannot stand alone and it does not express a complete thought \nFor example:\n1) Because it was raining \n2)If it raining \n3) Because it was wet	English 	{}	2026-03-14 07:36:50.113011	2026-03-14 07:36:50.113011
56215cb5-6872-48db-b008-9dac38344330	70dfaa3a-aca7-4c13-9d84-8bdf076f012b	Modal verbs 	Modal verbs are helping verbs that show how possible something is.\nThe modal verbs could, should and would show different degrees of possibility.\n1) Could \nCould shows that something is possible.\nExample:\nIt could rain today.\nShe could win the race \n2) Should\nShould shows that something is likely or expected to happen \nExample:\nThe bus should arrive soon.\nHe should finish his work today.\n3) Would \nWould shows what might happen in a certain situation situation.\nExample: \nI would help you if I had time \nShe would travel if she had money.\n	English 	{}	2026-03-14 08:01:21.659992	2026-03-14 08:01:21.659992
4ffde8f4-5fde-463a-95af-24e50059fb29	a1976679-96f1-4fd5-99ae-86f7e09fbc52	main memeory	Main Memory\nMain memory, also known as primary storage or RAM (Random Access Memory), is where the computer temporarily stores data that it needs to access quickly while performing tasks. Here are the key points:\n\nTemporary Storage: Main memory holds data and instructions that the CPU (Central Processing Unit) needs at the moment. When you open a program or file, it's loaded into the RAM for fast access.\n\nVolatile Memory: This type of memory is volatile, which means that it loses all stored data when the computer is turned off. So, it does not keep information after shutdown.\n\nSpeed: RAM is faster than other storage types, like hard drives, allowing the CPU to access data quickly, making the computer run smoothly and efficiently.\n\nTypes of Main Memory:\nRAM (Random Access Memory): Used for temporary storage while programs are running.\nROM (Read-Only Memory): Used to store permanent instructions, like the computer’s basic startup information, which is not lost when the computer is turned off.\nImportance:\nUnderstanding main memory is crucial because it plays a significant role in a computer's performance. The more RAM a computer has, the more programs it can run at the same time without slowing down.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask!\n\n\n	ICT	{}	2026-03-15 06:29:46.164441	2026-03-15 06:29:46.164441
53e4f166-eaf0-401a-aacd-efffb8e2ff56	70dfaa3a-aca7-4c13-9d84-8bdf076f012b	Adverbs 	Adverbs of time this adverbs tells us something happened when \nFor example;\n1) Tomorrow is our English test\n2)They will leave in one hour \n3) She gets homework once a week \nAdverbs of manner \nThese are adverbs that tell us how something happens \nFor example;\n1)She ran quickly\n2)He looked at them rudely\n3)She stormed out of the room angryly. 	English 	{}	2026-03-15 08:58:38.315896	2026-03-15 08:58:38.315896
89237682-d90e-480d-b082-193a9696edfd	70dfaa3a-aca7-4c13-9d84-8bdf076f012b	Comparative and Superlative adjective and adverbs	Comparative adjectives:Are used to compare two things which describes a noun.\nUsually we add -er at the end or use more before the adjective\nExamples;\n1)My bag is bigger than yours\n2)This book is more interesting than that one \nIf the adjective is short add er\nIf the adjective is long add more \nAdverbs-describe verbs comparative adverb:are used to compare how actors are done we usually add er or more \nExample;\n1)She runs faster than him\n2)He speaks more dearly than his brother \nSuperlative adjective and adverbs \nWhen we compare three or more things \nIt often end with est or we use most\nFor example;\n1) John is the tallest in the class\n2)This is the most exciting movie.	English 	{}	2026-03-15 11:28:23.873246	2026-03-15 11:28:23.873246
