64d03f3a-ec99-44ce-9f41-3dcc50455e5b	5dfe2abf-2909-426e-8e40-92872a7be7bb	Math - Easy	Math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762346658505_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["11", "12", "13", "10"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762346658505_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square always has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square is a quadrilateral with four equal sides and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762346658505_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The place value of the digit 5 in the number 352 is ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 352, the 3 is in the hundreds place, the 5 is in the tens place, and the 2 is in the ones place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1762346658505_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 6 units and width 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). So, 2 × (6 + 3) = 2 × 9 = 18 units.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762346658505_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "2/3", "3/5", "1/3"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying the numerator and denominator of 1/2 by the same number (2) gives 2/4, which has the same value.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}]	none	2025-11-05 12:44:18.518586	\N
d0d8a761-a488-4188-83eb-3d03ba64421f	efd5ad9d-a1ad-42cc-98f3-3ae4dd18b488	Lifecycle of a goat - Medium	Lifecycle of a goat	medium	[{"id": "q_1762369189806_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["September 1", "October 15", "November 20", "December 5"], "question": "You plan for kids to be born around March 15. Considering the average goat gestation of about 150 days, which breeding date is most appropriate for most temperate breeds?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Goat gestation averages roughly 150 days (about 5 months), with a normal range of 145–155 days. Counting back 150 days from March 15 lands around mid-October, making October 15 the most appropriate choice. Earlier (September 1) would yield winter kids, and later dates push kidding into late spring or early summer.", "correctAnswer": "October 15"}, {"id": "q_1762369189806_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "It is generally advisable to rebreed a doe immediately after she kids to maximize yearly productivity.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "After kidding, a doe needs time for uterine involution and metabolic recovery, typically 45–60 days. Immediate rebreeding can compromise fertility, milk production, and overall health because lactation is energy-demanding and body condition may be low. Sound lifecycle management balances reproduction with recovery, nutrition, and seasonality.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762369189806_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The typical length of a doe’s estrous cycle is about ____ days.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Most does have an estrous cycle near 21 days, though it can range roughly 18–24 days depending on breed, season, and individual variation. Knowing this interval helps time breeding attempts and pregnancy checks within the goat’s reproductive lifecycle.", "correctAnswer": "21"}, {"id": "q_1762369189806_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why must kids receive colostrum within the first 6–12 hours after birth, and what is one likely consequence if they do not?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Goat kids are born without mature immune defenses. Colostrum supplies high levels of immunoglobulins, energy, and protective factors. The intestine’s ability to absorb these antibodies rapidly declines (gut closure) within the first day, so early feeding is critical. Without timely colostrum, kids are much more susceptible to infections and poor early growth.", "correctAnswer": "Colostrum provides essential antibodies that the kid’s gut can absorb only shortly after birth; if missed, failure of passive transfer increases disease risk and early mortality."}, {"id": "q_1762369189806_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Abruptly stop all milk today and isolate kids for 48 hours.", "Reduce milk gradually over 7–10 days while ensuring ad lib hay, fresh water, and a coccidiostat-containing starter; wean in stable groups.", "Continue full milk ration until 16 weeks to maximize growth, then drop to zero in one day.", "Wean now but switch to a high-concentrate, grain-only diet for faster gains."], "question": "Bottle-raised kids are 10 weeks old, eating solid feed daily, and are healthy. Which weaning plan best supports rumen development and minimizes stress?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A gradual taper allows the rumen microbiome and papillae to adapt, while fiber and starter feed support proper rumen development. Providing water and managing coccidiosis risk reduce health problems. Keeping kids in stable groups reduces social stress. Abrupt weaning or grain-only diets increase stress, digestive upset, and disease risk.", "correctAnswer": "Reduce milk gradually over 7–10 days while ensuring ad lib hay, fresh water, and a coccidiostat-containing starter; wean in stable groups."}]	none	2025-11-05 18:59:49.907949	\N
ed84622f-a014-4636-a484-22648c3ad9ce	599e2a37-8fa5-41bf-8f98-53912cb1b09f	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409274071_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Homework", "Washing dishes", "Playing sports", "Painting houses"], "question": "According to the content, what is one thing AI can help us with?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content states that AI helps us with homework and study. Tasks like washing dishes or painting houses are not part of the educational support described.", "correctAnswer": "Homework"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "AI can help us learn new things like history, geography, and maths.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The statement directly matches the content, which lists history, geography, and maths as subjects AI can help us learn.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "AI helps us with homework and ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content explicitly says, “AI helps us with homework and study,” so the missing word is “study.”", "correctAnswer": "study"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one school subject AI can help you learn.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content lists history, geography, and maths as examples. History is one correct example; geography or maths would also be acceptable.", "correctAnswer": "history"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["By giving explanations and hints", "By grading government exams", "By building bridges", "By watering plants"], "question": "How can AI support your studying?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "AI tools can provide explanations, hints, and guidance when studying. The other options are unrelated to learning support.", "correctAnswer": "By giving explanations and hints"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "AI is only useful for adults and not helpful for students.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content highlights AI helping with homework and learning school subjects, showing it is helpful for students.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "AI can help you ______ new things like history and geography.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content says AI can help us learn new things, so the correct word is “learn.”", "correctAnswer": "learn"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If you are stuck on a homework problem, how can AI assist you?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "AI tools often provide step-by-step explanations and hints, which support understanding while you study.", "correctAnswer": "It can give hints or explanations to help you understand."}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["History", "Geography", "Maths", "Cooking"], "question": "Which of these is NOT a subject mentioned that AI can help you learn?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The content mentions history, geography, and maths. Cooking is not listed as a subject in the provided content.", "correctAnswer": "Cooking"}, {"id": "q_1762409274071_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "AI can assist with study, but you still need to think and practice yourself.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "AI supports homework and learning, but effective studying requires active thinking and practice to truly understand the material.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	9e577dab-1d73-4f9c-b174-eee996160ceb	2025-11-06 06:07:54.084889	\N
8c383d27-f9dc-4396-82c3-27ba1fe2379c	13f64334-b200-47a3-a85a-4f833920c622	Science - Medium	Science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409444810_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Attraction", "Repulsion", "No interaction", "They will align with no net force"], "question": "If the north pole of Magnet A is brought close to the north pole of Magnet B, what interaction will occur between the magnets?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Like poles (north facing north) repel. Only unlike poles attract, so two north poles push away from each other.", "correctAnswer": "Repulsion"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North", "South", "Neutral", "Cannot be determined"], "question": "You have a known magnet labeled N and S. When you bring the labeled N end near one end of an unmarked magnet, the magnets attract. What is the pole of the end on the unmarked magnet?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Attraction occurs between unlike poles. If the known north pole attracts that end of the unmarked magnet, that end must be a south pole.", "correctAnswer": "South"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North facing South", "North facing North", "South facing South", "Poles are at the center so ends are neutral"], "question": "Two identical bar magnets snap together end-to-end. Which pole arrangement at the touching ends is most consistent with this behavior?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Magnets stick when unlike poles meet. The touching ends are opposite poles (N-S), producing attraction.", "correctAnswer": "North facing South"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Flip one magnet 180° so the opposite pole faces", "Rotate both magnets 180° together", "Increase the temperature of the magnets", "Move the magnets sideways but keep the same facing ends"], "question": "A student sets two magnets so they repel. Which single action will most likely make them attract without changing the distance?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Repulsion means like poles are facing. Flipping one magnet exposes the unlike pole to the other, switching the interaction to attraction.", "correctAnswer": "Flip one magnet 180° so the opposite pole faces"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Like magnetic poles attract each other.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By definition, like poles (N-N or S-S) repel, while unlike poles (N-S) attract.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Turning one of two repelling magnets around can change the interaction to attraction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "If magnets are repelling, like poles are facing. Flipping one magnet presents the opposite pole, creating an unlike-pole pair that attracts.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When a north pole is brought near a south pole, they ______ each other.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Unlike poles attract, so the magnets pull together when a north faces a south.", "correctAnswer": "attract"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "To prevent two magnets from sticking together, face a north pole toward a _____ pole on the other magnet.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Like poles repel. North facing north produces repulsion, preventing the magnets from sticking.", "correctAnswer": "north"}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You want three bar magnets to form a stable chain end-to-end. How should the poles at the touching ends be arranged?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Each joint must be an unlike-pole contact (north to south) to ensure attraction and a stable chain.", "correctAnswer": "Arrange them so each touching pair is N facing S (alternating N-S-N-S along the chain)."}, {"id": "q_1762409444810_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Two magnets initially attract at their facing ends. Without moving them closer or farther apart, you adjust their orientation and they begin to repel. What changed at the facing ends?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Attraction indicates opposite poles were facing. Rotating one magnet can present the same type of pole, which causes repulsion.", "correctAnswer": "They changed from unlike poles facing (N-S) to like poles facing (N-N or S-S)."}]	20623f0c-ea87-4dc3-acdf-5f9f639f710b	2025-11-06 06:10:44.823119	\N
d5d686b0-e5e1-4d07-b5f4-a6cccde21a4b	8b8ef1ac-90ee-474f-aae2-365b5e6f6e60	science - Easy	science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409500970_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants take in from the air during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants absorb carbon dioxide (CO2) from the air and use water and sunlight to produce glucose for energy and growth, releasing oxygen as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1762409500970_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth completes one full orbit around the Sun in about 24 hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Twenty-four hours is the time it takes Earth to rotate once on its axis (a day). One full orbit around the Sun (a year) takes about 365 days.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409500970_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which a liquid changes into a gas at the surface is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation is when molecules at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to become gas. It can happen at temperatures below the boiling point.", "correctAnswer": "evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1762409500970_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is commonly used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature, often in degrees Celsius or Fahrenheit, using liquid expansion, electronic sensors, or infrared detection.", "correctAnswer": "Thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1762409500970_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a definite volume but no definite shape?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Liquids have a definite volume but take the shape of their container. Solids have both definite shape and volume, while gases have neither.", "correctAnswer": "Liquid"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:11:41.072011	\N
d449b79e-0dff-41aa-9462-009c2c8cf5d5	599e2a37-8fa5-41bf-8f98-53912cb1b09f	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409565199_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solve x^2 − 5x + 6 = 0.", "Solve x^2 − 5x + 6 = 0 and explain each step, define any formulas used, and check the solution.", "Tell me the answer fast.", "What is a quadratic?"], "question": "Which prompt will most likely make an AI give a clear, step-by-step explanation for solving a quadratic equation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "AI responds best to specific, structured prompts. Asking for steps, definitions, and a check guides the AI to teach the maths process, not just give the final answer.", "correctAnswer": "Solve x^2 − 5x + 6 = 0 and explain each step, define any formulas used, and check the solution."}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "AI should be used as the only source for historical facts without cross-checking.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "AI is helpful for study, but it can be imperfect. In ICT practice, you should verify historical information with reliable sources like textbooks, academic sites, or museum archives.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When using AI to study geography, you can ask it to create a __________ to compare climates of two regions.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A comparison table structures data side by side (e.g., temperature, rainfall), making geographic analysis clearer and easier to study.", "correctAnswer": "comparison table"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one effective way AI can assist with maths beyond giving the final answer.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "AI can guide learning by explaining steps, showing formulas, generating practice problems, or checking work—skills that build understanding rather than just results.", "correctAnswer": "provide step-by-step explanations"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Timeline builder", "Image colorizer", "Audio equalizer", "Random name generator"], "question": "Which AI feature is most helpful for learning history chronologically?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A timeline builder organizes events by date, helping students analyze cause-and-effect and sequence—key to studying history.", "correctAnswer": "Timeline builder"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Providing details like scale, direction, and key landmarks in your prompt helps AI give more accurate explanations of a geography map.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Clear, specific prompts supply context the AI needs to interpret geographic information correctly, improving the quality of the explanation.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "To avoid plagiarism when using AI for homework, students should __________ before submitting AI-assisted writing.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Responsible ICT use includes editing AI drafts into your own wording and citing sources or references to acknowledge information origins.", "correctAnswer": "use their own words and cite sources"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If AI gives conflicting answers to a history question, what should you do to ensure reliable learning?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Verifying with trusted materials (textbooks, scholarly sites) and requesting citations helps resolve inconsistencies and improves accuracy.", "correctAnswer": "cross-check with credible sources and ask the AI for references"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Accept the first AI answer without checking", "Ask the AI for sources and verify with an authoritative map or atlas", "Use emojis to summarize the result", "Avoid using AI entirely"], "question": "Which practice best ensures accuracy when AI helps you analyze the location of a city?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combining AI assistance with source verification (official maps, atlases) is good ICT practice for reliable geographic learning.", "correctAnswer": "Ask the AI for sources and verify with an authoritative map or atlas"}, {"id": "q_1762409565199_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Give me the final answer only.", "Explain step by step and show formulas used.", "Write a fiction story about this equation.", "Translate the problem into a different language."], "question": "In maths study, what instruction helps an AI show its working process?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Requesting step-by-step work and formulas guides the AI to present the reasoning, which supports deeper understanding in maths.", "correctAnswer": "Explain step by step and show formulas used."}]	9e577dab-1d73-4f9c-b174-eee996160ceb	2025-11-06 06:12:45.213088	\N
f7b7c0db-7f6c-4b48-960a-0c6c74c9d7fe	61aa1958-2f4d-4e74-a997-3876b9476362	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409598511_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30", "45", "60", "90"], "question": "You study for 30 minutes before dinner and 30 minutes after dinner. How many minutes in total did you study?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Adding two 30-minute sessions: 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Doubling 30 gives 70.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Doubling means multiplying by 2: 2 × 30 = 60, not 70. This also matches the fact that 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Compute: 120 − (30 + 30) = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First evaluate the parentheses: 30 + 30 = 60. Then 120 − 60 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You run for 30 minutes at 8 km/h and then 30 minutes at 10 km/h. What is your average speed over the entire outing (in km/h)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total time is 30 + 30 = 60 minutes = 1 hour. Distance: 8 × 0.5 = 4 km and 10 × 0.5 = 5 km; total distance is 9 km in 1 hour, so the average speed is 9 km/h.", "correctAnswer": "9 km/h"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30 + 25", "90 - 40", "2 * 30", "240 / 5"], "question": "Which expression equals 60?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "2 * 30 = 60 because 30 + 30 = 60. The other options evaluate to 55, 50, and 48 respectively.", "correctAnswer": "2 * 30"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "If 60 is split into two equal whole numbers, each number is 30.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Two equal addends that sum to 60 are 30 and 30 because 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for x: 30 + x = 60. x = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Subtract 30 from both sides: x = 60 − 30 = 30, which aligns with 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "30"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2:45", "3:00", "3:30", "4:00"], "question": "A clock shows 2:30. After 30 more minutes, what time does it show?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Adding 30 minutes to 2:30 reaches the next hour: 2:30 + 0:30 = 3:00. Since 30 + 30 = 60 minutes, that makes one full hour.", "correctAnswer": "3:00"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve for a: (a − 30) + (a − 30) = 60.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine like terms: 2a − 60 = 60, so 2a = 120 and a = 60. This uses the idea that two equal parts summing to 60 correspond to 30 + 30.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762409598511_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "question": "How many 30-minute intervals are there in 1.5 hours?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "1.5 hours = 90 minutes. 90 ÷ 30 = 3 intervals. Equivalently, 30 + 30 + 30 = 90.", "correctAnswer": "3"}]	0aa12fa7-a6a7-4276-a7c2-2b0e1400cc82	2025-11-06 06:13:18.600822	\N
2900383e-58aa-4edb-9270-5129319b441b	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409606782_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It increases", "It decreases", "It stays the same", "It becomes zero"], "question": "A satellite in a low Earth orbit experiences atmospheric drag and slowly spirals to a lower circular orbit. What happens to its orbital speed as it settles into the lower orbit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For circular orbits, orbital speed v is proportional to the square root of GM/r. As orbital radius r decreases, v increases. So moving to a lower orbit increases the satellite’s speed.", "correctAnswer": "It increases"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It doubles", "It halves", "It stays the same", "It quadruples"], "question": "A sealed syringe of gas is compressed from 100 mL to 50 mL at constant temperature. What happens to the gas pressure?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Boyle’s law states that for a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume (P ∝ 1/V). Halving the volume doubles the pressure.", "correctAnswer": "It doubles"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It roughly doubles", "It decreases to half", "It shows little to no increase", "It stops due to product inhibition"], "question": "An enzyme-catalyzed reaction is operating at a substrate concentration where the enzyme is saturated. If you double the substrate concentration, what is the expected effect on the reaction rate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At saturation, all active sites are occupied and the reaction is at Vmax. Adding more substrate does not significantly increase the rate because the enzyme is the limiting factor.", "correctAnswer": "It shows little to no increase"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Stronger seasonal temperature contrasts", "No significant seasons across the year", "A longer year", "More frequent eclipses"], "question": "If Earth’s axial tilt were reduced from about 23.5 degrees to 0 degrees, which change would most likely occur?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Seasons are caused by axial tilt. With no tilt, solar insolation at each latitude would not vary much through the year, greatly reducing seasonal changes.", "correctAnswer": "No significant seasons across the year"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2 A", "4 A", "6 A", "8 A"], "question": "Two resistors, 2 Ω and 6 Ω, are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery. What is the total current drawn from the battery?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The equivalent resistance in parallel is (2×6)/(2+6) = 12/8 = 1.5 Ω. The total current is I = V/R = 12 V / 1.5 Ω = 8 A.", "correctAnswer": "8 A"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Both homozygous dominant", "One homozygous dominant and one homozygous recessive", "Both heterozygous carriers", "Both homozygous recessive"], "question": "A couple has an unaffected daughter, but their son is affected by an autosomal recessive disorder. Both parents are unaffected. What is the most likely genotype of the parents?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Autosomal recessive traits require two recessive alleles to be expressed. Unaffected parents with an affected child are most likely both heterozygous carriers (each contributes one recessive allele).", "correctAnswer": "Both heterozygous carriers"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Overfishing", "Burning fossil fuels", "Irrigation runoff", "Urban noise pollution"], "question": "Which human activity most directly contributes to ocean acidification?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Burning fossil fuels releases CO2, which dissolves in seawater to form carbonic acid, lowering ocean pH. While other activities stress oceans, CO2 emissions are the primary driver of acidification.", "correctAnswer": "Burning fossil fuels"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: The Doppler effect occurs only for sound waves.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Doppler effect applies to all types of waves, including sound, light, and water waves. For light, a shift in frequency (redshift or blueshift) is observed due to relative motion.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: In an endothermic reaction at constant pressure, the enthalpy change (ΔH) is positive.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Endothermic reactions absorb heat from their surroundings. At constant pressure, this corresponds to a positive enthalpy change because the system’s heat content increases.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: All bacteria are harmful to humans.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Many bacteria are beneficial or harmless. They aid digestion, synthesize vitamins, and play key roles in ecosystems and biotechnology. Only a subset are pathogenic.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The organelle primarily responsible for modifying, sorting, and packaging proteins for secretion is the _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Golgi apparatus (Golgi complex) processes proteins from the rough endoplasmic reticulum, adding modifications and packaging them into vesicles for delivery or secretion.", "correctAnswer": "Golgi apparatus"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom is called its _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Ionization energy reflects how strongly an atom holds its outer electron. Trends across the periodic table relate to effective nuclear charge and atomic radius.", "correctAnswer": "ionization energy"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When white light passes through a prism, shorter wavelengths are bent more than longer wavelengths due to _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dispersion occurs because a material’s refractive index depends on wavelength. Shorter wavelengths (like blue) refract more than longer wavelengths (like red), spreading white light into a spectrum.", "correctAnswer": "dispersion"}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why sweating cools the body more effectively in dry air than in humid air.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Cooling from sweat relies on phase change. A steep vapor pressure gradient in dry air promotes evaporation, drawing thermal energy from the skin. In humid air, the gradient is smaller, evaporation slows, and less heat is removed.", "correctAnswer": "Evaporation is faster in dry air because the air’s low humidity allows more water to evaporate. Evaporation requires latent heat taken from the skin, so faster evaporation removes more heat and cools the body. High humidity slows evaporation, reducing cooling."}, {"id": "q_1762409606782_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A 1 kg block slides down a frictionless ramp and drops by 5 meters in height. Ignoring air resistance, how much kinetic energy has it gained at the bottom?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "On a frictionless path, mechanical energy is conserved. The loss in gravitational potential energy mgh converts entirely to kinetic energy, giving approximately 49 J.", "correctAnswer": "About 49 joules of kinetic energy, equal to mgh = (1 kg)(9.8 m/s^2)(5 m)."}]	none	2025-11-06 06:13:26.796268	\N
d52c2422-d8b6-4477-b855-82b682165314	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409645197_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Move the screen closer to the lens", "Move the screen farther from the lens", "Replace the lens with one of shorter focal length", "Flip the lens around"], "question": "A converging lens is projecting a sharp real image of a distant building onto a screen. The building then moves significantly closer to the lens but remains farther away than the lens’s focal length. To bring the image back into focus, what should you do?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By the thin lens equation (1/f = 1/do + 1/di), decreasing object distance (do) while keeping focal length (f) constant increases the image distance (di). That means the image forms farther from the lens, so you must move the screen away to refocus. Flipping the lens or changing focal length isn’t necessary for refocusing in this scenario.", "correctAnswer": "Move the screen farther from the lens"}, {"id": "q_1762409645197_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Increasing the concentration of reactants always increases the rate of a chemical reaction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "While higher reactant concentrations often increase reaction rates when the reactants appear with positive orders in the rate law, it is not universal. Zero-order reactions show rate independence from concentration, enzyme-catalyzed reactions can saturate (Michaelis–Menten kinetics), and some mechanisms are limited by a rate-determining step or diffusion, making further concentration increases ineffective.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409645197_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Most of the ATP produced during aerobic cellular respiration is generated by ________ using the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "During aerobic respiration, the electron transport chain creates a proton gradient that drives ATP synthase. The synthesis of ATP powered by this gradient is called oxidative phosphorylation. Glycolysis and the citric acid cycle produce some ATP directly, but the majority comes from oxidative phosphorylation.", "correctAnswer": "oxidative phosphorylation"}, {"id": "q_1762409645197_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why do coastal regions generally experience smaller temperature swings between day and night (and between seasons) than inland areas at the same latitude?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water’s high specific heat capacity means it requires more energy to change temperature compared to land. Oceans and large lakes store heat during the day/summer and release it at night/winter. This buffering (thermal inertia) smooths temperature fluctuations in coastal climates relative to inland regions.", "correctAnswer": "Because nearby water has a high specific heat and thermal inertia, the ocean absorbs and releases heat slowly, moderating air temperatures along the coast."}, {"id": "q_1762409645197_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It becomes 4 times greater in parallel", "It becomes 2 times greater in parallel", "It stays the same", "It becomes 1/4 as much in parallel"], "question": "Two identical resistors are first connected in series across an ideal battery and then reconnected in parallel across the same battery. How does the total power dissipated by the resistors change when going from series to parallel?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "With battery voltage V and each resistor R: series has equivalent resistance 2R, so total power is P_series = V^2/(2R). Parallel has equivalent resistance R/2, so P_parallel = V^2/(R/2) = 2V^2/R. The ratio P_parallel/P_series = (2V^2/R)/(V^2/(2R)) = 4. Thus, the parallel arrangement dissipates four times as much power.", "correctAnswer": "It becomes 4 times greater in parallel"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:14:05.302434	\N
70916625-0537-4d75-99be-c81111060e73	255a22c9-d03b-43d9-906a-f458ba3089eb	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1762409824568_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["x = 3 and x = 1/2", "x = 3 and x = −1/2", "x = −3 and x = 1/2", "x = 7/2 and x = −3/2"], "question": "Solve the quadratic equation 2x^2 − 7x + 3 = 0. What are the solutions?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor the quadratic: 2x^2 − 7x + 3 = (2x − 1)(x − 3). Setting each factor to zero gives solutions x = 1/2 and x = 3.", "correctAnswer": "x = 3 and x = 1/2"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "For any positive real numbers x and y and base a > 0, a ≠ 1, log_a(xy) = log_a(x) + log_a(y).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is the product rule for logarithms, which follows from the laws of exponents and the definition of logarithms: a^{log_a(x)} · a^{log_a(y)} = a^{log_a(x) + log_a(y)} corresponds to xy inside the log.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find the slope of the line passing through the points (2, −1) and (5, 5).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the slope formula m = (y2 − y1)/(x2 − x1) = (5 − (−1)) / (5 − 2) = 6/3 = 2.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the domain of the function f(x) = (x − 4) / √(9 − x). Give your answer in interval notation.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The expression under the square root must be positive and the denominator cannot be zero: 9 − x > 0 ⇒ x < 9. Thus the domain is all real x with x < 9, i.e., (−∞, 9).", "correctAnswer": "(-∞, 9)"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/9", "1/5", "1/9", "4/45"], "question": "A bag contains 5 red, 3 blue, and 2 green marbles. If two marbles are drawn at random without replacement, what is the probability that both are red?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Probability = (5/10) × (4/9) = 20/90 = 2/9. Alternatively, use combinations: C(5,2)/C(10,2) = 10/45 = 2/9.", "correctAnswer": "2/9"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-√2/2", "√2/2", "-√3/2", "-1/2"], "question": "Evaluate sin(225°).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "225° is in Quadrant III with reference angle 45°. Sine is negative in Quadrant III, and |sin(45°)| = √2/2, so sin(225°) = −√2/2.", "correctAnswer": "-√2/2"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "For all square matrices A and B of the same size, AB = BA.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Matrix multiplication is generally not commutative. For example, with A = [[1,0],[0,0]] and B = [[0,1],[0,0]], AB = [[0,1],[0,0]] while BA = [[0,0],[0,0]], which are not equal.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Compute the distance between the points (−3, 4) and (1, −2).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Distance formula: √[(1 − (−3))^2 + (−2 − 4)^2] = √[(4)^2 + (−6)^2] = √(16 + 36) = √52 = 2√13.", "correctAnswer": "2√13"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the system of equations: 3x + 2y = 14 and x − y = 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x − y = 1, get x = 1 + y. Substitute into 3x + 2y = 14: 3(1 + y) + 2y = 14 ⇒ 3 + 3y + 2y = 14 ⇒ 5y = 11 ⇒ y = 11/5. Then x = 1 + 11/5 = 16/5.", "correctAnswer": "x = 16/5, y = 11/5"}, {"id": "q_1762409824568_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["11", "1", "-11", "13"], "question": "Find the determinant of the matrix [[2, −1], [5, 3]].", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a 2×2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]], det = ad − bc. Here, det = 2·3 − (−1)·5 = 6 + 5 = 11.", "correctAnswer": "11"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:17:04.582364	\N
bd2e1121-e2ef-466d-ba59-54fc713faecd	924e2bce-af7e-4e7d-9ced-7823c40345f1	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409880422_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "7"], "question": "What is 9 − 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "9", "12", "15"], "question": "Which number is even?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2. 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so 12 is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["18", "16", "12", "20"], "question": "What is 3 × 6?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/3", "3/4", "1/4", "2/4"], "question": "Simplify 1/2 + 1/4.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Find a common denominator (4): 1/2 = 2/4, so 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "3/4"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "15", "10", "18"], "question": "What is the area of a rectangle with length 5 and width 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width: 5 × 3 = 15 square units.", "correctAnswer": "15"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/2", "1/4", "2/3", "3/5"], "question": "Which fraction is equal to 0.5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 means half of 1, which is 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "12", "16", "20"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a square with side length 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A square has 4 equal sides, so perimeter = 4 × 4 = 16 units.", "correctAnswer": "16"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "15 is a multiple of 3.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "15 = 3 × 5, so it is a multiple of 3.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A triangle has four sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has exactly three sides.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "0 is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is divisible by 2. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "2.75 is greater than 2.7.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Comparing decimals: 2.75 has more hundredths than 2.70, so it is larger.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "8 ÷ 2 = ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division splits a quantity into equal parts: 8 divided by 2 equals 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The place value of 7 in the number 372 is ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 372, the digits represent hundreds (3), tens (7), and ones (2). The 7 is in the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: 2, 4, 6, ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This sequence increases by 2 each time: 2, 4, 6, 8.", "correctAnswer": "8"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A right angle measures ___ degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a right angle is 90 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the median of the set {3, 5, 7}?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The median is the middle value when numbers are ordered. 3, 5, 7 has 5 in the middle.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Convert 100 centimeters to meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 100 centimeters in 1 meter, so 100 cm = 1 m.", "correctAnswer": "1"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of x if x + 5 = 12?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 5 from both sides: x = 12 − 5 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1762409880422_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the shape with 5 sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A polygon with 5 sides is called a pentagon.", "correctAnswer": "pentagon"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:18:00.515163	\N
41802bfb-6352-4d32-9745-758a86c7e7fb	402d3dbb-d28b-4728-aaa9-255d43f2c2cd	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409883209_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["13", "18", "21", "25"], "question": "Which of the following is an even number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 without a remainder. 18 ÷ 2 = 9, so 18 is even.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "24"], "question": "What is the value of 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 3 × 4 means 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ones", "Tens", "Hundreds", "Thousands"], "question": "In the number 352, what is the place value of the digit 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "352 has 2 in the ones place, 5 in the tens place, and 3 in the hundreds place. So 5 represents 50.", "correctAnswer": "Tens"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "6", "7", "9"], "question": "Simplify: 15 − 9", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 9 from 15 gives 6 because 15 − 9 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "3/8", "4/6", "1/3"], "question": "Which fraction is equal to one half?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies by dividing numerator and denominator by 2: 2/4 = 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "15", "16", "20"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 5 and width 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). 2 × (5 + 3) = 2 × 8 = 16.", "correctAnswer": "16"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles in a triangle is 180 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In Euclidean geometry, all triangles have interior angles that add up to 180 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "9 is a prime number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A prime number has exactly two distinct positive divisors: 1 and itself. 9 has divisors 1, 3, and 9, so it is not prime.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square is a quadrilateral with four equal sides and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Multiplying a number by 1 changes its value.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying by 1 leaves a number unchanged because n × 1 = n.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "8 + __ = 13", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing addend, subtract: 13 − 8 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The value of 20 ÷ 5 is __.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division asks how many groups of 5 are in 20. 20 ÷ 5 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "__ is the smallest whole number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Whole numbers start at 0 and increase: 0, 1, 2, 3, …", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "100 centimeters equal __ meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 100 centimeters in 1 meter, so 100 cm = 1 m.", "correctAnswer": "1"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the next number in the sequence: 2, 4, 6, 8, __?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time (even numbers). After 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Round 47 to the nearest ten.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "47 is closer to 50 than to 40 because the ones digit (7) is 5 or more, so we round up.", "correctAnswer": "50"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the area of a rectangle with length 6 units and width 2 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width. 6 × 2 = 12 square units.", "correctAnswer": "12 square units"}, {"id": "q_1762409883209_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve for x: x + 3 = 9", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 3 from both sides: x = 9 − 3 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:18:03.222852	\N
67cd633a-d752-426e-ae74-ac79c8fe27c3	0d02103f-ac59-4e60-816d-31043b43851f	Humanities - Hard	Humanities	hard	[{"id": "q_1762771575599_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A structuralist model privileging economic determinism and long-run statistical series over lived experience", "A cultural-materialist approach emphasizing class as a historical relationship reconstructed from workers’ lived experience and agency", "A Whig narrative asserting the inevitability of liberal progress driven by elite reformers", "A cliometric framework treating actors as rational utility maximizers within efficient markets"], "question": "In The Making of the English Working Class, which historiographical approach best characterizes E. P. Thompson’s method for understanding class formation?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Thompson rejects both rigid structural determinism and Whig teleology, arguing that class is not a thing but a historical relationship forged through struggle, culture, and experience. He seeks to recover workers’ agency and consciousness, critiquing the “enormous condescension of posterity.” This aligns with a cultural-materialist lens attentive to practice, culture, and conflict, rather than purely econometric or elite-driven narratives.", "correctAnswer": "A cultural-materialist approach emphasizing class as a historical relationship reconstructed from workers’ lived experience and agency"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It promotes didactic clarity through restrained emotion and minimal sensory engagement to avoid idolatry", "It uses theatricality, sensory intensity, and decorum to elicit affective piety and affirm doctrines such as saintly intercession", "It rejects sculptural illusionism in favor of iconoclastic minimalism consistent with Protestant critiques", "It confines itself to scriptural narratives only, excluding hagiographic accounts to ensure doctrinal purity"], "question": "Which interpretation best explains how Bernini’s Cornaro Chapel (The Ecstasy of Saint Teresa) aligns with Counter-Reformation aims articulated after the Council of Trent?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Post-Tridentine art was to be clear, decorous, and emotionally compelling, fostering devotion and educating the faithful. Bernini’s integrated use of sculpture, architecture, and light stages a visceral encounter designed to move viewers and legitimize Catholic practices like the veneration of saints, consistent with the Counter-Reformation’s pedagogical and affective goals.", "correctAnswer": "It uses theatricality, sensory intensity, and decorum to elicit affective piety and affirm doctrines such as saintly intercession"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Zero focalization", "Internal focalization", "External focalization", "Variable internal focalization"], "question": "In Gérard Genette’s narratology, a narrator who knows more than any character and can report any event exemplifies which type of focalization?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Genette distinguishes focalization (who sees) from narration (who speaks). Zero focalization (often labeled omniscience) occurs when the narrator’s knowledge exceeds that of all characters. Internal focalization confines knowledge to a character’s perspective, external focalization limits narration to observable behavior, and variable internal shifts between characters’ internal viewpoints.", "correctAnswer": "Zero focalization"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A linguist documenting Arabic dialects using collaborative methods that foreground local scholars’ analyses", "A 19th-century European travelogue depicting the Middle East as timeless, sensual, and irrational, informing colonial policy under a ‘civilizing’ rationale", "A co-taught seminar where Muslim and Christian theologians reciprocally critique doctrinal claims", "A postcolonial novel that satirizes Western stereotypes about the ‘East’ from a subaltern vantage"], "question": "Which scenario most accurately exemplifies Edward Said’s concept of Orientalism as a discourse of power/knowledge?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "For Said, Orientalism is a Western discourse that constructs the ‘Orient’ through essentializing tropes that authorize and naturalize imperial domination. Travelogues, philology, and policy reports often produced knowledge that depicted the East as exotic, backward, and in need of governance—thus entwining representation with power.", "correctAnswer": "A 19th-century European travelogue depicting the Middle East as timeless, sensual, and irrational, informing colonial policy under a ‘civilizing’ rationale"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Contemporary cognitive science largely endorses the strong version of the Sapir–Whorf hypothesis, claiming that language determines thought categories.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Modern research supports linguistic relativity in a weak sense—language can influence attention, memory, and categorization—but does not endorse strict linguistic determinism. Cross-linguistic studies show cognitive flexibility and non-linguistic influences on thought that contradict a strong Whorfian position.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "For Michel Foucault, power is best understood as a top-down juridical force that primarily represses subjects rather than producing them.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Foucault reframes power as diffuse, capillary, and productive—operating through disciplinary techniques, surveillance, and normalization to constitute subjects and fields of knowledge. While juridical forms exist, they do not exhaust the modalities of modern power, which works through the microphysics of everyday practices.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Homi K. Bhabha describes the ambivalent ‘in-between’ cultural space that unsettles colonial authority and fixed identities as ________.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Bhabha’s ‘hybridity’ conceptualizes how colonial encounters produce new, ambivalent forms that mimic yet transform power. This liminality destabilizes claims to cultural purity and undermines binary oppositions between colonizer and colonized by generating slippages in meaning and authority.", "correctAnswer": "hybridity"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In continuity editing, the convention that preserves consistent screen direction by keeping the camera on one side of an imaginary line between subjects is the __________.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "The 180-degree rule maintains spatial coherence by anchoring the camera to one side of the axis of action. Crossing the line without motivated strategies (e.g., neutral axis shots) risks disorienting the viewer, disrupting classical continuity’s legibility.", "correctAnswer": "180-degree rule"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "According to Roland Barthes’ essay ‘The Death of the Author,’ where does the locus of a text’s meaning reside?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Barthes argues that privileging authorial intention constrains interpretation; meaning emerges from the play of language and the reader’s engagement. The ‘birth of the reader’ displaces the author as the guarantor of a single, authoritative meaning.", "correctAnswer": "In the reader and in language rather than in the author’s intent"}, {"id": "q_1762771575599_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the Northwest Coast ceremonial exchange system discussed by Marcel Mauss as a paradigmatic ‘total social fact’ of competitive gift-giving.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Mauss analyzed the potlatch to show how gifts entail obligations to give, receive, and reciprocate, intertwining legal, religious, economic, and political dimensions. The potlatch’s competitive generosity illuminates how social bonds and status are constituted through exchange.", "correctAnswer": "Potlatch"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:46:15.612001	\N
df0177fb-58f9-48dc-b743-17de71bcc474	05903f07-aff8-4075-b4f4-d76422f33b3b	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1762771581232_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Primary Unit", "Central Programming Unit", "Control Process Unit"], "question": "What does CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is the 'brain' of the computer, responsible for executing instructions and processing data.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A mouse is an input device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer for processing. A mouse allows the user to move a pointer and select items, making it an input device.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The software that manages computer hardware and provides services for programs is called the ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (OS) manages hardware resources, provides a user interface, and offers services so application software can run (e.g., Windows, macOS, Linux).", "correctAnswer": "operating system"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".jpg", ".exe", ".txt", ".pptx"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for an image?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (or .jpeg) is a common image format. .exe is a program, .txt is plain text, and .pptx is a presentation file.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does ICT stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ICT stands for Information and Communication Technology, which covers technologies used to handle information and facilitate communication, such as computers, networks, and software.", "correctAnswer": "Information and Communication Technology"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM permanently stores data even when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) is volatile, meaning it loses its contents when power is off. Permanent storage is provided by devices like SSDs or HDDs.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A ______ is a network device that forwards data packets between computer networks, directing traffic.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A router connects multiple networks and routes data between them, choosing the best path for data to travel across the internet or local networks.", "correctAnswer": "router"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Google Chrome", "Microsoft Word", "Adobe Photoshop", "VLC Media Player"], "question": "Which of the following is a web browser?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Web browsers (e.g., Google Chrome, Firefox) are used to access and view web pages. Word is a word processor, Photoshop is image editing software, and VLC is a media player.", "correctAnswer": "Google Chrome"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Cloud storage allows you to save files on remote servers accessed via the internet.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cloud storage services (like Google Drive or Dropbox) store files on remote servers, letting you access them from different devices via the internet.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771581232_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Kilobyte (KB)", "Megabyte (MB)", "Gigabyte (GB)", "Terabyte (TB)"], "question": "Which is the largest unit of digital storage?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Storage units increase in size from KB to MB to GB to TB. A terabyte is larger than a gigabyte, megabyte, and kilobyte.", "correctAnswer": "Terabyte (TB)"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:46:21.24553	\N
d7d53994-f1ee-4929-9a91-7759801cd70f	60bd5f33-dc5c-4c4d-9202-ce52bef2d1f2	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409903079_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "question": "What is 2 + 2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 2 and 2 gives 4. You can count: 2, 3, 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "5", "6", "7"], "question": "What is 3 + 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "3 + 3 means two groups of 3, which totals 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["50", "0", "100", "1"], "question": "What is 50 - 50?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting a number from itself always gives 0, so 50 − 50 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["100", "0", "50", "150"], "question": "What does \\"50 out of 100\\" equal?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "50 out of 100 means the amount is 50 when the total is 100 (that is half of 100).", "correctAnswer": "50"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 2 + 2 equals 4.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2 + 2 = 4 is a basic addition fact.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 3 + 3 equals 5.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "3 + 3 equals 6, not 5.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 50 out of 100 equals 50.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "If the total is 100, having 50 out of that total is 50.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "2 + 2 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding two and two results in four.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "3 + __ = 6", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To make 6 from 3, you need to add another 3 because 3 + 3 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "3"}, {"id": "q_1762409903079_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If your score is 50 out of 100, what number is your score?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "50 out of a total of 100 means the score is 50.", "correctAnswer": "50"}]	1393d14b-212d-4478-84b9-b52ef4073059	2025-11-06 06:18:23.091967	\N
7081fa88-b44f-45c2-a8da-2c98e457be7f	255a22c9-d03b-43d9-906a-f458ba3089eb	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409934066_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["80", "90", "95", "100"], "question": "What is 45 + 45?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add tens and ones: 40 + 40 = 80 and 5 + 5 = 10. Then 80 + 10 = 90, so 45 + 45 = 90.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "45 plus 45 equals 90.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the given fact: two 45s make 90. It’s the same as doubling 45.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "45 + ____ = 90", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Find the missing addend by subtraction: 90 − 45 = 45. So you need another 45 to reach 90.", "correctAnswer": "45"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the sum of two numbers that are both 45?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding two identical numbers is doubling: 45 + 45 = 90.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["85", "90", "100", "45"], "question": "What number do you get when you double 45?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Doubling means multiplying by 2. Since 2 × 45 = 90, doubling 45 gives 90.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "90 divided by 2 equals 45.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division is the inverse of multiplication. Because 2 × 45 = 90, 90 ÷ 2 = 45.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "If you have 45 minutes and add another 45 minutes, you have ____ minutes in total.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Time adds like numbers: 45 + 45 = 90 minutes (which is 1 hour and 30 minutes).", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["35", "45", "50", "60"], "question": "What is 90 − 45?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtraction undoes addition. Since 45 + 45 = 90, removing one 45 from 90 leaves 45.", "correctAnswer": "45"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "45 is half of 90.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Half means dividing by 2. 90 ÷ 2 = 45, so 45 is exactly half of 90.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409934066_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "2 × 45 = ____", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying 45 by 2 is the same as adding it to itself once: 45 + 45 = 90.", "correctAnswer": "90"}]	6395e083-98ae-4550-b11a-fcd36be498aa	2025-11-06 06:18:54.14918	\N
44015fff-0364-44aa-88c5-fdd669c31316	a79afcac-aea6-4bae-9a80-fdb2738f7a44	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762409988387_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. You can count on from 7: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 (five steps).", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762409988387_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2 with no remainder. Since 0 ÷ 2 = 0 with no remainder, 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762409988387_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 - 4 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5. You can count back: 9 → 8 (1), 7 (2), 6 (3), 5 (4).", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762409988387_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the name of a polygon with three sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle is a three-sided polygon with three angles. The prefix “tri-” means three.", "correctAnswer": "triangle"}, {"id": "q_1762409988387_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "8", "34"], "question": "In the number 348, which digit is in the tens place?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Place value from right to left is ones, tens, hundreds. In 348, 8 is ones, 4 is tens, and 3 is hundreds.", "correctAnswer": "4"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:19:48.400387	\N
e4588e7f-b3bf-4026-a2a8-dd4670087287	8b8ef1ac-90ee-474f-aae2-365b5e6f6e60	maths - Easy	maths	easy	[{"id": "q_1762410095021_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 combines two groups of ones: 7 + 5 = 12. You can count up from 7 by five (8, 9, 10, 11, 12) to get the result.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762410095021_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are integers divisible by 2 with no remainder. Since 0 ÷ 2 = 0, zero is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410095021_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "7 + __ = 10", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing number, think of how much you need to add to 7 to reach 10. 10 − 7 = 3, so 7 + 3 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "3"}, {"id": "q_1762410095021_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 6 cm and width 2 cm?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle’s perimeter is the sum of all sides: 6 + 6 + 2 + 2 = 16 cm. Equivalently, use the formula 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (6 + 2) = 16 cm.", "correctAnswer": "16 cm"}, {"id": "q_1762410095021_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4", "7", "40", "47"], "question": "In the number 47, what digit is in the tens place?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In a two-digit number, the left digit is the tens place. In 47, the 4 represents 4 tens (which is 40).", "correctAnswer": "4"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:21:35.034509	\N
353129a4-de8a-48aa-9c87-f69426f3da1b	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762410129382_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. Think of 7 + 3 = 10, then add the remaining 2 to get 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "30", "300", "3,000"], "question": "In the number 3,405, what is the value of the digit 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The digit 3 is in the thousands place, so its value is 3 thousands, which is 3,000.", "correctAnswer": "3,000"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "15", "21"], "question": "Which of the following is an even number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 without a remainder. 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so 12 is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "24"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 4 and width 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (4 + 3) = 2 × 7 = 14.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "question": "Simplify: 8 − 3", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 3 from 8 leaves 5 because 8 − 3 counts down three steps: 7, 6, 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "3/6", "4/8", "All of the above"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dividing numerator and denominator of 1/2 by the same number keeps the value the same. 2/4, 3/6, and 4/8 all simplify to 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "All of the above"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "10", "12", "16"], "question": "What number comes next in the sequence: 2, 4, 6, 8, __?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time. After 8, adding 2 gives 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0", "1", "5", "10"], "question": "What is 5 × 0?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Any number multiplied by zero equals zero, because there are zero groups of the number.", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "A triangle has three sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a triangle is a polygon with exactly three sides.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles in a rectangle is 360 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has four right angles (each 90 degrees). 4 × 90° = 360°.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "The number 9 is a prime number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A prime number has exactly two positive factors: 1 and itself. 9 has factors 1, 3, and 9, so it is not prime.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "0 is less than 1.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "On the number line, 0 is to the left of 1, which means it is smaller.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "The number 1,000 has three zeros.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "1,000 is written as 1000 and contains three zeros after the 1.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "The commutative property of addition states that a + b = b + a.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Commutative property means order does not change the sum: adding in any order gives the same result.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "15 ÷ 3 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division asks how many groups of 3 are in 15. There are 5 groups because 3 × 5 = 15.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The value of 7 squared (7^2) is __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Squaring a number means multiplying it by itself: 7 × 7 = 49.", "correctAnswer": "49"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The decimal 0.5 is equal to __ as a fraction.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 means half of 1, which is represented as the fraction 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the place value (value) of the digit 6 in the number 264?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 6 is in the tens place, so its value is 6 tens, which equals 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the shape that has four equal sides and four right angles.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A square is a rectangle with all sides equal length and all angles 90 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "Square"}, {"id": "q_1762410129382_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the smallest whole number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Whole numbers start at 0 and increase by 1: 0, 1, 2, 3, ...", "correctAnswer": "0"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:22:09.485984	\N
1f829975-7d22-4eae-adc2-39a1cf61e8a9	ac2dbd58-1e47-4429-9925-827a237fd594	science notes - Hard	science notes	hard	[{"id": "q_1762410217045_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Sudden slip along pre-existing faults releases stored elastic energy, generating seismic waves that propagate through the crust.", "Plates must physically split into separate pieces; only then do waves form.", "Magma explosions at the surface are the primary cause of all earthquakes.", "Earthquakes occur only when plates move apart; they never happen where plates slide past or collide."], "question": "Evaluate the notes’ statement that \\"an earthquake forms when the plates crack and break apart and sends shock waves to the crust.\\" Which explanation best captures the actual mechanism of most earthquakes?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "The notes oversimplify the mechanism by focusing on plates cracking apart. In reality, stress accumulates in rocks over time; when frictional resistance is overcome, faults slip suddenly, releasing elastic energy as seismic (P and S) waves. This process can occur at subduction, transform, and even intraplate settings without the plates literally breaking into new pieces.", "correctAnswer": "Sudden slip along pre-existing faults releases stored elastic energy, generating seismic waves that propagate through the crust."}, {"id": "q_1762410217045_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A shallow undersea megathrust earthquake that vertically displaces a large area of the seafloor.", "A deep-focus earthquake 600 km beneath the ocean that causes shaking but minimal seafloor displacement.", "A surface windstorm that creates large waves over several hours.", "A small magnitude 3.0 earthquake near a lake shore."], "question": "The notes mention earthquakes can happen underwater and be associated with a big tsunami. Which scenario is most likely to generate a tsunami?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Tsunamis require rapid, large-scale displacement of water. The most efficient way to achieve this is vertical movement of the seafloor during a shallow megathrust event (common at subduction zones). Deep-focus earthquakes occur too far beneath the surface to displace the ocean effectively, wind waves are meteorological and not tsunamis, and small inland quakes lack the scale and setting to generate tsunamis.", "correctAnswer": "A shallow undersea megathrust earthquake that vertically displaces a large area of the seafloor."}, {"id": "q_1762410217045_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "The strategy \\"hiding under furniture\\" as stated in the notes is sufficient and universally safe during all earthquakes.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "While seeking cover is part of recommended practice, the comprehensive guidance is “Drop, Cover, and Hold On” under sturdy furniture that can protect from falling debris. Not all furniture is safe (glass-topped tables, unstable items), and additional precautions—staying away from windows, securing heavy objects, and being prepared for aftershocks—are essential. Context matters: in some buildings, evacuation after shaking may be necessary due to structural damage.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762410217045_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank to correct the cause-and-effect implied in the notes: Sudden fault slip releases energy as seismic waves; those waves are the shaking we experience as ______.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Earthquakes are the felt shaking caused by seismic waves radiating from a sudden release of energy at a fault. The notes invert the causal order by implying waves cause the earthquake; in reality, the rupture (fault slip) comes first, and the waves are what we experience as the earthquake.", "correctAnswer": "an earthquake"}, {"id": "q_1762410217045_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Critique the notes’ description of a 2015 earthquake in the Philippines that \\"killed 9000 people died and 25000 were injured which a big tsunami.\\" Identify at least two issues in clarity or accuracy, and suggest how to revise the statement to be scientifically and journalistically sound.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Scientific reporting should be precise, evidence-based, and clearly written. Redundant or ungrammatical phrasing reduces clarity, and unverified casualty figures can misinform. Including the event’s parameters (magnitude, setting, mechanism), the causal link to a tsunami (vertical seafloor displacement), and citations to authoritative sources (e.g., national agencies) makes the account accurate and useful.", "correctAnswer": "The description is unclear and likely inaccurate: (1) It redundantly states “killed 9000 people died” and uses ungrammatical phrasing (“which a big tsunami”), obscuring meaning. (2) It lacks essential details (location, magnitude, depth, mechanism, and verified sources) and may conflate events or figures. A sound revision would specify the exact earthquake (date, region within the Philippines), its magnitude and mechanism (e.g., megathrust), whether a tsunami occurred and why, and cite authoritative casualty figures from credible sources, written clearly (e.g., “The M7.x subduction-zone earthquake off [region] on [date] generated a tsunami due to seafloor uplift; official reports cite approximately [verified number] fatalities and [verified number] injuries.”)."}]	43883661-ac43-4dc0-aaed-ac3897b502cf	2025-11-06 06:23:37.05798	\N
69c38de7-fe06-4872-8b92-d1ee809fdfef	69cc647a-77c9-4376-b3bc-f2a160104dec	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762410222282_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants primarily take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants take in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil to produce glucose and release oxygen. Carbon dioxide is the key carbon source used to build sugars.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1762410222282_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The boiling point of pure water at sea level is 100°C.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm) found at sea level, pure water boils at 100°C. Changes in altitude and impurities can shift the boiling point.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410222282_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The force that pulls objects toward Earth's center is called ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. It keeps us on the ground and causes objects to fall toward Earth's center.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1762410222282_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the smallest unit of life that can carry out all life processes.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The cell is the basic unit of life. It can grow, reproduce, respond to stimuli, and carry out metabolism, making it the fundamental building block of living organisms.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1762410222282_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Electron", "Proton", "Neutron", "Nucleus"], "question": "Which part of the atom has a positive charge?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Protons carry a positive charge and are located in the atom’s nucleus alongside neutral neutrons. Electrons have a negative charge and orbit the nucleus.", "correctAnswer": "Proton"}]	none	2025-11-06 06:23:42.294832	\N
ac436f47-2001-4e38-ab88-6e9a02a80c20	ac2dbd58-1e47-4429-9925-827a237fd594	science notes - Medium	science notes	medium	[{"id": "q_1762410259393_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hide under sturdy furniture and protect your head, then hold on", "Stand in a doorway and watch for falling objects", "Run outside immediately to get away from the building", "Use the elevator to reach the ground floor quickly"], "question": "You are indoors when a strong earthquake begins. Based on the notes and standard safety guidance, which action should you take first?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The notes advise surviving an earthquake by hiding under furniture. This aligns with the widely recommended “Drop, Cover, and Hold On” guidance: get under a sturdy table or desk, protect your head and neck, and hold on until shaking stops. Running outside, using elevators, or standing in doorways are riskier because of falling debris and moving hazards.", "correctAnswer": "Hide under sturdy furniture and protect your head, then hold on"}, {"id": "q_1762410259393_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Earthquakes can form when stress causes parts of Earth’s plates to crack or slip, releasing energy that travels as shock waves through the crust.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The notes describe plates cracking and sending shock waves. Scientifically, earthquakes result from a sudden release of stored stress along faults in the crust or at plate boundaries. That energy radiates as seismic waves (shock waves) through the Earth, producing shaking at the surface.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410259393_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "An underwater earthquake that significantly displaces the seafloor can generate a large ______ that affects coastal areas.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Underwater earthquakes can transfer energy to the overlying water by moving the seafloor, creating long waves called tsunamis. These waves travel across the ocean and can cause severe coastal flooding when they reach shore.", "correctAnswer": "tsunami"}, {"id": "q_1762410259393_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Accept the figures as accurate because they appear specific", "Cross-check the claim with reliable sources and note that the wording and numbers may be inaccurate or unclear", "Assume all earthquakes cause tsunamis, so the claim must be correct", "Ignore casualty figures and focus only on the year mentioned"], "question": "The notes claim a 2015 earthquake in the Philippines killed 9,000, injured 25,000, and caused a “big tsunami.” What is the best critical response for a science student?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Scientific literacy includes evaluating the reliability of claims. Unsourced casualty numbers and vague phrasing should be verified against authoritative data (e.g., government or scientific agencies). Fact-checking helps avoid spreading misinformation and clarifies whether a tsunami occurred and what the actual impacts were.", "correctAnswer": "Cross-check the claim with reliable sources and note that the wording and numbers may be inaccurate or unclear"}, {"id": "q_1762410259393_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain how shock waves from an earthquake can be felt at the surface even if the quake starts deep underground or underwater.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When a fault slips, it releases energy as seismic waves (P and S waves) that propagate through the Earth. These waves move efficiently through solid materials and reach the surface, causing shaking. Even if the source is beneath the ocean, the waves travel through the crust and can be felt on land; separate water displacement can produce a tsunami.", "correctAnswer": "Seismic waves radiate outward from the rupture through Earth’s layers (rock and, if underwater, the crust beneath the ocean). As they travel, they carry energy to the surface, where the ground shakes; underwater quakes still send waves through solid Earth, so coastal land can feel the shaking."}]	43883661-ac43-4dc0-aaed-ac3897b502cf	2025-11-06 06:24:19.405634	\N
c41d6eaf-62ac-42af-af97-101d18d6809b	255a22c9-d03b-43d9-906a-f458ba3089eb	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1762410269562_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North", "Northeast", "East", "Southeast"], "question": "A robot is initially facing north. It turns 45° clockwise, then another 45° clockwise. Which direction is it facing now?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Each 45° turn adds up: 45° + 45° = 90°. A 90° clockwise turn from north points east.", "correctAnswer": "East"}, {"id": "q_1762410269562_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: In an isosceles right triangle, the two acute angles are 45° each, so together they sum to a right angle (90°).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A right triangle has one 90° angle, leaving 90° for the other two. In an isosceles right triangle the acute angles are equal, so each is 45°, and 45° + 45° = 90°.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410269562_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: Rotating a figure by 45° and then by 45° more gives a total rotation of ___ degrees.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total rotation adds: 45 + 45 = 90 degrees, which is a quarter turn (a right angle).", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762410269562_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You plan a 90-minute study block split evenly into two sessions. How many minutes is each session?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Splitting 90 minutes into two equal parts gives 45 minutes each because 45 + 45 = 90.", "correctAnswer": "45 minutes"}, {"id": "q_1762410269562_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3:45 PM", "4:00 PM", "4:45 PM", "5:00 PM"], "question": "A bus leaves at 3:15 PM, travels 45 minutes to a transfer station, then waits 45 minutes before the next leg. At what time does the second leg depart?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total time before the second leg is 45 minutes of travel plus 45 minutes of waiting: 45 + 45 = 90 minutes. Adding 90 minutes to 3:15 PM gives 4:45 PM.", "correctAnswer": "4:45 PM"}]	6395e083-98ae-4550-b11a-fcd36be498aa	2025-11-06 06:24:29.576174	\N
29f50d08-6858-474e-a5e8-7ed4c7fc8a4a	60bd5f33-dc5c-4c4d-9202-ce52bef2d1f2	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1762410306243_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/2", "50/1", "2/3", "1/4"], "question": "If 50 out of 100 marbles are blue, what fraction of the marbles are blue?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "“50 out of 100” means 50/100. Dividing numerator and denominator by 50 simplifies 50/100 to 1/2, which represents half the marbles.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1762410306243_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 100 - 50 - 50 = 0.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Subtracting 50 from 100 gives 50. Then 50 - 50 = 0, matching the given subtraction fact and showing the total is reduced to zero.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410306243_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Add the sum 2+2 to 50. The result is __.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First compute 2+2 = 4. Then add this to 50: 50 + 4 = 54.", "correctAnswer": "54"}, {"id": "q_1762410306243_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If 3+3 more students join a group that has 50 out of 100 members, how many members are there now, and what percent of 100 is that?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Compute the increase: 3+3 = 6. Add to the current count: 50 + 6 = 56. Out of 100 total, 56 out of 100 corresponds to 56%, indicating a bit more than half.", "correctAnswer": "56 members, 56%"}, {"id": "q_1762410306243_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["40", "44", "36", "50"], "question": "Evaluate: 100 - (3+3) - (2+2) - 50.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First compute the additions: 3+3 = 6 and 2+2 = 4. Then subtract step by step: 100 - 6 = 94, 94 - 4 = 90, and 90 - 50 = 40.", "correctAnswer": "40"}]	1393d14b-212d-4478-84b9-b52ef4073059	2025-11-06 06:25:06.340513	\N
dfb6ebd0-a5fe-42f6-b285-9db41bedf44c	e6820b4e-a083-4369-9899-9a09a5fd34b7	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762410348285_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["to support your body", "to digest food", "to make light", "to carry oxygen"], "question": "What is one main job of the bones in your body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bones form a framework that helps support your body, keeping it upright and giving it shape, as stated in the content.", "correctAnswer": "to support your body"}, {"id": "q_1762410348285_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Bones in your body help to support your body.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This statement directly matches the content: bones help to support your body.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410348285_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: The bones in your body help to _____ your body.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The missing word is 'support' because bones provide support to your body.", "correctAnswer": "support"}, {"id": "q_1762410348285_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In a few words, what do bones help do for your body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "According to the content, bones help to support your body.", "correctAnswer": "support your body"}, {"id": "q_1762410348285_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["bones", "eyelashes", "teeth", "stomach acid"], "question": "Which of these mainly helps support your body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bones provide the body with support and structure, which the other options do not.", "correctAnswer": "bones"}]	bf8b4fb6-8e31-43fa-a633-6858667811d1	2025-11-06 06:25:48.377046	\N
3966bfda-d809-433f-a320-9052927ec6a6	61aa1958-2f4d-4e74-a997-3876b9476362	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762410358827_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["50", "60", "70", "90"], "question": "What is 30 + 30?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 30 and 30 combines three tens with another three tens, making six tens, which is 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "30 plus 30 equals 60.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "30 + 30 is the same as doubling 30, and double of 30 is 60.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "30 + ___ = 60", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To reach 60 from 30, you add another 30 because 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "30"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What number do you get when you double 30?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Doubling a number means adding it to itself: 30 + 30 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["25 + 25", "40 + 10", "30 + 30", "20 + 30"], "question": "Which expression equals 60?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "30 + 30 is 60. The other sums are 50 (25 + 25, 40 + 10, and 20 + 30).", "correctAnswer": "30 + 30"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "60 is the sum of two 30s.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding two 30s gives 30 + 30, which equals 60.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The sum of thirty and thirty is ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Thirty plus thirty makes sixty because three tens plus three tens equals six tens.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30", "60", "90", "120"], "question": "If you start at 30 and add 30 more, what total do you reach?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Starting at 30 and adding another 30 gives 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "60 is half of 30.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "60 is double 30, not half. Half of 30 would be 15.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762410358827_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45", "60", "30", "90"], "question": "Which number is twice 30?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Twice 30 means 30 × 2, which equals 60.", "correctAnswer": "60"}]	0aa12fa7-a6a7-4276-a7c2-2b0e1400cc82	2025-11-06 06:25:58.841102	\N
628ee8a5-b720-4c30-a909-c17aa2df253b	328b3f62-7fca-4f57-8aba-ed4ced704635	English - Medium	English	medium	[{"id": "q_1762438783279_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hurrying to finish the essay, the printer jammed.", "Hurrying to finish the essay, I was interrupted when the printer jammed.", "Hurrying to finish the essay, the jammed printer delayed me.", "The printer, hurrying to finish the essay, jammed."], "question": "Which revision best fixes the dangling modifier in the sentence: \\"Hurrying to finish the essay, the printer jammed.\\"?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A modifier at the start of a sentence should clearly modify the subject that follows. In the original, the modifier appears to describe the printer, which is illogical. The correct revision makes the person (I) the subject being modified by \\"Hurrying to finish the essay,\\" thereby removing the dangling modifier.", "correctAnswer": "Hurrying to finish the essay, I was interrupted when the printer jammed."}, {"id": "q_1762438783279_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: A semicolon may join two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction, but it should not link an independent clause to a dependent clause.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Semicolons properly connect two independent clauses (e.g., \\"The deadline is tomorrow; we need to hurry.\\"). They do not connect an independent clause to a dependent clause (e.g., not \\"Because it was late; we stopped\\"), which requires a comma or different structure.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762438783279_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb form to complete the mandative subjunctive: \\"The committee recommends that the policy ____ revised immediately.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "After verbs of suggestion or recommendation (e.g., recommend, insist), English often uses the mandative subjunctive: base form of the verb without tense markers. Therefore, \\"be revised\\" is correct, not \\"is\\" or \\"was\\" revised.", "correctAnswer": "be"}, {"id": "q_1762438783279_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["My sister who loves jazz is moving to Chicago.", "My sister, who loves jazz, is moving to Chicago.", "My sister who loves jazz, is moving to Chicago.", "My sister, who loves jazz is moving to Chicago."], "question": "Given that the speaker has only one sister, which sentence correctly punctuates the nonrestrictive clause?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When the clause adds extra information about a unique noun (only one sister), it is nonrestrictive and should be set off with commas. Without commas, the clause would be restrictive, implying the speaker has multiple sisters and specifying which one.", "correctAnswer": "My sister, who loves jazz, is moving to Chicago."}, {"id": "q_1762438783279_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Identify the verb tense used in the sentence: \\"By next July, she will have been working here for ten years.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This tense combines \\"will have been\\" plus the present participle (working) to express an action that will be ongoing up to a specific future point. It emphasizes duration continuing until that future time.", "correctAnswer": "future perfect continuous"}]	none	2025-11-06 14:19:43.382684	\N
0864f405-9200-4020-8c54-11bebc8f94e6	328b3f62-7fca-4f57-8aba-ed4ced704635	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1762439622130_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["dog", "runs", "fast", "the"], "question": "Which word is a verb in the sentence: \\"The dog runs fast\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A verb is an action word. In the sentence, \\"runs\\" shows the action the dog does, so it is the verb.", "correctAnswer": "runs"}, {"id": "q_1762439622130_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A sentence should start with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In English, every sentence begins with a capital letter to show the start of a new thought.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762439622131_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb: \\"The apples ___ red.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"are\\" with plural nouns like \\"apples.\\" \\"Is\\" is used with singular nouns.", "correctAnswer": "are"}, {"id": "q_1762439622131_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Most regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed to the base form: walk → walked.", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1762439622131_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"She has ___ apple.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"an\\" before words that start with a vowel sound, like \\"apple.\\"", "correctAnswer": "an"}]	none	2025-11-06 14:33:42.219433	\N
1dd41dfd-1b2e-42d2-a970-55900609b623	13f64334-b200-47a3-a85a-4f833920c622	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762449030496_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Helium"], "question": "Which gas do plants absorb from the air during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants take in carbon dioxide (CO2) from the air and water from the soil to produce glucose and oxygen using sunlight. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1762449030496_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth takes about 24 hours to complete one full rotation on its axis.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "One full rotation of the Earth on its axis takes approximately 24 hours, which defines the length of a day and causes the cycle of day and night.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762449030496_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The boiling point of pure water at sea level is ____ degrees Celsius.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (1 atmosphere) at sea level, pure water boils at 100°C. Changes in altitude and pressure can shift the boiling point.", "correctAnswer": "100"}, {"id": "q_1762449030496_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the basic unit of structure and function in all living organisms?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The cell is the smallest unit of life that can perform all life processes. All organisms are made of one or more cells.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1762449030496_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Thermal energy", "Kinetic energy", "Chemical energy", "Potential energy"], "question": "Which type of energy is associated with motion?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because of its motion. Potential energy is stored energy, while thermal and chemical energy relate to heat and chemical bonds, respectively.", "correctAnswer": "Kinetic energy"}]	none	2025-11-06 17:10:30.620671	\N
85c1f9fb-3ffd-4bc0-8f05-03fbc18da3b9	9ea5a8a0-4dae-413c-8a6a-ce5c18e46dae	Human Biology - Medium	Human Biology	medium	[{"id": "q_1762534972166_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Increased insulin release from pancreatic beta cells, promoting glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis", "Increased glucagon release from pancreatic alpha cells, stimulating hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis", "Increased cortisol secretion, mobilizing amino acids and raising blood glucose", "Decreased parasympathetic activity, reducing insulin secretion and increasing blood glucose"], "question": "After a carbohydrate-rich meal, which physiological response best helps return blood glucose toward baseline in a healthy adult?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Postprandial hyperglycemia triggers insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells. Insulin increases GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue and stimulates hepatic and muscle glycogenesis, lowering blood glucose. Glucagon and cortisol elevate blood glucose, and parasympathetic activity generally supports insulin release rather than suppressing it.", "correctAnswer": "Increased insulin release from pancreatic beta cells, promoting glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis"}, {"id": "q_1762534972166_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "During vigorous exercise, rising CO2 and temperature in active muscles shift the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, enhancing oxygen unloading to tissues.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Increased CO2, H+ (lower pH), temperature, and 2,3-BPG shift the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the right (Bohr effect). A right shift decreases hemoglobin’s affinity for O2, facilitating oxygen unloading to active tissues. A left shift would increase affinity and hinder unloading.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762534972166_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the nephron, most filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the ______ via ______ on the apical membrane of tubular epithelial cells.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Glucose is reclaimed in the proximal convoluted tubule via SGLT proteins that use the sodium gradient (secondary active transport). Reabsorbed glucose then exits basolaterally via GLUT transporters into the peritubular capillaries.", "correctAnswer": "proximal convoluted tubule (PCT); sodium-glucose cotransporters (SGLT)"}, {"id": "q_1762534972166_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain how myelination affects both the conduction velocity and the energy efficiency of action potential propagation along an axon.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Myelin sheaths electrically insulate internodes, causing action potentials to regenerate only at nodes of Ranvier where voltage-gated channels cluster. This setup speeds conduction while reducing total ionic flux, so restoring gradients requires less ATP.", "correctAnswer": "Myelination enables saltatory conduction: it increases membrane resistance and decreases capacitance, allowing depolarization to travel farther and faster between nodes of Ranvier, which increases conduction velocity. Because fewer ions cross the membrane along myelinated internodes, less Na+ influx occurs and the Na+/K+ ATPase does less work, improving energy efficiency."}, {"id": "q_1762534972166_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Intracellular (cytosolic or nuclear) receptors that modulate gene transcription via hormone-response elements", "G protein–coupled receptors on the plasma membrane that rapidly increase cAMP", "Ligand-gated ion channels in the membrane that permit Ca2+ influx for immediate responses", "Receptor tyrosine kinases that autophosphorylate and trigger MAPK cascades for acute effects"], "question": "A newly discovered lipid-soluble steroid-like hormone exerts long-lasting effects. Which receptor location and primary mechanism are most consistent with this hormone class?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Steroid and thyroid hormones are lipid-soluble, diffuse through membranes, and bind intracellular receptors acting as transcription factors. This mechanism yields slower onset but longer-lasting genomic effects. Peptide hormones generally signal via membrane GPCRs or RTKs and act more rapidly.", "correctAnswer": "Intracellular (cytosolic or nuclear) receptors that modulate gene transcription via hormone-response elements"}]	none	2025-11-07 17:02:52.26246	\N
2573f9c7-ad48-473f-a4ae-8da7774866e3	4115d976-9b46-4dc0-bbd2-f8b430a95e65	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762617138421_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A north pole and a south pole", "Two north poles", "Two south poles", "No poles ever attract"], "question": "Which pair of magnet poles will attract each other?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Opposite poles (north and south) attract each other, while like poles (north-north or south-south) repel.", "correctAnswer": "A north pole and a south pole"}, {"id": "q_1762617138421_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: Two south poles of magnets will repel each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles repel. This includes both north-north and south-south pairs. Only opposite poles (north-south) attract.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762617138421_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Like poles (north-north or south-south) _____ each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When two of the same poles are near each other, they push away (repel). Opposite poles attract.", "correctAnswer": "repel"}, {"id": "q_1762617138421_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What happens when the north pole of one magnet is brought near the south pole of another magnet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A north pole and a south pole are opposite poles, and opposite poles attract.", "correctAnswer": "They attract each other."}, {"id": "q_1762617138421_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Every magnet has a north and a south pole", "Magnets have only north poles", "Magnets have only south poles", "Magnets have no poles"], "question": "Which statement is true about the poles of a magnet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a magnet has two poles: a north pole and a south pole.", "correctAnswer": "Every magnet has a north and a south pole"}]	3183131f-6504-4517-ba8d-c5241f653350	2025-11-08 15:52:18.506412	\N
0799c2b7-0083-428d-a7f6-12b39bb56243	4115d976-9b46-4dc0-bbd2-f8b430a95e65	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762623273990_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["kilograms (kg)", "newtons (N)", "meters (m)", "seconds (s)"], "question": "Which unit is used to measure mass?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mass is the amount of matter in an object and is measured in kilograms (kg). Weight, by contrast, is a force and is measured in newtons (N).", "correctAnswer": "kilograms (kg)"}, {"id": "q_1762623273990_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Weight is a force, and force is a push or pull.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Weight is the gravitational force acting on a mass, and a force is defined as a push or a pull. Therefore, weight is a type of force.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762623273990_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Weight is measured in ______ because it is a force.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Because weight is the gravitational force on an object, it is measured in newtons (N), which is the SI unit of force.", "correctAnswer": "newtons (N)"}, {"id": "q_1762623273990_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["They are the same thing.", "Weight is mass times gravitational field strength (g).", "Mass is force times distance.", "Weight is independent of gravity."], "question": "How are mass and weight related on Earth?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Weight (W) is calculated by W = m × g, where m is mass and g is the gravitational field strength. They are related but not the same.", "correctAnswer": "Weight is mass times gravitational field strength (g)."}, {"id": "q_1762623273990_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one key difference between mass and weight.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mass is an intrinsic property of an object and does not change with location, while weight depends on gravity and can change (for example, on the Moon vs. Earth).", "correctAnswer": "Mass is the amount of matter measured in kilograms (kg); weight is the gravitational force measured in newtons (N)."}]	6fc8c4b3-5de8-4a64-bbec-24e8edfc3ce9	2025-11-08 17:34:34.075278	\N
8d092fb7-b7b7-46b3-95e5-88d3f5c0981d	d07e546d-5b76-4037-8fbd-4af978426fee	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760256459_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["x ≥ 1", "x > 1", "x ≥ 1 and x ≠ 5", "x ≤ 1 or x ≥ 5"], "question": "Let f(x) = sqrt(x − 1) and g(x) = 1/(x − 2). What is the domain of h(x) = g(f(x))?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For f(x) = sqrt(x − 1) to be defined, we need x − 1 ≥ 0, so x ≥ 1. In h(x) = 1/(sqrt(x − 1) − 2), the denominator must be nonzero: sqrt(x − 1) ≠ 2, which implies x − 1 ≠ 4, so x ≠ 5. Therefore, the domain is all x ≥ 1 except x = 5.", "correctAnswer": "x ≥ 1 and x ≠ 5"}, {"id": "q_1762760256459_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If A and B are independent events with P(A) = 0.6 and P(B) = 0.5, then P(A ∪ B) = 0.8.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For any events, P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). Independence gives P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) = 0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3. Thus, P(A ∪ B) = 0.6 + 0.5 − 0.3 = 0.8.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760256459_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "For the quadratic equation x^2 − kx + 8 = 0, the discriminant is zero when k = ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The discriminant is Δ = b^2 − 4ac = k^2 − 4(1)(8) = k^2 − 32. Setting Δ = 0 gives k^2 = 32, so k = ±√32 = ±4√2.", "correctAnswer": "±4√2"}, {"id": "q_1762760256459_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the equation of the line in slope-intercept form that passes through (2, −1) and is perpendicular to 3x − 4y = 7.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite 3x − 4y = 7 as y = (3/4)x − 7/4, so its slope is 3/4. A perpendicular line has slope −4/3. Using point-slope form with (2, −1): y + 1 = (−4/3)(x − 2). Simplifying yields y = (−4/3)x + 8/3 − 1 = (−4/3)x + 5/3.", "correctAnswer": "y = (-4/3)x + 5/3"}, {"id": "q_1762760256459_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-13", "11", "1", "-11"], "question": "What is the determinant of the matrix A = [[2, −1], [5, 3]]?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a 2×2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]], det(A) = ad − bc. Here, det(A) = (2)(3) − (−1)(5) = 6 + 5 = 11. Since the determinant is nonzero, A is invertible.", "correctAnswer": "11"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:37:36.548121	\N
1790ad4e-43fd-4d8a-a3e3-b2f3c226d5b9	33a8e3a7-2ac6-4666-9cc1-81dcf655c859	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760274780_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762760274780_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2. Zero divided by 2 is 0 with no remainder, so zero is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760274780_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 - __ = 4", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing number, solve 9 - ? = 4. Adding 5 to 4 gives 9, so the blank is 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762760274780_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["triangle", "square", "rectangle", "circle"], "question": "What is the name of a shape with 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has exactly three sides. Squares and rectangles have four sides, and a circle has no straight sides.", "correctAnswer": "triangle"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve for x: x + 3 = 8", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 3 from both sides: x = 8 - 3 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "2/3", "1/3", "3/2"], "question": "Which fraction is equal to one-half?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies to 1/2 when you divide numerator and denominator by 2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The perimeter of a rectangle is the sum of all its side lengths.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter is the total distance around a shape, so you add all sides. For a rectangle it is 2 × (length + width).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The place value of 5 in the number 352 is __.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 352, the 3 is in the hundreds place, the 5 is in the tens place, and the 2 is in the ones place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "12", "18", "24"], "question": "What is 6 × 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 6 × 3 = 6 + 6 + 6 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762760274781_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A right angle measures 180 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle measures 90 degrees. An angle of 180 degrees is a straight angle.", "correctAnswer": "False"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:37:54.867145	\N
4bf2e4a7-1a44-4c13-a96d-4b6f45374b7a	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Setswana - Easy	Setswana	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760379512_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Dumela", "Tsamaya sentle", "Ke a leboga", "Ga ke itse"], "question": "In Setswana, which word is a common greeting meaning \\"Hello\\" when speaking to one person?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dumela is the standard singular greeting meaning \\"Hello.\\" The plural form is Dumelang. Tsamaya sentle means \\"Go well,\\" Ke a leboga means \\"Thank you,\\" and Ga ke itse means \\"I don’t know.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Dumela"}, {"id": "q_1762760379512_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Setswana phrase \\"Ke a leboga\\" means \\"Thank you.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ke a leboga literally means \\"I thank (you).\\" It is a polite way to express gratitude in Setswana. A common response is O amogetswe, meaning \\"You are welcome.\\"", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760379512_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence in Setswana: \\"Leina la me ___ Thabo.\\" (My name is Thabo.)", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The word ke functions like the verb \\"is/am\\" in this context. Leina la me means \\"my name,\\" so Leina la me ke Thabo translates to \\"My name is Thabo.\\"", "correctAnswer": "ke"}, {"id": "q_1762760379512_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the Setswana word for \\"water\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metsi means \\"water\\" in Setswana, a high-frequency everyday noun learners encounter early on.", "correctAnswer": "metsi"}, {"id": "q_1762760379512_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Pedi", "Nngwe", "Tharo", "Nne"], "question": "What is the Setswana word for the number 2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pedi means \\"two.\\" Nngwe is \\"one,\\" Tharo is \\"three,\\" and Nne is \\"four.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Pedi"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:39:39.523249	\N
15d15a75-61d7-465f-a5fb-667a0957b35c	47451ad5-3ccb-4c5d-a8f7-4a3f8b133b8b	english fantasy story - Easy	english fantasy story	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760456307_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A glowing silver key", "A golden crown", "A glass slipper", "A magic wand"], "question": "In the land of Everbright, what does Lily find beneath an old oak tree in Mistwood Forest?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The central object of the story is a glowing silver key, which begins Lily’s adventure by unlocking the hidden gate.", "correctAnswer": "A glowing silver key"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Rowan is a talking fox who helps guide Lily through the forest.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Rowan is introduced as a helpful, talking fox and serves as Lily’s companion and guide.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The hidden gate Lily unlocks is covered in _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The gate is described as being covered in ivy, emphasizing its secret, overgrown nature.", "correctAnswer": "ivy"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A crystal castle", "A deep cave", "A floating ship", "A mountain village"], "question": "Where does the hidden gate lead after Lily unlocks it?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Beyond the gate lies a crystal castle, the magical destination Lily reaches in her adventure.", "correctAnswer": "A crystal castle"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Eldrin the wizard", "King Arthur", "A dragon named Blaze", "The Queen of Winter"], "question": "Who lives in the crystal castle Lily visits?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Eldrin is the wise wizard who resides in the castle and represents guidance and wisdom in the story.", "correctAnswer": "Eldrin the wizard"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The glowing key works for anyone who picks it up.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The key only responds to kind hearts, teaching that character matters more than possession.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_6", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the name of the forest where Lily finds the key?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mistwood Forest is the setting where the adventure begins, grounding the story in a specific place.", "correctAnswer": "Mistwood Forest"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The Gloom", "The Shade", "The Whisper", "The Mire"], "question": "What is the shadow that dims the castle lights called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Gloom is the story’s gentle antagonist, a shadowy presence Lily must overcome.", "correctAnswer": "The Gloom"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Lily uses the key’s ______ to chase away the Gloom.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The key’s magic manifests as light, symbolizing hope and goodness pushing back darkness.", "correctAnswer": "light"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Lily returns home after midnight, long after her adventure ends.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "She returns before sunset, reinforcing that even small acts of bravery can happen within a single day.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_10", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Kindness", "Strength", "Wealth", "Fame"], "question": "Which personal quality helps Lily unlock the gate?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The key selects kind hearts, highlighting kindness as the key to opening new paths.", "correctAnswer": "Kindness"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The talking fox who guides Lily is named ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Rowan is Lily’s companion, underscoring the theme of friendship and guidance.", "correctAnswer": "Rowan"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_12", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What lesson does Lily learn from her adventure?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The story’s message is that a brave, kind heart unlocks opportunities and overcomes darkness.", "correctAnswer": "Bravery and kindness open doors."}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "Eldrin is a wise wizard who lives in the crystal castle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Eldrin’s wisdom and home in the castle tie together the magical setting and mentor archetype.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760456307_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["An old oak tree", "A tall pine tree", "A willow tree", "A cherry tree"], "question": "Under which tree does Lily discover the glowing key?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The old oak tree marks the start of the adventure and is a classic symbol of age and mystery in fantasy.", "correctAnswer": "An old oak tree"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:40:56.319844	\N
96e59b99-0327-446d-933b-a76a9e01ffe3	803c6bc1-d405-413c-863f-900f745fe5dc	ENGLISH  - Medium	ENGLISH 	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760491998_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["He likes hiking, swimming, and to bike.", "He likes to hike, swimming, and biking.", "He likes hiking, swimming, and biking.", "He likes to hike, to swim, and biking."], "question": "Which sentence demonstrates correct parallel structure?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires items in a series to be in the same grammatical form. \\"Hiking, swimming, and biking\\" are all gerunds, creating a balanced and clear list. The other options mix gerunds with infinitives, which breaks parallelism.", "correctAnswer": "He likes hiking, swimming, and biking."}, {"id": "q_1762760491998_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The candidate who everyone admired gave a powerful speech.", "The candidate whom gave a powerful speech impressed the crowd.", "The candidate to whom everyone gave their support won the election.", "The candidate, of who the panel was skeptical, answered every question."], "question": "Choose the sentence that correctly uses \\"who\\" or \\"whom.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "\\"Whom\\" is used as the object of a verb or preposition. In the correct sentence, \\"whom\\" is the object of the preposition \\"to.\\" Option A should use \\"whom\\" (object of admired), B incorrectly uses \\"whom\\" as a subject, and D should be \\"of whom,\\" not \\"of who.\\"", "correctAnswer": "The candidate to whom everyone gave their support won the election."}, {"id": "q_1762760491998_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a Shakespearean (English) sonnet, the volta typically occurs at line 9.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The volta (turn) in a Shakespearean sonnet often occurs near the final couplet, around line 13. The turn at line 9 is characteristic of the Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet form.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760491998_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct word to complete the sentence: \\"The new policy will ____ employee morale.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use \\"affect\\" as a verb meaning to influence. \\"Effect\\" is most commonly a noun meaning a result. Here, the sentence needs a verb describing what the policy will do to morale.", "correctAnswer": "affect"}, {"id": "q_1762760491998_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence to correct the dangling modifier: 'Walking down the street, the flowers caught Maria’s eye.'", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The opening participial phrase \\"Walking down the street\\" must modify the subject that immediately follows it. In the original, it incorrectly modifies \\"the flowers.\\" By making \\"Maria\\" the subject after the phrase, the sentence becomes clear and grammatically correct.", "correctAnswer": "Walking down the street, Maria noticed the flowers."}]	none	2025-11-10 07:41:32.088443	\N
5d54f8fa-7860-4f7b-be5b-77694d2d7d3a	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	Muscles - Easy	Muscles	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760585364_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Skeletal muscle", "Cardiac muscle", "Smooth muscle", "All muscle types"], "question": "Which type of muscle tissue is under voluntary control?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Skeletal muscles attach to bones and are controlled consciously to produce movement. Cardiac and smooth muscles work involuntarily—cardiac muscle in the heart and smooth muscle in organs like the stomach and blood vessels.", "correctAnswer": "Skeletal muscle"}, {"id": "q_1762760585364_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Muscles can only pull, not push.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Muscle fibers shorten when they contract, pulling on bones or other tissues. They do not push; opposite movements are created by pairs of muscles working against each other (e.g., flexors and extensors).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760585364_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The muscle responsible for pumping blood throughout the body is the ______ muscle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cardiac muscle is a specialized, involuntary muscle found only in the heart. Its rhythmic contractions pump blood through the circulatory system.", "correctAnswer": "cardiac"}, {"id": "q_1762760585364_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What connective structures attach skeletal muscles to bones?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tendons are strong, fibrous connective tissues that attach muscles to bones, transmitting the force of muscle contraction to create movement. (Ligaments, by contrast, connect bone to bone.)", "correctAnswer": "Tendons"}, {"id": "q_1762760585364_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["ATP", "DNA", "Glucose", "Oxygen"], "question": "Which molecule directly provides energy for muscle contraction?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) binds to myosin heads, allowing them to detach and re-cock during the cross-bridge cycle. While glucose and oxygen help produce ATP, ATP itself is the immediate energy source for contraction.", "correctAnswer": "ATP"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:43:05.377635	\N
a0c1fa53-1673-48c4-a811-ee93aae392e9	d9b86f8b-5eb9-43d6-a4c0-68261eaedf79	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760621221_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It becomes turgid as water enters and the cell wall prevents bursting", "It undergoes plasmolysis as water leaves the cell", "It lyses because the plasma membrane ruptures", "There is no net movement of water"], "question": "A plant cell is placed in a hypotonic solution. What is the most likely outcome?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In a hypotonic solution, water moves into the cell by osmosis because the solute concentration is lower outside than inside. Plant cells do not burst because the rigid cell wall provides structural support. Instead, they become turgid, which helps maintain plant structure. Plasmolysis occurs in hypertonic solutions, not hypotonic ones.", "correctAnswer": "It becomes turgid as water enters and the cell wall prevents bursting"}, {"id": "q_1762760621221_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Increasing enzyme concentration will always cause the reaction rate to increase proportionally, no matter how much substrate is present.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzyme-catalyzed reactions follow saturation kinetics (Michaelis–Menten). At low substrate concentrations, adding more enzyme can increase the rate, but not necessarily proportionally. At high substrate concentrations, the rate approaches a maximum (Vmax) determined by the amount of enzyme; once active sites are saturated, increasing enzyme only helps if more enzyme adds more active sites. The rate depends on both enzyme and substrate concentrations and reaches limits due to saturation.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760621221_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The amount of energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kilogram of a substance by 1°C is its ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Specific heat capacity (J/kg·°C) measures how much energy is needed to change a substance’s temperature. Water has a high specific heat capacity, which is why it moderates climate and resists rapid temperature changes compared to land.", "correctAnswer": "specific heat capacity"}, {"id": "q_1762760621221_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A 2 kg block starts from rest and slides down a frictionless ramp with a vertical height of 5 m. What is its speed at the bottom?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By conservation of energy, the loss of gravitational potential energy mgh converts to kinetic energy (1/2 mv^2). Solving v = sqrt(2gh) with g ≈ 9.8 m/s² and h = 5 m gives v ≈ sqrt(98) ≈ 9.9 m/s. The mass cancels, and the ramp angle does not affect the final speed without friction.", "correctAnswer": "Approximately 9.9 m/s"}, {"id": "q_1762760621221_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Less than 7", "Equal to 7", "Greater than 7", "It depends only on the initial acid concentration, not acid strength"], "question": "During the titration of a weak acid with a strong base, what is the pH at the equivalence point relative to 7?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At the equivalence point, the weak acid has been converted to its conjugate base, which hydrolyzes water to produce OH⁻, making the solution basic. Therefore, the pH is greater than 7. Only strong acid–strong base titrations yield pH = 7 at equivalence.", "correctAnswer": "Greater than 7"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:43:41.234877	\N
2a695c67-0215-4f99-b344-c3c2631bd09d	35808bd3-6ed2-4d5b-8a6a-5dc2ad9824b9	Maths angles  - Easy	Maths angles 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770738184012_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Acute angle", "Right angle", "Obtuse angle", "Straight angle"], "question": "Which term describes an angle that measures less than 90°?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An acute angle is any angle less than 90°. A right angle is exactly 90°, an obtuse angle is between 90° and 180°, and a straight angle is exactly 180°.", "correctAnswer": "Acute angle"}, {"id": "q_1770738184012_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Vertical (opposite) angles formed when two lines intersect are always equal in measure.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When two lines intersect, they form two pairs of opposite (vertical) angles. Each pair has equal measures because each vertical angle is supplementary to the same adjacent angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770738184012_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "An angle that measures 180° is called a ________ angle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A 180° angle opens to form a straight line, so it is called a straight angle.", "correctAnswer": "straight"}, {"id": "q_1770738184012_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the sum of the measures of angles that form a straight line?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Angles that lie on a straight line are supplementary, meaning their measures add up to 180 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "180 degrees"}, {"id": "q_1770738184012_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45°", "90°", "180°", "360°"], "question": "Two angles are complementary if their measures add up to:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles add to 90°. If angles add to 180°, they are supplementary; 360° is a full turn; 45° is just a specific angle measure, not a sum defining a relationship.", "correctAnswer": "90°"}]	none	2026-02-10 15:43:04.129743	\N
5ec7a58e-b1da-4790-b794-a1e9df6db4ca	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760692456_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It increases by about 2.8 times", "It increases by exactly 2 times", "It stays the same", "It decreases to about 0.7 times"], "question": "A satellite in a circular low Earth orbit is moved to a new circular orbit with twice the orbital radius. By what factor does its orbital period change?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For circular orbits around the same central mass, Kepler’s third law gives T ∝ r^(3/2). Doubling the radius multiplies the period by 2^(3/2) ≈ 2.828, so the period increases by about 2.8 times.", "correctAnswer": "It increases by about 2.8 times"}, {"id": "q_1762760692456_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Enzymes are consumed and permanently altered by the chemical reactions they catalyze.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzymes lower activation energy by forming transient enzyme–substrate complexes but they emerge unchanged at the end of the reaction and can catalyze many cycles. While conditions (e.g., pH, temperature) can denature enzymes, they are not consumed by the reactions they catalyze.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760692456_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When a weak acid and its conjugate base are present at equal concentrations in a buffer, the pH equals the ______ of the acid.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Henderson–Hasselbalch equation is pH = pKa + log([A−]/[HA]). If [A−] = [HA], then log(1) = 0, so pH = pKa. This is why buffers are most effective near the acid’s pKa.", "correctAnswer": "pKa"}, {"id": "q_1762760692456_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "At the same latitude, coastal cities tend to have smaller seasonal temperature swings than inland cities. What physical property of water is primarily responsible for this effect?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water’s high specific heat capacity means the ocean absorbs and releases large amounts of heat with relatively small temperature changes. This thermal buffering, along with mixing and evaporation, moderates coastal climates.", "correctAnswer": "High specific heat capacity of water"}, {"id": "q_1762760692456_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Incomplete dominance", "Codominance", "Polygenic inheritance", "Epistasis"], "question": "In a plant species, crossing a red-flowered (RR) plant with a white-flowered (WW) plant produces all pink-flowered (RW) offspring. Which inheritance pattern best explains this observation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incomplete dominance occurs when the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype (pink) between the two homozygotes (red and white). Codominance would show both traits fully at once, while polygenic inheritance and epistasis involve multiple genes or gene interactions rather than this single-locus intermediate effect.", "correctAnswer": "Incomplete dominance"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:44:52.469096	\N
3db81f85-f401-432e-b8d4-ff5f4fb8a15b	2a55c263-ffcf-4e49-9ddb-97097a9bb1bb	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760706506_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It decreases", "It increases", "It stays the same", "The block sinks completely"], "question": "A wooden block floats in freshwater with about 60% of its volume submerged. If salt is added to the water, increasing its density, what happens to the fraction of the block’s volume that is submerged?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Buoyant force equals the weight of the displaced fluid. As the fluid’s density increases, less volume needs to be displaced to balance the block’s weight, so a smaller fraction of the block remains submerged.", "correctAnswer": "It decreases"}, {"id": "q_1762760706506_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a closed system at chemical equilibrium, the concentrations of reactants and products are equal.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At equilibrium, the forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, not necessarily the concentrations. The actual concentrations are determined by the equilibrium constant (K), which depends on temperature and the reaction’s stoichiometry.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760706506_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 kilogram of a substance by 1 degree Celsius is its ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Specific heat capacity (often in J/kg·°C) quantifies how much energy is needed to change a substance’s temperature. Water’s high specific heat capacity explains its ability to moderate climate and buffer temperature changes.", "correctAnswer": "specific heat capacity"}, {"id": "q_1762760706506_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A patient stops taking antibiotics as soon as they feel better. Explain how this behavior can contribute to the development of antibiotic resistance in the bacterial population.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Antibiotics create strong selection pressure. If treatment ends before all bacteria are eradicated, more tolerant or partially resistant cells survive. They multiply and can share resistance genes (plasmids) with other bacteria, increasing the prevalence of resistant populations.", "correctAnswer": "Stopping early leaves the most tolerant bacteria alive; these survivors replicate and spread resistance genes (including via horizontal gene transfer), so selection favors resistant strains."}, {"id": "q_1762760706506_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The orbit gradually decays, lowering altitude until re-entry.", "The orbit becomes more circular and altitude increases.", "The orbit remains unchanged due to conservation of angular momentum.", "The satellite speeds up and moves to a higher orbit."], "question": "A satellite in low Earth orbit experiences atmospheric drag. If no corrective thrust is applied, what is the most likely long-term effect on its orbit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Atmospheric drag removes mechanical energy from the orbit, reducing altitude over time. The orbit often becomes more circular while shrinking, eventually leading to re-entry. Drag is a non-conservative force, so orbital energy and angular momentum are not conserved.", "correctAnswer": "The orbit gradually decays, lowering altitude until re-entry."}]	none	2025-11-10 07:45:06.608268	\N
b66a22f7-8f4a-4026-8342-c349bd560774	d07e546d-5b76-4037-8fbd-4af978426fee	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760725812_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "9", "12", "15"], "question": "Which of the following is an even number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder. 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so 12 is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "9", "14"], "question": "What is the value of 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition. 3 × 4 = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "3/5", "2/3", "3/4"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies to 1/2 because both numerator and denominator can be divided by 2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["ones", "tens", "hundreds", "thousands"], "question": "What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 452?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 452, the 2 is in the ones place, the 5 is in the tens place, and the 4 is in the hundreds place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "24"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 4 and width 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (4 + 3) = 14.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Triangle", "Square", "Pentagon", "Hexagon"], "question": "Which shape has exactly 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides. A square has 4, a pentagon has 5, and a hexagon has 6.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/2", "2/3", "5/10", "5/2"], "question": "What is 0.5 as a fraction in simplest form?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 equals 5/10, which reduces to 1/2 by dividing numerator and denominator by 5. The simplest form is 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "9 is greater than 6.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Since 9 > 6, the statement is true.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square has four sides of equal length and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of any two odd numbers is odd.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Odd + odd = even. For example, 3 + 5 = 8, which is even.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "1 meter is longer than 100 centimeters.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "1 meter equals 100 centimeters, so it is not longer; they are the same length.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "15 − 9 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 9 from 15 to get 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "1/4 of 20 is __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "One quarter means divide by 4: 20 ÷ 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The next number in the pattern 2, 4, 6, 8, __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time, so after 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "7 + __ = 13", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Find the missing addend: 13 − 7 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the decimal for the fraction 3/10.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tenths place means one decimal place: 3/10 = 0.3.", "correctAnswer": "0.3"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of x if x + 5 = 9?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 5 from both sides: x = 9 − 5 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How many degrees are in a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle measures 90 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762760725812_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the largest single digit in base-10?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Base-10 digits are 0 through 9; the largest single digit is 9.", "correctAnswer": "9"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:45:25.895393	\N
d073d8e2-f9e6-4121-a5e2-148e4e57fa8f	0867083e-bd2c-4295-a484-394b6889cbf2	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760726457_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Star", "Bus", "Ring", "Point-to-point"], "question": "An organization expects rapid growth in the number of client devices and wants easy centralized management and fault isolation. Which network topology is the best fit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A star topology connects all devices to a central switch or hub, making it easy to add new devices and isolate faults by port. Bus and ring topologies are less scalable and harder to troubleshoot. Point-to-point connections do not scale for many devices.", "correctAnswer": "Star"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Nightly full backups at midnight", "Weekly full backups with hourly incremental backups", "Daily differential backups plus a weekly full backup", "Real-time synchronous mirroring only"], "question": "A company sets an RPO of 1 hour and an RTO of 4 hours. Which backup strategy best meets these goals cost-effectively?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Hourly incremental backups cap potential data loss at roughly 1 hour (meeting the RPO) while restoring the latest full plus incrementals can fit within a 4-hour RTO in many environments. Nightly fulls risk up to 24 hours of loss; differentials daily don’t meet a 1‑hour RPO; synchronous mirroring meets both but is typically far more expensive.", "correctAnswer": "Weekly full backups with hourly incremental backups"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["FTP", "SFTP", "FTPS", "Telnet"], "question": "Which protocol runs over SSH and encrypts both authentication and data for secure file transfer?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol) operates over SSH and encrypts the entire session including credentials and file data. FTP transmits in clear text; FTPS adds TLS to FTP but is distinct from SSH; Telnet is an insecure remote terminal protocol.", "correctAnswer": "SFTP"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["SPF", "MX", "A", "CNAME"], "question": "Which DNS record type is used to specify authorized mail servers for a domain to help prevent email spoofing?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "SPF (Sender Policy Framework) records list the IPs or hosts allowed to send mail for a domain, reducing spoofing. MX specifies mail exchangers; A maps names to IPv4 addresses; CNAME creates aliases.", "correctAnswer": "SPF"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long, and NAT is a built-in requirement in IPv6 networks.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "IPv6 does use 128-bit addresses, but it was designed to provide abundant public addressing and reduce reliance on NAT. NAT is not a built-in requirement, even though translation mechanisms (e.g., NAT64) exist for specific scenarios.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a relational database, normalization reduces data redundancy and can improve update consistency.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Normalization organizes data into related tables to minimize duplication. This reduces anomalies during insert, update, and delete operations, improving data consistency and integrity.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process of converting plaintext into unreadable form during transmission is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Encryption transforms readable data into ciphertext using an algorithm and key so that only authorized parties can decrypt and read it, protecting confidentiality in transit and at rest.", "correctAnswer": "encryption"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The unique hardware identifier burned into a network interface card is its ______ address.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A MAC (Media Access Control) address uniquely identifies a network interface at Layer 2. It is often factory-assigned and used in local network communication.", "correctAnswer": "MAC"}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain two benefits of using virtualization in ICT infrastructure.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Virtualization allows multiple VMs to share physical hardware, reducing underutilization and costs (consolidation). It also isolates workloads, enabling safer testing, snapshot-based rollback, faster disaster recovery, and easier scaling/migration across hosts.", "correctAnswer": "Server consolidation to improve resource utilization; isolation and portability of workloads (snapshots, rapid recovery, easier scaling)."}, {"id": "q_1762760726457_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A small office needs to share files internally with access control and enable secure remote access for staff. What ICT solution would you recommend and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A NAS centralizes storage with granular access controls (SMB/NFS, user/group ACLs). Pairing it with a VPN provides encrypted remote access without exposing file services publicly, balancing security, manageability, and cost.", "correctAnswer": "Deploy a NAS with user/group permissions and expose it remotely via a company VPN (not directly to the internet)."}]	none	2025-11-10 07:45:26.470484	\N
c5f776db-4dd9-42f9-ae41-f0a757844050	1674da2b-054a-4620-b639-b3014fddbd89	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760823248_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["/25", "/26", "/27", "/28"], "question": "An office needs a subnet that can support about 50 devices with a small buffer for growth. Which CIDR prefix length is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A /26 network provides 64 total addresses, with 62 usable host addresses after reserving the network and broadcast addresses. That comfortably covers ~50 hosts. A /27 offers only 30 usable addresses, and /28 only 14. A /25 provides 126 usable addresses, which is larger than necessary for this scenario.", "correctAnswer": "/26"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAID 1 (mirroring) consistently improves write performance compared to using a single disk.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "RAID 1 writes the same data to two disks, so write performance is typically similar to a single disk and can even be slightly slower due to mirrored writes. RAID 1 mainly improves redundancy and can enhance read performance because reads can be served from either disk.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In SQL, the clause used to combine rows from two tables based on related columns is called a ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A JOIN combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column, such as a foreign key. Common types include INNER JOIN (matching rows), LEFT/RIGHT JOIN (including non-matching rows from one side), and FULL OUTER JOIN.", "correctAnswer": "JOIN"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Unauthorized request", "Too Many Requests (rate limited)", "Resource not found", "Conflict with current state"], "question": "A web API responds with HTTP status code 429. What is the most likely cause?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "HTTP 429 indicates the client has sent too many requests in a given time and is being rate limited. 401 is Unauthorized, 404 is Not Found, and 409 is Conflict.", "correctAnswer": "Too Many Requests (rate limited)"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain the difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption, and give one typical use case for each.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Symmetric algorithms are computationally efficient but require secure key sharing. Asymmetric algorithms solve the key distribution problem by using separate public/private keys but are slower; they are often used to establish secure channels (e.g., TLS) by exchanging a symmetric session key or for signing messages.", "correctAnswer": "Symmetric encryption uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt data (e.g., AES), making it fast and suitable for bulk data encryption. Asymmetric encryption uses a public/private key pair (e.g., RSA or ECC), enabling operations like key exchange and digital signatures; it is commonly used to securely share a symmetric key or verify identities."}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["IaaS", "PaaS", "SaaS", "FaaS (Serverless Functions)"], "question": "A development team wants to deploy applications without managing the underlying OS, middleware, or scaling, but still controls the application runtime environment (framework and language). Which cloud service model best fits?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Platform as a Service (PaaS) abstracts the OS and middleware while providing a managed runtime for developers. IaaS requires managing OS and middleware. SaaS delivers a complete application with no control over runtime. FaaS is event-driven and granular, but not a full application platform; it’s suited for stateless functions rather than entire runtimes.", "correctAnswer": "PaaS"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "IPv6 was designed with such a large address space that Network Address Translation (NAT) is not required for address conservation.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "IPv6 provides an enormous address space (128-bit), so NAT is not needed for conserving addresses. While some networks may still use NAT-like techniques for policy or isolation, IPv6’s design favors end-to-end addressing without NAT.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Converting analog audio to digital involves sampling and ______ of the signal amplitude.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Digitizing audio follows two main steps: sampling (measuring the signal at discrete time intervals) and quantization (mapping each sample’s amplitude to discrete numeric levels). These steps determine fidelity and introduce potential aliasing and quantization noise.", "correctAnswer": "quantization"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["B-tree index", "Hash index", "Full-text index", "Bitmap index"], "question": "Which index type is generally most effective for efficient prefix searches like WHERE name LIKE 'abc%' in a relational database?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "B-tree indexes support ordered traversal, making them effective for range and prefix queries. Hash indexes optimize equality lookups only. Full-text indexes are optimized for tokenized word searches, not simple prefixes. Bitmap indexes are effective for low-cardinality columns, not typical string prefixes.", "correctAnswer": "B-tree index"}, {"id": "q_1762760823248_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why is two-factor authentication (2FA) more secure than password-only authentication?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combining different factor types raises the security bar: knowledge (password), possession (a device or token), or inherence (biometrics). An attacker must compromise multiple factors simultaneously, significantly lowering the likelihood of unauthorized access.", "correctAnswer": "2FA requires two independent factors (e.g., something you know, and something you have or are), so even if an attacker steals a password, they still lack the second factor. This reduces risks from phishing, credential reuse, and brute-force attacks."}]	none	2025-11-10 07:47:03.261921	\N
54051349-ceb8-468d-a18c-a433b7aee38f	2a55c263-ffcf-4e49-9ddb-97097a9bb1bb	SETSWANA - Medium	SETSWANA	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760841244_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["basimane", "mosimane", "mesimane", "bomasimane"], "question": "Choose the correct plural form of the noun 'mosimane' (boy) in Setswana.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In Setswana, most human nouns use the mo-/ba- noun class pair: singular mo- (e.g., mosimane) and plural ba- (e.g., basimane). The other options either keep the singular form (mosimane) or use incorrect class prefixes (mesimane, bomasimane).", "correctAnswer": "basimane"}, {"id": "q_1762760841244_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: The present negative in Setswana is formed by placing 'ga' before the subject concord and changing the verb to end in '-e' (e.g., Ga ba bue).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Setswana negates the present tense with 'ga' plus the subject concord and a verb that typically ends in '-e': Ga ke bue (I do not speak), Ga o bue (you do not speak), Ga ba bue (they do not speak). Some verbs show a stem change in the negative, e.g., ja (eat) → je.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760841244_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct subject concord: __ a bala buka letsatsi le letsatsi. (We read a book every day.)", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The subject concord for first person plural (we) is 'Re'. In habitual present, the particle 'a' often appears: 'Re a bala' means we read/are reading. 'letsatsi le letsatsi' means every day.", "correctAnswer": "Re"}, {"id": "q_1762760841244_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Translate into Setswana: \\"We will speak Setswana at home.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Future tense uses 'tla' before the verb: 'Re tla bua' (we will speak). 'Setswana' is the language name. 'fa gae' means at home. Word order is Subject Concord + tla + Verb + Complement.", "correctAnswer": "Re tla bua Setswana fa gae."}, {"id": "q_1762760841244_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ga ba je nama", "Ba a ja nama", "Ba ga je nama", "Ga ba ja nama"], "question": "Which sentence correctly expresses \\"They do not eat meat\\" in Setswana?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Negative present is 'ga' + subject concord + verb with '-e' ending. The verb 'ja' (eat) becomes 'je' in the negative: 'Ga ba je nama' (They do not eat meat). 'Ba a ja nama' is affirmative. 'Ba ga je nama' misplaces 'ga'. 'Ga ba ja nama' fails to apply the negative stem change.", "correctAnswer": "Ga ba je nama"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:47:21.257423	\N
51d97256-ad4d-43bc-b990-0e295c93e97d	f99ffedd-e83f-4ae6-bd54-d1be16325a58	ENGLISH - Easy	ENGLISH	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760898736_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["dog", "runs", "fast", "the"], "question": "Which word is the noun in the sentence: \\"The dog runs fast\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. In this sentence, \\"dog\\" is a thing; \\"runs\\" is a verb, \\"fast\\" is an adverb, and \\"the\\" is an article.", "correctAnswer": "dog"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A verb is a word that shows an action or a state of being.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Verbs express actions (like \\"run\\") or states of being (like \\"is\\"). They are essential for forming predicates in sentences.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct verb: \\"There ____ two apples on the table.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"are\\" with plural nouns. Since there are two apples (plural), the correct form is \\"are,\\" not \\"is.\\"", "correctAnswer": "are"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["babys", "babies", "babyes", "baby's"], "question": "What is the correct plural form of \\"baby\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nouns ending in a consonant + y change the y to i and add -es in the plural: baby → babies.", "correctAnswer": "babies"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What punctuation mark is used at the end of a question?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Questions end with a question mark (?). It signals that a response is sought and helps readers identify interrogative sentences.", "correctAnswer": "question mark"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The word \\"their\\" is used to show possession.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Their\\" is the possessive form meaning \\"belonging to them.\\" It is different from \\"there\\" (a place) and \\"they're\\" (they are).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct homophone: \\"Please turn to the left, not _____.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Right\\" is the direction opposite left. It is a homophone of \\"write,\\" which means to put words on a page.", "correctAnswer": "right"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Maria", "reads", "every", "night"], "question": "Identify the subject of the sentence: \\"Maria reads every night.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The subject is who or what the sentence is about. \\"Maria\\" is the person performing the action \\"reads.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Maria"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs add -ed to form the simple past. Therefore, \\"walk\\" becomes \\"walked.\\"", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1762760898736_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"I want ___ apple.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"an\\" before words that start with a vowel sound. \\"Apple\\" begins with a vowel sound, so \\"an apple\\" is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:48:18.829734	\N
5493d8d4-0148-48ae-814f-a33fae9da9cd	c86d5cd4-b67a-413b-a3f9-0c0dc605992d	French - Medium	French	medium	[{"id": "q_1762760975303_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["soit", "est", "était", "sera"], "question": "Complétez: Bien que Marie ____ fatiguée, elle continue à travailler.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Après la conjonction « bien que », on emploie le subjonctif. Le verbe « être » au subjonctif présent à la 3e personne du singulier est « soit ». Les autres formes sont à l’indicatif et ne conviennent pas ici.", "correctAnswer": "soit"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["lisais / a sonné", "ai lu / sonnait", "lisais / sonnait", "ai lu / a sonné"], "question": "Choisissez la combinaison correcte de temps: Je ____ (lire) quand il ____ (sonner) à la porte.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "On utilise l’imparfait pour l’action en cours (« je lisais ») et le passé composé pour l’action soudaine qui l’interrompt (« il a sonné »). Les autres combinaisons ne respectent pas cette distinction de valeur aspectuelle.", "correctAnswer": "lisais / a sonné"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Le pronom « y » remplace des groupes introduits par à qui désignent des choses ou des lieux, mais pas des personnes.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "« Y » remplace des compléments de lieu ou des compléments introduits par à quand il s’agit de choses/lieux: « Je pense à mon travail » → « J’y pense ». Pour les personnes, on emploie plutôt les pronoms COI (lui/leur).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complétez avec le pronom relatif approprié: Le livre ____ je t'ai parlé est sur la table.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "On utilise « dont » pour remplacer un complément introduit par de: « parler de quelque chose ». « Le livre dont je t’ai parlé » est la construction correcte, et non « qui » ou « que ».", "correctAnswer": "dont"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["tous", "toutes", "tout", "toute"], "question": "Choisissez la forme correcte de « tout » : Elles sont ____ prêtes.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "« Elles » est féminin pluriel, donc on choisit « toutes ». « Tous » est masculin pluriel, « tout » est masculin singulier, et « toute » est féminin singulier.", "correctAnswer": "toutes"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Quel pronom COI remplace « à ma sœur » dans la phrase: J'ai écrit à ma sœur ?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Le complément d’objet indirect au singulier (à + personne) se remplace par « lui ». La phrase devient: « Je lui ai écrit. »", "correctAnswer": "lui"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["depuis", "pendant", "pour", "dans"], "question": "Complétez avec la préposition appropriée: Il habite à Paris ____ dix ans et y est encore.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "« Depuis » exprime une durée qui a commencé dans le passé et continue au présent. « Pendant » indique une durée achevée, « pour » exprime une intention/futur, et « dans » marque un moment futur.", "correctAnswer": "depuis"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "La plupart des adjectifs descriptifs se placent avant le nom en français.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "La majorité des adjectifs se placent après le nom. Certains (souvent BAGS: beauté, âge, bonté, taille) se placent avant, comme « grand », « petit », « beau », « jeune ». La règle générale est post-nominale.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complétez la négation: Personne n'____ venu.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Le verbe « venir » se conjugue avec « être » au passé composé (« est venu »). Avec « personne » sujet, on a « Personne n’est venu ». On élide « ne » devant une voyelle: « n’ » + « est ».", "correctAnswer": "est"}, {"id": "q_1762760975303_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Transformez en discours indirect: Il dit: « Je viendrai demain. »", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Avec un verbe introducteur au présent (« il dit »), le futur reste le futur (« viendra »). Les marqueurs temporels changent: « demain » devient « le lendemain » au discours indirect.", "correctAnswer": "Il dit qu'il viendra le lendemain."}]	none	2025-11-10 07:49:35.315998	\N
c80a76dd-f785-4594-a388-420b3343faf0	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	blood - Easy	blood	easy	[{"id": "q_1762760998397_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Plasma", "Platelets", "Red blood cells", "White blood cells"], "question": "Which component of blood primarily carries oxygen to the body's tissues?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, a protein that binds oxygen in the lungs and releases it to tissues throughout the body.", "correctAnswer": "Red blood cells"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Plasma, the liquid portion of blood, is mostly water.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plasma is about 90% water. It carries nutrients, hormones, waste products, and other substances throughout the body.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The iron-containing protein in red blood cells that binds oxygen is called ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hemoglobin is an iron-rich protein that gives red blood cells their color and allows them to carry oxygen from the lungs to tissues.", "correctAnswer": "hemoglobin"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Arteries", "Veins", "Capillaries", "Venules"], "question": "Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure. Veins return blood to the heart, and capillaries are small vessels where exchange occurs.", "correctAnswer": "Arteries"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the four main components of blood.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Blood is made of plasma (the liquid), red blood cells (oxygen transport), white blood cells (immune defense), and platelets (clotting).", "correctAnswer": "Plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "White blood cells are primarily responsible for fighting infections.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "White blood cells are part of the immune system and help defend the body against bacteria, viruses, and other pathogens.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Fight infection", "Transport oxygen", "Help blood clot", "Produce hormones"], "question": "What is the main function of platelets?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Platelets are small cell fragments that stick together at injury sites and help form clots, preventing excessive bleeding.", "correctAnswer": "Help blood clot"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The ABO blood group system refers to different types of ____ present on red blood cells.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ABO blood types (A, B, AB, O) are determined by specific antigens on the surface of red blood cells, which can trigger immune responses.", "correctAnswer": "antigens"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Veins usually have valves to prevent blood from flowing backward.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Many veins, especially in the limbs, contain one-way valves that help return blood to the heart against gravity and prevent backflow.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762760998397_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Plasma", "Platelets", "Hemoglobin", "Lymph"], "question": "Which component of blood is the liquid that transports cells, nutrients, and waste products?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plasma is the fluid portion of blood. It carries blood cells, nutrients, hormones, and waste products throughout the body.", "correctAnswer": "Plasma"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:49:58.485924	\N
a1184dd0-cd53-4f8c-8680-069b7ceebc05	47451ad5-3ccb-4c5d-a8f7-4a3f8b133b8b	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762761121400_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 combines to 12 because 7 + 3 = 10 and the remaining 2 makes 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762761121400_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square is a quadrilateral with four equal sides and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762761121400_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: 15 − 9 = ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 9 from 15 leaves 6 because 15 − 10 = 5, and adding 1 back gives 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762761121400_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "1/3", "3/2", "2/3"], "question": "Which fraction is equal to one half?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies to 1/2 because both the numerator and denominator can be divided by 2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1762761121400_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In the number 4,507, what value does the digit 4 represent?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 4 is in the thousands place, so it represents 4 thousands, which is 4000.", "correctAnswer": "4000"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:52:01.41309	\N
54ff821a-7b78-4b1b-9878-d6b5c0cd40d9	47451ad5-3ccb-4c5d-a8f7-4a3f8b133b8b	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1762761392538_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "12", "13", "15"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 equals 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "question": "What is 9 − 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 equals 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "10", "13", "15"], "question": "Which of the following is an even number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder. 10 is even; 7, 13, and 15 are odd.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "3/5", "4/6", "5/8"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dividing numerator and denominator of 1/2 by the same number keeps the value. 2/4 simplifies to 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ones", "Tens", "Hundreds", "Thousands"], "question": "What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 356?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 356, the digits represent hundreds (3), tens (5), and ones (6). So 5 is in the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "Tens"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Circle", "Triangle", "Rectangle", "Square"], "question": "Which shape has exactly 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides. Rectangles and squares have 4, and a circle has no sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are integers divisible by 2. Zero divided by 2 is zero, so zero is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles in a triangle is 200 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The sum of interior angles in any triangle is 180 degrees, not 200 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "3 × 0 = 0.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Any number multiplied by zero equals zero.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A rectangle has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has opposite sides equal. If all four sides are equal, it's a square (which is a special type of rectangle).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "6 × __ = 30", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "30 divided by 6 equals 5, so 6 multiplied by 5 gives 30.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The next number in the sequence 2, 4, 6, 8, __ is:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Each number increases by 2. After 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "12 ÷ 3 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "12 divided by 3 equals 4 because 3 × 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the fraction for 3 shaded squares out of 8 squares.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Fractions represent part over whole. With 3 shaded out of 8 total, the fraction is 3/8.", "correctAnswer": "3/8"}, {"id": "q_1762761392538_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the perimeter of a square with side length 5 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a square is 4 times the side length: 4 × 5 = 20 units.", "correctAnswer": "20"}]	none	2025-11-10 07:56:35.001784	\N
68ee4379-f648-41ed-b528-8743816fe596	c1ec787d-38b9-43e7-92fa-717902cd68f0	HISTORY - Medium	HISTORY	medium	[{"id": "q_1762770714007_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To centralize the state and fund a modern conscript army and bureaucracy", "To avoid paying indemnities imposed by China after the Sino-Japanese War", "To revive a traditional Confucian hierarchy and strengthen feudal domains", "To comply with the Treaty of Kanagawa’s requirement to eliminate the samurai class"], "question": "Why did the Meiji government in Japan abolish samurai stipends and privileges in the 1870s?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Abolishing samurai stipends and privileges helped dismantle the feudal order and redirected resources to build a national army (conscription law of 1873) and a modern bureaucracy. This fiscal and institutional centralization was essential to Japan’s rapid modernization, not a foreign treaty requirement or a revival of feudalism.", "correctAnswer": "To centralize the state and fund a modern conscript army and bureaucracy"}, {"id": "q_1762770714007_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The primary aim of the U.S. Marshall Plan (1948) was to rebuild Western European economies and contain communism, rather than punish former Axis powers.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Marshall Plan provided over $13 billion to jump-start economic recovery in Western Europe, stabilize democratic governments, and limit communist appeal. It emphasized reconstruction and cooperation, not retribution against former Axis states.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762770714007_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The 1648 treaties ending the Thirty Years’ War are collectively known as the Peace of _______, often credited with establishing the principle of state sovereignty in Europe.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Peace of Westphalia recognized the authority of territorial states over their internal affairs and helped shape the modern international system based on sovereign, legally equal states.", "correctAnswer": "Westphalia"}, {"id": "q_1762770714007_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain one significant way the Columbian Exchange transformed societies in Africa.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Columbian Exchange moved crops, people, and diseases across continents. In Africa, high-calorie American crops supported demographic growth and agricultural resilience, while Atlantic trade networks deepened, with profound social and political consequences tied to the slave trade.", "correctAnswer": "New World crops such as cassava and maize were introduced, boosting food security and population in parts of Africa; additionally, trans-Atlantic demand for plantation labor intensified the slave trade linking African societies more tightly to Atlantic markets."}, {"id": "q_1762770714007_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The expansion of printing presses and vernacular pamphlets produced by urban printer networks", "Luther’s exclusive access to royal messengers in every European court", "Mandatory papal sermons that required priests to read the theses aloud", "Near-universal literacy across Europe by the early 16th century"], "question": "Which factor best explains the rapid spread of Martin Luther’s 95 Theses after 1517?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Movable-type printing enabled rapid, low-cost reproduction and dissemination of Luther’s ideas in vernacular languages. Printer networks and urban readerships amplified his critique far beyond Wittenberg; literacy was growing but far from universal, and there was no official church promotion.", "correctAnswer": "The expansion of printing presses and vernacular pamphlets produced by urban printer networks"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:31:54.017983	\N
f37573f3-9fca-411a-b54b-fb9fafaaba3a	c6991598-421a-4185-8c34-061f5252824e	ict - Easy	ict	easy	[{"id": "q_1762770776386_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which device is primarily used to input text into a computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A keyboard is an input device used to enter letters, numbers, and symbols into a computer. A monitor displays output, a printer produces hard copies, and speakers output sound.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1762770776386_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "An IP address uniquely identifies a device on a network.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An IP address is a numerical label assigned to each device on a network, allowing data to be routed to the correct destination. While addresses can change (e.g., via DHCP), each active device has a unique address on its network at a given time.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762770776386_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The global network of interconnected computers is called the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is the worldwide system of interconnected networks. The World Wide Web is a service that runs on the Internet, consisting of web pages and websites.", "correctAnswer": "Internet"}, {"id": "q_1762770776386_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does 'USB' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "USB stands for Universal Serial Bus, a standard interface used to connect peripherals (like keyboards, mice, and storage devices) to computers and transfer data and power.", "correctAnswer": "Universal Serial Bus"}, {"id": "q_1762770776386_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["JPEG", "DOCX", "WAV", "EXE"], "question": "Which file format is commonly used for compressed digital photos?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "JPEG is a common image format that uses lossy compression suited to photographs. DOCX is a word processing document, WAV is uncompressed audio, and EXE is an executable program file.", "correctAnswer": "JPEG"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:32:56.398539	\N
cfdcc17f-8b56-4045-a66a-8eefac7ca8b7	0d1b2b53-9f3c-4ff9-adde-97a6f8d058c6	Algebra - Medium	Algebra	medium	[{"id": "q_1762770803805_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["17/4", "9/2", "7/4", "5/2"], "question": "Solve for x: 3(2x − 1) − 4 = 2(x + 5).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Distribute: 3(2x − 1) − 4 = 6x − 3 − 4 = 6x − 7. Set equal to the right side: 6x − 7 = 2x + 10. Subtract 2x: 4x − 7 = 10. Add 7: 4x = 17. Divide by 4: x = 17/4.", "correctAnswer": "17/4"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_1", "type": "true_false", "options": [], "question": "For f(x) = x^2 − 4x + 3, the identity f(a + 1) − f(a) = 2a − 1 holds for all real a.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Compute f(a + 1) = (a + 1)^2 − 4(a + 1) + 3 = a^2 − 2a. Then f(a + 1) − f(a) = (a^2 − 2a) − (a^2 − 4a + 3) = 2a − 3, not 2a − 1.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_2", "type": "fill_blank", "options": [], "question": "Simplify the rational expression: (x^2 − 9) / (x^2 − x − 6). Give your answer in lowest terms.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor numerator and denominator: x^2 − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3), x^2 − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2). Cancel (x − 3) to get (x + 3)/(x + 2), with restrictions x ≠ 3 and x ≠ −2.", "correctAnswer": "(x+3)/(x+2)"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["17", "18", "16", "9"], "question": "Two numbers have a sum of 26 and a difference of 8 (larger minus smaller). What is the larger number?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let x be the larger and y the smaller. Then x + y = 26 and x − y = 8. Adding gives 2x = 34, so x = 17.", "correctAnswer": "17"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_4", "type": "short_answer", "options": [], "question": "Solve the inequality |2x − 5| < 7 and express the solution in interval notation.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Translate |2x − 5| < 7 to −7 < 2x − 5 < 7. Add 5: −2 < 2x < 12. Divide by 2: −1 < x < 6, which is the interval (−1, 6).", "correctAnswer": "(-1, 6)"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(3, 4)", "(−3, −4)", "(3, −4)", "(−3, 4)"], "question": "Find the vertex of the parabola y = −x^2 + 6x − 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For y = ax^2 + bx + c, the x-coordinate of the vertex is −b/(2a) = −6/(2·(−1)) = 3. Then y(3) = −9 + 18 − 5 = 4. So the vertex is (3, 4).", "correctAnswer": "(3, 4)"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_6", "type": "true_false", "options": [], "question": "For all real numbers x, x/(x + 1) + 1/(x + 1) = 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Algebraically, x/(x + 1) + 1/(x + 1) = (x + 1)/(x + 1) = 1 when x ≠ −1. However, at x = −1 the expression is undefined, so it is not true for all real x.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_7", "type": "fill_blank", "options": [], "question": "Solve for t in terms of A, P, and r: A = P(1 + r t).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Divide both sides by P: A/P = 1 + rt. Subtract 1: A/P − 1 = rt. Divide by r: t = (A/P − 1)/r = (A − P)/(Pr).", "correctAnswer": "(A - P)/(Pr)"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "question": "Solve for x: 3^(2x − 1) = 27.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Since 27 = 3^3, set exponents equal: 2x − 1 = 3. Then 2x = 4 and x = 2.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1762770803805_9", "type": "short_answer", "options": [], "question": "An arithmetic sequence has a1 = 7 and a5 = 23. Find the common difference d and the general term a_n.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For an arithmetic sequence, a5 = a1 + 4d. So 23 = 7 + 4d, giving d = 4. Then a_n = a1 + (n − 1)d = 7 + 4(n − 1) = 4n + 3.", "correctAnswer": "d = 4; a_n = 4n + 3"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:33:23.904784	\N
01fc58fa-28e1-4104-8613-9727e3a4125e	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	Humaties: Communities, Geographical , cultural, religious, economic,add challenges, stengths, characteristics, world veiw, beliefs system, values and ethics, cultural and social context - Easy	Humaties: Communities, Geographical , cultural, religious, economic,add challenges, stengths, characteristics, world veiw, beliefs system, values and ethics, cultural and social context	easy	[{"id": "q_1770742516750_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A group of people who share a place or common interests", "One person living alone", "A large building", "A government document"], "question": "Which best describes a community?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A community is made up of people connected by location, interests, culture, or goals. It is not a single person or an object like a building.", "correctAnswer": "A group of people who share a place or common interests"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Geography can influence the foods people eat.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Geography shapes climate and available natural resources. Coastal communities may eat more seafood, while inland or mountainous areas rely on different local crops and animals.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The shared ideas about right and wrong in a community are called values and ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Values and ethics guide behavior by defining what is considered acceptable or unacceptable in a community.", "correctAnswer": "ethics"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Language", "Blood type", "Height", "Eye color"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of a cultural characteristic?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cultural characteristics include learned behaviors and symbols like language, traditions, and customs, not biological traits like blood type or eye color.", "correctAnswer": "Language"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common economic challenge many communities face.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Economic challenges affect well-being and opportunities. Beginners should recognize common issues such as unemployment or poverty that impact many communities.", "correctAnswer": "Answers may vary: examples include unemployment, poverty, lack of jobs, or unequal access to resources."}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "A belief system is a set of ideas that helps people make sense of the world.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Belief systems (religious or non-religious) provide frameworks for understanding life, purpose, and moral guidance.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A map showing countries", "The way a person or group understands and interprets the world", "A list of government laws", "A traditional holiday"], "question": "What is a worldview?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Worldview refers to the overall perspective people use to interpret life, events, and experiences, shaping beliefs and behavior.", "correctAnswer": "The way a person or group understands and interprets the world"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A strength of a diverse community is access to many different ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Diversity brings varied perspectives, ideas, and skills, which can help communities solve problems and innovate.", "correctAnswer": "perspectives"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Festivals and rituals", "Tax rates", "Road speed limits", "Soil types"], "question": "Which of the following is often part of religious practice in a community?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Religious practices commonly include rituals, ceremonies, festivals, and gatherings that express shared beliefs and traditions.", "correctAnswer": "Festivals and rituals"}, {"id": "q_1770742516750_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Social context includes the relationships, roles, and norms that shape how people interact in a community.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Social context refers to the web of relationships and expectations—such as family roles, peer groups, and community norms—that influence behavior and communication.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2026-02-10 16:55:21.882887	\N
970901d7-abe9-4fad-9624-0fe61a8b6a8c	a3f861c1-835c-48a2-bb62-423cebf081a7	English, Maths n science - Medium	English, Maths n science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762770821337_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["She likes jogging, to swim, and biking.", "She likes jogging, swimming, and biking.", "She likes to jog, swimming, and biking.", "She likes jogging, to swim, and to bike."], "question": "Which sentence uses parallel structure correctly?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure means matching grammatical forms in a series. Using all gerunds (jogging, swimming, biking) creates parallelism. The other options mix infinitives (to swim/to bike) with gerunds, which breaks parallel structure.", "correctAnswer": "She likes jogging, swimming, and biking."}, {"id": "q_1762770821337_1", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Printer A prints 30 pages per minute and Printer B prints 20 pages per minute. They start together to print a 300-page document. After 3 minutes, Printer B jams and stops. How many total minutes are needed to finish the job?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Together for the first 3 minutes, they print (30 + 20) × 3 = 150 pages. Remaining pages: 300 − 150 = 150. Printer A continues alone at 30 pages/min, so time needed is 150 ÷ 30 = 5 minutes. Total time: 3 + 5 = 8 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "8 minutes"}, {"id": "q_1762770821337_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "In an enzyme-catalyzed reaction, increasing substrate concentration will keep increasing the reaction rate indefinitely.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "As substrate concentration increases, the reaction rate rises until all enzyme active sites are saturated. At saturation, the rate reaches a maximum (Vmax) and cannot increase further without adding more enzyme. This is described by Michaelis–Menten kinetics.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762770821337_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the word that correctly completes the sentence: 'These shoes ______ your outfit perfectly.'", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Complement means to complete or enhance something by providing a good match. Compliment means to express praise. The sentence refers to matching the outfit, so 'complement' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "complement"}, {"id": "q_1762770821337_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Its acceleration is zero because the speed is constant.", "It has a constant-magnitude acceleration directed toward the center of the circle.", "Its acceleration is tangent to the track in the direction of motion.", "Its acceleration points outward, away from the center."], "question": "A car travels at a constant speed around a circular track. Which statement about its acceleration is correct?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, and velocity includes both speed and direction. Even at constant speed, the direction changes continuously in circular motion, producing a centripetal acceleration of magnitude v²/r directed toward the center.", "correctAnswer": "It has a constant-magnitude acceleration directed toward the center of the circle."}]	none	2025-11-10 10:33:41.348539	\N
44ce4ef7-2ceb-422a-aef9-8902cac91f06	d54e1ee4-093f-4291-8897-b3fa9db42620	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762770832259_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)", "Platform as a Service (PaaS)", "Software as a Service (SaaS)", "On-premises virtual machines"], "question": "Your team needs to deploy a custom web application quickly without managing servers or operating systems. You want automatic scaling, a managed runtime, and integrated build/deploy tools. Which cloud service model best fits?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "PaaS provides a managed platform (runtime, scaling, deployment pipelines) so you can focus on code, not servers. IaaS gives you VMs and you still manage OS and runtime. SaaS is a complete application you consume, not a platform for your own code. On-premises VMs still require you to manage hardware and OS, slowing delivery.", "correctAnswer": "Platform as a Service (PaaS)"}, {"id": "q_1762770832259_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "WPA3-Personal reduces the risk of offline dictionary attacks compared to WPA2-PSK by using Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE), even if an attacker captures the handshake.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "WPA3-Personal replaces WPA2-PSK’s handshake with SAE, a password-authenticated key exchange that prevents an attacker from validating password guesses offline. SAE also provides forward secrecy, so captured handshakes cannot be brute-forced in the same way as WPA2-PSK.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762770832259_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A backup that copies only the data changed since the last backup of any type (full or incremental) is called an ________ backup.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incremental backups record only changes since the most recent backup of any type, minimizing backup time and storage. In contrast, differential backups copy changes since the last full backup, growing larger each day until the next full backup.", "correctAnswer": "incremental"}, {"id": "q_1762770832259_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain how two-factor authentication (2FA) improves account security and give one example that uses two different factor categories.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "2FA combines distinct categories—knowledge (password/PIN), possession (phone/token), and inherence (biometrics). Even if one factor is compromised (e.g., password reused or phished), the second factor blocks unauthorized access, significantly reducing the risk from credential stuffing and phishing.", "correctAnswer": "2FA requires two independent factor types (such as something you know and something you have), so an attacker who learns a password alone still cannot log in. Example: password (knowledge) + one-time code from an authenticator app or hardware token (possession)."}, {"id": "q_1762770832259_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Office ISP uplink (50 Mbps)", "Office LAN (1 Gbps)", "Client Wi‑Fi link (300 Mbps)", "Server disk throughput (600 MB/s)"], "question": "An office file server (disk: 600 MB/s), connected via a 1 Gbps LAN, uploads files to the internet over a 50 Mbps uplink. A home user with 200 Mbps internet and 300 Mbps Wi‑Fi downloads the file. Which link most likely limits the user’s maximum download speed?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "End-to-end throughput is constrained by the slowest link on the path. The office uplink at 50 Mbps is the lowest capacity, so it becomes the bottleneck. The server disk (~4800 Mbps when converting 600 MB/s × 8), the 1 Gbps LAN, and the client’s Wi‑Fi/ISP downlink are all faster than 50 Mbps.", "correctAnswer": "Office ISP uplink (50 Mbps)"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:33:52.271811	\N
7fadbb41-6c84-4910-9ccc-c048e72ec26f	05903f07-aff8-4075-b4f4-d76422f33b3b	W0RLD WAR 1 - Easy	W0RLD WAR 1	easy	[{"id": "q_1762770907409_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand", "Germany's invasion of Poland", "The attack on Pearl Harbor", "The French Revolution"], "question": "Which event is widely considered the spark that triggered World War I?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914, set off a chain reaction of alliance obligations and mobilizations that led to the outbreak of World War I.", "correctAnswer": "The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["France, Russia, and Great Britain", "Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Italy", "United States, Japan, and Italy", "Great Britain, Germany, and the Ottoman Empire"], "question": "Which countries formed the Triple Entente before World War I?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Triple Entente was the pre-war alliance of France, Russia, and Great Britain, intended to counterbalance the Triple Alliance of Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Italy.", "correctAnswer": "France, Russia, and Great Britain"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "The United States entered World War I in 1917.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The United States declared war on Germany in April 1917, influenced by factors such as unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "World War I began in the year ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War I began in 1914 after a series of escalating events following the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand and subsequent declarations of war by major powers.", "correctAnswer": "1914"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one country that was part of the Central Powers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Central Powers included Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria. Any one of these is a correct response.", "correctAnswer": "Germany"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What style of fighting dominated much of the Western Front in World War I?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Trench warfare defined the Western Front, with opposing armies digging extensive trench systems and fighting from static positions across No Man's Land.", "correctAnswer": "Trench warfare"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Tanks were first used in combat during World War I.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tanks were introduced during World War I, first used by the British in 1916 at the Battle of the Somme to break through entrenched lines.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Sarajevo", "Belgrade", "Vienna", "Berlin"], "question": "In which city was Archduke Franz Ferdinand assassinated?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Franz Ferdinand was assassinated in Sarajevo, the capital of Bosnia (then under Austro-Hungarian rule), an event that directly led to the July Crisis of 1914.", "correctAnswer": "Sarajevo"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The Zimmermann Telegram", "The Magna Carta", "The Monroe Doctrine", "The Emancipation Proclamation"], "question": "Which intercepted message helped push the United States closer to war with Germany in 1917?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Zimmermann Telegram was a secret German proposal urging Mexico to join the war against the U.S.; its interception and publication angered Americans and influenced the decision to enter the war.", "correctAnswer": "The Zimmermann Telegram"}, {"id": "q_1762770907409_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The Treaty of ______ officially ended the war between Germany and the Allied Powers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Signed on June 28, 1919, the Treaty of Versailles ended the state of war between Germany and the Allies and imposed reparations and territorial losses on Germany.", "correctAnswer": "Versailles"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:35:07.502604	\N
e4c895d5-8cff-4c99-901a-473e9ed785db	00ae670a-9bc3-45d5-80b5-a21ca24ca68d	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762770921840_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It becomes turgid as water enters and the cell wall limits expansion", "It undergoes plasmolysis and shrivels", "There is no net movement of water; the cell remains flaccid", "It bursts due to water influx"], "question": "A plant cell is placed in a solution with a lower solute concentration than its cytoplasm. What is the most likely outcome?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The solution is hypotonic relative to the cell, so water moves into the cell by osmosis. In plant cells, the rigid cell wall prevents bursting, and the central vacuole fills, creating turgor pressure that makes the cell firm (turgid). Plasmolysis occurs in hypertonic solutions, and isotonic solutions produce little net movement.", "correctAnswer": "It becomes turgid as water enters and the cell wall limits expansion"}, {"id": "q_1762770921840_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A satellite in low Earth orbit experiences weightlessness because Earth's gravity at that altitude is essentially zero.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Gravity at low Earth orbit is still strong—only slightly less than at Earth's surface. Astronauts feel weightless because the spacecraft and everything in it are in continuous free fall around Earth, creating a microgravity environment where apparent weight is near zero.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762770921840_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a series circuit, adding more identical resistors will ______ the total current for a fixed voltage source.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In a series circuit, resistances add directly, so the equivalent resistance increases with each added resistor. By Ohm’s law (I = V/R), a higher total resistance with the same voltage results in a lower current.", "correctAnswer": "decrease"}, {"id": "q_1762770921840_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why lunar eclipses do not occur at every full moon.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Eclipses require precise alignment. The tilt of the Moon’s orbit means the full moon usually misses Earth’s umbra. When the full moon is near a node (the intersection of the orbital planes), alignment is sufficient for Earth’s shadow to fall on the Moon, producing an eclipse.", "correctAnswer": "Because the Moon’s orbital plane is tilted about 5 degrees relative to Earth’s orbital plane, most full moons pass above or below Earth’s shadow. A lunar eclipse only occurs when a full moon happens near one of the nodes where the two planes intersect, aligning the Sun, Earth, and Moon."}, {"id": "q_1762770921840_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Advection fog", "Radiation fog", "Steam fog", "Upslope fog"], "question": "A coastal region frequently develops fog when warm, moist air moves over a cold ocean current. What type of fog is this?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Advection fog forms when warm, moist air is transported horizontally over a colder surface, cooling the air to its dew point. Radiation fog forms from ground cooling at night under clear skies, steam fog forms when cold air passes over warm water, and upslope fog occurs as air is lifted and cooled along terrain.", "correctAnswer": "Advection fog"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:35:21.852714	\N
34db2913-ca3a-43b3-997a-c21638bf057b	a3f861c1-835c-48a2-bb62-423cebf081a7	English - Medium	English	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771093862_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["She enjoys reading, to jog, and cooking.", "She enjoys reading, jogging, and cooking.", "She enjoys to read, jogging, and to cook.", "She enjoys reading, jogging, and to cook."], "question": "Which sentence maintains proper parallel structure?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires items in a series to use the same grammatical form. Using three gerunds (reading, jogging, cooking) maintains consistency. The other options mix infinitives (to jog, to cook) with gerunds, which breaks parallelism.", "correctAnswer": "She enjoys reading, jogging, and cooking."}, {"id": "q_1762771093862_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: A colon can be used after a complete sentence to introduce a list, explanation, or quotation.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A colon follows a complete independent clause and introduces material that illustrates or explains it—such as a list, a clarifying statement, or a quotation. It should not be placed directly after a verb or preposition; the part before the colon must stand alone as a sentence.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771093862_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct verb form: \\"Neither the consultant nor the executives ___ willing to compromise.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "With \\"neither...nor,\\" the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. Here, \\"executives\\" is plural and closest to the verb, so the correct form is \\"were.\\" Writing \\"was\\" would incorrectly agree with the singular noun \\"consultant\\" instead of the nearer plural noun.", "correctAnswer": "were"}, {"id": "q_1762771093862_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Identify the rhetorical device used in the sentence: \\"The city never sleeps.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Personification gives human traits to nonhuman things. A city cannot literally sleep; attributing the human action of sleeping to a city is a classic example of personification, suggesting constant activity.", "correctAnswer": "personification"}, {"id": "q_1762771093862_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Walking down the street, the skyscrapers towered over me.", "Walking down the street, I felt the skyscrapers tower over me.", "Walking down the street, towering over me were the skyscrapers.", "Walking down the street, the feeling of skyscrapers towering was overwhelming."], "question": "Which sentence correctly places the introductory modifier?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "An introductory participial phrase (\\"Walking down the street\\") should modify the subject that immediately follows. In the correct sentence, the subject \\"I\\" is the one walking. In the other options, the subjects (skyscrapers/the feeling) are not doing the walking, creating a dangling modifier.", "correctAnswer": "Walking down the street, I felt the skyscrapers tower over me."}]	none	2025-11-10 10:38:13.873089	\N
898d3aec-9204-49b2-a8cd-852a7cd5455b	2a0d8f96-4f89-4d95-b9c0-b41def6f2a69	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771259925_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["58%", "60%", "62%", "It will sink completely"], "question": "An object floats with 60% of its volume submerged in freshwater. If the same object is placed in seawater of density about 1.03 g/cm³, approximately what fraction of its volume will be submerged?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a floating object, weight equals buoyant force: ρfluid × Vsubmerged = constant. The submerged fraction is inversely proportional to fluid density. Going from 1.00 to 1.03 g/cm³, the new fraction ≈ 0.60 × (1.00/1.03) ≈ 0.58, or 58%.", "correctAnswer": "58%"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4.46", "4.76", "5.06", "3.76"], "question": "A buffer solution contains equal concentrations of acetic acid and acetate (pKa = 4.76). After adding a small amount of strong acid, the base-to-acid ratio becomes 0.50. What is the new pH?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation: pH = pKa + log([A−]/[HA]) = 4.76 + log(0.50) = 4.76 − 0.30 ≈ 4.46. The buffer resists large pH changes, so the shift is modest.", "correctAnswer": "4.46"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "In humans, insulin directly increases glucose uptake in nearly all body cells.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Insulin primarily stimulates GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue. Many cells (e.g., neurons, red blood cells) use insulin-independent transporters like GLUT1/GLUT3.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The boundary where a cold air mass undercuts a warm air mass, often producing narrow bands of intense thunderstorms, is called a ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A cold front forms when denser cold air wedges beneath warmer air, forcing rapid uplift that can trigger cumulonimbus clouds and severe weather.", "correctAnswer": "cold front"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What causes the apparent retrograde motion of Mars in the night sky, and when is it most noticeable?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Because Earth orbits closer to the Sun, it moves faster and periodically passes Mars. During opposition, this geometry causes an apparent reversal (westward loop) relative to distant stars.", "correctAnswer": "Earth overtakes Mars near opposition, making Mars appear to move westward against the background stars."}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Doubling the frequency of a sound wave traveling in the same medium doubles its speed.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In a given medium, wave speed is set by the medium’s properties. If frequency doubles, wavelength halves (v = fλ), leaving speed unchanged.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a galvanic (voltaic) cell, electrons flow through the external circuit from the ____ to the ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Oxidation occurs at the anode, releasing electrons that travel through the external circuit to the cathode, where reduction occurs.", "correctAnswer": "anode to the cathode"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Vmax unchanged; Km increases", "Vmax decreases; Km unchanged", "Vmax decreases; Km increases", "Vmax unchanged; Km decreases"], "question": "In competitive inhibition of an enzyme, how are Vmax and Km affected?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Competitive inhibitors bind the active site and can be overcome by high substrate concentration, so Vmax stays the same. Apparent Km increases because more substrate is needed to reach half-maximal velocity.", "correctAnswer": "Vmax unchanged; Km increases"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Chemical weathering of silicate rocks leading to carbonate formation", "Photosynthesis by terrestrial plants", "Evaporation from oceans", "Nitrogen fixation by soil bacteria"], "question": "Which process acts as a long-term sink for atmospheric CO2 on geologic timescales?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Silicate weathering consumes CO2 to form bicarbonate that ultimately precipitates as carbonates in the oceans, sequestering carbon over millions of years. Photosynthesis acts on short timescales and is balanced by respiration.", "correctAnswer": "Chemical weathering of silicate rocks leading to carbonate formation"}, {"id": "q_1762771259925_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A bacterial culture is exposed to a bactericidal antibiotic during the stationary phase. Why is it generally less effective than if applied during the exponential (log) phase?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Many antibiotics kill best when cells are actively growing and synthesizing macromolecules. In stationary phase, nutrient limitation and stress responses downregulate these pathways, decreasing susceptibility.", "correctAnswer": "Cells in stationary phase are metabolically slow and not actively dividing, so growth-dependent targets (e.g., cell wall synthesis) are reduced, lowering antibiotic efficacy."}]	none	2025-11-10 10:40:59.937212	\N
c3df76f0-ce0f-4da7-85ec-3e08b53d80f4	00ae670a-9bc3-45d5-80b5-a21ca24ca68d	english - Easy	english	easy	[{"id": "q_1762771324482_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["childs", "children", "childes", "child's"], "question": "What is the correct plural form of the noun \\"child\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Many English nouns form the plural by adding -s, but some are irregular. \\"Child\\" is an irregular noun, and its plural is \\"children,\\" not \\"childs\\" or \\"childes.\\" \\"Child's\\" is a possessive form, not a plural.", "correctAnswer": "children"}, {"id": "q_1762771324482_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "An adjective is a word that describes a noun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adjectives provide more information about nouns, such as size, color, or quality. For example, in \\"red apple,\\" the word \\"red\\" is an adjective describing the noun \\"apple.\\"", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771324482_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct form of the verb \\"to be\\": She ___ happy.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the present simple tense, the third-person singular form of \\"to be\\" is \\"is\\" (I am, you are, he/she/it is). Therefore, \\"She is happy\\" is correct.", "correctAnswer": "is"}, {"id": "q_1762771324482_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"go\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Go\\" is an irregular verb. Instead of adding -ed, it changes to \\"went\\" in the simple past (e.g., Yesterday, I went to school).", "correctAnswer": "went"}, {"id": "q_1762771324482_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".", "?", "!", ","], "question": "Which punctuation mark correctly ends this sentence as a question: \\"Where are you\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Direct questions in English end with a question mark. \\"Where are you?\\" is a question, so it should be finished with \\"?\\" rather than a period, exclamation mark, or comma.", "correctAnswer": "?"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:42:04.495324	\N
44e5309b-c0a3-4cde-85d3-71990ca2f44c	86fa1956-30b6-431f-826f-a73f19635b15	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771406124_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0.50 atm", "2.0 atm", "3.0 atm", "4.0 atm"], "question": "A sample of gas at 1.0 atm and 2.0 L is compressed at constant temperature to 0.50 L. What is the new pressure of the gas?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a gas at constant temperature, Boyle’s law applies: P1V1 = P2V2. Solving for P2 gives P2 = (P1 × V1) / V2 = (1.0 atm × 2.0 L) / 0.50 L = 4.0 atm. Pressure increases when volume decreases at constant temperature.", "correctAnswer": "4.0 atm"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In cellular respiration, the majority of ATP is produced during glycolysis.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Glycolysis produces a small amount of ATP (net 2 ATP per glucose). The majority of ATP is generated during oxidative phosphorylation in the electron transport chain, powered by NADH and FADH2 from earlier stages.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In aerobic respiration, the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the electron transport chain, forming water. This drives proton pumping and ATP synthesis; without oxygen, the chain backs up and ATP yield drops.", "correctAnswer": "oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why is salt spread on icy roads, and what physical principle explains its effectiveness?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dissolved ions disrupt the formation of the solid lattice of ice, reducing the temperature at which water freezes. Sodium chloride can lower the freezing point several degrees below 0°C, so the resulting brine melts existing ice and prevents refreezing until the temperature drops below the depressed freezing point.", "correctAnswer": "Salt lowers the freezing point of water via colligative properties (freezing point depression), forming a brine that stays liquid below 0°C and melts ice."}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["East", "West", "Upward", "South"], "question": "A proton moves north through a region with a uniform magnetic field pointing upward. In which direction is the magnetic force on the proton?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The magnetic force is q(v × B). Using the right-hand rule for a positive charge: index finger (velocity) north, middle finger (field) up, thumb points east. A negative charge would experience the opposite direction.", "correctAnswer": "East"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The half-life of a radioactive isotope depends on the initial amount of the sample.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Half-life is a characteristic property of the isotope’s nucleus and is independent of the initial quantity. It is the time for half of any amount to decay, governed by the decay constant.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Carbonate ion concentration increases", "Ocean pH increases (more alkaline)", "Availability of carbonate for calcification decreases", "No change in carbonate chemistry"], "question": "As atmospheric CO2 increases and dissolves into seawater, which change most directly affects shell-building organisms like corals and mollusks?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "CO2 reacts with water to form carbonic acid, which releases H+ and shifts carbonate equilibria toward bicarbonate. This lowers pH and reduces carbonate ion (CO3^2−) availability, making calcification more difficult.", "correctAnswer": "Availability of carbonate for calcification decreases"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process of copying genetic information from DNA into messenger RNA is called ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Transcription uses RNA polymerase to read a DNA template and synthesize complementary mRNA, which is then translated into protein by ribosomes.", "correctAnswer": "transcription"}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why do satellites in low Earth orbit gradually lose altitude over time if they do not perform periodic boosts?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Even at low Earth orbit, residual air molecules create drag. This dissipative force converts orbital mechanical energy into heat, lowering the satellite’s speed and thus its orbital altitude; periodic reboosts counteract this decay.", "correctAnswer": "Thin atmospheric drag in the upper atmosphere removes orbital energy, causing the orbit to decay and altitude to decrease."}, {"id": "q_1762771406124_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0.050 M", "0.100 M", "0.125 M", "0.250 M"], "question": "A 25.0 mL sample of 0.100 M HCl is titrated to equivalence with NaOH. If it takes 20.0 mL of NaOH to reach the endpoint, what is the NaOH concentration?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a 1:1 acid-base reaction, M_acid × V_acid = M_base × V_base. M_base = (0.100 M × 25.0 mL) / 20.0 mL = 0.125 M.", "correctAnswer": "0.125 M"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:43:26.142864	\N
f3f43b9c-5c0a-470e-8574-1a1ff460479e	2a0d8f96-4f89-4d95-b9c0-b41def6f2a69	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771429192_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["80 m", "160 m", "20 m", "40 m"], "question": "A car traveling at 20 m/s requires 40 m to come to a stop on a dry road. If the driver is traveling at 40 m/s on the same road and applies the brakes with the same constant deceleration, approximately how far will the car travel before stopping?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Braking distance under constant deceleration is proportional to the square of the speed because the work done by friction (force × distance) must remove the car’s kinetic energy (1/2 mv^2). Doubling the speed from 20 m/s to 40 m/s quadruples the kinetic energy, so the stopping distance increases by a factor of four: 4 × 40 m = 160 m.", "correctAnswer": "160 m"}, {"id": "q_1762771429192_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: Increasing temperature will always increase the rate of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction in a cell.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzyme activity increases with temperature only up to an optimum. Beyond that, increased thermal motion disrupts the enzyme’s structure (denaturation) and active site, reducing catalytic efficiency. Therefore, raising temperature does not always increase the rate and can eventually decrease it.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771429192_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A rigid, insulated container is divided into two equal volumes by a valve. One side contains an ideal gas at 1 atm and room temperature; the other side is a vacuum. When the valve is opened and the gas expands to fill the entire container, the final temperature of the gas, compared to its initial temperature, is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is a free expansion in an insulated (adiabatic) system. No heat is exchanged (Q = 0) and no work is done on the surroundings (W = 0). For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature, so ΔU = 0 implies ΔT = 0. The gas cools in real gases, but for an ideal gas the temperature is unchanged.", "correctAnswer": "the same"}, {"id": "q_1762771429192_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why coastal locations typically have smaller daily and seasonal temperature ranges than inland locations at the same latitude.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water has a much higher specific heat capacity than land, so it warms and cools more slowly. This thermal buffering, combined with air circulation driven by sea–land breezes, evens out temperature fluctuations near coasts. Inland areas lack this moderating influence and experience larger swings.", "correctAnswer": "Because the ocean’s high specific heat and thermal inertia absorb and release heat slowly, and sea–land breezes enhance heat exchange, moderating nearby air temperatures and reducing daily and seasonal extremes."}, {"id": "q_1762771429192_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Series connection", "Parallel connection", "Same in both cases", "It depends on the value of R"], "question": "Two identical resistors, each of resistance R, are connected to the same ideal battery of fixed voltage V. In which configuration is the total power dissipated by the resistors greater?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total power for a fixed-voltage source is P = V^2 / R_eq. For two identical resistors: in series, R_eq = 2R, so P_series = V^2/(2R); in parallel, R_eq = R/2, so P_parallel = 2V^2/R. Since R/2 is less than 2R, the parallel configuration draws more current and dissipates more total power.", "correctAnswer": "Parallel connection"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:43:49.205657	\N
dbda3572-b59c-4853-8573-93e1ff0f5454	05903f07-aff8-4075-b4f4-d76422f33b3b	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771470527_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["SMTP", "IMAP", "POP3", "SNMP"], "question": "A company wants employees to read the same mailbox from multiple devices while keeping messages synchronized on the server. Which protocol best supports this requirement?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "IMAP is designed for server-side mail storage and synchronization across devices. POP3 typically downloads and may remove mail from the server, making multi-device sync harder. SMTP is for sending mail, not retrieving. SNMP is a management protocol unrelated to email.", "correctAnswer": "IMAP"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Using Network Address Translation (NAT) can help mitigate IPv4 address exhaustion by allowing multiple private hosts to share a single public IP.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "NAT maps many private IP addresses to one or a few public IPs, enabling multiple devices to access the internet without each requiring a unique public IPv4 address. This conserves public address space but introduces statefulness and complexity.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a relational database, the process of organizing tables to reduce redundancy and improve integrity is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Normalization structures tables into normal forms (e.g., 1NF, 2NF, 3NF) to eliminate redundant data and update anomalies, improving data integrity and efficient storage.", "correctAnswer": "normalization"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How many usable host addresses are available in the subnet 192.168.1.0/26?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A /26 mask leaves 6 host bits (32−26). Usable hosts = 2^6 − 2 = 64 − 2 = 62, subtracting network and broadcast addresses.", "correctAnswer": "62"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Software as a Service (SaaS)", "Platform as a Service (PaaS)", "Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)", "Function as a Service (FaaS)"], "question": "A development team wants to deploy web apps with auto-scaling and managed runtime, without maintaining VMs or the operating system. Which cloud service model fits best?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "PaaS provides managed platforms (runtime, middleware, scaling) so teams focus on application code without managing OS or VMs. SaaS delivers finished applications. IaaS offers VMs and storage but requires OS management. FaaS runs discrete functions and is event-driven, not entire app platforms.", "correctAnswer": "Platform as a Service (PaaS)"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "AES is an asymmetric encryption algorithm primarily used for public-key cryptography.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "AES is a symmetric block cipher that uses the same secret key for encryption and decryption. Asymmetric algorithms include RSA and ECC, which use key pairs for public-key operations.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "On Windows, the command-line tool commonly used to query DNS records and test name resolution is ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "nslookup sends queries to DNS servers to retrieve records like A, AAAA, MX, and CNAME, helping diagnose name resolution issues. On Unix-like systems, dig is also widely used.", "correctAnswer": "nslookup"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "An organization performs a full backup every Sunday and daily differential backups. On Thursday, how many backup sets are needed to fully restore?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Differential backups capture changes since the last full backup. To restore Thursday, you need the last full (Sunday) and the latest differential (Thursday), totaling two sets.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Lossless compression", "Lossy compression", "Run-length encoding", "Vector graphics"], "question": "After repeatedly editing and re-saving a JPEG image, you notice blocky artifacts and quality degradation. Which characteristic of JPEG explains this?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "JPEG uses lossy compression, discarding some data to reduce file size. Each re-save can compound losses, causing visible artifacts. Lossless methods preserve all data; vector graphics describe shapes rather than pixels.", "correctAnswer": "Lossy compression"}, {"id": "q_1762771470527_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["NAT", "VLANs", "Subnetting with routers", "Port mirroring"], "question": "A network team wants to isolate traffic between departments into separate broadcast domains without adding new physical switches or routers. What technology should they implement?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Virtual LANs (VLANs) logically segment a switch into multiple broadcast domains, isolating traffic per department without extra hardware. NAT translates addresses, not segment broadcast domains. Subnetting with routers requires routing devices. Port mirroring duplicates traffic for analysis.", "correctAnswer": "VLANs"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:44:30.654934	\N
15b9693d-d5e0-4291-810c-2c560a9fe159	00ae670a-9bc3-45d5-80b5-a21ca24ca68d	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771478268_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Higher speed and lower mechanical energy", "Lower speed and higher mechanical energy", "Higher speed and higher mechanical energy", "Lower speed and lower mechanical energy"], "question": "A satellite is moved from a low circular Earth orbit to a higher circular orbit. Compared to the initial orbit, in the final orbit the satellite’s orbital speed and total mechanical energy are:", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a circular orbit, speed v = sqrt(GM/r), so as orbital radius r increases, speed decreases. The total mechanical energy per unit mass is E = -GM/(2r), which becomes less negative (i.e., increases) as r increases. Therefore, in a higher orbit the satellite moves more slowly and has higher (less negative) mechanical energy.", "correctAnswer": "Lower speed and higher mechanical energy"}, {"id": "q_1762771478268_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "When titrating a weak acid with a strong base, the pH at the equivalence point is exactly 7.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At the equivalence point of a weak acid–strong base titration, the solution contains the conjugate base of the weak acid and no excess strong base. The conjugate base hydrolyzes water to form OH⁻, making the solution basic, so the pH is greater than 7.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771478268_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "During transcription initiation, RNA polymerase binds to a specific DNA sequence upstream of a gene called the ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The promoter is the regulatory sequence where RNA polymerase (and associated factors) assemble to begin transcription. Promoter motifs help position the polymerase and determine transcription start sites.", "correctAnswer": "promoter"}, {"id": "q_1762771478268_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why do coastal regions typically experience smaller temperature variations than inland areas?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water’s high specific heat means it can store large amounts of thermal energy with small temperature changes. Coastal air is moderated by the ocean’s thermal inertia, plus evaporative cooling and the mixing effects of currents and sea breezes, all of which dampen extremes.", "correctAnswer": "Because nearby oceans have a high specific heat, absorbing and releasing heat slowly; evaporation and mixing also moderate temperature, reducing daily and seasonal swings."}, {"id": "q_1762771478268_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Increase the temperature", "Decrease the pressure", "Add an inert gas at constant volume", "Compress the reaction mixture (decrease volume)"], "question": "Consider the exothermic gas-phase reaction: A(g) + B(g) ⇌ C(g) + heat. Which single change would shift the equilibrium to favor formation of C?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "There are 2 moles of gas on the reactant side and 1 on the product side. Decreasing volume (increasing pressure) shifts equilibrium toward fewer gas moles, favoring C. Increasing temperature would favor the reactants for an exothermic reaction. Decreasing pressure favors more gas moles (reactants). Adding an inert gas at constant volume doesn’t change partial pressures of A, B, or C and thus doesn’t shift equilibrium.", "correctAnswer": "Compress the reaction mixture (decrease volume)"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:44:38.281098	\N
114f4834-4d1c-4644-ae16-ac3ce651f709	0d1b2b53-9f3c-4ff9-adde-97a6f8d058c6	Algebra - Easy	Algebra	easy	[{"id": "q_1762771508805_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "question": "Solve for x: x + 3 = 7", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 3 from both sides to isolate x: x + 3 - 3 = 7 - 3, so x = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762771508805_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 3x + 2x = 5x", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like terms add by combining their coefficients: 3x + 2x = (3 + 2)x = 5x.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771508805_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Evaluate 2(a + 4) when a = 3. Fill in the blank: ___", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Substitute a = 3: 2(3 + 4) = 2(7) = 14.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1762771508805_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the coefficient of y in the term -7y?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable. In -7y, the number multiplying y is -7.", "correctAnswer": "-7"}, {"id": "q_1762771508805_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3(x + 2) = 3x + 6", "3x + 2 = 5x", "x/x = x^0", "(x^2)^3 = x^5"], "question": "Which equation shows the distributive property?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The distributive property states a(b + c) = ab + ac. Here, 3(x + 2) becomes 3x + 6.", "correctAnswer": "3(x + 2) = 3x + 6"}]	none	2025-11-10 10:45:08.888843	\N
63eb708d-e98f-4e08-b839-6a40a8a8b5e2	05903f07-aff8-4075-b4f4-d76422f33b3b	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1762771546969_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30", "32", "62", "14"], "question": "You are assigned the subnet 192.168.10.0/27 for a small department. How many usable host addresses are available?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "/27 provides 32 total addresses (2^(32−27) = 32). The network and broadcast addresses are not usable, leaving 30 usable host addresses.", "correctAnswer": "30"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "HTTPS alone guarantees that any website you visit is trustworthy.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "HTTPS encrypts data in transit and authenticates the server’s domain via certificates, but it does not vouch for the site’s intentions. Phishing sites can have valid certificates; trust depends on the domain and content, not just HTTPS.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In relational databases, the process of structuring tables to minimize redundancy and update anomalies is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Normalization organizes data into well-defined tables and relationships (e.g., 1NF, 2NF, 3NF) to reduce duplication and maintain data integrity during updates.", "correctAnswer": "normalization"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Briefly distinguish symmetric from asymmetric encryption and give one practical use case for each.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Symmetric encryption is fast and suitable for large data but requires secure key sharing. Asymmetric encryption enables secure key exchange and authentication; it’s often used to establish secure channels (e.g., TLS) and verify signatures, then a symmetric key is used for bulk data.", "correctAnswer": "Symmetric uses the same key for encryption and decryption (e.g., AES for data at rest). Asymmetric uses a public/private key pair (e.g., RSA for key exchange or digital signatures)."}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["INNER JOIN", "LEFT JOIN", "RIGHT JOIN", "CROSS JOIN"], "question": "You need to list all customers and, where present, their orders. Customers without orders should still appear. Which SQL JOIN should you use?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left (customers) table and matching rows from the right (orders) table. Non-matching right side rows are NULL, ensuring customers without orders still appear.", "correctAnswer": "LEFT JOIN"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Rebasing a shared branch in Git can rewrite commit history and may disrupt collaborators.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Git rebase rewrites commits, changing their IDs. If others have based work on the original history, rebasing a shared branch forces complex merges and can cause inconsistencies. Use rebase on private branches and merge for shared branches.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["SaaS", "PaaS", "IaaS", "FaaS"], "question": "Your team needs full control over the operating system and runtime on virtual machines while the provider manages the physical infrastructure. Which cloud service model best fits?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) provides virtualized compute, storage, and networking. You manage the OS and applications. PaaS abstracts the OS/runtime, SaaS delivers complete applications, and FaaS runs discrete functions without server management.", "correctAnswer": "IaaS"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The system that translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses is ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Domain Name System (DNS) is a hierarchical, distributed naming system that resolves domain names (like example.com) to IP addresses used for routing on networks.", "correctAnswer": "DNS"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["VLOOKUP", "HLOOKUP", "INDEX-MATCH", "SUMIF"], "question": "Which spreadsheet lookup approach is more robust than VLOOKUP when the lookup column is not the first column and you may insert columns?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "INDEX-MATCH can look up values in any column and return from any other column, independent of position. VLOOKUP requires the lookup column to be leftmost and is brittle when columns are inserted.", "correctAnswer": "INDEX-MATCH"}, {"id": "q_1762771546969_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the key difference between hashing and encryption, and when would you use each?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Hash functions produce fixed-size digests and are designed to be infeasible to reverse, useful for integrity checks and password storage (with salt). Encryption transforms data with a key so it can be decrypted, protecting sensitive information while allowing authorized access.", "correctAnswer": "Hashing is one-way for integrity verification (e.g., storing password hashes). Encryption is reversible for confidentiality (e.g., securing files or data in transit)."}]	none	2025-11-10 10:45:46.980945	\N
434b7ec0-dc01-424e-813e-2bec7e0daeab	5cc002fe-fac1-4d15-9a1e-474da492c43a	characteristics of living things - Easy	characteristics of living things	easy	[{"id": "q_1762781526580_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ability to move from place to place", "Use energy", "Made of one or more cells", "Can reproduce"], "question": "Which of the following is NOT a characteristic shared by all living things?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "All living things use energy, are made of cells, and reproduce (either sexually or asexually). Movement from place to place is not universal—many organisms, like plants and some bacteria, do not move their whole bodies even though they may show internal movements at the cellular level.", "correctAnswer": "Ability to move from place to place"}, {"id": "q_1762781526581_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: All living things are composed of one or more cells.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Being made of cells is a fundamental characteristic of life. Cells are the basic unit of structure and function in living organisms, whether the organism is unicellular (one cell) or multicellular (many cells).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762781526581_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The chemical processes by which organisms obtain and use energy are called ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metabolism includes all the chemical reactions in a living thing that build up or break down materials to provide energy and support life functions, such as growth and repair.", "correctAnswer": "metabolism"}, {"id": "q_1762781526581_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What molecule stores hereditary (genetic) information in most living organisms?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) carries the instructions for building and maintaining an organism. Genes made of DNA are passed from parents to offspring, ensuring continuity of traits.", "correctAnswer": "DNA"}, {"id": "q_1762781526581_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Response to stimuli", "Reproduction", "Excretion", "Homeostasis"], "question": "A plant bending toward a light source is an example of which characteristic of life?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bending toward light (phototropism) shows how organisms sense and respond to changes in their environment—a key characteristic of living things.", "correctAnswer": "Response to stimuli"}]	none	2025-11-10 13:32:06.663808	\N
6c8df272-156e-43bf-8f5a-8d5898f00eb0	5cc002fe-fac1-4d15-9a1e-474da492c43a	characteristics of living things - Medium	characteristics of living things	medium	[{"id": "q_1762781569219_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["After a meal, the pancreas releases insulin to lower rising blood glucose levels.", "A seed begins to germinate when placed in moist soil.", "A bird gathers twigs to build a nest in spring.", "Over many generations, a population’s allele frequencies shift due to natural selection."], "question": "Which scenario best demonstrates homeostasis in a living organism?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment. Insulin release after eating helps regulate blood glucose, keeping it within a narrow range. Germination is growth and development, nest-building is a reproductive behavior, and changes in allele frequencies reflect evolution at the population level.", "correctAnswer": "After a meal, the pancreas releases insulin to lower rising blood glucose levels."}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cells as the basic unit of structure", "Genetic material", "Ability to evolve over generations", "Being made of atoms"], "question": "Which feature do viruses lack that is required in most definitions of living organisms?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Most definitions of life require cellular organization—being composed of one or more cells. Viruses contain genetic material and can evolve, but they are not cellular and do not carry out metabolism independently, which is why many biologists do not classify them as living.", "correctAnswer": "Cells as the basic unit of structure"}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "An individual organism evolves during its lifetime to adapt to its environment.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Evolution is a change in heritable traits of populations over generations, not changes within an individual's lifetime. Individuals can adapt via physiological or behavioral responses, but these are not evolutionary changes.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which organisms maintain a stable internal environment despite external changes is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Homeostasis involves regulatory mechanisms (like temperature regulation and blood sugar control) that keep internal conditions within optimal ranges for survival and function.", "correctAnswer": "homeostasis"}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Identify two characteristics of living things illustrated by photosynthesis in plants and briefly explain.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is a metabolic process that captures light energy and converts it into chemical energy (ATP and sugars), demonstrating energy use/metabolism. The produced sugars serve as building blocks and energy for growth and development, supporting the organism’s increase in size and complexity.", "correctAnswer": "Energy use/metabolism and growth (synthesis of organic molecules for building and storage)."}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A bacterial cell divides into two identical daughter cells after reaching a certain size.", "An athlete’s muscle fibers increase in diameter after training.", "A caterpillar increases in mass as it eats leaves.", "Tree rings increase in number and thickness as the tree matures."], "question": "Which situation best represents reproduction rather than growth?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Binary fission in bacteria produces new individuals and is reproduction. The other options describe growth—cells enlarging, organisms increasing in mass, and accumulation of wood layers in a tree.", "correctAnswer": "A bacterial cell divides into two identical daughter cells after reaching a certain size."}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "All living things must move from place to place to be considered alive.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Movement is not a required characteristic of life. Many living organisms (such as plants) do not relocate, though their cells may show internal movements or growth responses. Core characteristics include cellular organization, metabolism, homeostasis, reproduction, and response to stimuli, not locomotion.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In multicellular organisms, cells develop specialized structures and functions through a process called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Differentiation is the process by which unspecialized cells become specialized (e.g., muscle, nerve, or epithelial cells), allowing tissues and organs to perform distinct roles within the organism.", "correctAnswer": "differentiation"}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why is a dry, dormant seed considered a living organism even though it appears inactive?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dormant seeds still possess cellular organization and intact DNA. Their metabolism is greatly reduced but not absent, and they can respond to favorable environmental cues (water, temperature) to germinate, grow, and eventually reproduce—meeting key criteria for life.", "correctAnswer": "It has cells and genetic material, maintains minimal metabolism, and can resume growth and reproduce when conditions are favorable."}, {"id": "q_1762781569219_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A moth flying toward a porch light at night", "Kidneys regulating blood pH levels", "A muscle fiber increasing in size after training", "A mushroom releasing spores into the air"], "question": "Which example best illustrates a living organism responding to a stimulus?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Phototaxis (movement toward light) is a response to an external stimulus. Kidney regulation of blood pH is homeostasis, muscle fiber enlargement is growth, and spore release is a reproductive process.", "correctAnswer": "A moth flying toward a porch light at night"}]	none	2025-11-10 13:32:49.315953	\N
99d4275b-d8ef-4ffb-bdd9-55e5cbb33a1c	5cc002fe-fac1-4d15-9a1e-474da492c43a	characteristics of living things - Hard	characteristics of living things	hard	[{"id": "q_1762781669603_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Metabolism", "Genetic material", "Evolution", "Response to stimuli"], "question": "A biologist argues that viruses should not be classified as living because, when isolated from host cells, they cannot carry out any energy-transforming reactions. Which single characteristic of life is most clearly lacking in viruses independently of a host?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "The hallmark they lack on their own is metabolism: they do not generate ATP, synthesize macromolecules, or perform energy-transduction without hijacking a host's machinery. While viruses possess genetic material and can evolve in populations, their inability to sustain autonomous chemical processes distinguishes them from cellular life.", "correctAnswer": "Metabolism"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mitochondria contain circular DNA and bacterial-like ribosomes", "Mitochondria can photosynthesize in animal cells", "Mitochondria have linear chromosomes like eukaryotic nuclei", "Mitochondria lack membranes and rely on cytosolic enzymes"], "question": "Which evidence most strongly supports the endosymbiotic origin of mitochondria as once free-living bacteria?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Mitochondria possess circular DNA, 70S ribosomes, and replicate in a manner reminiscent of bacteria—key lines of evidence for an endosymbiotic origin. They do not photosynthesize (that is chloroplasts), their chromosomes are not linear like nuclear DNA, and they have double membranes consistent with engulfment.", "correctAnswer": "Mitochondria contain circular DNA and bacterial-like ribosomes"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It remains living because it maintains organization and can resume metabolism", "It is nonliving because metabolism has stopped", "It becomes living only upon rehydration", "It is dead because reproduction ceased"], "question": "A tardigrade enters cryptobiosis, showing no detectable metabolism, growth, or reproduction for years, yet revives upon rehydration. Based on core characteristics of life, which conclusion is most justified?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Life is assessed by more than instantaneous metabolic activity. Cryptobiosis preserves cellular organization and the potential to resume metabolism and homeostasis upon favorable conditions; thus the organism remains living in a suspended state rather than becoming nonliving or dead.", "correctAnswer": "It remains living because it maintains organization and can resume metabolism"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A mouse has a higher mass-specific metabolic rate than an elephant", "A mouse and an elephant have equal mass-specific metabolic rates", "An elephant has a higher mass-specific metabolic rate than a mouse", "Both have identical basal metabolic rates as individuals regardless of size"], "question": "Comparing a mouse to an elephant, which statement best reflects metabolic scaling (Kleiber's law) regarding mass-specific metabolic rate?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Kleiber's law describes that metabolic rate scales to body mass with an exponent near 3/4, so smaller organisms have higher metabolic rates per unit mass. Mice burn more energy per gram than elephants due to surface area-to-volume constraints and transport network scaling.", "correctAnswer": "A mouse has a higher mass-specific metabolic rate than an elephant"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Coordinated matrix production and quorum sensing stabilize internal microenvironments", "Individual bacteria remain independent with no shared regulation", "Biofilms are inert structures; only secreted materials persist", "Homeostasis requires complex organs absent in microbes"], "question": "In a mature bacterial biofilm, cells produce extracellular matrix and coordinate behavior via quorum sensing to maintain gradients of nutrients and waste. Which argument best supports describing the biofilm as exhibiting organization and homeostasis at the collective level?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Biofilms display emergent organization: cells communicate to regulate gene expression, produce matrix, and shape local conditions—maintaining relatively stable internal environments despite external fluctuations. This fits the homeostasis criterion at a collective scale.", "correctAnswer": "Coordinated matrix production and quorum sensing stabilize internal microenvironments"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Response to stimuli and regulation of internal state", "Reproduction at the species level", "Evolution through natural selection", "Growth via cell enlargement only"], "question": "A population of E. coli switches from repression to activation of the lac operon when lactose appears, modulating transport and metabolism in minutes. Which characteristic of life is most directly illustrated?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "The rapid gene expression change in response to lactose exemplifies responsiveness and regulatory homeostasis: cells sense environmental signals and adjust internal processes to maintain efficient, balanced function.", "correctAnswer": "Response to stimuli and regulation of internal state"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "All individual living organisms must be capable of reproduction to be considered alive.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Reproduction is a characteristic of life at the level of populations/species, not a strict requirement for each individual. Sterile mules and post-reproductive individuals are still living despite not reproducing.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Homeostasis necessarily means maintaining a constant internal temperature.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Homeostasis refers to regulation of multiple internal variables (pH, ion concentrations, glucose, osmotic balance). Many organisms are poikilothermic yet maintain homeostasis of other parameters; even endotherms adjust temperature set points (e.g., fever).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Virions outside host cells are metabolically inactive.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Outside a host, virions do not carry out metabolic reactions or synthesize macromolecules; they are inert particles until they enter cells and hijack cellular machinery.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Evolution as a characteristic of life applies to populations rather than individual organisms.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Evolution is the change in heritable traits in populations over generations via mechanisms like natural selection and drift. Individuals do not evolve; they develop or acclimate.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The primary energy currency used by cells to drive endergonic processes is _____.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) stores energy in its phosphate bonds and couples exergonic reactions to endergonic cellular work, central to metabolism in all known life.", "correctAnswer": "ATP"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Bacterial communication via diffusible autoinducers that coordinates group behaviors is called _____.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Quorum sensing allows bacteria to sense population density and regulate gene expression collectively, enabling behaviors like biofilm formation and virulence—key examples of responsiveness and regulation.", "correctAnswer": "quorum sensing"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Short-term, reversible physiological adjustment to environmental change within an organism’s lifetime is called _____.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Acclimation (a form of phenotypic plasticity) alters physiology or gene expression without genetic change, distinct from adaptation, which is evolutionary and occurs across generations.", "correctAnswer": "acclimation"}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Fire grows, spreads, and releases energy. Explain concisely why fire is not considered a living system.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Living systems are organized into cells, store and use genetic information to reproduce with heritable variation, and regulate internal conditions. Fire is a chemical reaction that propagates but has no cells, genome, or feedback control maintaining a stable internal state.", "correctAnswer": "It lacks cellular organization, genetic information with heredity, and regulated homeostasis; it does not reproduce with inherited variation."}, {"id": "q_1762781669603_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A synthetic protocell divides its membrane when supplied with externally produced enzymes but cannot encode or synthesize those catalysts itself. Which criterion of life is most missing, and why?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Life requires internal, genetically directed metabolic capability. Dependence on external enzymes for core processes indicates a lack of self-sustaining metabolism and regulation, even if division occurs.", "correctAnswer": "Autonomous metabolism is missing because it cannot internally generate or regulate the biochemical machinery needed for growth and division."}]	none	2025-11-10 13:34:32.033279	\N
21bac7f8-b8db-4fd4-978f-39a4fb89ab20	5cc002fe-fac1-4d15-9a1e-474da492c43a	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1762782199379_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["50 adults", "70 adults", "80 adults", "40 adults"], "question": "A movie theater sold 120 tickets for a show. Adult tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $8. If the total revenue was $1160, how many adult tickets were sold?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let a be adult tickets and c be child tickets. Then a + c = 120 and 12a + 8c = 1160. Multiply the first equation by 8: 8a + 8c = 960. Subtract from the revenue equation: (12a + 8c) − (8a + 8c) = 1160 − 960, giving 4a = 200, so a = 50. Therefore, 50 adult tickets were sold.", "correctAnswer": "50 adults"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "For any linear function f(x) = ax + b, the average rate of change between any two distinct x-values is equal to a.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The average rate of change from x = p to x = q is (f(q) − f(p)) / (q − p). For f(x) = ax + b, f(q) − f(p) = a(q − p), so dividing by (q − p) yields a. This shows the rate of change is constant and equal to the slope a.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find k such that the line y = kx − 7 is perpendicular to the line y = 3x + 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals. The slope of y = 3x + 1 is 3, so the perpendicular slope is −1/3. Therefore, k = −1/3.", "correctAnswer": "-1/3"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the interquartile range (IQR) of the data set: 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 22.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The data are already ordered. With 9 values, the median is the 5th value, 13. The lower half is 6, 8, 9, 11 with Q1 as the median of these, (8 + 9)/2 = 8.5. The upper half is 15, 18, 20, 22 with Q3 = (18 + 20)/2 = 19. The IQR is Q3 − Q1 = 19 − 8.5 = 10.5.", "correctAnswer": "10.5"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["90", "60", "100", "80"], "question": "Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF with a scale factor of 1.5 from ABC to DEF. If the area of triangle ABC is 40 square units, what is the area of triangle DEF?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Areas scale by the square of the linear scale factor. The area of DEF is 40 × (1.5)^2 = 40 × 2.25 = 90 square units.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/11", "1/6", "3/11", "1/9"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is 9 given that at least one die shows a 4?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Condition on at least one 4: possible outcomes are 11 pairs [(4,1) to (4,6), and (1,4) to (6,4) except double-counting (4,4) only once]. Among these, sums of 9 are (4,5) and (5,4), so 2 favorable outcomes out of 11 total, giving 2/11.", "correctAnswer": "2/11"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The quadratic equation x^2 − 4x + 5 = 0 has two real solutions.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The discriminant is b^2 − 4ac = (−4)^2 − 4(1)(5) = 16 − 20 = −4, which is negative. A negative discriminant means there are no real solutions (two complex solutions).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for x: 2^x = 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Taking log base 2 of both sides gives x = log_2(5). Using natural logs, x = ln(5)/ln(2) ≈ 2.3219.", "correctAnswer": "log_2(5)"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to 4x − 2y + 7 = 0 that passes through the point (2, −3).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite 4x − 2y + 7 = 0 as y = 2x + 3.5, so its slope is 2. A perpendicular line has slope −1/2. Using point-slope form with point (2, −3): y + 3 = (−1/2)(x − 2), so y = −1/2 x − 2.", "correctAnswer": "y = -1/2 x - 2"}, {"id": "q_1762782199379_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["670", "620", "700", "760"], "question": "An arithmetic sequence has first term a1 = 5 and common difference d = 3. What is the sum of the first 20 terms?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the sum formula S_n = n/2 [2a1 + (n − 1)d]. Here S_20 = 20/2 [2(5) + 19(3)] = 10 [10 + 57] = 10 × 67 = 670.", "correctAnswer": "670"}]	none	2025-11-10 13:43:19.481269	\N
980174a6-5d6e-4e35-a08b-08f68396e101	f8ee6fb5-463f-48e4-ac3b-377c8685b5c1	Biology  - Medium	Biology 	medium	[{"id": "q_1762784549132_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Feedback inhibition via allosteric binding", "Competitive inhibition at the active site", "Noncompetitive inhibition by substrate", "Irreversible inhibition of the enzyme"], "question": "An enzyme in a metabolic pathway shows a sigmoidal relationship between substrate concentration and reaction rate. A downstream product of the pathway binds to a site other than the enzyme’s active site and decreases its activity. Which regulatory mechanism best explains this observation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sigmoidal kinetics suggest cooperativity, a hallmark of allosteric enzymes. When a downstream (end) product binds to a regulatory site distinct from the active site to reduce activity, this is classic allosteric feedback inhibition, which helps maintain homeostasis of pathway end products.", "correctAnswer": "Feedback inhibition via allosteric binding"}, {"id": "q_1762784549132_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In prokaryotes, transcription and translation can occur simultaneously on the same mRNA because there is no nuclear envelope separating these processes.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Prokaryotes lack a nucleus, so ribosomes can bind mRNA while it is still being synthesized by RNA polymerase. This coupling enables rapid gene expression in response to environmental changes.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762784549132_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In C4 plants, CO2 is initially fixed in mesophyll cells by the enzyme _____ before being delivered to the Calvin cycle in bundle sheath cells.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 into a four-carbon compound (usually oxaloacetate) in mesophyll cells. This compound is transported to bundle sheath cells where CO2 is released for the Calvin cycle, minimizing photorespiration by concentrating CO2 around RuBisCO.", "correctAnswer": "PEP carboxylase"}, {"id": "q_1762784549132_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain how a mutation in a gene’s promoter region can alter protein levels without changing the amino acid sequence of the protein.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The promoter controls initiation of transcription. Mutations in promoter motifs (e.g., TATA box, enhancer-binding sites) affect transcription initiation frequency, modifying mRNA abundance and protein output without altering the coding region, so protein structure remains unchanged.", "correctAnswer": "Promoter mutations can change how strongly transcription factors and RNA polymerase bind, altering the rate of transcription. Increased binding can raise mRNA (and thus protein) levels, while decreased binding lowers them. Because the coding sequence is unchanged, the amino acid sequence stays the same, but expression levels differ."}, {"id": "q_1762784549132_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Directional selection acting on pre-existing genetic variation", "Lamarckian inheritance of acquired resistance", "Genetic drift in a large, stable population", "Artificial selection by humans intentionally breeding resistant insects"], "question": "A population of insects is exposed to a new insecticide. Within a few generations, most individuals are resistant. Which mechanism best explains the rapid increase in resistance?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rare alleles conferring resistance likely existed before exposure. The insecticide imposes strong selective pressure, allowing resistant individuals to survive and reproduce more, shifting allele frequencies in a directional manner across generations.", "correctAnswer": "Directional selection acting on pre-existing genetic variation"}]	none	2025-11-10 14:22:31.553246	\N
46316f6c-564d-4a79-9ec4-d45d4bf85035	2c58ace8-b4a5-4ea6-90eb-402bb25ef2cc	Photosynthesis  - Hard	Photosynthesis 	hard	[{"id": "q_1762789813994_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Enhanced cyclic electron flow around PSI increases ATP production while O2 evolution and NADPH formation both decline.", "Noncyclic electron flow increases, boosting both NADPH and O2 evolution to match ATP demand.", "State transitions increase PSII excitation sufficiently to keep O2 evolution unchanged without altering ATP/NADPH balance.", "ATP production decreases because PSI cannot pump protons, but NADPH remains unchanged due to water splitting at PSI."], "question": "A leaf is illuminated predominantly with far‑red light (>700 nm) that excites PSI much more than PSII. Which outcome best describes the immediate response of the photosynthetic apparatus to maintain energy balance?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Far‑red light preferentially excites PSI, limiting PSII activity and thereby suppressing linear (noncyclic) electron flow. As a result, O2 evolution (from water splitting at PSII) and NADPH production drop. To compensate for the ATP shortfall and maintain the ATP/NADPH ratio, plants upregulate cyclic electron flow around PSI, which drives proton translocation via cytochrome b6f to make ATP without producing NADPH or O2. PSI does not split water, and state transitions cannot fully overcome far‑red bias.", "correctAnswer": "Enhanced cyclic electron flow around PSI increases ATP production while O2 evolution and NADPH formation both decline."}, {"id": "q_1762789813994_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Reduced oxygenation and photorespiration, increasing net CO2 assimilation.", "No change, because Rubisco is saturated with CO2 at low Ci.", "Lower photosynthesis because the higher specificity increases ATP cost per carboxylation.", "Decreased growth due to nitrogen reallocation offsetting any biochemical benefit."], "question": "You engineer a C3 plant to express Rubisco with a higher specificity factor (Sc/o) for CO2 over O2, while its catalytic turnover (kcat) remains unchanged. Under high temperature and low intercellular CO2, what is the most likely effect on net photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "At high temperatures, CO2 becomes less soluble relative to O2, and Rubisco’s oxygenation reaction increases, intensifying photorespiration. A higher Sc/o reduces oxygenation events without sacrificing carboxylation turnover (kcat is unchanged), decreasing photorespiratory losses and improving net assimilation, especially when Ci is low. Rubisco is not saturated under low Ci, and specificity does not inherently raise ATP costs per carboxylation.", "correctAnswer": "Reduced oxygenation and photorespiration, increasing net CO2 assimilation."}, {"id": "q_1762789813994_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "In CAM plants, nocturnal CO2 fixation by phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase uses PEP generated largely from starch breakdown, producing malate stored in vacuoles; during the day, malate is decarboxylated to raise CO2 around Rubisco while stomata remain mostly closed.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "CAM plants open stomata at night, convert starch to PEP, and fix CO2 via PEPC into oxaloacetate, typically reduced to malate and stored in vacuoles. In the day, stomata close to limit water loss; malate is decarboxylated in the cytosol/chloroplast to elevate CO2 at Rubisco, sustaining photosynthesis while minimizing transpiration.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762789813994_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "During photorespiration in C3 plants, the mitochondrial multienzyme complex that converts glycine to serine while releasing CO2 and NH3 is the ______.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "The glycine decarboxylase complex (GDC), working with serine hydroxymethyltransferase, catalyzes the conversion of glycine to serine in mitochondria, releasing CO2 and NH3. This step is central to the photorespiratory pathway that recovers carbon from Rubisco’s oxygenation product (2‑phosphoglycolate).", "correctAnswer": "glycine decarboxylase complex"}, {"id": "q_1762789813994_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why does increasing atmospheric O2 decrease the quantum yield of CO2 fixation in C3 plants but has little effect in C4 plants? Provide the mechanistic basis.", "difficulty": "hard", "explanation": "Quantum yield reflects CO2 fixed per absorbed photon. In C3 plants, more O2 shifts Rubisco toward oxygenation, producing 2‑phosphoglycolate that must be recycled through photorespiration, consuming ATP and reducing equivalents and releasing CO2—thus the quantum yield drops. C4 plants use PEPC in mesophyll to form C4 acids, decarboxylate them in bundle sheath cells, elevating local CO2 and effectively saturating Rubisco with CO2. This minimizes oxygenation, making the quantum yield largely insensitive to ambient O2.", "correctAnswer": "Higher O2 increases Rubisco’s oxygenation reaction in C3 plants, diverting energy and reducing power into photorespiration and lowering CO2 fixed per photon. C4 plants concentrate CO2 in bundle sheath cells via a CO2‑concentrating mechanism, suppressing Rubisco oxygenation, so elevated O2 minimally affects their quantum yield."}]	none	2025-11-10 15:50:14.084434	\N
a11d41f5-1d00-417e-8429-434bd62e53f6	4b3c6d3c-fd6a-4843-9b11-b462ee416148	Ict - Medium	Ict	medium	[{"id": "q_1762795785956_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["/23 (255.255.254.0)", "/24 (255.255.255.0)", "/25 (255.255.255.128)", "/26 (255.255.255.192)"], "question": "You are designing an IPv4 subnet for a department with about 300 usable host addresses on a single network. Which subnet mask is the most appropriate to minimize address waste while meeting the requirement?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A /23 provides 2^(32−23) = 512 total addresses, with about 510 usable (excluding network and broadcast), which covers 300 hosts. A /24 allows only ~254 hosts, /25 ~126, and /26 ~62. Therefore, /23 is the smallest mask that meets the requirement with minimal waste.", "correctAnswer": "/23 (255.255.254.0)"}, {"id": "q_1762795785956_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Using WPA2 with AES encryption is significantly more secure than using WEP for Wi-Fi networks.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "WEP uses RC4 and weak initialization vectors, making it easily crackable. WPA2 with AES (CCMP) implements strong encryption and integrity protection. While configuration and password strength still matter, WPA2-AES is the industry-standard baseline for secure Wi-Fi compared to WEP.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762795785956_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The technique that allows a router to map multiple private IPv4 addresses to a single public IP when accessing the internet is called ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "NAT conserves public IPv4 addresses by translating private addresses to a public one at the edge. In most home/office setups, Port Address Translation (PAT) is used, multiplexing sessions via different source ports so many internal hosts share one public IP.", "correctAnswer": "Network Address Translation (NAT)"}, {"id": "q_1762795785956_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Briefly explain the difference between incremental and differential backups, and state when you might choose each in a daily backup strategy.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incrementals minimize daily backup size and time, but restores require the full backup plus every subsequent incremental (longer chain). Differentials grow larger each day after the full, but restoration is simpler and faster because you only need the full and the latest differential. Choose based on restore-time objectives vs. backup window/storage constraints.", "correctAnswer": "Incremental backups copy changes since the last backup of any type; differential backups copy changes since the last full backup. Use incrementals for smaller, faster daily backups; use differentials to speed restore (full + latest differential) at the cost of larger daily backups."}, {"id": "q_1762795785956_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1NF", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "question": "A table has a composite primary key (OrderID, ProductID) and includes the attribute ProductName, which depends only on ProductID. Which normal form specifically resolves this partial dependency?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Second Normal Form (2NF) eliminates partial dependencies of non-key attributes on a part of a composite key by splitting the table so each non-key attribute depends on the whole key. 3NF addresses transitive dependencies, and BCNF is stricter but the specific issue here is partial dependency, resolved by 2NF.", "correctAnswer": "2NF"}]	none	2025-11-10 17:29:46.093965	\N
c644358d-87f1-431d-a327-e8f872f98dff	4b3c6d3c-fd6a-4843-9b11-b462ee416148	Ict - Medium	Ict	medium	[{"id": "q_1762796287824_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["FTP", "SFTP", "FTPS (Explicit)", "TFTP"], "question": "Your company allows remote staff to upload payroll spreadsheets to an internal server over the internet. The IT team requires end-to-end encryption and prefers a simple firewall setup that uses a single well-known TCP port. Which file transfer protocol best meets these requirements?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol) encrypts both commands and data over SSH and typically uses a single port (TCP 22), simplifying firewall rules. FTPS (Explicit) is also secure but often requires opening multiple dynamic ports for data channels, complicating firewall configuration. FTP and TFTP are unencrypted.", "correctAnswer": "SFTP"}, {"id": "q_1762796287824_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A backup strategy using one full backup followed by daily incremental backups provides faster restore times than a strategy using one full backup followed by daily differential backups.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incremental restores require the last full backup plus every incremental since then, which can be time-consuming and failure-prone. Differential restores typically need only the last full backup and the latest differential, making recovery faster despite larger differential files over time.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762796287824_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a spreadsheet, to count how many orders meet multiple conditions (for example, Region = \\"East\\" and Sales > 1000), you would use the ______ function.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "COUNTIFS evaluates multiple criteria across one or more ranges, returning the count of cells that satisfy all specified conditions. COUNTIF handles only a single criterion, while SUMIFS sums values rather than counting matches.", "correctAnswer": "COUNTIFS"}, {"id": "q_1762796287824_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "After adding an unmanaged switch to an office network, users begin reporting IP address conflicts. Which network service should you check for duplication on the LAN, and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Duplicate DHCP services on the same broadcast domain can hand out conflicting IP addresses to clients. Verifying that only the intended DHCP server is active (and disabling rogue DHCP on routers, access points, or other devices) resolves the conflict.", "correctAnswer": "The DHCP server, because multiple DHCP servers can issue overlapping leases, causing duplicate IP addresses."}, {"id": "q_1762796287824_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Anonymization", "Pseudonymization", "Encryption at rest", "Hashing with salt"], "question": "A hospital wants to share patient records with researchers to build a predictive model, but authorized staff still need the ability to re-identify individuals when necessary. Which data-handling approach is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Pseudonymization replaces direct identifiers with tokens or pseudonyms while keeping a secure mapping that allows re-identification by authorized personnel. Anonymization removes identifiers irreversibly, while hashing with salt is one-way and cannot be reversed reliably. Encryption at rest protects storage but does not, by itself, enable controlled sharing with re-identification for research.", "correctAnswer": "Pseudonymization"}]	none	2025-11-10 17:38:08.297307	\N
7d89428b-7027-4c5a-83fd-a1d386c3e734	b39e47ff-5745-470a-a06b-7138adc6e423	Science - Medium	Science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762798735815_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Speed decreases, wavelength decreases", "Speed decreases, wavelength increases", "Speed increases, wavelength decreases", "Speed increases, wavelength increases"], "question": "When a light ray passes from air into glass at an angle (neglecting total internal reflection), what happens to its speed and wavelength within the glass?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In a denser optical medium like glass, light slows down. The frequency of light remains constant across media, so with v = fλ, a decrease in speed means the wavelength must also decrease. Thus, both speed and wavelength decrease.", "correctAnswer": "Speed decreases, wavelength decreases"}, {"id": "q_1762798735815_1", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A solution that resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added is called a ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A buffer is typically composed of a weak acid and its conjugate base (or a weak base and its conjugate acid). It minimizes pH changes by neutralizing added H+ or OH− ions, a behavior described by the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation.", "correctAnswer": "buffer"}, {"id": "q_1762798735815_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Enzymes accelerate reactions by lowering the activation energy but do not change the overall free energy change (ΔG) of the reaction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzymes are catalysts: they provide an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy, increasing the reaction rate. They do not alter the initial and final energy states, so ΔG and the reaction’s equilibrium position remain unchanged.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762798735815_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What type of plate boundary is associated with a dipping zone of earthquakes known as a Wadati–Benioff zone?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Wadati–Benioff zones trace the path of a subducting oceanic plate descending beneath another plate at a convergent boundary. Earthquakes occur from shallow near the trench to deep within the mantle as the slab sinks.", "correctAnswer": "convergent (subduction) boundary"}, {"id": "q_1762798735815_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Summers would be hotter and winters colder in both hemispheres", "Seasons would become milder everywhere", "The Northern and Southern Hemispheres would experience the same seasons at the same time", "Day length at the equinoxes would increase globally"], "question": "If Earth’s axial tilt increased from about 23.5° to 30°, how would the intensity of seasons change?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A greater axial tilt increases the contrast in solar angle and daylight duration between summer and winter, amplifying seasonal temperature differences. Hemispheres remain out of phase (opposite seasons), and equinox day length stays about 12 hours worldwide.", "correctAnswer": "Summers would be hotter and winters colder in both hemispheres"}]	none	2025-11-10 18:18:58.216952	\N
034f390e-3cc0-4203-ab59-bb9a9853c869	f51cfb5f-3073-458d-a3b5-5e97b5f3de80	the moon and the earth - Easy	the moon and the earth	easy	[{"id": "q_1762840752487_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Earth", "Sun", "Mars", "Jupiter"], "question": "Which object does the Moon orbit?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Moon is Earth's natural satellite, meaning it orbits Earth roughly once every 27.3 days (its sidereal period).", "correctAnswer": "Earth"}, {"id": "q_1762840752487_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The same side of the Moon always faces Earth.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Moon is tidally locked with Earth, so its rotation period matches its orbital period and the same hemisphere always faces us.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762840752487_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The time it takes Earth to complete one rotation on its axis is approximately ____ hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A day is about 24 hours long because that is the time Earth takes to rotate once relative to the Sun (the solar day).", "correctAnswer": "24 hours"}, {"id": "q_1762840752487_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What primarily causes ocean tides on Earth?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tides are caused by the Moon’s gravity pulling on Earth’s oceans; the Sun also affects tides but less than the nearby Moon.", "correctAnswer": "The gravitational pull of the Moon (with the Sun also contributing)."}, {"id": "q_1762840752487_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Earth moves between the Sun and the Moon, casting a shadow on the Moon", "The Moon moves between the Sun and Earth, blocking sunlight", "Clouds cover the Moon so it cannot be seen", "The Moon stops moving in its orbit"], "question": "What happens during a lunar eclipse?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A lunar eclipse occurs when Earth is directly between the Sun and the Moon, causing Earth’s shadow to fall on the Moon, usually during a full Moon.", "correctAnswer": "Earth moves between the Sun and the Moon, casting a shadow on the Moon"}]	none	2025-11-11 05:59:12.616	\N
91ed0031-7806-4045-9a8e-2bc233fabfa4	5cc002fe-fac1-4d15-9a1e-474da492c43a	speed grade 8 - Medium	speed grade 8	medium	[{"id": "q_1762873160654_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["12 km/h", "15 km/h", "20 km/h", "25 km/h"], "question": "A cyclist travels 45 km in 3 hours, then 30 km in 2 hours without stopping. What is the cyclist’s average speed for the entire trip?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Total distance = 45 km + 30 km = 75 km. Total time = 3 h + 2 h = 5 h. So average speed = 75 ÷ 5 = 15 km/h. Average speed depends on total distance and total time, not the speeds of individual parts alone.", "correctAnswer": "15 km/h"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If two vehicles travel the same distance, the one with the greater average speed will always take less time.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Time = distance ÷ speed. For the same distance, a higher average speed gives a smaller time, because time is inversely proportional to speed.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Convert 72 km/h to meters per second (m/s).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To convert km/h to m/s, multiply by 1000 and divide by 3600: 72 × 1000 ÷ 3600 = 72 ÷ 3.6 = 20 m/s.", "correctAnswer": "20 m/s"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A runner maintains a steady speed of 3 m/s. How long will it take to run 2 km?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Time = distance ÷ speed. Convert 2 km to meters: 2000 m. Then time = 2000 ÷ 3 ≈ 666.7 s. Converting to minutes: 666.7 ÷ 60 ≈ 11.1 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "666.7 s (≈11.1 min)"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30 km/h", "40 km/h", "45 km/h", "50 km/h"], "question": "A car travels 60 km at 60 km/h and then 60 km at 30 km/h. What is its average speed for the entire 120 km trip?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total distance = 120 km. Time for first part = 60 ÷ 60 = 1 h; second part = 60 ÷ 30 = 2 h. Total time = 3 h. Average speed = 120 ÷ 3 = 40 km/h. Note that the average speed is not the simple average of 60 and 30.", "correctAnswer": "40 km/h"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "On a distance–time graph, a steeper line means the object is moving more slowly.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The slope of a distance–time graph is speed. A steeper slope means a larger change in distance per unit time, which indicates a higher speed, not a lower one.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A car travels at 18 m/s for 10 seconds, then at 12 m/s for 20 seconds. Assuming straight-line motion, what total distance does it cover?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Distance for first interval = 18 × 10 = 180 m. Distance for second interval = 12 × 20 = 240 m. Total distance = 180 + 240 = 420 m.", "correctAnswer": "420 m"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Two runners start together on a straight track. Runner A runs at 8 km/h and Runner B at 10 km/h. How far apart are they after 30 minutes?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The separation grows at the relative speed: 10 − 8 = 2 km/h. In 30 minutes (0.5 h), separation = 2 × 0.5 = 1 km.", "correctAnswer": "1 km"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["m/s", "km/h", "m/min", "km"], "question": "Which of the following is NOT a valid unit of speed?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Speed is distance per time (e.g., m/s, km/h, m/min). Kilometers (km) measure distance only, not distance per unit time.", "correctAnswer": "km"}, {"id": "q_1762873160654_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A train covers 150 km in 2.5 hours. What is its speed in m/s?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First find km/h: 150 ÷ 2.5 = 60 km/h. Convert to m/s: 60 × 1000 ÷ 3600 = 16.7 m/s (rounded to one decimal place).", "correctAnswer": "16.7 m/s"}]	none	2025-11-11 14:59:20.756574	\N
32a7d6aa-6340-48fd-8296-79e9104ca7be	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762876163913_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Carbon dioxide", "Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants absorb carbon dioxide (CO2) from the air and use sunlight to convert it into glucose. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1762876163913_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth revolves around the Sun once every 24 hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Earth rotates on its axis once every ~24 hours, which causes day and night. It revolves around the Sun once every ~365 days, which defines a year.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762876163913_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Water changes from liquid to gas at its _____ point.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The boiling point is the temperature at which a liquid turns into a gas. For pure water at sea level, this is 100°C (212°F), though it varies with air pressure.", "correctAnswer": "boiling point"}, {"id": "q_1762876163913_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the force that pulls objects toward Earth's center.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. It keeps us on the ground and causes objects to fall toward Earth.", "correctAnswer": "Gravity"}, {"id": "q_1762876163913_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a fixed shape and a fixed volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In solids, particles are closely packed and only vibrate in place, giving solids a fixed shape and volume. Liquids have a fixed volume but change shape; gases have neither fixed shape nor volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}]	none	2025-11-11 15:49:24.017487	\N
1cf4b3a3-15d5-451f-9231-af27cb25eb40	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	Multiplication - Easy	Multiplication	easy	[{"id": "q_1762882896618_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "18", "12", "21"], "question": "What is 6 × 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication can be thought of as repeated addition. 6 × 3 means three groups of 6: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18. You can also think of it as six groups of 3: 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762882896618_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The order of factors does not change the product (for example, 2 × 5 = 5 × 2).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the commutative property of multiplication. Swapping the order of the numbers being multiplied doesn’t change the result: 2 × 5 = 10 and 5 × 2 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762882896618_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "7 × __ = 0", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Any number multiplied by 0 equals 0. This is the zero property of multiplication: zero groups of any amount is zero.", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1762882896618_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If each bag has 4 apples and there are 5 bags, how many apples are there in total?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 5 equal groups of 4 apples: 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20. That’s the same as 4 × 5 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "20"}, {"id": "q_1762882896618_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "4", "1", "0"], "question": "Which number is equal to 4 × 1?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying by 1 does not change a number; this is the identity property of multiplication. So 4 × 1 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}]	none	2025-11-11 17:41:36.724512	\N
5820453e-5e69-4818-a41f-43125e468258	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	Arithmetc - Easy	Arithmetc	easy	[{"id": "q_1762883919800_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12. You can think of it as counting 5 steps forward from 7.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "question": "What is 9 − 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "3 × 0 = 3", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying any number by 0 equals 0. So 3 × 0 = 0, not 3.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_3", "type": "true_false", "question": "8 is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2. Since 8 ÷ 2 = 4 with no remainder, 8 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_4", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "____ + 6 = 10", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing number, subtract 6 from 10. 10 − 6 = 4, so 4 + 6 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is 15 ÷ 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "15 divided by 3 equals 5 because 3 × 5 = 15.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "7", "34"], "question": "Which number is the tens digit in 347?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 347, 3 is the hundreds digit, 4 is the tens digit, and 7 is the ones digit.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "12", "14", "8"], "question": "What is 2 × 6?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 2 × 6 is 6 + 6, which equals 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Subtracting a larger number from a smaller number can result in a negative number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "If the number you subtract is larger (e.g., 3 − 5), the result is negative (3 − 5 = −2).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The sum of 1, 2, and 3 is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add the numbers: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_10", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is 20 − 17?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 17 from 20: 20 − 17 = 3.", "correctAnswer": "3"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_11", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30", "34", "35", "40"], "question": "Round 34 to the nearest ten.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "34 is closer to 30 than to 40. Numbers with ones digit 0–4 round down to the previous ten.", "correctAnswer": "30"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "In addition, changing the order of the numbers changes the sum.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Addition is commutative: a + b = b + a, so the order does not change the sum.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "5 + ____ = 5", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 0 to a number keeps it the same. This is the additive identity property.", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1762883919800_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/4", "2/2", "3/4", "2/4"], "question": "Which fraction is equal to one-half?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies to 1/2 by dividing numerator and denominator by 2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}]	none	2025-11-11 17:58:43.181478	\N
867fb1ff-1e7e-4b23-956d-be16d7b34bc9	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	Multiplication - Medium	Multiplication	medium	[{"id": "q_1762884103935_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["102.5", "100.25", "820", "10.25"], "question": "A rectangular garden measures 12.5 meters by 8.2 meters. What is its area?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width. Multiply 12.5 × 8.2. Using distributive reasoning: 12.5 × (8 + 0.2) = 12.5×8 + 12.5×0.2 = 100 + 2.5 = 102.5 square meters.", "correctAnswer": "102.5"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "(-7)(5 + -3) equals (-7)(5) + (-7)(-3).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This statement demonstrates the distributive property: a(b + c) = ab + ac. Compute both sides: Left side is (-7) × (2) = -14. Right side is (-35) + 21 = -14. Both sides match, confirming the property.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Use the associative property to find 25 × 16 × 4 = ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The associative property lets us group factors to make multiplication easier. Group 25 × 4 first: 25 × 4 = 100. Then 100 × 16 = 1600. Same result as any grouping because multiplication is associative.", "correctAnswer": "1600"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A printer outputs 48 pages per minute. How many pages will it print in 2.5 hours?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Convert hours to minutes: 2.5 hours = 150 minutes. Multiply rate × time: 48 × 150 = (48 × 100) + (48 × 50) = 4800 + 2400 = 7200 pages.", "correctAnswer": "7200"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["20", "25", "30", "22.5"], "question": "Solve for x: 3.6 × x = 90.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To find x, divide by 3.6: x = 90 ÷ 3.6. Notice 36 × 25 = 900, so 3.6 × 25 = 90. Therefore x = 25.", "correctAnswer": "25"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The product of two negative numbers is negative.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A negative times a negative is positive. For example, (-4) × (-3) = +12. This follows from consistent properties of multiplication and number opposites.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Compute using the distributive property: 17 × 29 = ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Break 29 as 30 − 1. Then 17 × 29 = 17 × (30 − 1) = 17×30 − 17×1 = 510 − 17 = 493.", "correctAnswer": "493"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A rectangular room is 6 m by 4 m, and an attached rectangular closet is 2 m by 1.5 m. What is the total floor area?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Find each area and add: Room area = 6 × 4 = 24 m². Closet area = 2 × 1.5 = 3 m². Total = 24 + 3 = 27 m².", "correctAnswer": "27"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "12", "14", "18"], "question": "Calculate: (one-third of 24) × 1.5", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "One-third of 24 is 24 × (1/3) = 8. Then multiply 8 × 1.5 = 12. Thinking in fractions: 8 × 3/2 = 24/2 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762884103935_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A recipe uses 2.75 cups of flour per batch. How many cups are needed for 6 batches?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Multiply 2.75 × 6. Compute: 2 × 6 = 12, 0.75 × 6 = 4.5. Sum: 12 + 4.5 = 16.5 cups.", "correctAnswer": "16.5"}]	none	2025-11-11 18:01:44.049149	\N
be4434ca-5ac0-4ac3-a55e-64d608b1d6bf	047a0390-cf51-4e8a-9333-57a234452238	Multiplication - Easy	Multiplication	easy	[{"id": "q_1762884231257_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "24"], "question": "What is 3 x 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 3 x 4 means three groups of 4, so 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1762884231257_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: Multiplying any number by 1 gives the same number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the identity property of multiplication. The number 1 is the multiplicative identity, so n x 1 = n for any number n.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762884231257_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: 5 x ___ = 20", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing factor, divide the product by the known factor: 20 ÷ 5 = 4. Therefore, 5 x 4 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1762884231257_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Each bag has 6 apples. How many apples are in 3 bags?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 3 groups of 6 apples: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18, which is 3 x 6 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1762884231257_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "0", "1", "90"], "question": "What is 0 x 9?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Any number multiplied by 0 equals 0. With zero groups, the total is zero.", "correctAnswer": "0"}]	none	2025-11-11 18:03:53.67743	\N
