, "correctAnswer": "Water"}, {"id": "q_1762932073185_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound can travel through a vacuum (empty space).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound needs a medium like air, water, or solids to travel; it cannot move through a vacuum where there are no particles.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932073185_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest planet in our solar system is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Jupiter is the largest planet by diameter and mass in our solar system, known for its Great Red Spot and many moons.", "correctAnswer": "Jupiter"}, {"id": "q_1762932073185_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the three common states of matter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Matter commonly exists as solids (fixed shape), liquids (fixed volume, variable shape), and gases (variable shape and volume).", "correctAnswer": "solid, liquid, gas"}, {"id": "q_1762932073185_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cell membrane", "Nucleus", "Cytoplasm", "Ribosome"], "question": "Which part of a cell contains the genetic material (DNA)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In eukaryotic cells, the nucleus houses DNA, which contains instructions for the cell's structure and function.", "correctAnswer": "Nucleus"}]	none	2025-11-12 07:21:13.198121	\N
2d8d0c0e-baaa-444e-8126-317096ebc6a4	f7947bdc-a4cf-47e6-a66e-bd07b51189ee	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1762932177843_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0°C", "50°C", "100°C", "212°F"], "question": "At sea level, what is the boiling point of water in degrees Celsius?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Water boils at 100°C under standard atmospheric pressure (sea level). 0°C is the freezing point of water, and 212°F is the boiling point in Fahrenheit.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth revolves around the Sun once every 24 hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Earth rotates on its axis approximately every 24 hours, causing day and night. It revolves around the Sun once per year (about 365 days).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which plants make their own food using sunlight is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is how plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose and oxygen, with chlorophyll in leaves capturing light energy.", "correctAnswer": "photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the organ that pumps blood throughout the human body.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues and removing waste products.", "correctAnswer": "heart"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Helium"], "question": "Which gas do humans need to breathe to survive?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Humans require oxygen for cellular respiration, the process by which cells produce energy. Air is about 21% oxygen and 78% nitrogen.", "correctAnswer": "Oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Magnetism", "Friction", "Gravity", "Electricity"], "question": "What force keeps us on the ground and causes objects to fall?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. It keeps us anchored to Earth and causes objects to fall toward the ground.", "correctAnswer": "Gravity"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster in air than in water.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound travels faster in water than in air because particles in water are closer together, transmitting vibrations more efficiently.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The smallest unit of life is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cells are the basic structural and functional units of living organisms. All living things are made of one or more cells.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the state of matter that has a fixed volume but no fixed shape?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Liquids have a definite volume but take the shape of their container, unlike solids (fixed shape and volume) and gases (neither fixed shape nor volume).", "correctAnswer": "liquid"}, {"id": "q_1762932177843_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Roots", "Stem", "Leaves", "Flowers"], "question": "Which part of a plant anchors it in the soil and absorbs water?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and minerals from the soil. Stems support the plant, and leaves carry out photosynthesis.", "correctAnswer": "Roots"}]	none	2025-11-12 07:22:57.927107	\N
76df5362-2e2b-4d4c-a5c3-9ff42d93b20e	3d658f72-72de-4207-a499-f775b41ed19f	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762932213661_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2 Hz", "4 Hz", "5 Hz", "25 Hz"], "question": "A water wave travels at 20 m/s and has a wavelength of 5 m. What is the wave’s frequency?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Wave frequency f is calculated by f = v/λ. With speed v = 20 m/s and wavelength λ = 5 m, f = 20/5 = 4 Hz. Higher wavelengths at the same speed yield lower frequencies.", "correctAnswer": "4 Hz"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Increasing substrate concentration can overcome noncompetitive inhibition of an enzyme.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Noncompetitive inhibitors bind to an allosteric site and reduce the enzyme’s overall activity (lower Vmax) regardless of substrate concentration. Only competitive inhibition can be overcome by adding more substrate because it competes for the active site.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a galvanic (voltaic) cell, the electrode where oxidation occurs is the ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode. In a galvanic cell, the anode is typically the negative electrode, and electrons flow from the anode through the external circuit to the cathode.", "correctAnswer": "anode"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why do coastal regions tend to have milder temperature swings than inland areas?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water has a high specific heat capacity, so it warms and cools slowly. Coastal air temperatures are buffered by the ocean, which absorbs heat during warm periods and releases it during cool periods, reducing extremes compared to inland locations.", "correctAnswer": "Because water’s high specific heat capacity allows oceans to absorb and release large amounts of heat, moderating nearby air temperatures."}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mid-ocean ridge and basaltic lava flows", "Deep ocean trench and continental volcanic arc", "Transform fault with strike-slip earthquakes", "Rift valley and new ocean basin"], "question": "At a convergent boundary where an oceanic plate subducts beneath a continental plate, which geological features are most likely to form?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Subduction of an oceanic plate beneath a continental plate creates a deep trench at the boundary and a volcanic arc on the continent due to melting and magma generation from fluids released by the subducting slab.", "correctAnswer": "Deep ocean trench and continental volcanic arc"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Rubisco catalyzes carbon fixation and binds only CO2, not O2.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rubisco can bind both CO2 and O2. When it binds O2, photorespiration occurs, which consumes energy and releases CO2, reducing photosynthetic efficiency, especially under high temperatures and low CO2 conditions.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The gas law stating that pressure is inversely proportional to volume at constant temperature is ____ law.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Boyle’s law states that for a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, P ∝ 1/V (PV = constant). Compressing the gas increases pressure; expanding it decreases pressure.", "correctAnswer": "Boyle's law"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 3 hours. Starting with 80 g, how much remains after 9 hours?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Nine hours is three half-lives. The mass successively halves: 80 g → 40 g (3 h) → 20 g (6 h) → 10 g (9 h).", "correctAnswer": "10 g"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8 Ω", "4 Ω", "2 Ω", "1 Ω"], "question": "Two 4 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For parallel resistors, 1/R_eq = 1/R1 + 1/R2 = 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2, so R_eq = 2 Ω. Parallel connections reduce total resistance compared to each individual resistor.", "correctAnswer": "2 Ω"}, {"id": "q_1762932213661_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In an energy pyramid, roughly ____% of energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "About 10% of energy typically passes to the next trophic level; the rest is lost as heat, movement, and metabolic processes. This low transfer efficiency limits the number of trophic levels.", "correctAnswer": "10%"}]	none	2025-11-12 07:23:33.675055	\N
efbf1b54-60b6-495f-9406-62c5f5b56d55	3d658f72-72de-4207-a499-f775b41ed19f	SCIENCE - Easy	SCIENCE	easy	[{"id": "q_1762932308039_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cell", "Atom", "Molecule", "Organ"], "question": "What is the basic unit of life in all living organisms?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all life processes. Atoms and molecules are chemical units, and organs are made of tissues and multiple cells.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1762932308039_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth completes one rotation on its axis approximately every 24 hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Earth’s rotation takes about 24 hours, which creates the cycle of day and night. Its revolution around the Sun takes about 365 days.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762932308039_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "At sea level, pure water boils at ____ degrees Celsius.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Under standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm) at sea level, water boils at 100°C. Boiling point can change with altitude because pressure changes.", "correctAnswer": "100"}, {"id": "q_1762932308039_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the gas that plants release into the air during photosynthesis.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants use carbon dioxide and water to produce glucose and release oxygen as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1762932308039_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Electron", "Proton", "Neutron", "Nucleus"], "question": "Which part of an atom carries a positive electric charge?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Protons have a positive charge, electrons have a negative charge, and neutrons are neutral. The nucleus contains protons and neutrons, but the positively charged particle itself is the proton.", "correctAnswer": "Proton"}]	none	2025-11-12 07:25:08.145129	\N
3840d99a-50d8-4110-b40d-77cad9cad2d0	b0bd11ca-6cfd-451a-bfc2-d8eb207921f4	science - Medium	science	medium	[{"id": "q_1762932341088_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Halve the curve's radius", "Double the curve's radius", "Increase the banking angle slightly inward", "Switch to a higher-friction surface"], "question": "A car of mass m travels at a constant speed v around a flat circular track. Without changing m or v, which modification to the track would double the required centripetal force acting on the car?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The required centripetal force is F = mv^2/r. If speed and mass are fixed, reducing the radius r increases the force. Halving r doubles F. Banking and friction affect how the force is supplied (by normal force components or friction), but they do not change the required net centripetal force for a given speed and radius. Doubling the radius would halve the required force.", "correctAnswer": "Halve the curve's radius"}, {"id": "q_1762932341088_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In an isolated system, the total entropy cannot decrease over time.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The second law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of an isolated system (no exchange of matter or energy) never decreases; it either stays the same (for reversible processes) or increases (for irreversible processes). While microscopic fluctuations can occur, the macroscopic trend is non-decreasing entropy.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762932341088_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the Calvin cycle of photosynthesis, the enzyme that catalyzes the fixation of CO2 to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "RuBisCO (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) catalyzes the first step of carbon fixation, attaching CO2 to RuBP to form 3-phosphoglycerate. It is one of the most abundant enzymes on Earth and a key control point in photosynthetic carbon assimilation.", "correctAnswer": "RuBisCO"}, {"id": "q_1762932341088_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A patient has type AB blood. Which blood types can they safely receive for red blood cell transfusions, and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Type AB individuals do not have anti-A or anti-B antibodies in their plasma, so they can receive red blood cells from any ABO type. Rh compatibility must still be respected: AB+ can receive Rh+ or Rh−, while AB− should receive Rh−.", "correctAnswer": "A, B, AB, and O (with compatible Rh factor)"}, {"id": "q_1762932341088_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3.0", "4.0", "4.95", "5.5"], "question": "A weak acid HA has a pKa of 4.0. At approximately what pH will a buffer solution be 90% in the deprotonated form (A−) and 10% in the protonated form (HA)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation: pH = pKa + log([A−]/[HA]). With 90% A− and 10% HA, the ratio is 9:1. Thus pH = 4.0 + log(9) ≈ 4.0 + 0.95 = 4.95.", "correctAnswer": "4.95"}]	none	2025-11-12 07:25:41.195258	\N
415527bc-647f-46de-991e-6706b3538c97	12e98eb9-9787-44fc-b0e1-a72e4605b1fc	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1762932517814_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Its orbit gradually decays, lowering altitude until reentry", "Its orbit circularizes and altitude increases", "Its orbit becomes more elliptical with a higher apogee", "No significant change because gravity dominates"], "question": "A small satellite in low Earth orbit experiences slight atmospheric drag. Over months, what is the most likely change to its orbit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Atmospheric drag removes orbital energy from the satellite. As energy decreases, the orbit shrinks, lowering both apogee and perigee. The satellite speeds up slightly at lower altitudes but continues to lose energy, eventually reentering the atmosphere.", "correctAnswer": "Its orbit gradually decays, lowering altitude until reentry"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["pH drops slightly as acetate converts to acetic acid", "pH rises markedly due to added Cl−", "pH remains exactly unchanged because it's a buffer", "The buffer is immediately destroyed and pH equals that of HCl"], "question": "An acetic acid/acetate buffer near pH 4.76 has a small amount of strong acid (HCl) added. What primarily happens?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Buffers resist pH changes by converting between conjugate base and acid. Added H+ reacts with acetate (the conjugate base) to form acetic acid, causing only a small pH decrease until buffering capacity is exceeded. Chloride is a spectator ion and does not affect pH.", "correctAnswer": "pH drops slightly as acetate converts to acetic acid"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Km increases; Vmax unchanged", "Km decreases; Vmax unchanged", "Km unchanged; Vmax decreases", "Km increases; Vmax decreases"], "question": "In the presence of a competitive inhibitor, how do the apparent Km and Vmax change for an enzyme-catalyzed reaction?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Competitive inhibitors bind the active site and compete with substrate, so a higher substrate concentration is needed to reach half-max velocity (Km appears higher). At sufficiently high substrate, the inhibitor's effect is overcome, so Vmax remains the same.", "correctAnswer": "Km increases; Vmax unchanged"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_3", "type": "true_false", "question": "In photosynthesis, the oxygen gas released to the atmosphere primarily originates from carbon dioxide.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The O2 produced in photosynthesis comes from splitting water during the light reactions. Carbon dioxide is reduced to carbohydrates; it is not the source of the released oxygen gas.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_4", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The atmospheric layer where most ozone resides and ultraviolet radiation is absorbed is the _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The ozone layer is concentrated in the stratosphere, where ozone molecules absorb UV-B and UV-C radiation, protecting life and heating the stratosphere.", "correctAnswer": "stratosphere"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why does adding salt to icy roads help melt ice even when the air temperature is below 0°C?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Freezing point depression is a colligative property: dissolved solute lowers water’s chemical potential, so the ice–liquid equilibrium shifts and a salt–water solution remains liquid at temperatures below pure water’s 0°C freezing point.", "correctAnswer": "It lowers the freezing point of water by dissolving ions that disrupt ice formation, making liquid brine stable below 0°C (freezing point depression)."}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It is thermodynamically favorable but proceeds slowly", "It is thermodynamically unfavorable and does not occur", "It releases heat rapidly", "A catalyst would make ΔG more negative to start the reaction"], "question": "A reaction has a negative ΔG at room temperature but a large activation energy. Which statement best describes its behavior without a catalyst?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Negative ΔG means the reaction is spontaneous in principle, but a high activation energy creates a kinetic barrier. Without a catalyst to lower the activation energy, the reaction rate is slow. Catalysts change the rate, not ΔG.", "correctAnswer": "It is thermodynamically favorable but proceeds slowly"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "On a warm exoplanet with low surface gravity, lighter gases like hydrogen are more likely to escape into space than heavier gases.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Atmospheric escape depends on thermal speeds and escape velocity. Lighter molecules move faster at a given temperature and can exceed the lower escape velocity more readily on planets with weak gravity, making hydrogen and helium more prone to loss.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In genetics, crossing an organism of unknown genotype with a homozygous recessive individual is called a _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A test cross reveals the unknown genotype by examining offspring phenotypes. If any recessive phenotypes appear, the unknown parent must carry the recessive allele (be heterozygous).", "correctAnswer": "test cross"}, {"id": "q_1762932517814_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In a galvanic (voltaic) cell, which electrode undergoes oxidation, which undergoes reduction, and in which direction do electrons flow through the external circuit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In galvanic cells, the anode is the source of electrons where oxidation occurs, and the cathode is where reduction consumes electrons. Electrons move through the external wire from the anode to the cathode, while ions move through the salt bridge to balance charge.", "correctAnswer": "Oxidation occurs at the anode; reduction at the cathode; electrons flow from anode to cathode."}]	none	2025-11-12 07:28:37.91774	\N
8f12cef8-5234-445c-9300-da5582fd1f7e	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Setswana - Medium	Setswana	medium	[{"id": "q_1762958147514_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["ba ya", "o ya", "re ya", "go ya"], "question": "Complete the sentence with the correct subject concord and verb: Bana ___ sekolong.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Bana (children) is a class 2 noun (plural of ngwana), and the subject concord for class 2 is ba-. In the present tense with the motion verb ya (to go), the correct agreement is Bana ba ya sekolong.", "correctAnswer": "ba ya"}, {"id": "q_1762958147514_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The prefix mo- in Setswana always marks a person noun (class 1) regardless of meaning.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "While mo- is used for class 1 (often people, e.g., motho), it is also used for class 3 nouns that are not people (e.g., motlhware ‘pangolin’, moretlwa ‘tree’). Therefore, mo- does not always indicate a person; it signals membership in class 1 or class 3 depending on the noun.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1762958147514_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct relative concord: Monna ___ o tsamayang ke morutabana. (The man who is walking is a teacher.)", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Monna is a class 1 noun, and the class 1 relative concord is yo. Thus, Monna yo o tsamayang… expresses ‘The man who is walking…’. Other classes take different relative concords (e.g., ba for class 2, e for class 9).", "correctAnswer": "yo"}, {"id": "q_1762958147514_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Transform this sentence into the negative present: Ke a go rata.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To form the negative present, add the negative marker ga before the subject concord, drop the emphatic a, and change the final -a of the verb to -e: Ke a go rata → Ga ke go rate.", "correctAnswer": "Ga ke go rate."}, {"id": "q_1762958147514_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["mo", "e", "ba", "lo"], "question": "Choose the correct object concord to complete the sentence: Monna o rata mosadi; o a ___ bona letsatsi le letsatsi. (He sees her every day.)", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Mosadi is a class 1 noun, and the class 1 object concord is mo-. Therefore, the correct form is o a mo bona ‘he sees her’. The other options are object concords for other classes (e.g., e for class 9, ba for class 2).", "correctAnswer": "mo"}]	none	2025-11-12 14:35:49.922367	\N
44ea3023-7734-41e2-83a9-d597b8ffffe8	6ce9a895-7e62-4393-b1b8-947b4bc70bb6	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1763028168540_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["11", "12", "13", "10"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "12", "15", "21"], "question": "Which of the following is an even number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2. 12 ÷ 2 = 6 with no remainder, so it is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "9", "14"], "question": "What is the value of 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 3 × 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "2/3", "1/3", "3/4"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dividing both the numerator and denominator of 2/4 by 2 gives 1/2, so 2/4 is equivalent to 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["ones", "tens", "hundreds", "thousands"], "question": "What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 352?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 352, the digits are 3 hundreds, 5 tens, and 2 ones, so the 5 is in the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "15", "16", "10"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 5 and width 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). So 2 × (5 + 3) = 2 × 8 = 16.", "correctAnswer": "16"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/5", "1/2", "5/10", "2/5"], "question": "Convert 0.5 to a fraction in simplest form.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 equals 5/10, which simplifies to 1/2 by dividing numerator and denominator by 5.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "3"], "question": "What is 9 − 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "The number 0 is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are integers divisible by 2. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles in a triangle is 90 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle’s interior angles always add up to 180 degrees, not 90.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "7 is greater than 10.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "On the number line, 7 is to the left of 10, so it is smaller.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square is always a rectangle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has four right angles. A square has four right angles and equal sides, so every square is a rectangle.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "3/4 is greater than 1/2.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Comparing fractions with the same denominator shows 3/4 = 0.75 and 1/2 = 0.5, so 3/4 is greater.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "Multiplying a number by 1 changes its value.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The identity property of multiplication states that any number times 1 equals the original number.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "6 + __ = 10", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To reach 10 from 6, add 4 because 6 + 4 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "5 squared equals __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Squared means multiplied by itself: 5 × 5 = 25.", "correctAnswer": "25"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The mean (average) of 2, 4, and 6 is __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add the numbers (2 + 4 + 6 = 12) and divide by 3. 12 ÷ 3 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the smallest prime number?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A prime number has exactly two positive factors: 1 and itself. The smallest such number is 2.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Simplify the fraction 6/8 to simplest form.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Divide numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor, 2: 6/8 ÷ 2/2 = 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "3/4"}, {"id": "q_1763028168540_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the next number in the sequence: 2, 4, 6, 8, __?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The sequence increases by 2 each time, so after 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}]	none	2025-11-13 10:02:48.627021	\N
6afe82b0-9788-40da-9425-b7602f989aff	83e6a8dd-d485-4b64-971a-2315284da720	science - Easy	science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763028535688_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["H2O", "CO2", "O2", "NaCl"], "question": "What is the chemical formula for water?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Water is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, giving it the chemical formula H2O.", "correctAnswer": "H2O"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mercury", "Venus", "Earth", "Mars"], "question": "Which planet is closest to the Sun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mercury is the innermost planet in our solar system, orbiting closest to the Sun.", "correctAnswer": "Mercury"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Nitrogen", "Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas makes up most of Earth’s atmosphere?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nitrogen makes up about 78% of Earth’s atmosphere, while oxygen is about 21%.", "correctAnswer": "Nitrogen"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ampere", "Volt", "Ohm", "Watt"], "question": "What is the SI unit of electric current?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The ampere (A) is the International System of Units (SI) base unit for electric current.", "correctAnswer": "Ampere"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Heart", "Brain", "Lungs", "Kidney"], "question": "Which organ pumps blood throughout the human body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues.", "correctAnswer": "Heart"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Melting", "Freezing", "Condensation", "Sublimation"], "question": "What is the change of a solid to a liquid called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Melting is the process where a solid turns into a liquid when heated to its melting point.", "correctAnswer": "Melting"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Sun is a star.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Sun is a typical star that produces energy through nuclear fusion, emitting light and heat.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Plants make their own food using sunlight in a process called photosynthesis.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis uses sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose and oxygen in plants.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster than light.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Light travels much faster than sound. In air, sound travels about 343 m/s, while light travels about 300,000 km/s.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Adult humans have 206 bones in their skeleton.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Most adult humans have 206 bones; babies have more that fuse together as they grow.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Table salt is a pure element.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Table salt is a compound called sodium chloride (NaCl), made of sodium and chlorine elements.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Pure water boils at 100°C at sea level.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (sea level), pure water boils at 100°C. Boiling point changes with altitude and impurities.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which liquid water turns into vapor is called ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation is when molecules at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to become gas.", "correctAnswer": "Evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The force that pulls objects toward Earth is ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses that causes objects to fall toward Earth.", "correctAnswer": "Gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest planet in our solar system is ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Jupiter is the largest planet, a gas giant with a diameter about 11 times that of Earth.", "correctAnswer": "Jupiter"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic unit of life is the ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all life processes, forming the building blocks of organisms.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the plant organ where photosynthesis mainly occurs.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Leaves contain chloroplasts with chlorophyll, where most photosynthesis takes place in plants.", "correctAnswer": "Leaves"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the main source of energy driving Earth’s climate system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solar radiation from the Sun provides the energy that warms Earth, powers weather, and drives the climate.", "correctAnswer": "The Sun"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature using devices such as liquid expansion, electronic sensors, or infrared.", "correctAnswer": "Thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1763028535688_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which part of the atom carries a positive charge?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Protons are positively charged particles found in the nucleus of an atom; electrons are negatively charged and neutrons are neutral.", "correctAnswer": "Proton"}]	none	2025-11-13 10:08:58.112779	\N
eee58db8-eba9-41db-942d-1a72b37cebdf	a750dd2e-44fa-42f8-bc01-6dcd57cfce42	maths addition - Medium	maths addition	medium	[{"id": "q_1763041925614_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["411", "421", "431", "441"], "question": "A café sold 128 muffins on Monday, 156 on Tuesday, and 137 on Wednesday. How many muffins did the café sell in total over these three days?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Add the daily totals: 128 + 156 = 284, and 284 + 137 = 421. Grouping addends strategically (e.g., 128 + 137 = 265, then 265 + 156 = 421) can also make mental addition easier.", "correctAnswer": "421"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The expression (27 + 45) + 8 equals 27 + (45 + 8).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is the associative property of addition: when adding, you can change the grouping of numbers without changing the sum. (27 + 45) + 8 = 72 + 8 = 80, and 27 + (45 + 8) = 27 + 53 = 80.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Calculate 12.75 + 8.6 + 0.95. Write your answer as a decimal.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Align decimal places: 12.75 + 8.60 = 21.35, then 21.35 + 0.95 = 22.30, which is 22.3 when written without a trailing zero.", "correctAnswer": "22.3"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A runner logged 3.25 km on Monday, 4.8 km on Tuesday, and 2.95 km on Wednesday. What is the total distance run over these three days? Give your answer in kilometers.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Add the distances carefully aligning decimals: 3.25 + 4.80 = 8.05, then 8.05 + 2.95 = 11.00. The total is 11 km.", "correctAnswer": "11"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2", "-2", "20", "-20"], "question": "Compute −15 + 26 + (−9).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine the negatives first: (−15) + (−9) = −24. Then add 26: −24 + 26 = 2. Grouping helps manage signs in integer addition.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_5", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Compute 3/4 + 5/8. Give your answer as a fraction in simplest form.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Convert 3/4 to eighths: 3/4 = 6/8. Then 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8. This is an improper fraction; as a mixed number it is 1 3/8.", "correctAnswer": "11/8"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8:22 PM", "8:07 PM", "7:42 PM", "8:35 PM"], "question": "A movie starts at 6:15 PM. The previews last 20 minutes, the ads last 12 minutes, and the film itself is 1 hour 35 minutes. What time does the event end?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First add previews and ads: 20 + 12 = 32 minutes. Total duration is 1 hour 35 minutes + 32 minutes = 2 hours 7 minutes. Add to 6:15 PM: 6:15 + 2:07 = 8:22 PM.", "correctAnswer": "8:22 PM"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "When adding three numbers, changing the order of the addends changes the sum.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By the commutative property of addition, the order of addends does not affect the sum. For example, 4 + 7 + 9 equals 7 + 9 + 4.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the sum of the first five multiples of 7 (that is, 7, 14, 21, 28, 35).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor out 7: 7(1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5) = 7 × 15 = 105. Recognizing structure can simplify multi-term addition.", "correctAnswer": "105"}, {"id": "q_1763041925614_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the equation 3,486 + ____ = 7,209, what number goes in the blank?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the inverse of addition: the missing addend is 7,209 − 3,486 = 3,723. Checking: 3,486 + 3,723 = 7,209.", "correctAnswer": "3,723"}]	none	2025-11-13 13:52:05.717673	\N
ca16f648-58ef-431e-a447-06d490fedea9	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763046743760_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plants use carbon dioxide, water, and sunlight to make glucose during photosynthesis. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1763046743760_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The boiling point of pure water at sea level is 100°C.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm, typical at sea level), pure water boils at 100°C. This temperature changes with altitude because air pressure changes.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763046743760_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The smallest unit of life that can function independently is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cells are the basic structural and functional units of living organisms. All living things are made of one or more cells.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1763046743760_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What force keeps planets in orbit around the Sun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. The Sun’s gravity pulls on the planets, keeping them in stable orbits.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763046743760_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Inner core", "Outer core", "Mantle", "Crust"], "question": "Which layer of the Earth is composed mostly of liquid iron and nickel?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Earth’s outer core is a liquid layer made primarily of iron and nickel. The inner core is solid, while the mantle and crust are rocky layers.", "correctAnswer": "Outer core"}]	none	2025-11-13 15:12:23.844304	\N
a2f2fea9-07c7-4ff9-8821-f4d0cbef5e7f	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	maths - Easy	maths	easy	[{"id": "q_1763046930777_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["24", "17", "26", "14"], "question": "What is the value of 7 + 5 × 2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS), multiplication is done before addition. Compute 5 × 2 = 10, then add 7: 7 + 10 = 17.", "correctAnswer": "17"}, {"id": "q_1763046930777_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: 3/4 is greater than 2/3.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Convert to decimals: 3/4 = 0.75 and 2/3 ≈ 0.666..., so 0.75 > 0.666.... Alternatively, cross-multiply: 3 × 3 = 9 and 2 × 4 = 8; since 9 > 8, 3/4 > 2/3.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763046930777_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). If a rectangle has length 6 cm and width 4 cm, its perimeter is ____ cm.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add the length and width: 6 + 4 = 10. Then multiply by 2: 2 × 10 = 20 cm.", "correctAnswer": "20"}, {"id": "q_1763046930777_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Convert the decimal 0.25 to a fraction in simplest form.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.25 = 25/100. Divide numerator and denominator by 25 to simplify: 25 ÷ 25 = 1 and 100 ÷ 25 = 4, giving 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "1/4"}, {"id": "q_1763046930777_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["13", "7", "10", "-13"], "question": "Solve for x: x − 3 = 10.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add 3 to both sides to isolate x: x = 10 + 3 = 13.", "correctAnswer": "13"}]	none	2025-11-13 15:15:30.863891	\N
c36052c1-c2a6-41eb-874d-1cd28102ec0f	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	Speed - Easy	Speed	easy	[{"id": "q_1763047649836_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["meters per second (m/s)", "Newton (N)", "kilometer (km)", "meter (m)"], "question": "What is the SI unit of speed?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Speed measures how much distance is covered in a given time. In the International System of Units (SI), distance is measured in meters and time in seconds, so the SI unit of speed is meters per second (m/s).", "correctAnswer": "meters per second (m/s)"}, {"id": "q_1763047649836_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Speed is a scalar quantity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A scalar quantity has magnitude only, while a vector has both magnitude and direction. Speed describes how fast something moves and does not include direction, so it is scalar. Velocity, in contrast, is a vector because it includes direction.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763047649836_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic formula for speed is ______ divided by time.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Speed is defined as the distance traveled per unit time. Mathematically, speed = distance ÷ time.", "correctAnswer": "distance"}, {"id": "q_1763047649836_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A car travels 100 km in 2 hours. What is its average speed in km/h?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 100 km ÷ 2 h = 50 km/h.", "correctAnswer": "50 km/h"}, {"id": "q_1763047649836_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0.2 m/s", "5 m/s", "80 m/s", "320 m/s"], "question": "A runner covers 400 m in 80 s. What is the runner's speed?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Speed = distance ÷ time = 400 m ÷ 80 s = 5 m/s. The other options either invert the calculation or are unrealistic for this scenario.", "correctAnswer": "5 m/s"}]	none	2025-11-13 15:27:31.957463	\N
2bb0f45f-dd44-4857-ae59-11e737019064	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	ict - Medium	ict	medium	[{"id": "q_1763047874397_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["TCP", "UDP", "FTP", "HTTP/2"], "question": "A company is streaming real-time sensor data from a factory to a dashboard. Occasional packet loss is acceptable, but low latency is critical. Which transport protocol is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "UDP is connectionless and has minimal overhead, making it suitable for real-time applications where low latency is more important than perfect delivery. TCP ensures reliable delivery through acknowledgments and retransmissions, which add delay. FTP and HTTP/2 are application-layer protocols that typically run over TCP, inheriting its latency characteristics.", "correctAnswer": "UDP"}, {"id": "q_1763047874397_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Symmetric encryption alone guarantees both confidentiality and integrity of data.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Symmetric encryption provides confidentiality by making data unreadable to unauthorized parties. Integrity (detecting tampering) requires additional mechanisms such as a message authentication code (MAC) or using authenticated encryption modes like AES-GCM. Without integrity protection, altered ciphertext could go undetected.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763047874397_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a public key infrastructure, the trusted entity that issues and signs digital certificates to bind a public key to an identity is called a ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A Certificate Authority (CA) validates the identity of entities (users, services, domains) and signs their certificates, creating a chain of trust. Clients verify certificates by checking the CA’s signature against trusted root or intermediate CAs.", "correctAnswer": "Certificate Authority"}, {"id": "q_1763047874397_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Differentiate incremental and differential backups, and give one scenario where each is preferable.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incrementals are small and fast to run but can require many backup sets to restore, increasing restore time. Differentials grow larger over time but simplify recovery to two steps (full + latest differential). Choosing between them balances backup window, storage usage, and recovery time objectives (RTO).", "correctAnswer": "Incremental backups copy only data changed since the last backup of any type (full or incremental); differential backups copy data changed since the last full backup. Use incremental when minimizing backup time and storage is critical (e.g., nightly backups of large datasets); use differential when faster restores are needed because you only restore the last full plus the latest differential."}, {"id": "q_1763047874397_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["IaaS", "PaaS", "SaaS", "FaaS (Serverless Functions)"], "question": "A development team wants to deploy web applications quickly without provisioning or managing virtual machines, operating systems, or middleware. They still need control over the application code and runtime configuration. Which cloud service model best fits?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides managed runtimes, middleware, and services so teams can focus on application code while retaining control over configurations and dependencies. IaaS requires managing VMs and OS. SaaS delivers finished applications with little to no control over code. FaaS is event-driven and abstracts the runtime further, which may not suit full web app deployments needing persistent services.", "correctAnswer": "PaaS"}]	none	2025-11-13 15:31:14.506471	\N
25032065-70fd-48ae-9b82-fa98b791c76c	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	ict - Easy	ict	easy	[{"id": "q_1763047936993_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Processing Unit", "Central Peripheral Unit", "Core Processing Utility"], "question": "What does CPU stand for in a computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is often called the 'brain' of the computer because it performs calculations and executes instructions for programs. The other options are common misinterpretations or incorrect expansions.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1763047936993_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A keyboard is an input device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "True. A keyboard sends data (your keystrokes) into the computer, making it an input device. In contrast, devices like monitors and printers are output devices because they display or produce results.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763047936993_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The global network of interconnected computers that use IP to communicate is called the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is the worldwide system of interconnected networks that use the Internet Protocol (IP) to communicate. It is the infrastructure that supports services like the World Wide Web, email, and streaming.", "correctAnswer": "Internet"}, {"id": "q_1763047936993_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".jpg", ".mp3", ".docx", ".xls"], "question": "Which of the following file extensions is commonly used for an image?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (JPEG) is a common image format. .mp3 is an audio format, .docx is a word processing document (Microsoft Word), and .xls is a spreadsheet file (Microsoft Excel).", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1763047936993_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym ICT stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ICT stands for Information and Communication Technology. It encompasses technologies used to handle information and facilitate communication, including computers, networks, the Internet, and telecommunications.", "correctAnswer": "Information and Communication Technology"}]	none	2025-11-13 15:32:17.100685	\N
347931d8-09fc-41b2-88c5-0c97a04aa597	ab846143-07cd-433a-8f3b-559056fc4608	ict - Easy	ict	easy	[{"id": "q_1763049852498_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which device is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer; a keyboard lets you enter text and commands. Monitors, printers, and speakers are output devices.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Processing Utility", "Control Program Unit", "Central Peripheral Unit"], "question": "What does CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is the main processor that executes instructions and performs calculations in a computer.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Operating system", "Mouse", "Keyboard", "Monitor"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Software refers to programs and instructions. An operating system is software that manages hardware and applications. Mouse, keyboard, and monitor are hardware.", "correctAnswer": "Operating system"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Uniform Resource Locator", "Universal Routing Link", "User Reference List", "Unified Registry Login"], "question": "What does URL stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "URL stands for Uniform Resource Locator. It is the address used to access resources on the web.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".exe", ".jpg", ".mp3", ".txt"], "question": "Which file extension commonly represents an image?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (or .jpeg) is a common image file format. .exe is an executable, .mp3 is audio, and .txt is plain text.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A hardware device that connects a computer to a printer", "A program used to view web pages", "A type of email account", "An operating system"], "question": "What is a web browser?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A web browser is software, such as Chrome or Firefox, that retrieves and displays web pages from the internet.", "correctAnswer": "A program used to view web pages"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Byte", "Kilobyte (KB)", "Megabyte (MB)", "Gigabyte (GB)"], "question": "Which unit of digital storage is the largest?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Storage units increase in size from byte to kilobyte to megabyte to gigabyte, so a gigabyte is larger than the others listed.", "correctAnswer": "Gigabyte (GB)"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["USB flash drive", "Google Drive", "CD-ROM", "External hard disk"], "question": "Which is an example of a cloud storage service?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Google Drive is a cloud storage service accessed over the internet. USB drives, CDs, and external hard disks are local physical storage devices.", "correctAnswer": "Google Drive"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is volatile memory (data is lost when power is off).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Random Access Memory (RAM) stores data temporarily and requires power to retain its contents. When power is lost, RAM is cleared.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "An IP address uniquely identifies a device on a network.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An IP address is a numerical label assigned to each device connected to a network, enabling proper routing of data to that device.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Antivirus software protects against hardware damage only.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Antivirus software protects against malware like viruses and worms, which are software threats. It does not specifically prevent physical hardware damage.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Wi-Fi refers to a wired network connection.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wi-Fi is a wireless networking technology that allows devices to connect to a network without cables.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The _____ is the physical parts of a computer.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hardware includes the tangible components like the keyboard, mouse, monitor, and motherboard, as opposed to software which consists of programs.", "correctAnswer": "hardware"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a spreadsheet, each box where a row and column meet is called a _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A cell is the intersection of a row and a column in a spreadsheet, where data or formulas are entered.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The protocol used for secure websites is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) encrypts data between the browser and the website, helping protect privacy and security.", "correctAnswer": "HTTPS"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Copying important files to another location to prevent data loss is called a _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A backup is a copy of data stored separately so it can be restored if the original is lost or corrupted.", "correctAnswer": "backup"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one output device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A monitor displays information from the computer. Other output devices include printers and speakers.", "correctAnswer": "monitor"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does \\"OS\\" stand for in computing?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "OS stands for Operating System, the software that manages computer hardware and provides services for applications.", "correctAnswer": "Operating System"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the main purpose of an email client?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An email client is an application like Outlook or the Gmail app that enables users to compose, receive, organize, and reply to emails.", "correctAnswer": "To send, receive, and manage emails"}, {"id": "q_1763049852498_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does LAN stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "LAN stands for Local Area Network, a network that connects devices within a limited area such as a home, school, or office.", "correctAnswer": "Local Area Network"}]	none	2025-11-13 16:04:14.962473	\N
4225c7b4-4412-432a-bf84-d248a07a9af0	eb0479dc-9509-4cc1-9cd8-b456c7bb7f28	English - Medium	English	medium	[{"id": "q_1763055126781_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Walking down the street, the skyscrapers towered over the pedestrians.", "Walking down the street, I saw the skyscrapers towering above me.", "As the skyscrapers walked down the street, they towered above me.", "Walking down the street, towering skyscrapers were above."], "question": "Which revision best corrects the dangling modifier in the sentence: \\"Walking down the street, the skyscrapers towered above me.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A participial phrase must logically modify the subject that follows it. In the correct option, the subject \\"I\\" immediately follows \\"Walking down the street,\\" making the relationship clear. The other options leave the modifier dangling or assign it to an illogical subject (e.g., skyscrapers cannot walk).", "correctAnswer": "Walking down the street, I saw the skyscrapers towering above me."}, {"id": "q_1763055126781_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "For hypothetical, contrary-to-fact conditions, \\"If I were\\" is generally preferred over \\"If I was\\" in formal English.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Formal English uses the subjunctive mood for unreal or contrary-to-fact situations, taking \\"were\\" for all persons (e.g., \\"If I were taller...\\"). \\"If I was\\" can be used in informal contexts or for indicative, real past situations (e.g., \\"If I was late yesterday...\\").", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763055126781_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence with the correct comparative: \\"The editor found ________ mistakes in the second draft than in the first.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use \\"fewer\\" with countable nouns like mistakes; use \\"less\\" with uncountable nouns (e.g., \\"less time,\\" \\"less water\\").", "correctAnswer": "fewer"}, {"id": "q_1763055126781_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence in active voice while preserving meaning: \\"It is believed by many that social media influences public opinion.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Converting passive to active clarifies the agent and improves concision. The subject becomes \\"Many,\\" and the passive construction \\"It is believed by\\" is replaced with the active verb \\"believe.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Many believe that social media influences public opinion."}, {"id": "q_1763055126781_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The report was late, however the client did not mind.", "The report was late; however, the client did not mind.", "The report was late; however the client did not mind.", "The report was late however; the client did not mind."], "question": "Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon and a conjunctive adverb to join two independent clauses?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When connecting independent clauses with a conjunctive adverb (e.g., however, therefore), place a semicolon before the adverb and a comma after it. The other options either create a comma splice or misplace punctuation.", "correctAnswer": "The report was late; however, the client did not mind."}]	none	2025-11-13 17:32:06.880207	\N
b6fedb92-bd6b-4c3f-8da8-5aa34d4660c5	356fc30a-093d-4fca-a702-11389f625ac7	neutralstion - Medium	neutralstion	medium	[{"id": "q_1763056866905_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["6.25 mL", "12.5 mL", "25.0 mL", "50.0 mL"], "question": "How many milliliters of 0.200 M NaOH are required to completely neutralize 25.0 mL of 0.100 M HCl?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Neutralization of a strong acid by a strong base follows a 1:1 mole ratio for H+ and OH-. Moles of HCl = 0.0250 L × 0.100 M = 0.00250 mol. You need 0.00250 mol NaOH. Volume of 0.200 M NaOH = n/M = 0.00250 mol / 0.200 M = 0.0125 L = 12.5 mL.", "correctAnswer": "12.5 mL"}, {"id": "q_1763056866905_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a titration of a weak acid (like acetic acid) with a strong base (like NaOH), the pH at the equivalence point is exactly 7.0.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At equivalence, the solution contains the conjugate base of the weak acid (e.g., acetate), which hydrolyzes to produce OH−, making the solution basic (pH > 7). A pH of ~7 at equivalence occurs only for strong acid–strong base titrations.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763056866905_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: The net ionic equation for the neutralization of any strong acid with a strong base is ____ + ____ → ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Strong acids and bases dissociate completely. Spectator ions (e.g., Na+ and Cl−) cancel, leaving H+ from the acid and OH− from the base combining to form water: H+ + OH− → H2O.", "correctAnswer": "H+ + OH- → H2O"}, {"id": "q_1763056866905_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "An antacid tablet contains 1.50 g of CaCO3 and neutralizes stomach acid according to CaCO3 + 2 HCl → CaCl2 + H2O + CO2. What volume of 0.100 M HCl can it neutralize?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Molar mass CaCO3 ≈ 100.09 g/mol, so moles CaCO3 = 1.50 g / 100.09 g/mol ≈ 0.0150 mol. The reaction uses 2 mol HCl per 1 mol CaCO3, so moles HCl = 0.0300 mol. Volume at 0.100 M = n/M = 0.0300 / 0.100 = 0.300 L = 300 mL.", "correctAnswer": "300 mL"}, {"id": "q_1763056866905_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Phenolphthalein", "Methyl orange", "Bromothymol blue", "Methyl red"], "question": "Which indicator is most appropriate for titrating acetic acid (weak acid) with NaOH (strong base)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For a weak acid–strong base titration, the equivalence point is basic (typically pH ≈ 8.5–9.5). Phenolphthalein changes color around pH 8.3–10.0, making it suitable. Methyl orange (pH 3.1–4.4) and Methyl red (pH 4.4–6.2) change too low, while Bromothymol blue (pH 6.0–7.6) is centered near neutral.", "correctAnswer": "Phenolphthalein"}]	none	2025-11-13 18:01:09.370293	\N
396a46a0-b8d6-4441-bffa-5ff40608ee47	7c5357b1-2f16-4805-9ad0-33791be658f6	SCIENCE POLLINATION - Easy	SCIENCE POLLINATION	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099021165_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The transfer of pollen from the anther to the stigma of a flower", "The process of making food in leaves", "The movement of seeds by animals", "The absorption of water by roots"], "question": "What is pollination?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pollination is the movement of pollen from the male part (anther) to the female part (stigma) of a flower. This step is needed before fertilization can occur and seeds can form.", "correctAnswer": "The transfer of pollen from the anther to the stigma of a flower"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Pollination can only be done by bees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "While bees are important pollinators, many other agents also pollinate, including butterflies, birds, bats, and even wind and water. Plants use a variety of strategies to move pollen.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The male flower part that produces pollen is the ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pollen is made in the anther, which is part of the stamen (the male reproductive structure). The anther releases pollen that must reach a stigma for pollination.", "correctAnswer": "anther"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Stigma", "Anther", "Ovary", "Sepal"], "question": "Which flower part receives pollen during pollination?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The stigma is the sticky top of the pistil (female part) that catches pollen. After landing on the stigma, pollen can grow a tube to reach the ovary for fertilization.", "correctAnswer": "Stigma"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common animal pollinator.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bees are among the most common and effective pollinators. Other examples include butterflies, moths, bats, and hummingbirds.", "correctAnswer": "bee"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Wind can act as a pollinating agent.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Some plants, such as grasses and many trees, rely on wind to carry lightweight pollen from one flower to another.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Brightly colored petals and sweet ____ help attract pollinators to flowers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nectar is a sugary liquid produced by flowers to entice pollinators. Color, scent, and nectar work together to guide animals to the flower.", "correctAnswer": "nectar"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Fertilization leading to seed and fruit formation", "Leaves grow larger", "Roots absorb more minerals", "Photosynthesis stops"], "question": "What usually happens after successful pollination?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "After pollen reaches the stigma and fertilizes the ovule, seeds develop, and often fruits form around them to protect and help disperse the seeds.", "correctAnswer": "Fertilization leading to seed and fruit formation"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is cross-pollination?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cross-pollination moves pollen between different plants of the same species, increasing genetic diversity and often producing stronger offspring.", "correctAnswer": "Transfer of pollen from one plant to the flower of another plant of the same species"}, {"id": "q_1763099021166_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Small, inconspicuous flowers with lots of light pollen", "Large, brightly colored flowers with strong fragrance", "Thick, sticky pollen designed to cling to animals", "Deep tubular flowers rich in nectar"], "question": "Which feature is typical of wind-pollinated plants?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wind-pollinated plants often have small, plain flowers and produce large amounts of lightweight pollen that can be easily carried by the breeze.", "correctAnswer": "Small, inconspicuous flowers with lots of light pollen"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:43:41.302801	\N
4b09eaf9-9a2e-4b2d-98f9-a5c26d03fca0	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	science - Easy	science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099365809_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["100°C", "0°C", "50°C", "212°C"], "question": "What is the boiling point of water at sea level in degrees Celsius?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm), pure water boils at 100°C. The value 212°F is the equivalent temperature in the Fahrenheit scale, which is a common point of confusion.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth revolves around the Sun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Earth orbits the Sun once approximately every 365 days. This heliocentric model has been confirmed by extensive astronomical observations.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic unit of life is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cells are the smallest units that can carry out all life processes. All living organisms are made of one or more cells.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What gas do plants primarily take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plants use carbon dioxide and water, along with sunlight, to produce glucose and oxygen during photosynthesis.", "correctAnswer": "carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mars", "Venus", "Jupiter", "Mercury"], "question": "Which planet is known as the Red Planet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mars is called the Red Planet because its surface is rich in iron oxide (rust), which gives it a reddish appearance.", "correctAnswer": "Mars"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound can travel through a vacuum.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound is a mechanical wave that needs a medium (like air, water, or solids) to travel. In a vacuum, there are no particles to carry the vibrations.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The force that pulls objects toward Earth is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is a fundamental force that attracts objects with mass toward each other. On Earth, it gives weight to physical objects.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the process by which liquid water turns into water vapor.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation is the change of state from liquid to gas at the surface of a liquid, commonly occurring when energy (heat) is added.", "correctAnswer": "evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Proton", "Neutron", "Electron", "Photon"], "question": "Which part of the atom has a positive charge?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Protons carry a positive electrical charge and are found in the atomic nucleus. Electrons are negative, and neutrons are neutral.", "correctAnswer": "Proton"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Humans have 206 bones in the adult skeleton.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An average adult human has 206 bones, though the number can vary slightly due to anatomical differences like extra ribs or vertebrae.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_10", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Water", "Hydrogen", "Oxygen", "Salt"], "question": "What is H2O commonly known as?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "H2O is the chemical formula for water, indicating two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.", "correctAnswer": "Water"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest organ of the human body by surface area is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Skin is the body's largest organ, providing a protective barrier and helping regulate temperature. The liver is the largest internal solid organ.", "correctAnswer": "skin"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_12", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature using various principles, such as expansion of liquids or electrical resistance changes.", "correctAnswer": "thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Moon produces its own light.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Moon does not emit light; it reflects sunlight. The phases of the Moon are due to changes in how much of its sunlit side we can see from Earth.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a fixed shape and a fixed volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids have a fixed shape and volume because their particles are tightly packed and vibrate in place.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_15", "type": "true_false", "question": "Photosynthesis mainly occurs in the chloroplasts of plant cells.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the organelles where photosynthesis takes place, converting light energy to chemical energy.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_16", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solar energy", "Coal", "Natural gas", "Oil"], "question": "Which energy source is renewable?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solar energy is renewable because it is continuously supplied by the Sun, unlike fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas.", "correctAnswer": "Solar energy"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_17", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The chemical symbol for sodium is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sodium's symbol is Na, derived from its Latin name 'Natrium.' Chemical symbols often come from Latin or historical names.", "correctAnswer": "Na"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call the path a planet takes as it moves around the Sun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An orbit is the curved path of an object around a point in space due to gravity, such as a planet around the Sun.", "correctAnswer": "orbit"}, {"id": "q_1763099365809_19", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Heart", "Brain", "Lungs", "Stomach"], "question": "Which organ pumps blood throughout the human body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood through the circulatory system, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues.", "correctAnswer": "Heart"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:49:25.914212	\N
760d1b49-5839-4994-8b4d-1efc8b085303	705dbd2a-65f0-4e6a-a840-e5287e6edb30	science pollination - Easy	science pollination	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099586143_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The transfer of pollen from the anther to the stigma of a flower", "The process by which plants make food from sunlight", "The germination of seeds into seedlings", "The absorption of water by plant roots"], "question": "What is pollination?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pollination is specifically the movement of pollen grains from the male part (anther) to the female part (stigma) of a flower. This step is essential for fertilization, which leads to seed formation.", "correctAnswer": "The transfer of pollen from the anther to the stigma of a flower"}, {"id": "q_1763099586143_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Wind can act as a pollinator for some plants.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Many plants, such as grasses and many trees, rely on wind to carry pollen from one flower to another. This type of pollination is called anemophily (wind pollination).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099586143_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The male part of a flower that produces pollen is the _____", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The anther is the pollen-producing structure at the tip of the stamen, which is the male reproductive organ of a flower.", "correctAnswer": "anther"}, {"id": "q_1763099586143_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common animal that helps pollinate flowers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bees are among the most important pollinators, transferring pollen as they collect nectar. Other pollinators include butterflies, moths, bats, birds (like hummingbirds), and some beetles.", "correctAnswer": "bee"}, {"id": "q_1763099586143_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Seeds develop, often inside fruits", "Leaves change color immediately", "Roots stop growing", "The plant wilts"], "question": "What usually happens after successful pollination in most flowering plants?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "After pollination and fertilization, the ovules develop into seeds and the ovary often becomes a fruit that protects and helps disperse the seeds.", "correctAnswer": "Seeds develop, often inside fruits"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:53:06.241351	\N
cd8a9dd2-341a-4b6a-9225-29f7a20bd966	d096c4e9-3f93-4351-982b-650c22b34824	world war - Easy	world war	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099592127_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (1914)", "The invasion of Poland (1939)", "The bombing of Pearl Harbor (1941)", "The Russian Revolution (1917)"], "question": "Which event is widely considered the immediate trigger for World War I?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War I began shortly after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary in June 1914, which set off a chain of alliances and declarations of war.", "correctAnswer": "The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (1914)"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1914", "1939", "1941", "1945"], "question": "In which year did World War II begin?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War II began in 1939 when Germany invaded Poland, prompting Britain and France to declare war on Germany.", "correctAnswer": "1939"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Germany, Italy, and Japan", "United States, United Kingdom, and Soviet Union", "France, China, and Canada", "Spain, Sweden, and Switzerland"], "question": "Which group was known as the Axis Powers in World War II?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Axis Powers were primarily Germany, Italy, and Japan, who formed a military alliance against the Allied Powers during World War II.", "correctAnswer": "Germany, Italy, and Japan"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["World War I", "World War II", "The Crimean War", "The Cold War"], "question": "The Treaty of Versailles officially ended which war?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Signed in 1919, the Treaty of Versailles formally ended World War I and imposed heavy penalties and reparations on Germany.", "correctAnswer": "World War I"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The Allied invasion of Normandy, France", "The German bombing campaign against London", "The atomic bombing of Hiroshima", "The signing of the United Nations Charter"], "question": "What was D-Day during World War II?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "D-Day, on June 6, 1944, was the Allied amphibious invasion of Normandy, which opened a Western front against Nazi Germany.", "correctAnswer": "The Allied invasion of Normandy, France"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Winston Churchill", "Neville Chamberlain", "Clement Attlee", "Margaret Thatcher"], "question": "Who served as the British Prime Minister during most of World War II?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Winston Churchill led the United Kingdom through most of World War II, known for his leadership and speeches that boosted morale.", "correctAnswer": "Winston Churchill"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The League of Nations was formed after World War II.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The League of Nations was created after World War I to promote peace and prevent future conflicts. After World War II, it was replaced by the United Nations.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Trench warfare is most closely associated with the Western Front in World War I.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Trench warfare became a defining feature of the Western Front in World War I, especially in France and Belgium, leading to stalemates and high casualties.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "The United Nations was established in 1919.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The United Nations was established in 1945 after World War II to promote international cooperation and prevent future wars.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Battle of Stalingrad during World War II was fought between Germany and the Soviet Union.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Battle of Stalingrad (1942–1943) was a major confrontation between Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union and marked a turning point on the Eastern Front.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The United States entered World War II after the Japanese attack on ________ in 1941.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Japan attacked the U.S. naval base at Pearl Harbor on December 7, 1941, leading the United States to declare war and enter World War II.", "correctAnswer": "Pearl Harbor"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The 1917 revolution in ________ led to the country’s withdrawal from World War I.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Russian Revolution in 1917 led to the Bolsheviks taking power and signing the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in 1918, which withdrew Russia from World War I.", "correctAnswer": "Russia"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The German strategy of rapid, coordinated attacks using tanks and aircraft was called ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Blitzkrieg, meaning \\"lightning war,\\" was a tactic that combined fast-moving tanks, infantry, and air support to overwhelm opponents in World War II.", "correctAnswer": "Blitzkrieg"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the 1919 treaty that imposed heavy reparations on Germany after World War I.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Treaty of Versailles set terms that punished Germany economically and territorially after World War I, influencing interwar tensions.", "correctAnswer": "Treaty of Versailles"}, {"id": "q_1763099592127_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which country did Germany invade on September 1, 1939, prompting Britain and France to declare war?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Germany’s invasion of Poland on September 1, 1939, led Britain and France to declare war on Germany, marking the start of World War II in Europe.", "correctAnswer": "Poland"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:53:12.140252	\N
ce821dba-f314-445f-ab26-a5245c2a0dc3	85dad80b-c425-47a5-9d9d-5703d8510cfe	MATHS - Medium	MATHS	medium	[{"id": "q_1763099614473_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(-1, 5)", "(1, -5)", "(1, 5)", "(-1, -5)"], "question": "For the function g(x) = -2(x + 1)^2 + 5, what is the vertex of the parabola?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In vertex form y = a(x - h)^2 + k, the vertex is (h, k). Here, g(x) = -2(x + 1)^2 + 5 can be written as -2(x - (-1))^2 + 5, so h = -1 and k = 5. The vertex is (-1, 5).", "correctAnswer": "(-1, 5)"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(5, 3)", "(4, 2)", "(6, 4)", "(7, 5)"], "question": "Solve the system of equations: 2x + 3y = 19 and x - y = 2. What is the ordered pair (x, y)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x - y = 2, we get x = y + 2. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 19: 2(y + 2) + 3y = 19 → 2y + 4 + 3y = 19 → 5y = 15 → y = 3. Then x = 3 + 2 = 5. So (x, y) = (5, 3).", "correctAnswer": "(5, 3)"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/12", "1/9", "5/36", "1/6"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum of the two dice is 9?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sum 9 can occur with (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3): 4 outcomes. There are 36 total outcomes, so probability = 4/36 = 1/9.", "correctAnswer": "1/9"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["12√5", "24", "30", "18√2"], "question": "Find the area of a triangle with side lengths 7, 8, and 9 units.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use Heron's formula. Semiperimeter s = (7 + 8 + 9)/2 = 12. Area = √[s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)] = √[12 × 5 × 4 × 3] = √720 = √(144 × 5) = 12√5.", "correctAnswer": "12√5"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The product of any two consecutive integers is always even.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Among any two consecutive integers, one must be even, and the product of an even number with any integer is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "For real numbers a and b, if a^2 = b^2, then a must equal b.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "If a^2 = b^2, then a = b or a = −b. For example, 3^2 = (−3)^2, but 3 ≠ −3.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve the inequality |2x − 5| < 3. Give your answer as an interval.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "|2x − 5| < 3 means −3 < 2x − 5 < 3. Add 5: 2 < 2x < 8. Divide by 2: 1 < x < 4. In interval notation, this is (1, 4).", "correctAnswer": "(1, 4)"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Simplify f(x) = (x^2 − 9) / (x − 3) for x ≠ 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor the numerator: x^2 − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3). Then f(x) = [(x − 3)(x + 3)] / (x − 3) = x + 3 for x ≠ 3.", "correctAnswer": "x + 3"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A car travels 60 km at 30 km/h and then 60 km at 60 km/h. What is its average speed for the entire 120 km trip?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Total distance = 120 km. Time1 = 60/30 = 2 h, Time2 = 60/60 = 1 h, total time = 3 h. Average speed = total distance / total time = 120/3 = 40 km/h.", "correctAnswer": "40 km/h"}, {"id": "q_1763099614473_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the equation of the line passing through the points (2, −3) and (6, 5). Give your answer in slope-intercept form.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Slope m = (5 − (−3)) / (6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2. Using y = mx + b and point (2, −3): −3 = 2(2) + b → −3 = 4 + b → b = −7. So the equation is y = 2x − 7.", "correctAnswer": "y = 2x − 7"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:53:34.48609	\N
434b7d5d-9b28-4780-96c8-510b669ba280	0008cf8d-eb05-4f01-adcf-02154939f8b6	science pollination - Medium	science pollination	medium	[{"id": "q_1763099649382_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Plant a different, compatible apple cultivar that blooms at the same time within 10 meters", "Hand-remove stamens from the flowers to reduce self-pollen", "Spray sugar water on blossoms to attract even more bees", "Bag the blossoms to protect them from cross-pollen"], "question": "Two 'Honeycrisp' apple trees planted 10 meters apart show heavy bee activity during bloom but set very few fruits. Which change is most likely to increase fruit set next season?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Many apple cultivars, including 'Honeycrisp', are self-incompatible and require pollen from a genetically different but bloom-synchronous cultivar to set fruit. Bees were present, so the limitation was compatible pollen, not pollinator abundance. Adding a compatible pollinizer nearby increases effective cross-pollination and fruit set.", "correctAnswer": "Plant a different, compatible apple cultivar that blooms at the same time within 10 meters"}, {"id": "q_1763099649382_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A coastal grass species produces copious, lightweight pollen, has small greenish flowers, and large feathery stigmas exposed to the air. It is most likely insect-pollinated.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "These traits are characteristic of wind pollination: inconspicuous flowers (no need to attract animals), abundant light pollen for dispersal, and feathery stigmas to catch airborne pollen. Insect-pollinated plants typically have showy, scented flowers with sticky pollen and less-exposed stigmas.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099649382_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Many flowering plants prevent self-fertilization through a genetic __________ system that rejects pollen carrying the same alleles as the maternal plant, promoting cross-pollination.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Self-incompatibility is a genetic mechanism where pollen sharing specific S-alleles with the stigma is recognized and prevented from completing fertilization (often by inhibiting pollen tube growth). This increases outcrossing and maintains genetic diversity.", "correctAnswer": "self-incompatibility"}, {"id": "q_1763099649382_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why hummingbird-pollinated flowers are often red and tubular with relatively dilute nectar, and how these traits reduce bee visitation.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Pollination syndromes reflect coadaptation between plants and pollinators. Hummingbirds are attracted to red, can hover and probe long corollas with specialized tongues, and prefer abundant but dilute nectar. Bees are less sensitive to red wavelengths and have shorter mouthparts, so deep, red tubes with dilute nectar reduce bee rewards and access, focusing visits by effective bird pollinators.", "correctAnswer": "Red, tubular flowers match hummingbirds’ vision and bill/tongue shape; birds see red well, while bees are less sensitive to red. Deep tubes position nectar where hummingbirds can reach but bees cannot, and the nectar is more dilute to suit birds’ high-volume sipping. These traits attract hummingbirds and deter or limit access by bees, increasing pollination efficiency by the target pollinator."}, {"id": "q_1763099649382_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Wind pollination alone is sufficient for high fruit set in strawberries", "Insect pollinators are key contributors to strawberry fruit set", "The mesh prevented pollen release from the anthers", "Self-pollination guarantees similar fruit set with or without insects"], "question": "A grower covers some strawberry flowers with fine mesh that excludes insects but allows wind; other flowers are left open. Open flowers set ~90% fruit, while covered flowers set ~20%. What is the best conclusion?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The large drop in fruit set when insects are excluded indicates that animal-mediated pollination greatly increases fertilization success in strawberries. While some selfing or wind transfer may occur (20% set), insects like bees significantly improve pollen deposition across many ovules, boosting fruit set and quality.", "correctAnswer": "Insect pollinators are key contributors to strawberry fruit set"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:54:09.469221	\N
6095f1a2-9efa-4e29-b1c7-417cb8e8300a	89c4bde0-7f85-4abb-8cb3-b2737b9b0a36	Fractions - Medium	Fractions	medium	[{"id": "q_1763099799744_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5/8", "11/16", "They are equal", "Cannot be determined"], "question": "Which fraction is greater: 5/8 or 11/16?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Convert 5/8 to sixteenths: 5/8 = 10/16. Since 11/16 > 10/16, 11/16 is greater.", "correctAnswer": "11/16"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7/6", "17/12", "2/3", "5/6"], "question": "Evaluate and simplify: 3/4 + 5/12", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Find a common denominator of 12: 3/4 = 9/12. Then 9/12 + 5/12 = 14/12 = 7/6 after simplifying.", "correctAnswer": "7/6"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["21/36", "7/12", "9/28", "14/27"], "question": "Simplify the product: (7/9) × (3/4)", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Multiply numerators and denominators: 7×3 = 21, 9×4 = 36, so 21/36. Simplify by dividing by 3 to get 7/12.", "correctAnswer": "7/12"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5/4", "4/5", "7/9", "8/9"], "question": "Compute: (5/6) ÷ (2/3)", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dividing by a fraction is multiplying by its reciprocal: (5/6) × (3/2) = 15/12 = 5/4 after simplifying.", "correctAnswer": "5/4"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/10 cup", "1/5 cup", "3/10 cup", "1/2 cup"], "question": "A recipe needs 3/5 cup of sugar, and you have 1/2 cup. How much more sugar is needed?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Find a common denominator of 10: 3/5 = 6/10 and 1/2 = 5/10. The difference is 6/10 − 5/10 = 1/10 cup.", "correctAnswer": "1/10 cup"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3/5, 2/3, 7/10", "2/3, 3/5, 7/10", "7/10, 2/3, 3/5", "3/5, 7/10, 2/3"], "question": "Order the fractions from least to greatest: 7/10, 3/5, 2/3", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use a common denominator of 30: 3/5 = 18/30, 2/3 = 20/30, 7/10 = 21/30. Thus 18/30 < 20/30 < 21/30.", "correctAnswer": "3/5, 2/3, 7/10"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The fraction 18/24 simplifies to 3/4.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Divide numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor 6: 18/24 = (18÷6)/(24÷6) = 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Adding the same number to both the numerator and denominator of a fraction leaves its value unchanged.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In general, (a+k)/(b+k) ≠ a/b unless k = 0 or a = b. Changing both parts typically changes the value.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "The number 2 1/3 is an improper fraction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "2 1/3 is a mixed number. An improper fraction has a numerator larger than its denominator, e.g., 7/3.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "If two fractions have the same denominator, the fraction with the larger numerator is greater.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "With equal denominators, more parts (a larger numerator) means a larger fraction. For example, 5/9 > 4/9.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Convert 9/4 to a mixed number.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Divide 9 by 4: 9 ÷ 4 = 2 remainder 1. So 9/4 = 2 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "2 1/4"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Write a fraction equivalent to 5/7 with a denominator of 42.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To get denominator 42, multiply 7 by 6. Multiply the numerator by the same factor: 5 × 6 = 30, so 30/42.", "correctAnswer": "30/42"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A student read 18 pages of a 48-page book. What fraction of the book is read, in simplest form?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "18/48 simplifies by dividing numerator and denominator by 6: (18÷6)/(48÷6) = 3/8.", "correctAnswer": "3/8"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A tank is 3/8 full. If you add water equal to 1/4 of the tank’s capacity, what fraction of the tank is full now?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Add the fractions using a common denominator of 8: 3/8 + 1/4 = 3/8 + 2/8 = 5/8.", "correctAnswer": "5/8"}, {"id": "q_1763099799744_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which is greater: 4/9 or 5/11?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Compare by cross-multiplying: 4×11 = 44 and 5×9 = 45. Since 45 > 44, 5/11 is greater.", "correctAnswer": "5/11"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:56:39.756398	\N
eb36fa71-c390-49f0-a610-673644b12086	7c6e706d-49e8-495f-849b-177cbf5f8158	MATHS - Easy	MATHS	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099808501_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. You can count up from 7 by 5 (8, 9, 10, 11, 12) to see the result.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763099808501_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Square", "Triangle", "Pentagon", "Circle"], "question": "Which shape has exactly 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides and 3 angles. A square has 4 sides, a pentagon has 5, and a circle has no straight sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1763099808501_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ones", "Tens", "Hundreds", "Thousands"], "question": "In the number 452, what is the place value of the digit 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "From right to left, the places are ones (2), tens (5), and hundreds (4). So the 5 is in the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "Tens"}, {"id": "q_1763099808501_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "1/3", "3/5", "2/3"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying the numerator and denominator of 1/2 by the same number (2) gives 2/4, which has the same value as 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1763099808501_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "6", "7", "3"], "question": "What is 9 − 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 3 from 9 leaves 6. On a number line, move 3 steps left from 9 to land on 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1763099808501_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099808502_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "A rectangle has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has opposite sides equal and four right angles. If all four sides are equal, the shape is a special rectangle called a square.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099808502_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "3 × ____ = 15", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "15 divided by 3 is 5, so 3 multiplied by 5 equals 15.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1763099808502_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The perimeter of a square with side length 4 units is ____ units.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a square is 4 times the side length. 4 × 4 = 16 units.", "correctAnswer": "16"}, {"id": "q_1763099808502_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of x if 2x = 10?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Divide both sides by 2: 2x ÷ 2 = 10 ÷ 2, so x = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:56:48.514622	\N
4619e8a3-fb3c-4337-9ea6-d21171e434b2	7c5357b1-2f16-4805-9ad0-33791be658f6	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099856258_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which device is primarily used to enter text and numbers into a computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A keyboard is an input device designed for typing letters, numbers, and symbols. Monitors and speakers are output devices, and a printer produces a hard copy of digital documents.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1763099856258_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is a type of temporary memory that is cleared when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) stores data that the CPU needs while the computer is running. It is volatile, meaning its contents are lost when power is removed. This is different from storage devices like SSDs or HDDs, which are non-volatile.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099856258_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The global network that connects millions of computers worldwide is called the ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is the worldwide system of interconnected computer networks. It is different from the World Wide Web (WWW), which is a collection of websites and pages that run on top of the Internet.", "correctAnswer": "Internet"}, {"id": "q_1763099856258_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym URL stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "URL stands for Uniform Resource Locator. It is the address used to access resources on the web, such as https://www.example.com.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1763099856258_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".docx", ".xlsx", ".pptx", ".pdf"], "question": "Which file extension is commonly used for a Microsoft Word document?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".docx is the standard file extension for Microsoft Word documents. .xlsx is used by Excel, .pptx by PowerPoint, and .pdf is a portable document format not specific to Word.", "correctAnswer": ".docx"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:57:36.356862	\N
425046f2-e540-4b2c-84a3-7d9d254b87b3	1f34c9e4-1d6d-49aa-b516-6d3a08b12df9	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1763099867446_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Newton's third law", "Conservation of momentum", "Conservation of energy", "Hooke's law"], "question": "When a car brakes and its brake pads heat up, which principle best explains the temperature increase?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The car’s kinetic energy is converted into thermal energy due to friction in the brakes. Energy isn’t lost; it changes form, consistent with the law of conservation of energy.", "correctAnswer": "Conservation of energy"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Enzymes increase the activation energy required for chemical reactions.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzymes lower the activation energy by stabilizing the transition state, allowing reactions to proceed faster at the same temperature.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "What is the pH of a solution with a hydrogen ion concentration of 1×10^-5 M?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "pH = −log10[H+]. For [H+] = 1×10^-5 M, pH = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0.2 A", "0.5 A", "2.0 A", "60 A"], "question": "A 60 W light bulb is connected to a 120 V supply. What current flows through the bulb?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Power P = VI, so I = P/V = 60 W / 120 V = 0.5 A.", "correctAnswer": "0.5 A"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What two characteristics of pollutants lead to biomagnification in food webs?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Persistent, fat-soluble chemicals resist degradation and are stored in fatty tissues. As predators eat multiple prey, the concentration increases at higher trophic levels.", "correctAnswer": "Persistence and lipophilicity (fat solubility)"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Changes in Earth’s distance from the Sun", "Earth’s elliptical orbit speed changes", "Earth’s axial tilt", "Solar activity cycles"], "question": "What is the main reason Earth experiences seasons?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The 23.5° tilt causes different angles and durations of sunlight throughout the year, producing seasons. Distance from the Sun changes only slightly and is not the primary cause.", "correctAnswer": "Earth’s axial tilt"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "In an isolated system, entropy tends to decrease over time.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The second law of thermodynamics states that entropy of an isolated system tends to increase or remain the same, not decrease.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a cross showing incomplete dominance between red (RR) and white (rr) flowers, the F1 offspring phenotype is ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incomplete dominance results in heterozygotes (Rr) exhibiting an intermediate phenotype—in this case, all F1 are pink.", "correctAnswer": "100% pink"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hydrochloric acid and sodium chloride", "Sodium hydroxide and potassium chloride", "Ammonia and ammonium chloride", "Acetic acid and sodium acetate"], "question": "Which combination best forms a buffer solution near pH 4.7?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A buffer is typically a weak acid and its conjugate base. Acetic acid (pKa ≈ 4.76) and sodium acetate resist pH changes around 4.7 via the Henderson–Hasselbalch relationship.", "correctAnswer": "Acetic acid and sodium acetate"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A 2 kg block is pulled across a rough surface at constant velocity. What is the net force on the block and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Newton’s second law, net force equals mass times acceleration. Constant velocity means acceleration is zero, so the applied force balances friction.", "correctAnswer": "Zero; constant velocity implies zero acceleration"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Most earthquakes occur at tectonic plate boundaries.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Stress accumulates and is released where plates interact (divergent, convergent, transform boundaries), causing the majority of earthquakes.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The speed of a wave equals frequency multiplied by ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Wave speed v = f × λ, where f is frequency and λ is wavelength.", "correctAnswer": "wavelength"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 increases, loading more oxygen", "Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 decreases, releasing more oxygen to tissues", "There is no change in oxygen delivery", "Hemoglobin denatures under moderate exercise"], "question": "During vigorous exercise, the oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the right. What does this indicate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Higher CO2, temperature, and acidity (Bohr effect) reduce hemoglobin’s oxygen affinity, enhancing O2 delivery to active tissues.", "correctAnswer": "Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 decreases, releasing more oxygen to tissues"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "A hypothesis must be proven true to be considered scientific.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Scientific hypotheses must be testable and falsifiable. They can be supported by evidence but are not “proven” in an absolute sense.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099867446_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It halves", "It doubles", "It increases by √2", "It remains unchanged"], "question": "A gas at constant pressure has its temperature doubled (in Kelvin). What happens to its volume?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Charles’s law (V ∝ T at constant pressure), doubling the absolute temperature doubles the volume.", "correctAnswer": "It doubles"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:57:47.461415	\N
5069cdfc-70c8-472b-8bbe-8419b04622c1	da812d7c-ba8f-47c8-b6c5-7b6458a7ec70	ict - Easy	ict	easy	[{"id": "q_1763099951291_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An input device sends data to the computer for processing. A keyboard is used to input text and commands, while a monitor, printer, and speaker are output devices that display, print, or play information from the computer.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1763099951291_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The internet and the World Wide Web are the same thing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The internet is the global network of connected computers and devices, while the World Wide Web is a service that runs on the internet using web pages and browsers. The web is just one part of the internet.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099951291_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The main circuit board inside a computer that connects all components is called the _______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The motherboard is the central circuit board that holds and connects the CPU, memory (RAM), storage interfaces, and other components so they can communicate.", "correctAnswer": "motherboard"}, {"id": "q_1763099951291_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In ICT, what does the abbreviation CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is often called the brain of the computer because it executes instructions and processes data.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1763099951291_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".docx", ".mp3", ".png", ".exe"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for a word processing document created with common office software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".docx is the standard file extension for Microsoft Word documents and many word processors can open it. .mp3 is audio, .png is an image format, and .exe is an executable program file.", "correctAnswer": ".docx"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:59:11.303871	\N
f469e8ef-a365-470a-af0c-e9f21d13c302	89c4bde0-7f85-4abb-8cb3-b2737b9b0a36	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1763099974275_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["255.255.255.224", "255.255.255.240", "255.255.255.248", "255.255.255.192"], "question": "You are assigned the 192.168.10.0/24 network and must create at least 6 equal-size subnets while keeping the maximum number of usable host addresses per subnet. Which subnet mask should you choose?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To get at least 6 subnets from a /24, you need to borrow 3 bits from the host portion (2^3 = 8 subnets). That yields a /27 mask, which is 255.255.255.224. Each /27 subnet supports 2^(32−27) − 2 = 30 usable hosts. /26 gives only 4 subnets (not enough), while /28 and /29 provide more subnets but fewer hosts per subnet.", "correctAnswer": "255.255.255.224"}, {"id": "q_1763099974275_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Using HTTPS guarantees that a website is legitimate and safe from phishing.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "HTTPS encrypts data in transit and authenticates the connection to the domain in the certificate, providing confidentiality and integrity. However, attackers can obtain certificates for deceptive domains (e.g., typosquats), so HTTPS does not guarantee that content is trustworthy or that the site is not a phishing attempt.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763099974276_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In relational databases, reorganizing data to reduce redundancy and prevent update anomalies by structuring it into related tables linked by keys is called __________.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Normalization is the process of structuring a database into normal forms (e.g., 1NF, 2NF, 3NF) to minimize redundancy and dependency issues. It separates data into related tables connected by primary and foreign keys, improving data integrity and maintainability.", "correctAnswer": "normalization"}, {"id": "q_1763099974276_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the minimum number of bits needed to uniquely represent 100 different values in binary?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The number of bits required is the smallest n such that 2^n ≥ 100. Since 2^6 = 64 and 2^7 = 128, 7 bits are needed to represent at least 100 distinct values.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1763099974276_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Elasticity", "Scalability", "Fault tolerance", "Load balancing"], "question": "A startup’s web app experiences unpredictable traffic spikes. They want resources to scale up automatically during peaks and scale down afterward, paying only for what they use. Which cloud characteristic best fits this need?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Elasticity is the automatic, rapid provisioning and deprovisioning of resources to match current demand, helping control cost. Scalability is the broader ability to handle growth (often via design changes). Load balancing distributes traffic across instances, and fault tolerance focuses on surviving failures.", "correctAnswer": "Elasticity"}]	none	2025-11-14 05:59:34.288544	\N
4f6842a2-1c09-4dd4-a2b0-d25ece78384f	b2e7dec0-f2ca-4ea9-8a3b-70886ffe63c7	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1763100030257_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Mouse", "Printer"], "question": "Which device is primarily used to input text into a computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A keyboard is an input device designed specifically for entering text and commands. A monitor and printer are output devices, while a mouse is an input device but mainly for pointing and clicking, not typing.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1763100030257_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Internet and the World Wide Web are the same thing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is the global network of interconnected computers and infrastructure. The World Wide Web is a service that runs on the Internet, using web pages and browsers to access information. The Web is part of the Internet, not the whole thing.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763100030257_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The __________ is the main software that manages a computer’s hardware and lets other programs run.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (OS) like Windows, macOS, or Linux controls hardware resources, provides a user interface, and allows applications to run. It manages memory, files, and devices.", "correctAnswer": "operating system"}, {"id": "q_1763100030257_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common web browser.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Web browsers are programs used to access the World Wide Web. Examples include Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari, and Microsoft Edge. Any of these would be a correct example.", "correctAnswer": "Google Chrome"}, {"id": "q_1763100030257_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Word processing document", "Spreadsheet", "Presentation", "Database"], "question": "The file extension \\".docx\\" is typically used for which type of file?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".docx is the default format for Microsoft Word documents, which are word processing files. Spreadsheets often use .xlsx (Excel), presentations use .pptx (PowerPoint), and databases have other specialized formats.", "correctAnswer": "Word processing document"}]	none	2025-11-14 06:00:30.345077	\N
965f84ba-b782-4e7c-9023-d8dde98a9f04	f7c2132c-4fb3-44ca-be0f-dc2b322f601b	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763112255387_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Venus", "Mars", "Jupiter", "Mercury"], "question": "Which planet is known as the Red Planet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mars is called the Red Planet because its surface contains iron oxide (rust), which gives it a reddish appearance.", "correctAnswer": "Mars"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Hydrogen"], "question": "What gas do plants take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "During photosynthesis, plants take in carbon dioxide and water and use sunlight to produce glucose, releasing oxygen as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster than light.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Light travels much faster than sound. Light moves at about 300,000 km/s, while sound in air travels around 343 m/s.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which liquid water turns into vapor is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation is when molecules at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to become gas, often due to heat.", "correctAnswer": "evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the force that pulls objects toward Earth called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. On Earth, it pulls objects toward the planet’s center.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Nucleus", "Cell wall", "Cytoplasm", "Ribosome"], "question": "Which part of the cell contains genetic material (DNA)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In eukaryotic cells, DNA is stored in the nucleus, which controls cell activities.", "correctAnswer": "Nucleus"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Water", "Oxygen", "Hydrogen", "Salt"], "question": "What is H2O commonly called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "H2O is the chemical formula for water, indicating two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.", "correctAnswer": "Water"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "The heart is a muscle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood through the circulatory system.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Coal", "Oil", "Wind", "Natural gas"], "question": "Which energy source is renewable?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wind is a renewable resource because it is naturally replenished, unlike fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas.", "correctAnswer": "Wind"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A magnet has two poles called north and south.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets have two ends, or poles, labeled north and south, which determine magnetic attraction and repulsion.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The boiling point of pure water at sea level is ____ degrees Celsius.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (sea level), pure water boils at 100°C.", "correctAnswer": "100"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_11", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the organ that is primarily responsible for breathing in humans.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The lungs take in oxygen and expel carbon dioxide during breathing.", "correctAnswer": "lungs"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which phase of matter has a fixed shape and a fixed volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids have particles packed closely together, giving them a definite shape and volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth orbits the Moon.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Moon orbits the Earth, and together they orbit the Sun.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Barometer", "Thermometer", "Hygrometer", "Compass"], "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature. Barometers measure air pressure, hygrometers measure humidity, and compasses show direction.", "correctAnswer": "Thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_15", "type": "true_false", "question": "Plants make their own food using sunlight.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Through photosynthesis, plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to make glucose (food) and oxygen.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest planet in our solar system is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Jupiter is the largest planet by diameter and mass in our solar system.", "correctAnswer": "Jupiter"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call animals that only eat plants?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Herbivores are animals whose diet consists mainly or entirely of plant material.", "correctAnswer": "herbivores"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_18", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Photosynthesis", "Metamorphosis", "Germination", "Respiration"], "question": "What do we call the change from a caterpillar to a butterfly?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metamorphosis is a biological process of transformation during an animal's life cycle, such as caterpillar to butterfly.", "correctAnswer": "Metamorphosis"}, {"id": "q_1763112255387_19", "type": "true_false", "question": "Metals are generally good conductors of electricity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metals conduct electricity well because their electrons can move freely through the material.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2025-11-14 09:24:15.478106	\N
360988f6-5357-49f5-a8c9-371474559f4e	7c5357b1-2f16-4805-9ad0-33791be658f6	HISTORY - Easy	HISTORY	easy	[{"id": "q_1763128355474_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["George Washington", "John Adams", "Thomas Jefferson", "James Madison"], "question": "Who was the first President of the United States?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "George Washington served as the first U.S. President from 1789 to 1797 after leading the Continental Army during the American Revolution. He was unanimously elected and helped set many presidential precedents.", "correctAnswer": "George Washington"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Aztec", "Inca", "Maya", "Olmec"], "question": "Which ancient civilization built Machu Picchu?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Machu Picchu was constructed by the Inca civilization in the 15th century in the Andes Mountains of Peru. It is one of the best-preserved examples of Inca engineering and architecture.", "correctAnswer": "Inca"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Latin", "Greek", "Arabic", "Hebrew"], "question": "What was the main official language of the Roman Empire?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Latin was the official language of the Roman government, law, and military. While Greek was widely used in the eastern provinces, Latin remained the core language of Roman administration.", "correctAnswer": "Latin"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Silk Road", "Spice Route", "Trans-Saharan Highway", "Amber Road"], "question": "What was the name of the trade route that connected China to the Mediterranean world?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Silk Road was a network of land and sea routes that facilitated trade and cultural exchange between East Asia and the Mediterranean, carrying goods like silk, spices, and ideas across continents.", "correctAnswer": "Silk Road"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Great Wall is located in China.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Great Wall of China was built and rebuilt over centuries to help protect China’s northern borders. It stretches across northern China and is one of the world’s most famous landmarks.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The American Civil War was fought in the 18th century.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The American Civil War occurred in the 19th century, from 1861 to 1865. The 18th century refers to the 1700s, which is earlier and includes events like the American Revolution.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In 1492, ____ sailed from Spain across the Atlantic Ocean.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Christopher Columbus sailed under the Spanish flag in 1492, reaching the Caribbean islands. His voyage used three ships: the Niña, the Pinta, and the Santa María.", "correctAnswer": "Christopher Columbus"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The monumental tombs built by ancient Egyptians are called ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ancient Egyptians built pyramids as monumental tombs for pharaohs and elites. The Great Pyramid of Giza is one of the most famous and oldest surviving structures.", "correctAnswer": "pyramids"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What document, adopted in 1776, announced the American colonies’ independence from Britain?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adopted on July 4, 1776, the Declaration of Independence explained the colonies’ reasons for separation and asserted their right to self-government. Thomas Jefferson was its principal author.", "correctAnswer": "The Declaration of Independence"}, {"id": "q_1763128355474_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What city served as the capital of the Byzantine Empire (Eastern Roman Empire)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Constantinople, founded by Emperor Constantine in 330 CE, was the capital of the Byzantine Empire. It is now known as Istanbul, located in modern-day Turkey.", "correctAnswer": "Constantinople"}]	none	2025-11-14 13:52:35.612359	\N
adafd7db-004a-4519-903e-f887f2a9f128	3b6c8b80-0d2d-4c8a-8baf-8b86f8938da1	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1763131645233_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Power Unit", "Control Program Utility", "Central Peripheral Unit"], "question": "What does \\"CPU\\" stand for in computing?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is the primary component that processes instructions and performs calculations. The other options are common misconceptions or incorrect expansions of the acronym.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1763131645233_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A web browser is software used to access and view webpages on the internet.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "True. A web browser (such as Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, or Safari) lets users request, download, and display webpages and other internet content.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763131645233_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The physical parts of a computer, such as the keyboard, mouse, and monitor, are called ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "These tangible components are known as hardware. In contrast, software refers to the programs and operating systems that run on the hardware.", "correctAnswer": "hardware"}, {"id": "q_1763131645233_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common image file format used for photos on the web.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "JPEG is a widely used image format that balances quality and file size, making it ideal for photos on the web. Other common formats include PNG and GIF, but JPEG is the most typical for photographs.", "correctAnswer": "JPEG"}, {"id": "q_1763131645233_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cell", "Field", "Table", "Range"], "question": "In a spreadsheet (e.g., Excel or Google Sheets), the intersection of a row and a column is called a what?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The intersection of a row and a column is a cell, identified by a cell reference like A1. A field is a database term, a table is a collection of cells, and a range is a group of selected cells.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}]	none	2025-11-14 14:47:25.332483	\N
262cce71-1d33-400c-a015-11118156f8c7	9024006e-67b6-4dd8-ae1b-0e664f64c780	Science  - Medium	Science 	medium	[{"id": "q_1763133221733_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Doubling the substrate concentration", "Adding a competitive inhibitor", "Lowering the pH by 2 units away from the optimum", "Raising the temperature well above the enzyme’s denaturation point"], "question": "An enzyme-catalyzed reaction is running at low substrate concentration, near its optimal pH and temperature. Which change will most immediately increase the reaction rate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Under Michaelis–Menten kinetics, when substrate concentration is low relative to Km, the reaction rate is approximately proportional to [S]. Doubling substrate increases the rate. Competitive inhibitors reduce the rate by raising the apparent Km, moving pH away from the optimum decreases activity, and excessive temperature denatures the enzyme.", "correctAnswer": "Doubling the substrate concentration"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a sealed, rigid container, increasing the temperature of a gas increases its pressure proportionally.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Gay-Lussac’s law states that at constant volume and moles, gas pressure is directly proportional to absolute temperature (P ∝ T). As temperature rises, molecular kinetic energy increases, causing more frequent and forceful collisions with the container walls.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The energy required to remove the outermost electron from a neutral gaseous atom is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Ionization energy is the minimum energy needed to remove an electron from an atom in the gas phase. It increases across a period due to higher effective nuclear charge and generally decreases down a group as atomic radius increases.", "correctAnswer": "ionization energy"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why are the seasons opposite in the Southern Hemisphere compared to the Northern Hemisphere?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Earth’s 23.5° axial tilt causes differential solar heating. As Earth orbits the Sun, one hemisphere is oriented toward the Sun (longer, more direct sunlight) while the other is oriented away (shorter, less direct sunlight), leading to opposite seasons.", "correctAnswer": "Because Earth’s axis is tilted; when the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun (summer), the Southern Hemisphere is tilted away (winter), and vice versa."}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It doubles", "It halves", "It remains the same", "It becomes zero"], "question": "In a circuit with a fixed 12 V battery, you replace a 6 Ω resistor with a 3 Ω resistor. Neglect internal resistance. What happens to the current through the resistor?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Ohm’s law, I = V/R. Initially I = 12 V / 6 Ω = 2 A. After replacement, I = 12 V / 3 Ω = 4 A. The current doubles, and the power dissipated (P = VI or V^2/R) increases accordingly.", "correctAnswer": "It doubles"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Convection currents in the mantle", "Solar radiation pressure on the crust", "Tidal forces from the Moon", "Differential chemical weathering"], "question": "Which mechanism primarily drives the movement of Earth’s tectonic plates?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Heat from Earth’s interior creates convection currents in the mantle that move the lithospheric plates. Slab pull and ridge push are related processes. Solar radiation and weathering do not provide sufficient forces, and lunar tides have only minor effects.", "correctAnswer": "Convection currents in the mantle"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "DNA replication is semi-conservative, producing daughter molecules that each contain one original (parental) strand and one newly synthesized strand.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Meselson–Stahl experiment demonstrated semi-conservative replication. Each daughter DNA retains one parental strand, providing a template that helps ensure accurate base pairing during synthesis.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "During photosynthesis, the Calvin cycle primarily fixes ______ into organic molecules.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Calvin cycle uses CO2 along with ATP and NADPH from the light reactions to synthesize glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P), which is used to form glucose and other carbohydrates.", "correctAnswer": "carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A 2 kg block slides from rest down a frictionless track, dropping 5 m in height. What is its speed at the bottom?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By energy conservation, mgh = ½mv². Solving gives v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) ≈ √98 ≈ 9.9 m/s. The mass cancels, so the speed depends only on the height and g.", "correctAnswer": "About 9.9 m/s"}, {"id": "q_1763133221733_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Phenolphthalein (color change around pH 8.2–10)", "Methyl orange (color change around pH 3.1–4.4)", "Litmus paper", "Bromothymol blue (color change around pH 6.0–7.6)"], "question": "You titrate a weak acid (HA) with a strong base (NaOH). Which indicator is most suitable for detecting the endpoint?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Weak acid–strong base titrations have an equivalence point in the basic range (pH > 7). Phenolphthalein’s transition range matches this, giving a sharp endpoint. Methyl orange and bromothymol blue transition too acidic or near neutral, and litmus is not precise.", "correctAnswer": "Phenolphthalein (color change around pH 8.2–10)"}]	none	2025-11-14 15:13:44.21656	\N
7334cfe8-483b-46bf-a86e-4f27a378777c	a750dd2e-44fa-42f8-bc01-6dcd57cfce42	sience - Easy	sience	easy	[{"id": "q_1763133550165_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Brain", "Heart", "Lungs", "Stomach"], "question": "Which organ pumps blood through the body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood through the circulatory system, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues. The brain controls body functions, the lungs exchange gases, and the stomach helps digest food.", "correctAnswer": "Heart"}, {"id": "q_1763133550165_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth orbits the Sun once every 24 hours.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Twenty-four hours is the time it takes Earth to rotate once on its axis (a day). Earth completes one orbit around the Sun in about 365.25 days (a year).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763133550165_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which green plants make their own food using sunlight is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is the process where plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose (food) and oxygen, primarily in the chloroplasts containing chlorophyll.", "correctAnswer": "photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1763133550165_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a definite volume but takes the shape of its container?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Liquids have a fixed volume but no fixed shape; they flow to fit the container. Solids have fixed shape and volume, while gases and plasmas have neither fixed shape nor fixed volume.", "correctAnswer": "Liquid"}, {"id": "q_1763133550165_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What gas in the air do humans need to breathe in for cellular respiration?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Humans inhale oxygen (O2), which cells use in mitochondria to release energy from food. Carbon dioxide (CO2) is produced as a waste product and exhaled.", "correctAnswer": "Oxygen"}]	none	2025-11-14 15:19:10.265782	\N
1de6bde4-f448-4688-8e8f-59b6a5bdaff3	a750dd2e-44fa-42f8-bc01-6dcd57cfce42	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1763133844210_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "7", "9", "11"], "question": "A theater sells adult tickets for $12 and child tickets for $8. If 20 tickets were sold for a total of $188, how many adult tickets were sold?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let a be the number of adult tickets and c be the number of child tickets. Then a + c = 20 and 12a + 8c = 188. Substituting c = 20 − a into the revenue equation gives 12a + 8(20 − a) = 188 → 12a + 160 − 8a = 188 → 4a = 28 → a = 7. Therefore, 7 adult tickets were sold.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1763133844210_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The function g(x) = |2x − 5| is differentiable at x = 2.5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The expression inside the absolute value, 2x − 5, is zero at x = 2.5. Absolute value functions have a sharp corner (cusp) where their argument is zero, so the derivative does not exist there. Thus g(x) is not differentiable at x = 2.5.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763133844210_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for x (with domain x > 2): log2(x) + log2(x − 2) = 4. x = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the logarithm property log2(x) + log2(y) = log2(xy): log2(x(x − 2)) = 4, so x(x − 2) = 16. This gives x^2 − 2x − 16 = 0. Solving, x = [2 ± sqrt(4 + 64)]/2 = [2 ± sqrt(68)]/2 = 1 ± sqrt(17). The domain requires x > 2 (so x − 2 > 0), hence x = 1 + sqrt(17) only.", "correctAnswer": "1 + sqrt(17)"}, {"id": "q_1763133844210_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices (1, 2), (4, 6), and (7, 2).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Points (1,2) and (7,2) lie on the same horizontal line, so they form a base of length 6. The height is the vertical distance from (4,6) to y = 2, which is 4. Area = (1/2) × base × height = (1/2) × 6 × 4 = 12 square units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763133844210_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/3", "1/6", "5/36", "7/36"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is at least 10?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Favorable sums are 10, 11, and 12. Number of outcomes: for 10 → (4,6), (5,5), (6,4) = 3; for 11 → (5,6), (6,5) = 2; for 12 → (6,6) = 1. Total favorable = 6. Total outcomes = 36. Probability = 6/36 = 1/6.", "correctAnswer": "1/6"}]	none	2025-11-14 15:24:06.654174	\N
aa819116-3648-4c8a-ba41-e36575ee41f8	a750dd2e-44fa-42f8-bc01-6dcd57cfce42	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1763134008016_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12. You can think of it as counting up 5 from 7: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763134008016_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2 with no remainder. 0 ÷ 2 = 0 with no remainder, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763134008016_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "1/2 of 8 is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Taking half means dividing by 2. 8 ÷ 2 = 4, so one-half of 8 is 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1763134008016_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of the digit 3 in the number 3,204?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 3 is in the thousands place, so its value is 3 × 1000 = 3000.", "correctAnswer": "3000"}, {"id": "q_1763134008016_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Triangle", "Square", "Circle", "Rectangle"], "question": "Which shape has exactly three sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has three sides. A square and a rectangle have four sides, and a circle has no straight sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}]	none	2025-11-14 15:26:48.103962	\N
