 {"id": "q_1763135403264_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2", "4", "6", "8"], "question": "Evaluate: 10 - 6 + 2", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Addition and subtraction are done left to right: (10 - 6) + 2 = 4 + 2 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square is a rectangle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has four right angles and opposite sides equal; a square meets these conditions and also has all sides equal.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The number 0 is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder; 0 ÷ 2 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "0.5 is greater than 0.75.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 equals 1/2 and 0.75 equals 3/4; one-half is less than three-fourths.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of any two odd numbers is odd.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Odd + odd equals even (for example, 3 + 5 = 8).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 - 4 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 gives 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "3/4 of 8 is __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiply 8 by 3/4: 8 × 3/4 = 24/4 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The next number in the pattern 2, 4, 6, 8, __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time, so 8 + 2 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The area of a rectangle with length 7 units and width 2 units is __ square units.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width: 7 × 2 = 14 square units.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Convert 250 centimeters to meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 100 centimeters in a meter. 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5 meters.", "correctAnswer": "2.5 meters"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the mean (average) of 2, 4, 6, and 8?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Add the numbers (2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20) and divide by 4: 20 ÷ 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If x + 5 = 12, what is the value of x?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 5 from both sides: x = 12 - 5 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1763135403264_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the shape that has exactly three sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle is a polygon with three sides and three angles.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}]	none	2025-11-14 15:50:03.37126	\N
bc0a5343-0cb8-4926-bb0f-2f20f32e76e0	a750dd2e-44fa-42f8-bc01-6dcd57cfce42	sience - Easy	sience	easy	[{"id": "q_1763136608447_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["206", "208", "200", "190"], "question": "How many bones are in the adult human skeleton?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An adult human has about 206 bones. This internal framework supports the body, protects organs, enables movement, stores minerals, and produces blood cells.", "correctAnswer": "206"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The skeleton provides structural support and protection for the body's soft tissues and internal organs.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The skeleton acts as a framework that supports soft tissues and shields organs like the brain (skull) and heart/lungs (rib cage).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mineral storage", "Blood cell formation", "Photosynthesis", "Assisting movement by serving as levers for muscles"], "question": "Which of the following is NOT a function of the skeleton?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is a plant process, not a skeletal function. The skeleton stores minerals (like calcium and phosphate), forms blood cells in marrow, and enables movement by acting as levers for muscles.", "correctAnswer": "Photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The skeleton acts as a reservoir for minerals like _____ and phosphate.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bones store important minerals such as calcium and phosphate, helping maintain mineral balance and strength in the body.", "correctAnswer": "calcium"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the term for an internal skeleton found in vertebrates?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Vertebrates (like humans) have an endoskeleton, an internal framework of bones that supports and protects the body from within.", "correctAnswer": "endoskeleton"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Endoskeleton", "Exoskeleton", "No skeleton", "Cartilage-only skeleton"], "question": "What type of skeleton do insects have?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Insects have an exoskeleton—an external hard covering that supports and protects their bodies from the outside.", "correctAnswer": "Exoskeleton"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "A \\"skeleton crew\\" refers to the minimal staff needed to keep something operating.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In everyday language, a “skeleton crew” means the essential, minimal number of people necessary to run an organization or task.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "In figurative language, \\"skeleton\\" can describe a very thin person or animal.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Figuratively, calling someone a \\"skeleton\\" suggests extreme thinness. The term also appears in phrases like \\"a skeleton in the closet\\" for a hidden secret.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The marrow inside certain bones produces _____ cells.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bone marrow is a soft tissue that produces blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.", "correctAnswer": "blood"}, {"id": "q_1763136608447_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Skeleton key", "Skeleton in the closet", "Skeleton crew", "Skeleton sled"], "question": "Which phrase means a hidden secret?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Skeleton in the closet\\" is an idiom meaning a hidden or embarrassing secret. The other options refer to different meanings of \\"skeleton.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Skeleton in the closet"}]	fe33e129-2916-44b5-9057-62310d616742	2025-11-14 16:10:08.459936	\N
f0d78d52-0cd8-45a8-9d82-004f475b0d18	884cde6e-1ffd-462b-8503-e8ff82421ce1	world war 2 - Medium	world war 2	medium	[{"id": "q_1763136980419_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Germany could redirect major forces to North Africa, regaining the initiative there", "Germany was forced into a sustained defensive posture as the Red Army seized the initiative and pushed westward", "The Soviet Union collapsed, leading to a swift German advance toward Moscow", "Germany secured the Caucasus oil fields, stabilizing its fuel shortages"], "question": "After the German defeat at Stalingrad (1942–43), what was the most significant strategic consequence for the Eastern Front?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Stalingrad destroyed a large German field army and shattered the aura of Wehrmacht invincibility. From early 1943 onward, the Soviets held the strategic initiative, mounting successive offensives (e.g., Kursk, Operation Bagration) that steadily drove German forces back toward Germany. The defeat prevented any renewed German bid for decisive victory in the East.", "correctAnswer": "Germany was forced into a sustained defensive posture as the Red Army seized the initiative and pushed westward"}, {"id": "q_1763136980419_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To capture every Japanese-held island, regardless of strategic value", "To bypass heavily fortified positions, cut Japanese supply lines, and seize airfields that brought bombers closer to Japan", "To avoid naval engagements entirely and fight only on land", "To prioritize political independence movements over military objectives"], "question": "What was the main rationale behind the U.S. ‘island-hopping’ strategy in the Pacific?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Island-hopping focused on capturing strategically valuable islands (like the Gilberts, Marshalls, Marianas) while isolating strongholds such as Rabaul. This approach conserved resources, undermined Japanese logistics, and provided bases for long-range bombing of Japan, hastening the path to the home islands.", "correctAnswer": "To bypass heavily fortified positions, cut Japanese supply lines, and seize airfields that brought bombers closer to Japan"}, {"id": "q_1763136980419_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact of 1939 included secret protocols dividing Eastern Europe into spheres of influence, facilitating the joint German–Soviet invasion of Poland.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The pact’s secret protocols outlined German and Soviet spheres in Eastern Europe, including the partition of Poland. Germany invaded from the west on September 1, 1939, and the USSR invaded from the east on September 17, effectively dismembering Poland and validating the clandestine agreements.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763136980419_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The Allied deception plan that misled the Germans into expecting a landing at the Pas-de-Calais rather than Normandy was Operation ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Operation Fortitude (North and South) was part of the broader deception scheme (Operation Bodyguard). It used fake units, double agents, and radio traffic to convince German command that the main invasion would target the Pas-de-Calais, keeping forces pinned away from Normandy during D-Day.", "correctAnswer": "Fortitude"}, {"id": "q_1763136980419_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What declaration issued in July 1945 called for Japan’s unconditional surrender and warned of “prompt and utter destruction” if Japan refused?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Allied leaders at the Potsdam Conference released the Potsdam Declaration on July 26, 1945. It outlined surrender terms for Japan and warned of severe consequences for noncompliance, foreshadowing the atomic bombings and Japan’s eventual capitulation.", "correctAnswer": "Potsdam Declaration"}]	none	2025-11-14 16:16:20.521649	\N
26ca2a04-34bf-46ef-81ed-8a957e10c0da	bdb0cdef-4fc7-42ee-bf94-4af2d8d6d124	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1763137532075_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["dog", "runs", "quickly", "very"], "question": "Which word is a noun in the sentence: \\"The dog runs quickly\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. In the sentence, 'dog' is the thing being talked about, so it is the noun.", "correctAnswer": "dog"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"___ apple\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'an' before words that start with a vowel sound. 'Apple' begins with the vowel sound /a/, so 'an apple' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["walk", "walked", "walking", "walks"], "question": "What is the past tense of \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed. Therefore, 'walk' becomes 'walked' in the past tense.", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["joyful", "angry", "tired", "noisy"], "question": "Which word is a synonym for \\"happy\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. 'Joyful' means 'happy,' while the other options do not.", "correctAnswer": "joyful"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["warm", "cold", "heat", "spicy"], "question": "Which word is an antonym of \\"hot\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An antonym is a word with the opposite meaning. The opposite of 'hot' in temperature is 'cold.'", "correctAnswer": "cold"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["under", "sing", "green", "quickly"], "question": "Which of the following is a preposition?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Prepositions show relationships, often of place or time. 'Under' shows location. 'Sing' is a verb, 'green' an adjective, and 'quickly' an adverb.", "correctAnswer": "under"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["childs", "children", "childs'", "childrens"], "question": "What is the correct plural of \\"child\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Some nouns are irregular in the plural form. 'Child' becomes 'children,' not 'childs' or 'childrens.'", "correctAnswer": "children"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["period", "question mark", "exclamation mark", "comma"], "question": "Choose the correct punctuation to complete the sentence: \\"How are you\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The sentence is a direct question, so it should end with a question mark.", "correctAnswer": "question mark"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "In English, sentences should start with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Capitalization rules state that the first letter of a sentence should be capitalized.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "\\"Their\\" is a contraction meaning \\"they are.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Their' is a possessive pronoun. The contraction for 'they are' is 'they're.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "In the sentence \\"She runs fast,\\" the word \\"fast\\" is an adverb.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An adverb describes how an action is done. 'Fast' describes how she runs, so it functions as an adverb here.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The subject of the sentence \\"The cats sleep\\" is \\"sleep.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The subject is the person or thing doing the action. 'Cats' are doing the sleeping, so 'cats' is the subject. 'Sleep' is the verb.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence: \\"He ___ to school every day.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With third-person singular (he/she/it), the verb 'go' takes -es in the present simple: 'he goes.'", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the plural form: \\"I have two ___.\\" (plural of \\"box\\")", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nouns ending in -x form the plural by adding -es, so 'box' becomes 'boxes.'", "correctAnswer": "boxes"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"Please pass ___ salt.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'the' for specific or known items, such as an item shared by speakers like 'the salt' on the table.", "correctAnswer": "the"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the comparative: \\"She is taller ___ her sister.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When comparing two things, we use 'than' after comparative adjectives: 'taller than.'", "correctAnswer": "than"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of \\"eat\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Eat' is an irregular verb. Its past tense form is 'ate.'", "correctAnswer": "ate"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the punctuation mark used to end a normal statement.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A declarative sentence ends with a period to show a complete statement.", "correctAnswer": "period"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What pronoun replaces \\"Tom and I\\" in the sentence \\"___ are going to the park\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The subject pronoun for 'Tom and I' is 'we' because it refers to the first person plural.", "correctAnswer": "We"}, {"id": "q_1763137532075_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give one example of a conjunction.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Conjunctions join words or parts of sentences. 'And' is a common coordinating conjunction.", "correctAnswer": "and"}]	none	2025-11-14 16:25:32.175167	\N
b4c90b10-e4c5-4944-bf41-f5d01c488bc0	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1763139672296_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which device is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An input device sends data to the computer for processing. A keyboard is used to input text and commands. Monitors, printers, and speakers are output devices because they display, print, or play information from the computer.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The operating system manages hardware and software resources.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The operating system (e.g., Windows, macOS, Linux) controls hardware like memory and storage and coordinates software applications, providing an interface and managing tasks and resources.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The acronym \\"URL\\" stands for ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the address used to locate resources on the web, such as http://example.com.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the purpose of antivirus software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Antivirus software scans files and programs to identify malicious code (viruses, spyware, ransomware) and helps protect the system by blocking or removing threats.", "correctAnswer": "To detect, prevent, and remove malware from a computer."}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".jpg", ".docx", ".xlsx", ".pptx"], "question": "Which file extension typically indicates an image file?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (or .jpeg) is a common image format. .docx is a Word document, .xlsx is an Excel spreadsheet, and .pptx is a PowerPoint presentation.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is permanent storage that keeps data even when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) is volatile; it temporarily stores data while the computer is running and loses its contents when power is off. Permanent storage uses devices like SSDs or HDDs.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a spreadsheet, the intersection of a row and a column is called a ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A cell is the basic unit in a spreadsheet where you enter data. Each cell is identified by its column letter and row number, like A1.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one web browser.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Common web browsers include Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, and Apple Safari. Any of these is a correct example.", "correctAnswer": "Google Chrome"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ctrl+C", "Ctrl+V", "Ctrl+X", "Ctrl+Z"], "question": "What keyboard shortcut commonly copies selected text?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ctrl+C copies the selection. Ctrl+V pastes, Ctrl+X cuts, and Ctrl+Z undoes the last action. On macOS, the equivalent for copy is Command+C.", "correctAnswer": "Ctrl+C"}, {"id": "q_1763139672296_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Phishing emails often try to trick users into revealing personal information.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Phishing is a social engineering technique where attackers impersonate trusted entities to get users to share passwords, credit card numbers, or other sensitive data.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2025-11-14 17:01:12.415992	\N
88bc673f-7ceb-4618-b3d4-9d6e180b30bb	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Humaniteis - Easy	Humaniteis	easy	[{"id": "q_1763140139294_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["William Shakespeare", "Geoffrey Chaucer", "Charles Dickens", "Jane Austen"], "question": "Who wrote the play \\"Romeo and Juliet\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "William Shakespeare, an English playwright of the Elizabethan era, wrote \\"Romeo and Juliet\\". It is one of his most famous tragedies, exploring themes of love, fate, and family conflict.", "correctAnswer": "William Shakespeare"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Rosetta Stone was crucial for deciphering Egyptian hieroglyphs.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Rosetta Stone contains the same text in Greek, Demotic, and hieroglyphic scripts. Because scholars could read Greek, they used it to unlock the meaning of hieroglyphs, a breakthrough led by Jean-François Champollion.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The ancient Greek philosopher known for the \\"Socratic method\\" is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Socrates is famous for a teaching method that uses probing questions to stimulate critical thinking and illuminate ideas, influencing later philosophers like Plato and Aristotle.", "correctAnswer": "Socrates"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Leonardo da Vinci", "Michelangelo", "Raphael", "Sandro Botticelli"], "question": "Who painted the Mona Lisa?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Leonardo da Vinci, a central figure of the Italian Renaissance, painted the Mona Lisa. The work is renowned for its subtle use of sfumato and the subject’s enigmatic expression.", "correctAnswer": "Leonardo da Vinci"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the term for a traditional story that explains natural phenomena or cultural origins, often involving gods or heroes?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A myth is a traditional narrative found in many cultures. Myths often explain the origins of the world, human customs, or natural events and are part of the broader study of mythology in the humanities.", "correctAnswer": "myth"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "In literature, a protagonist is the main character of a story.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The protagonist is the central figure around whom the story’s main events revolve. They face challenges and drive the narrative forward, often contrasted with the antagonist.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The period in European history known for a \\"rebirth\\" of arts and learning is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Renaissance, beginning in the 14th century, was a cultural movement emphasizing the revival of classical learning, humanism, and advances in art, science, and literature.", "correctAnswer": "Renaissance"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Spanish", "Portuguese", "French", "Italian"], "question": "Which language is primarily spoken in Brazil?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Brazil was colonized by Portugal, making Portuguese the official and most widely spoken language in the country, unlike most of Latin America where Spanish predominates.", "correctAnswer": "Portuguese"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "7", "8", "12"], "question": "In Western music theory, how many distinct notes are in a standard major scale?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A major scale consists of seven distinct pitch classes (do, re, mi, fa, sol, la, ti). The octave repeats the first note, so while you may count eight including the octave, there are seven unique notes.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1763140139294_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Ethics is the branch of philosophy that studies moral principles and how we should act.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ethics examines concepts like right and wrong, virtue, duty, and justice. It provides frameworks for evaluating human behavior and moral decision-making.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2025-11-14 17:08:59.398392	\N
bd3715a2-adb1-4318-8c48-a9f864738f47	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	EMS - Easy	EMS	easy	[{"id": "q_1763140255758_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Airway", "Breathing", "Circulation", "Disability"], "question": "In the primary survey (ABCDE) used by EMS, what is assessed first?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The ABCDE approach prioritizes life threats in order: Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability (neurologic), and Exposure. Airway comes first because without a patent airway, oxygen cannot reach the lungs, making all other interventions ineffective.", "correctAnswer": "Airway"}, {"id": "q_1763140255758_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Placing an unresponsive, breathing patient with no suspected spinal trauma in the recovery position helps maintain an open airway.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The recovery position (on the side) helps keep the tongue from obstructing the airway and allows vomit or secretions to drain, reducing the risk of aspiration, provided there is no concern for spine injury.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763140255758_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the SAMPLE history mnemonic used by EMS, the letter \\"A\\" stands for ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SAMPLE stands for Signs/Symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past medical history, Last oral intake, and Events leading up to the illness/injury. Asking about allergies helps prevent harmful exposures to medications or environmental triggers.", "correctAnswer": "Allergies"}, {"id": "q_1763140255758_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name two standard PPE items EMS providers use to reduce exposure to bloodborne pathogens.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Standard precautions include wearing gloves and eye protection (such as goggles or a face shield) to prevent contact with blood and body fluids. Other PPE can include masks and gowns, depending on the situation.", "correctAnswer": "Gloves and eye protection"}, {"id": "q_1763140255758_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["60–80 per minute", "80–100 per minute", "100–120 per minute", "140–160 per minute"], "question": "For adult CPR, what is the recommended rate of chest compressions?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "High-quality adult CPR targets a compression rate of 100–120 per minute to optimize blood flow. Rescuers should also push hard and fast at an appropriate depth, allow full chest recoil, and minimize interruptions.", "correctAnswer": "100–120 per minute"}]	none	2025-11-14 17:10:55.847921	\N
b575ebd7-465f-4352-b1f9-003dd2b529f0	683ec62f-a3d7-43e2-8709-8968da12fd5d	Math - Expert	Math	expert	[{"id": "q_1763181166685_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["f is constant", "f is a polynomial of degree at most 1", "f is a polynomial of degree at most 2", "f has infinitely many zeros unless it is constant"], "question": "Let f be an entire function satisfying |f(z)| ≤ A|z|^2 + B for all z ∈ C and some constants A, B ≥ 0. Which statement must be true?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Cauchy estimates imply that if |f(z)| ≤ C R^2 on |z| = R, then |f^{(k)}(0)| ≤ k! C R^{2-k}. Letting R → ∞ shows f^{(k)}(0) = 0 for all k ≥ 3, so f is a polynomial of degree at most 2. This is a standard consequence of Liouville-type arguments and Cauchy’s integral formula for derivatives.", "correctAnswer": "f is a polynomial of degree at most 2"}, {"id": "q_1763181166685_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If a finite group G acts transitively on a finite set X, then for any x ∈ X the stabilizer G_x is a normal subgroup of G.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "In a transitive action, stabilizers are conjugate but not necessarily normal. Counterexample: S3 acting on {1,2,3} by permutation is transitive, but the stabilizer of 1 is {e, (23)}, which is not normal in S3 (it is not invariant under conjugation by, e.g., (12)).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763181166685_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Let (X, d) be a complete metric space and T: X → X a contraction with Lipschitz constant L ∈ (0,1). For the Picard iterates x_{n+1} = T(x_n) starting from x_0, the unique fixed point x* satisfies the a priori bound d(x_n, x*) ≤ ____.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "By the contraction property, d(x_{k+1}, x_k) ≤ L^k d(x_1, x_0). Summing the telescoping series from k = n to ∞ gives d(x_n, x*) ≤ Σ_{k=n}^∞ d(x_{k+1}, x_k) ≤ (Σ_{k=n}^∞ L^k) d(x_1, x_0) = (L^n / (1 - L)) d(x_1, x_0).", "correctAnswer": "(L^n / (1 - L)) d(x_1, x_0)"}, {"id": "q_1763181166685_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the natural density of squarefree positive integers?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "An integer is squarefree iff it is not divisible by p^2 for any prime p. Assuming independence heuristics justified by inclusion–exclusion with the Möbius function, the density equals ∏_p (1 − 1/p^2) = 1/ζ(2) = 6/π^2.", "correctAnswer": "6/π^2"}, {"id": "q_1763181166685_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Determine the Galois group over Q of the polynomial x^4 − 2.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "x^4 − 2 is irreducible over Q by Eisenstein at p = 2, so [Q(2^{1/4}):Q] = 4. The splitting field is Q(2^{1/4}, i), since the roots are 2^{1/4}ζ where ζ is a 4th root of unity. As i ∉ Q(2^{1/4}) (that field is real), adjoining i doubles the degree to 8. Thus the Galois group has order 8 and is generated by sending 2^{1/4} → i·2^{1/4} (a 4-cycle on the roots) and complex conjugation (reflection), yielding the semidirect product C4 ⋊ C2 ≅ D4.", "correctAnswer": "Dihedral group of order 8 (D4)"}]	none	2025-11-15 04:32:46.699394	\N
6ce27232-2728-4d6c-8a5d-f6dd8100a9c0	683ec62f-a3d7-43e2-8709-8968da12fd5d	Maths - Medium	Maths	medium	[{"id": "q_1763181239982_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(2, 5)", "(-∞, 2) ∪ (5, ∞)", "(-∞, 5)", "(2, ∞)"], "question": "Solve the inequality (x + 1)/(x - 2) > 2. Which interval represents the solution set?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Bring all terms to one side: (x+1)/(x-2) - 2 > 0 ⇒ ((x+1) - 2(x-2))/(x-2) > 0 ⇒ (-x + 5)/(x - 2) > 0. Critical points are x = 2 (undefined) and x = 5 (zero). Test intervals: (-∞,2): negative; (2,5): positive; (5,∞): negative. Since we need > 0 and x ≠ 2, the solution is (2, 5).", "correctAnswer": "(2, 5)"}, {"id": "q_1763181239982_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "10", "12", "14"], "question": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices A(1,2), B(4,6), and C(7,2).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the coordinate area formula: Area = 1/2 * |x1(y2−y3) + x2(y3−y1) + x3(y1−y2)|. Substituting: 1/2 * |1(6−2) + 4(2−2) + 7(2−6)| = 1/2 * |4 + 0 − 28| = 1/2 * 24 = 12 square units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763181239982_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "If a square matrix has determinant 0, its columns are linearly independent.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A determinant of 0 means the matrix is singular and does not have full rank. This implies its columns are linearly dependent, not independent. A nonzero determinant is required for column independence in a square matrix.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763181239982_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for x: log_3(x − 1) + log_3(x − 4) = 2. Enter the exact value.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine logs: log_3[(x−1)(x−4)] = 2 ⇒ (x−1)(x−4) = 3^2 = 9. Expand: x^2 − 5x + 4 = 9 ⇒ x^2 − 5x − 5 = 0. Solve: x = [5 ± sqrt(25 + 20)]/2 = (5 ± 3*sqrt(5))/2. Domain requires x > 4, so the valid solution is (5 + 3*sqrt(5))/2.", "correctAnswer": "(5 + 3*sqrt(5))/2"}, {"id": "q_1763181239982_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A fair six-sided die is rolled three times. What is the probability that the sum of the three rolls is 10?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Count solutions to a+b+c=10 with 1≤a,b,c≤6. First count positive solutions: transform to nonnegatives with a'=a−1, etc., giving a'+b'+c'=7 ⇒ C(9,2)=36. Subtract cases where a≥7 (or b or c): each contributes 3 solutions, and overlaps are impossible, so subtract 9. Valid outcomes = 36−9=27. Total outcomes = 6^3=216, so probability = 27/216 = 1/8.", "correctAnswer": "1/8"}]	none	2025-11-15 04:34:00.08764	\N
0f6fc78b-01f9-4880-a1bf-3262cf2ce27b	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1763181884509_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The cat", "sleeps", "on the mat", "the mat"], "question": "In the sentence \\"The cat sleeps on the mat,\\" what is the subject?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The subject is the person, place, thing, or idea that performs the action. Here, \\"The cat\\" is performing the action of sleeping.", "correctAnswer": "The cat"}, {"id": "q_1763181884509_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The first word in a sentence should always begin with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "English sentences start with a capital letter to signal the beginning of a new sentence, which improves readability and follows standard conventions.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763181884509_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct verb form: \\"She ___ to school every day.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With third-person singular subjects (he, she, it), present tense verbs usually take an -s: \\"She goes\\" is correct, not \\"go.\\"", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1763181884509_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed to the base form. \\"Walk\\" becomes \\"walked.\\"", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1763181884509_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["joyful", "angry", "tired", "sad"], "question": "Which word is a synonym for \\"happy\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. \\"Joyful\\" means the same or very close in meaning to \\"happy,\\" while the others do not.", "correctAnswer": "joyful"}]	none	2025-11-15 04:44:44.603282	\N
c3b6ee5f-6497-4c98-bd39-b095aa771823	5bc34fa2-61ce-4d62-9340-f2ff7985216b	The circulatory system  - Easy	The circulatory system 	easy	[{"id": "q_1763182386801_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Left atrium", "Left ventricle", "Right atrium", "Right ventricle"], "question": "Which heart chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the rest of the body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The left ventricle has thick muscular walls and pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, which distributes it to the body. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs, and the right side of the heart handles deoxygenated blood.", "correctAnswer": "Left ventricle"}, {"id": "q_1763182386801_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Arteries always carry oxygenated blood.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "While most arteries carry oxygenated blood, the pulmonary arteries are an important exception: they carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. Similarly, pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood back to the heart.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763182386801_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The liquid part of blood that transports nutrients, hormones, and wastes is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plasma is the straw-colored fluid portion of blood. It is mostly water and serves as the transport medium for cells, nutrients, hormones, carbon dioxide, and waste products.", "correctAnswer": "plasma"}, {"id": "q_1763182386801_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What are the tiny blood vessels where exchange of oxygen, carbon dioxide, and nutrients occurs between blood and tissues?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Capillaries are the smallest blood vessels with thin, one-cell-thick walls that allow diffusion of gases and nutrients to and from surrounding tissues.", "correctAnswer": "Capillaries"}, {"id": "q_1763182386801_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Fighting infection", "Clotting blood", "Carrying oxygen", "Producing hormones"], "question": "What is the primary function of red blood cells?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, a protein that binds oxygen in the lungs and releases it to tissues. White blood cells fight infection, and platelets help with blood clotting.", "correctAnswer": "Carrying oxygen"}]	none	2025-11-15 04:53:06.910336	\N
592895fe-a32f-4795-8ff8-683b6d4e202f	5bc34fa2-61ce-4d62-9340-f2ff7985216b	Heartbeat and pulse  - Easy	Heartbeat and pulse 	easy	[{"id": "q_1763182702027_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["40–60 beats per minute", "60–100 beats per minute", "100–140 beats per minute", "140–180 beats per minute"], "question": "What is the typical resting heart rate range for a healthy adult?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "For most healthy adults, a normal resting heart rate is between 60 and 100 beats per minute (bpm). Below 60 bpm may be bradycardia (often normal in trained athletes), and above 100 bpm is tachycardia.", "correctAnswer": "60–100 beats per minute"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Thumb", "Index finger", "Middle finger", "Ring finger"], "question": "Which finger should you avoid using when checking someone’s pulse?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The thumb has its own strong pulse, which can be mistaken for the other person’s pulse. Use the index and middle fingers to feel for a pulse.", "correctAnswer": "Thumb"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It increases", "It decreases", "It stays exactly the same", "It stops temporarily"], "question": "During moderate exercise, what usually happens to heart rate?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Exercise raises the body’s demand for oxygen and nutrients. The heart beats faster to pump more blood to muscles, so heart rate increases.", "correctAnswer": "It increases"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Stethoscope", "Thermometer", "Otoscope", "Sphygmomanometer"], "question": "Which instrument is commonly used to listen directly to heart sounds?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A stethoscope is used to auscultate (listen to) heart and lung sounds. A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure, a thermometer measures temperature, and an otoscope examines the ear.", "correctAnswer": "Stethoscope"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "A pulse is the rhythmic expansion of an artery caused by the heart’s contraction.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When the heart contracts (systole), it pushes blood into the arteries, causing them to expand. This expansion is felt as the pulse.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Bradycardia refers to a heart rate faster than 100 beats per minute.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bradycardia means a slower-than-normal heart rate, typically under 60 bpm in adults. A rate over 100 bpm is called tachycardia.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Heart rate is commonly expressed as ____ per minute (bpm).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Heart rate is measured in beats per minute (bpm). The term bpm literally means the number of heartbeats in one minute.", "correctAnswer": "beats"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The pulse felt at the side of the neck is called the ____ pulse.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The carotid arteries run along the sides of the neck. The carotid pulse is strong and is often checked in emergencies.", "correctAnswer": "carotid"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How do you convert the number of beats counted in 15 seconds into beats per minute (BPM)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Since there are 60 seconds in a minute, you multiply the 15-second count by 4 (60 ÷ 15 = 4) to get the BPM.", "correctAnswer": "Multiply by 4"}, {"id": "q_1763182702027_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the heartbeat?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heartbeat generates pressure to circulate oxygen-rich blood to tissues and return deoxygenated blood to the lungs, sustaining life.", "correctAnswer": "To pump blood throughout the body"}]	none	2025-11-15 04:58:22.11164	\N
4779bc4f-bcda-47bd-90e2-ef82e228efb0	0ea7cc83-2df0-4305-9d2b-f2ed029ae064	Maths, temperature and mass - Easy	Maths, temperature and mass	easy	[{"id": "q_1763184379256_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. You can think of it as starting at 7 and counting 5 more steps: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763184379256_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Water freezes at 0 degrees Celsius at standard atmospheric pressure.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "On the Celsius scale, 0°C is defined as the freezing point of pure water at 1 atmosphere of pressure. This is a key reference point in temperature measurement.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184379256_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "1 kilogram equals ____ grams.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the metric system, the prefix 'kilo' means 1000. Therefore, 1 kilogram (kg) is 1000 grams (g).", "correctAnswer": "1000"}, {"id": "q_1763184379256_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the boiling point of water at sea level in degrees Celsius?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At sea level (1 atmosphere of pressure), pure water boils at 100°C. This is the upper reference point in the traditional Celsius scale.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1763184379256_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["500 g", "750 g", "1 kg", "100 g"], "question": "Which has the greatest mass?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "1 kilogram equals 1000 grams, which is more than 750 g, 500 g, or 100 g. Therefore, 1 kg has the greatest mass among the options.", "correctAnswer": "1 kg"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:26:19.346419	\N
6c70fb07-0e5c-4f6e-9c84-515e145bb105	5f02535e-668b-49d8-a638-19cf89db155f	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1763184480615_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "12", "15", "21"], "question": "Which number is even?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2. 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so 12 is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "16"], "question": "What is the value of 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition. 3 × 4 = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "1/4", "3/4", "2/3"], "question": "Which fraction equals 0.5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 means one half. 2/4 simplifies to 1/2, which equals 0.5.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Square", "Triangle", "Rectangle", "Circle"], "question": "Which shape has 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides; squares and rectangles have 4, and circles have no sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["ones", "tens", "hundreds", "thousands"], "question": "What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 352?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 352, 3 is hundreds, 5 is tens, and 2 is ones. So 5 is in the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "question": "What is 15 − 9?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 9 from 15 leaves 6 because 15 − 9 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7 cm", "10 cm", "12 cm", "14 cm"], "question": "The perimeter of a rectangle with sides 2 cm and 5 cm is:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). 2 × (5 + 2) = 14 cm.", "correctAnswer": "14 cm"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an odd number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Odd numbers are not divisible by 2. Zero is divisible by 2, so it is even.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "8 ÷ 2 = 4.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division is splitting into equal groups. 8 divided by 2 equals 4.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square is a rectangle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has four right angles. A square has four right angles and equal sides, so it is a special rectangle.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of any number and zero is the same number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Zero is the additive identity. Adding 0 does not change the value: n + 0 = n.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "6 + ____ = 10", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To reach 10 from 6, add 4 because 6 + 4 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The decimal 0.25 equals ____ as a fraction in simplest form.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.25 = 25/100, and dividing numerator and denominator by 25 gives 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "1/4"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "____ is the smallest prime number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A prime number has exactly two distinct positive divisors: 1 and itself. 2 is the first and only even prime.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 × ____ = 27", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "27 divided by 9 equals 3, so 9 × 3 = 27.", "correctAnswer": "3"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of 5^2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Exponent 2 means squaring: 5 × 5 = 25.", "correctAnswer": "25"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the next number in the sequence: 2, 4, 6, 8, __.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the sequence of even numbers, increasing by 2 each time. 8 + 2 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the area of a rectangle with length 4 units and width 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width: 4 × 3 = 12 square units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763184480615_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "If you have 3/4 of a pizza and you eat 1/4, how much is left?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract fractions with the same denominator: 3/4 − 1/4 = 2/4, which simplifies to 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:28:00.716667	\N
0e37e70e-5ba8-49c4-b07c-4a6b48a391e8	5f02535e-668b-49d8-a638-19cf89db155f	science - Easy	science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763184926608_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0°C", "50°C", "100°C", "212°C"], "question": "What is the boiling point of water at sea level in degrees Celsius?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure (sea level), pure water boils at 100°C. This is a key reference point in the Celsius temperature scale.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Lungs", "Liver", "Heart", "Stomach"], "question": "Which organ pumps blood throughout the human body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that contracts to pump blood through the circulatory system, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues.", "correctAnswer": "Heart"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The Moon", "Polaris", "Mars", "The Sun"], "question": "The Earth revolves around which star?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Earth orbits the Sun, which is the star at the center of our solar system. This yearly revolution causes the seasons.", "correctAnswer": "The Sun"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a definite shape and a definite volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids have particles packed closely in fixed positions, giving them a definite shape and volume. Liquids have definite volume but change shape, and gases have neither definite shape nor volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Helium"], "question": "Which gas do plants take in during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plants use carbon dioxide, water, and sunlight to produce glucose and oxygen during photosynthesis.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Liter", "Meter", "Second", "Kilogram"], "question": "Which unit is commonly used to measure mass in the metric system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The kilogram (kg) is the SI base unit for mass. Liters measure volume, meters measure length, and seconds measure time.", "correctAnswer": "Kilogram"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Moon produces its own light.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Moon does not emit light; it reflects sunlight. This reflected light makes the Moon visible from Earth.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster in water than in air.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound moves faster in denser media. Water's particles are closer together than air's, allowing sound waves to travel more quickly.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Adult humans have 206 bones.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An adult human skeleton typically contains 206 bones. Babies have more bones that fuse together as they grow.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "An element is a substance made of only one type of atom.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Elements are pure substances consisting of a single kind of atom, such as carbon or oxygen. Compounds contain two or more different atoms bonded together.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "The equator is an imaginary line that divides Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The equator encircles the Earth halfway between the poles, separating the planet into Northern and Southern Hemispheres.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which liquid water turns into gas is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation occurs when molecules at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to become water vapor. It is part of the water cycle.", "correctAnswer": "Evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The center of an atom is called the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The nucleus contains protons and neutrons and accounts for most of an atom’s mass.", "correctAnswer": "Nucleus"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The force that pulls objects toward Earth is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. It keeps us grounded and governs the motion of planets and stars.", "correctAnswer": "Gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest planet in our solar system is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Jupiter is the largest planet, a gas giant with a mass more than twice that of all the other planets combined.", "correctAnswer": "Jupiter"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic unit of life is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cells are the smallest structural and functional units of living organisms. All living things are made of one or more cells.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature, often using liquid expansion (like mercury or alcohol) or digital sensors to indicate heat levels.", "correctAnswer": "Thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the three primary states of matter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Matter commonly exists as solid (fixed shape), liquid (fixed volume but variable shape), and gas (no fixed shape or volume).", "correctAnswer": "Solid, liquid, gas"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What vitamin does the body produce when the skin is exposed to sunlight?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sunlight helps the skin synthesize vitamin D, which is important for bone health and calcium absorption.", "correctAnswer": "Vitamin D"}, {"id": "q_1763184926608_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call the change of state from solid to liquid?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Melting occurs when a solid absorbs heat and its particles gain enough energy to move into the liquid state.", "correctAnswer": "Melting"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:35:26.711455	\N
6100dc67-5792-444c-b6dd-715761579955	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1763184997606_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The", "cat", "sleeps", "sleep"], "question": "In the sentence \\"The cat sleeps,\\" which word is a noun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, or thing. \\"Cat\\" is a thing. \\"The\\" is an article and \\"sleeps\\" is a verb showing action.", "correctAnswer": "cat"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A verb shows an action or a state of being.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Verbs tell what someone or something does (action) or is/feels (state), such as \\"run,\\" \\"eat,\\" or \\"is.\\"", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "She ate ___ apple.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"an\\" before words that start with a vowel sound. \\"Apple\\" begins with the vowel sound /a/.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed: walk → walked.", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["go", "goes", "going", "gone"], "question": "Choose the correct form: He ___ to school every day.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With third-person singular (he/she/it) in the present simple, add -s: \\"he goes.\\" \\"Go\\" is the base form, \\"going\\" is a gerund/participle, and \\"gone\\" is a past participle.", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Proper nouns are always capitalized.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Proper nouns name specific people, places, or things (e.g., Maria, London) and begin with capital letters.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "I would like to ___ a letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Write\\" means to form words on paper. \\"Right\\" means correct or the opposite of left; it would not fit here.", "correctAnswer": "write"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What punctuation mark is used at the end of a question?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Questions end with a question mark (?).", "correctAnswer": "?"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["blue", "car", "is", "fast"], "question": "In \\"The blue car is fast,\\" which word directly modifies the noun \\"car\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An adjective modifies a noun. \\"Blue\\" directly describes the noun \\"car.\\" \\"Fast\\" is a predicate adjective, but it follows the linking verb and does not directly modify the noun.", "correctAnswer": "blue"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sentence \\"They is happy\\" is grammatically correct.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The plural subject \\"They\\" needs the verb \\"are\\": \\"They are happy.\\"", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The book is ___ the table.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use the preposition \\"on\\" to show that something is resting atop a surface.", "correctAnswer": "on"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_11", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the plural of \\"baby\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nouns ending in -y after a consonant change y to i and add -es: baby → babies.", "correctAnswer": "babies"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "some"], "question": "Choose the correct article: I saw ___ elephant.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"an\\" before words starting with a vowel sound. \\"Elephant\\" begins with the vowel sound /e/.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "\\"You're\\" is the contraction of \\"you are.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"You're\\" = \\"you are.\\" \\"Your\\" is a possessive adjective (e.g., your book).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "This test is ___ than the last one.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The comparative form of \\"easy\\" is \\"easier\\" (change y to i and add -er).", "correctAnswer": "easier"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_15", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the subject in the sentence \\"Maria reads books\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The subject is who or what performs the action. Here, \\"Maria\\" does the reading.", "correctAnswer": "Maria"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_16", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["recieve", "receive", "receeve", "recevie"], "question": "Which is the correct spelling?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Receive\\" is spelled with \\"ei\\" after \\"c\\" (i before e except after c). The other options are misspellings.", "correctAnswer": "receive"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_17", "type": "true_false", "question": "A sentence must begin with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Standard English conventions require sentences to start with a capital letter.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_18", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "___ am learning English.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use the first-person singular pronoun \\"I\\" with \\"am\\": \\"I am.\\"", "correctAnswer": "I"}, {"id": "q_1763184997606_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give one synonym for \\"happy.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Synonyms have similar meanings. \\"Glad\\" is a common synonym for \\"happy.\\" Others include \\"joyful\\" and \\"cheerful.\\"", "correctAnswer": "glad"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:36:37.707406	\N
6f3ac9e0-5e6a-412e-b780-69a88c7d1a69	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Setswana - Easy	Setswana	easy	[{"id": "q_1763185351244_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Dumelang", "Dumela", "Ke a leboga", "Sala sentle"], "question": "Which Setswana greeting is used to say 'Hello' to more than one person?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dumelang is the plural or polite greeting meaning 'Hello' to a group. Dumela is used to one person, Ke a leboga means 'Thank you', and Sala sentle is a goodbye to someone staying behind.", "correctAnswer": "Dumelang"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ke a leboga", "O kae?", "Ke teng", "Ntshwarele"], "question": "How do you say 'Thank you' in Setswana?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ke a leboga means 'Thank you'. O kae? means 'How are you? (to one person)', Ke teng means 'I am here/fine', and Ntshwarele means 'Forgive me/Sorry'.", "correctAnswer": "Ke a leboga"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["How are you? (to a group)", "Where are you?", "What is your name?", "How old are you?"], "question": "'Le kae?' translates to which of the following?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Le kae? is the plural/polite form of asking 'How are you?' addressed to more than one person. The singular form is O kae?", "correctAnswer": "How are you? (to a group)"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ke teng.", "Ke a leboga.", "Tsamaya sentle.", "Ga ke itse."], "question": "What is an appropriate reply to 'O kae?' (How are you?)", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ke teng literally means 'I am here' and is a common, polite way to say 'I am fine/present' in response to O kae?. The other options do not directly answer how you are.", "correctAnswer": "Ke teng."}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "In Setswana, 'Ke' means 'I'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ke is the first-person singular pronoun in Setswana, equivalent to 'I'. For example, 'Ke nna' means 'It is me/I am'.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "'Tsamaya sentle' is said to someone who is staying behind.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tsamaya sentle means 'Go well' and is said to someone who is leaving. 'Sala sentle' means 'Stay well' and is said to someone who remains.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the farewell said to someone staying: '____ sentle'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sala sentle means 'Stay well' and is used when you are leaving and the other person is staying. The counterpart for a person leaving is 'Tsamaya sentle'.", "correctAnswer": "Sala"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Write the Setswana word for the number two: ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pedi is the basic cardinal number for 'two' in Setswana. Other common numbers include 'ngoe' (one) and 'tharo' (three), though forms can vary with noun classes.", "correctAnswer": "pedi"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the Setswana word for 'water'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metsi means 'water' in Setswana. For example, 'Ke batla metsi' means 'I want water'.", "correctAnswer": "Metsi"}, {"id": "q_1763185351244_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How do you say 'Go well' (goodbye to someone who is leaving) in Setswana?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tsamaya sentle is used to wish someone who is leaving a good journey or farewell. The reply for someone staying is 'Sala sentle'.", "correctAnswer": "Tsamaya sentle"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:42:31.366744	\N
5582c9fa-f92b-4db0-868e-086893a0f44a	5f02535e-668b-49d8-a638-19cf89db155f	english - Easy	english	easy	[{"id": "q_1763185354181_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["book", "quickly", "sing", "bright"], "question": "Which word is a noun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. 'Book' is a thing, so it's a noun. 'Quickly' is an adverb, 'sing' is a verb, and 'bright' is an adjective.", "correctAnswer": "book"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article to complete the sentence: \\"I ate ___ apple.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'an' before words that start with a vowel sound. 'Apple' starts with the vowel sound /a/, so 'an apple' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["go", "goes", "going", "gone"], "question": "She ___ to the park on Sundays.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present tense, add -s to the verb with third-person singular subjects (he, she, it). Therefore, 'She goes' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["quiet", "sing", "quickly", "tomorrow"], "question": "Which word is an adjective?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adjectives describe nouns (people, places, or things). 'Quiet' describes a noun (a quiet room). 'Sing' is a verb, 'quickly' is an adverb, and 'tomorrow' is a noun/adverb.", "correctAnswer": "quiet"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["boxs", "boxes", "boxies", "box"], "question": "What is the plural of 'box'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nouns ending in -x typically form the plural by adding -es, so 'box' becomes 'boxes'.", "correctAnswer": "boxes"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["period", "exclamation mark", "question mark", "comma"], "question": "Which punctuation mark usually ends a question?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Questions in English typically end with a question mark (?). Periods end statements, exclamation marks show strong feeling, and commas separate parts within a sentence.", "correctAnswer": "question mark"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["on", "quickly", "happy", "running"], "question": "The cat is ___ the table.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Prepositions show location or relationship. 'On' correctly indicates position: 'The cat is on the table.' The other options are not prepositions.", "correctAnswer": "on"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "The word 'they' is a pronoun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'They' replaces a noun (like 'the students') and is used to refer to people or things, so it is a pronoun.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "'I goes to school' is grammatically correct.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With 'I' in the simple present, the base form of the verb is used: 'I go to school,' not 'I goes.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Capital letters are used at the beginning of sentences.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In English, the first word of a sentence starts with a capital letter. Proper nouns are also capitalized.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "In English, verbs never change with time.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Verbs change form to show tense (time), such as 'walk' (present) and 'walked' (past).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The opposite of 'hot' is 'cold'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Opposite words have contrasting meanings; 'cold' is the antonym of 'hot'.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "I ___ my homework yesterday.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use the simple past tense to talk about completed actions in the past. The past tense of 'do' is 'did'.", "correctAnswer": "did"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "There ___ a book on the table.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'is' with singular nouns: 'There is a book.' Use 'are' with plural nouns.", "correctAnswer": "is"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "He is taller ___ his brother.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Than' is used to make comparisons: 'taller than'.", "correctAnswer": "than"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "We ___ English every day.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present tense, use the base form with 'we/you/they': 'We study.'", "correctAnswer": "study"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of 'go'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The verb 'go' has an irregular past tense: 'went', not 'goed'.", "correctAnswer": "went"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the five main vowel letters in English.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The primary vowel letters are A, E, I, O, U. The letter Y sometimes functions as a vowel, but it is not one of the main five.", "correctAnswer": "A, E, I, O, U"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give a synonym for 'happy' that is commonly used.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. 'Glad' is a common synonym for 'happy'.", "correctAnswer": "glad"}, {"id": "q_1763185354181_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the plural of 'child'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Some nouns have irregular plurals. 'Child' becomes 'children', not 'childs'.", "correctAnswer": "children"}]	none	2025-11-15 05:42:34.193245	\N
e48e6bec-4cc1-46df-99c3-6430eec636f4	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1763186824625_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["$1.50", "$2.00", "$2.25", "$2.50"], "question": "At a store, 3 notebooks and 2 pens cost $8.50, while 5 notebooks and 3 pens cost $13.50. What is the price of a pen?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let n be the price of a notebook and p be the price of a pen. The system is: 3n + 2p = 8.50 and 5n + 3p = 13.50. Eliminate p: multiply the first equation by 3 (9n + 6p = 25.50) and the second by 2 (10n + 6p = 27.00). Subtract to get n = 1.50. Substitute back: 3(1.50) + 2p = 8.50 ⇒ 4.50 + 2p = 8.50 ⇒ 2p = 4.00 ⇒ p = 2.00.", "correctAnswer": "$2.00"}, {"id": "q_1763186824625_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The function g(x) = √(x − 2) is defined for all real numbers x.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Square roots of real numbers require the radicand to be nonnegative. For g(x) = √(x − 2), we must have x − 2 ≥ 0, so x ≥ 2. The domain is [2, ∞), not all real numbers.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1763186824625_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The slope of the line perpendicular to 3x − 4y = 8 is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite 3x − 4y = 8 as y = (3/4)x − 2, so the slope of the given line is 3/4. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals, so the perpendicular slope is −4/3.", "correctAnswer": "-4/3"}, {"id": "q_1763186824625_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls equals 8?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "There are 36 equally likely outcomes for two dice. The outcomes that sum to 8 are (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), and (6,2): 5 favorable outcomes. Thus, the probability is 5/36.", "correctAnswer": "5/36"}, {"id": "q_1763186824625_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1 < x < 4", "x < 4", "-4 < x < 1", "1 ≤ x ≤ 4"], "question": "Solve the inequality |2x − 5| < 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For |A| < b, we have −b < A < b. So −3 < 2x − 5 < 3. Add 5: 2 < 2x < 8. Divide by 2: 1 < x < 4. Note the strict inequality excludes the endpoints; interval notation is (1, 4).", "correctAnswer": "1 < x < 4"}]	none	2025-11-15 06:07:04.711079	\N
c3777380-b4be-475c-b82a-28d7f6a8ba13	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1763187445822_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0°C", "50°C", "100°C", "212°C"], "question": "At sea level, what is the boiling point of water in degrees Celsius?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure (sea level). 0°C is the freezing point, and 212°F is the boiling point on the Fahrenheit scale.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Earth revolves around the Sun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heliocentric model shows that Earth orbits the Sun once every year, which explains seasons and the length of a year.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which plants make food using sunlight is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is how plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose (food) and oxygen.", "correctAnswer": "photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the force that pulls objects toward Earth.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses. It keeps us on the ground and causes objects to fall.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do humans need for respiration?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Humans use oxygen in cellular respiration to release energy from food. Carbon dioxide is a waste product, and nitrogen is mostly inert in human lungs.", "correctAnswer": "Oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Water expands when it freezes due to its molecular structure, making ice less dense than liquid water so it floats.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Roots", "Stem", "Leaves", "Flowers"], "question": "Which part of a plant absorbs water from the soil?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and minerals from the soil, which are transported through the stem to the leaves.", "correctAnswer": "Roots"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The smallest unit of life is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cells are the basic structural and functional units of all living organisms.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature, often using liquid expansion, electronic sensors, or infrared detection.", "correctAnswer": "thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Earth's shadow", "The Moon's rotation", "The Moon's orbit around Earth", "Changes in the Moon's size"], "question": "What causes the phases of the Moon?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "As the Moon orbits Earth, we see different portions of its sunlit side, creating phases. Earth's shadow causes eclipses, not regular phases.", "correctAnswer": "The Moon's orbit around Earth"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Metals generally conduct electricity well.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Metals have free-moving electrons that allow electric current to pass through easily, making them good conductors.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_11", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mars", "Venus", "Jupiter", "Mercury"], "question": "Which planet is known as the Red Planet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mars appears reddish due to iron oxide (rust) on its surface, earning it the nickname the Red Planet.", "correctAnswer": "Mars"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a fixed shape and volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids have a fixed shape and volume. Liquids have a fixed volume but take the shape of their container, and gases have neither fixed shape nor volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_13", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Coal", "Oil", "Wind", "Natural gas"], "question": "Which of these is a renewable energy source?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wind is renewable because it is naturally replenished. Coal, oil, and natural gas are fossil fuels and nonrenewable.", "correctAnswer": "Wind"}, {"id": "q_1763187445822_14", "type": "true_false", "question": "The heart is part of the digestive system.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is part of the circulatory system, pumping blood around the body. The digestive system includes organs like the stomach and intestines.", "correctAnswer": "False"}]	none	2025-11-15 06:17:25.90879	\N
1d520344-74b8-4e43-918f-a8edc9fb00b8	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	math - Easy	math	easy	[{"id": "q_1763189469696_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. You can count on from 7: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square has four equal sides and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "15 - __ = 9", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "To find the missing number, subtract 9 from 15: 15 − 9 = 6. Check: 15 − 6 = 9.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 3 groups of 4 is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "15", "21"], "question": "Which of the following numbers is even?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8. The number 12 ends in 2, so it is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "50", "500", "55"], "question": "In the number 354, what is the value of the digit 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 5 is in the tens place, so its value is 5 tens, which is 50.", "correctAnswer": "50"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of any number and zero is the same number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Zero is the additive identity, meaning adding 0 does not change a number.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "1/2 + 1/2 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Two halves make a whole: 1/2 + 1/2 = 1.", "correctAnswer": "1"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How many degrees are in a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle measures exactly 90 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1763189469696_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "15", "16", "30"], "question": "A rectangle has length 5 units and width 3 units. What is its perimeter?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter is the distance around: 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (5 + 3) = 16.", "correctAnswer": "16"}]	none	2025-11-15 06:51:09.812439	\N
cf1f6157-6264-4166-8755-19125fe4e6e6	5124dde0-a185-457c-aea0-6278d0117844	English - Medium	English	medium	[{"id": "q_1765308777733_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["If I was taller, I could reach the top shelf.", "If she were here, we would start the meeting.", "If they are ready, we will leave now.", "If he would be more careful, he wouldn’t make mistakes."], "question": "Which sentence correctly uses the subjunctive mood to express a hypothetical situation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The subjunctive mood uses 'were' for all persons to express hypothetical or contrary-to-fact situations. 'If she were here...' correctly signals a hypothetical condition. 'If I was...' is common in speech but not standard subjunctive, 'If they are...' is a real condition, and 'If he would be...' is awkward and not the standard subjunctive construction.", "correctAnswer": "If she were here, we would start the meeting."}, {"id": "q_1765308777733_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In parallel structure, items in a list or series should maintain the same grammatical form to improve clarity and rhythm.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallelism requires matching grammatical forms across coordinated elements (e.g., 'running, jumping, and swimming' rather than 'running, to jump, and swimming'). Consistent structure makes sentences clearer and more balanced, aiding comprehension and style.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765308777733_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb form: \\"She is one of those writers who ___ always experimenting with form.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The relative pronoun 'who' refers to 'writers,' which is plural, so the verb in the relative clause should be plural: 'who are always experimenting.' The main verb 'is' agrees with 'She,' but within the clause, agreement follows the antecedent 'writers.'", "correctAnswer": "are"}, {"id": "q_1765308777733_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence in active voice: \\"The final draft was approved by the editor after two revisions.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Active voice places the subject before the verb, showing who performs the action. Here, 'The editor' is the agent performing 'approved.' The rest of the sentence remains intact to preserve meaning and sequence.", "correctAnswer": "The editor approved the final draft after two revisions."}, {"id": "q_1765308777733_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Antithesis", "Alliteration", "Anaphora", "Hyperbole"], "question": "Which rhetorical device is primarily illustrated by the sentence: \\"It was the best of times, it was the worst of times\\"?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Antithesis juxtaposes contrasting ideas in parallel structures, as seen with 'best' versus 'worst' in the same syntactic frame. While the repetition of 'It was' could be seen as anaphora, the primary device emphasized is the contrast, making antithesis the best answer.", "correctAnswer": "Antithesis"}]	none	2025-12-09 19:32:58.172044	\N
d0fa7f58-1501-4463-b68e-09d947e5d996	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	Science’ - Easy	Science’	easy	[{"id": "q_1765352375250_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North and South", "East and West", "Positive and Negative", "Top and Bottom"], "question": "What are the two poles of a magnet called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets have two ends called poles. These are named the north pole and the south pole.", "correctAnswer": "North and South"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Unlike poles attract each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unlike poles (north and south) pull toward each other, which is called attraction.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Like poles attract each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles (north–north or south–south) push away from each other; they repel.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["They attract", "They repel", "Nothing happens", "They lose magnetism"], "question": "What happens when a north pole is brought near a south pole?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A north pole and a south pole are unlike poles, and unlike poles attract (pull together).", "correctAnswer": "They attract"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["They repel", "They attract", "They stick permanently", "They rotate and stop"], "question": "What happens when two north poles are brought close together?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Two north poles are like poles, and like poles repel (push apart).", "correctAnswer": "They repel"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_5", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The force a magnet uses can be described with two words: ____ and ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnetic interactions are often described as pull (attraction) and push (repulsion).", "correctAnswer": "pull and push"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_6", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which type of poles repel: like poles or unlike poles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles (north–north or south–south) push away from each other; this is repulsion.", "correctAnswer": "Like poles"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which type of poles attract: like poles or unlike poles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unlike poles (north–south) pull toward each other; this is attraction.", "correctAnswer": "Unlike poles"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "A magnet has two different poles.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Every magnet has a north pole and a south pole.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Repel", "Attract", "Do nothing", "Change poles"], "question": "If a magnet’s south pole is near another magnet’s south pole, they will:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "South–south is a pair of like poles, which repel (push apart).", "correctAnswer": "Repel"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_10", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Another north pole", "A south pole", "Any metal object", "A wooden object"], "question": "The north pole of a magnet will be attracted to:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "North and south are unlike poles, and unlike poles attract.", "correctAnswer": "A south pole"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The two poles of a magnet are called the ____ pole and the ____ pole.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We name the ends of a magnet the north pole and the south pole.", "correctAnswer": "north and south"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "“Push” describes attraction between magnets.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Push refers to repulsion (like poles). Pull refers to attraction (unlike poles).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_13", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North–North", "North–South", "East–West", "Positive–Negative"], "question": "Which of the following is a pair of like poles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles are the same: north–north or south–south. North–north is a like pair.", "correctAnswer": "North–North"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Attraction", "Repulsion", "Friction", "Gravity"], "question": "Which word describes a magnet pulling objects together?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Attraction is the pulling force between unlike poles (north and south).", "correctAnswer": "Attraction"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_15", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What happens when like poles are brought together?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles (same kind) push away from each other due to magnetic repulsion.", "correctAnswer": "They repel."}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "An unlike pole pair (north–south) will ____ each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unlike poles pull together; this is attraction.", "correctAnswer": "attract"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_17", "type": "true_false", "question": "The words “pull” and “push” can be used to describe magnetic forces.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets can pull (attract) or push (repel) depending on the poles facing each other.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_18", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Like poles", "Unlike poles", "North and South", "It’s impossible to tell"], "question": "If you feel a magnet pushing another away, which poles are facing each other?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A pushing force is repulsion, which happens with like poles (north–north or south–south).", "correctAnswer": "Like poles"}, {"id": "q_1765352375250_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one pair of unlike poles.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unlike poles are different kinds, such as north and south, and they attract.", "correctAnswer": "North and South"}]	f4b206cc-6c81-46bb-81c4-6b712da2e760	2025-12-10 07:39:35.370656	\N
