04_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["They attract", "They repel", "Nothing happens", "They turn into south poles"], "question": "If you bring two north poles together, what happens?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Two north poles are like poles. Like poles repel, so they push apart.", "correctAnswer": "They repel"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["North–South", "North–North", "South–South", "North–East"], "question": "Which pair of poles will attract?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A north pole and a south pole are unlike poles, and unlike poles attract each other. North–East is not a standard magnetic pole pair.", "correctAnswer": "North–South"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Push and pull", "Heat and cool", "Rise and fall", "Grow and shrink"], "question": "A magnet’s force is best described by which two actions?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets can push (repel) and pull (attract) other magnets depending on the poles facing each other.", "correctAnswer": "Push and pull"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Like poles repel each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Like poles (north–north or south–south) push apart due to repulsion.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Unlike poles repel each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unlike poles (north and south) attract each other; they do not repel.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "A south pole attracts a north pole.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A south pole and a north pole are unlike poles, and unlike poles attract.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Magnets only pull; they never push.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets can both pull (attract) and push (repel) depending on the poles.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "North and north poles attract each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "North–north is a like-pole pair, and like poles repel rather than attract.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "South and south poles repel each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "South–south is a like-pole pair, and like poles repel.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A magnet can ____ and ____ on other magnets.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets can push (repel) and pull (attract). The order of the words does not change the meaning.", "correctAnswer": "push and pull"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Unlike poles ______ each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A north pole and a south pole are unlike poles, and they pull together (attract).", "correctAnswer": "attract"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Like poles ______ each other.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Two north poles or two south poles push apart (repel).", "correctAnswer": "repel"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the two poles of a magnet.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Magnets have two poles called north and south. These labels identify the two different magnetic ends.", "correctAnswer": "North and South"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What happens when you bring a north pole close to another magnet’s north pole?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "North–north is a like-pole pairing, and like poles push apart (repel).", "correctAnswer": "They repel"}, {"id": "q_1765390444304_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What happens when you bring a south pole close to a north pole?", "difficulty": "easy", 