08817f14-cef1-4f06-a498-95a17155491e	8819f08f-399e-492c-a63b-3c7111d09ac3	Maths - Easy	Maths	easy	[{"id": "q_1769786808078_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because you combine the two quantities: 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1769786808078_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2 with no remainder. Since 0 ÷ 2 = 0 with no remainder, 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1769786808078_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The perimeter of a rectangle with length 6 cm and width 4 cm is ____ cm.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is P = 2(l + w). Substituting l = 6 and w = 4: P = 2(6 + 4) = 2(10) = 20 cm.", "correctAnswer": "20"}, {"id": "q_1769786808078_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Simplify the expression: 3x + 2x.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Combine like terms by adding coefficients: 3x + 2x = (3 + 2)x = 5x.", "correctAnswer": "5x"}, {"id": "q_1769786808078_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["12", "3", "0", "6"], "question": "Evaluate 9 − 3 × 2 using the order of operations.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Follow PEMDAS: multiply before subtracting. First 3 × 2 = 6, then 9 − 6 = 3.", "correctAnswer": "3"}]	none	2026-01-30 15:26:48.187509	\N
b53ee1ba-d521-4874-a892-f618c2821031	9b9e86ad-e186-4bc8-b28f-1666bda02787	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1770096805618_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Higher frequency and shorter wavelength as it approaches, lower frequency and longer wavelength as it recedes", "Lower frequency and longer wavelength as it approaches, higher frequency and shorter wavelength as it recedes", "Increased amplitude only, frequency unchanged", "Speed of sound in air increases when the source moves"], "question": "A siren on a moving ambulance sounds higher in pitch as it approaches and lower as it moves away. Which change in wave parameters best explains this observation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is the Doppler effect: motion toward the observer compresses wavefronts, increasing frequency (pitch) and decreasing wavelength; motion away stretches wavefronts, decreasing frequency and increasing wavelength. The speed of sound in a given medium is essentially constant for small source speeds, so perceived frequency changes rather than the wave speed.", "correctAnswer": "Higher frequency and shorter wavelength as it approaches, lower frequency and longer wavelength as it recedes"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The equilibrium shifts toward products (NH3)", "The equilibrium shifts toward reactants (N2 and H2)", "The equilibrium remains unchanged", "Temperature only affects reaction rate, not equilibrium position"], "question": "Consider the exothermic reaction at equilibrium: N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 + heat (Haber process). What happens to the equilibrium position if the temperature is decreased?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Le Châtelier's principle, lowering temperature favors the exothermic direction because the system counteracts the change by producing heat. For the Haber process, this means shifting toward NH3 formation. (Increasing pressure also favors products due to fewer moles of gas on the product side.)", "correctAnswer": "The equilibrium shifts toward products (NH3)"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Vmax decreases; Km remains the same", "Vmax remains the same; Km increases", "Vmax increases; Km decreases", "Both Vmax and Km decrease"], "question": "An enzyme is studied in the presence of a noncompetitive inhibitor. Which changes in kinetic parameters are expected?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Noncompetitive inhibitors reduce the number of active enzyme molecules effectively available (or their catalytic efficiency), lowering Vmax. They do not affect the substrate's binding affinity at the active site, so Km remains unchanged. Competitive inhibitors, by contrast, increase apparent Km but do not change Vmax.", "correctAnswer": "Vmax decreases; Km remains the same"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Frequent shallow earthquakes with little to no volcanism", "Formation of deep ocean trenches and strong volcanism", "Creation of new crust at mid-ocean ridges", "Mountain building from continental collision with widespread volcanism"], "question": "At a transform plate boundary like the San Andreas Fault, which geologic activity is most characteristic?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Transform boundaries involve lateral sliding of plates past each other. This produces shear stress and frequent shallow earthquakes but typically does not generate the magma needed for volcanism, unlike subduction (trenches and arc volcanism) or divergent boundaries (seafloor spreading).", "correctAnswer": "Frequent shallow earthquakes with little to no volcanism"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0.01", "0.1", "0.5", "1.0"], "question": "An exoplanet transiting a Sun-like star causes a 1% dip in the star’s brightness. What is the approximate ratio of the planet’s radius to the star’s radius (Rp/Rs)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Transit depth approximately equals (Rp/Rs)^2. A 1% dip is 0.01, so Rp/Rs ≈ √0.01 = 0.1. This means the planet’s radius is about one-tenth that of the star.", "correctAnswer": "0.1"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "In photosynthesis, the molecular oxygen released comes from carbon dioxide.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The O2 produced during photosynthesis comes from the splitting (photolysis) of water (H2O) in the light reactions, not from CO2. CO2 carbon becomes part of carbohydrates during the Calvin cycle.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster in air than it does in water.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sound travels faster in water (~1480 m/s) than in air (~343 m/s at room temperature) because water is stiffer (higher bulk modulus) and efficiently transmits pressure waves despite higher density.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The biological process that converts atmospheric nitrogen (N2) into ammonia (NH3), making nitrogen available to living organisms, is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Nitrogen fixation is carried out by certain bacteria and archaea (some symbiotic with plants, like rhizobia). It transforms inert N2 into ammonia, which can be incorporated into amino acids and other biomolecules.", "correctAnswer": "nitrogen fixation"}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Coastal cities often experience smaller temperature swings than inland areas at the same latitude. Explain why.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water’s high specific heat capacity means it requires more energy to change temperature. Oceans store heat in summer and release it in winter, buffering temperature changes. Additionally, mixing and evaporation further moderate coastal climates.", "correctAnswer": "Large bodies of water have high specific heat and thermal inertia, absorbing and releasing heat slowly, which moderates nearby air temperatures."}, {"id": "q_1770096805618_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A metal object and a wooden object are both at the same room temperature. Why does the metal feel colder to the touch?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Perceived temperature depends on heat flow. Metals conduct heat away quickly, causing a rapid drop in skin temperature and a cold sensation. Wood is a good insulator, limiting heat transfer, so it feels closer to skin temperature.", "correctAnswer": "Metal has much higher thermal conductivity, so it draws heat from your skin faster, making it feel colder even though both objects are at the same temperature."}]	none	2026-02-03 05:33:25.719402	\N
71b36dca-b571-4593-b91d-ad2a979e0fa0	3566b1de-3607-4759-aa95-75efdf6b52c5	MATHS - Medium	MATHS	medium	[{"id": "q_1770096994052_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(16/5, 11/5)", "(3, 2)", "(2, 3)", "(5/2, 3/2)"], "question": "Solve the system of equations: 2x + 3y = 13 and x − y = 1. What is (x, y)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x − y = 1, we get x = y + 1. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 13: 2(y + 1) + 3y = 13 → 5y + 2 = 13 → 5y = 11 → y = 11/5. Then x = y + 1 = 16/5. So (x, y) = (16/5, 11/5).", "correctAnswer": "(16/5, 11/5)"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The function f(x) = |x − 3| is differentiable at x = 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At x = 3, the absolute value function has a sharp corner (cusp). The left-hand derivative is −1 and the right-hand derivative is +1, so they are not equal and the function is not differentiable at that point.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find the sum of the first 20 terms of an arithmetic sequence with first term 7 and common difference 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use S_n = n/2[2a_1 + (n − 1)d]. Here n = 20, a_1 = 7, d = 3. So S_20 = 20/2 [2·7 + 19·3] = 10[14 + 57] = 10·71 = 710.", "correctAnswer": "710"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/4", "5/36", "1/3", "7/36"], "question": "Two fair dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is 8 or 9?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "There are 36 total outcomes. Sum of 8 occurs in 5 ways: (2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2). Sum of 9 occurs in 4 ways: (3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3). Total favorable = 9, so probability = 9/36 = 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "1/4"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the derivative of g(x) = x^3 ln(x).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the product rule: d/dx[x^3]·ln x + x^3·d/dx[ln x] = 3x^2 ln x + x^3(1/x) = 3x^2 ln x + x^2 = x^2(3 ln x + 1).", "correctAnswer": "x^2(3 ln x + 1)"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1 + sqrt(10)", "1 − sqrt(10)", "3", "9"], "question": "Solve for x: log_3(x) + log_3(x − 2) = 2.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine logs: log_3[x(x − 2)] = 2 ⇒ x(x − 2) = 9 ⇒ x^2 − 2x − 9 = 0. Solving gives x = 1 ± sqrt(10). Domain requires x > 2, so x = 1 + sqrt(10).", "correctAnswer": "1 + sqrt(10)"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The mean of a data set must be one of the data values.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The mean is the average and need not coincide with any observed value. For example, the mean of {1, 2, 3, 4} is 2.5, which is not in the set.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices at (0, 0), (4, 3), and (6, 1).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Using the shoelace formula: Area = 1/2 |x1(y2−y3) + x2(y3−y1) + x3(y1−y2)| = 1/2 |0(3−1) + 4(1−0) + 6(0−3)| = 1/2 |0 + 4 − 18| = 1/2 · 14 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-1 - 2i", "1 - 2i", "-1 + 2i", "3 - i"], "question": "Compute (3 − 4i) / (1 + 2i).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Multiply numerator and denominator by (1 − 2i). Numerator: (3 − 4i)(1 − 2i) = 3 − 6i − 4i + 8i^2 = 3 − 10i − 8 = −5 − 10i. Denominator: 1 + 4 = 5. Result: (−5 − 10i)/5 = −1 − 2i.", "correctAnswer": "-1 - 2i"}, {"id": "q_1770096994052_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the value of k such that the line y = 2x + k is tangent to the parabola y = x^2 − 4x + 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Set x^2 − 4x + 5 = 2x + k ⇒ x^2 − 6x + (5 − k) = 0. For tangency, discriminant = 0: (−6)^2 − 4(1)(5 − k) = 36 − 20 + 4k = 16 + 4k = 0 ⇒ k = −4.", "correctAnswer": "-4"}]	none	2026-02-03 05:36:34.173865	\N
ed47be23-ad2c-4406-aa2c-a9ad9c0bf834	36557ace-da21-4210-9514-0f66339553c7	math - Medium	math	medium	[{"id": "q_1770097157374_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0%", "+4%", "-4%", "+20%"], "question": "A jacket costs $100. It is first marked up by 20% and then discounted by 20%. What is the final percent change relative to the original price?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A 20% markup makes the price 100 × 1.20 = 120. A 20% discount on 120 is 120 × 0.80 = 96. The final price is $96, which is $4 less than $100, so the net change is a 4% decrease, not zero. Percent increases and decreases are taken relative to the current amount, not the original.", "correctAnswer": "-4%"}, {"id": "q_1770097157374_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The natural logarithm function ln(x) is defined for all real numbers x.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The domain of ln(x) is x > 0. It is undefined for x ≤ 0 because logarithms of non-positive numbers are not real.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770097157374_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find the slope of a line perpendicular to the line 2y − 4x = 8.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite the given line in slope-intercept form: 2y = 4x + 8 → y = 2x + 4. Its slope is 2. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals, so the perpendicular slope is −1/2.", "correctAnswer": "-1/2"}, {"id": "q_1770097157374_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the vertex of the parabola y = x^2 − 4x + 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Complete the square: y = x^2 − 4x + 1 = (x − 2)^2 − 4 + 1 = (x − 2)^2 − 3. In vertex form y = (x − h)^2 + k, the vertex is (h, k) = (2, −3).", "correctAnswer": "(2, -3)"}, {"id": "q_1770097157374_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/6", "1/9", "5/36", "1/12"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum of the two dice is 9?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "There are 36 equally likely outcomes for two dice. The sum 9 occurs with (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), and (6,3): 4 favorable outcomes. So the probability is 4/36 = 1/9.", "correctAnswer": "1/9"}]	none	2026-02-03 05:39:17.385927	\N
b2cb1bd1-b092-4586-9264-9587d4502980	1620ab83-f2cd-4cef-9669-a032d1d3314f	Biology - Easy	Biology	easy	[{"id": "q_1770097159677_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mitochondria", "Ribosomes", "Nucleus", "Golgi apparatus"], "question": "Which organelle is known as the \\"powerhouse of the cell\\" because it releases energy from food molecules?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mitochondria perform cellular respiration, converting energy stored in food into ATP for the cell to use. Ribosomes make proteins, the nucleus stores genetic information, and the Golgi apparatus modifies and packages molecules.", "correctAnswer": "Mitochondria"}, {"id": "q_1770097159677_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In eukaryotic cells, most of the DNA is located inside the nucleus.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Eukaryotic cells have a membrane-bound nucleus that houses most of their DNA. While mitochondria (and chloroplasts in plants) contain small amounts of DNA, the majority is in the nucleus.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770097159677_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms is the ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cell theory states that all living things are made of cells, cells are the basic units of structure and function, and all cells come from pre-existing cells.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1770097159677_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the process by which green plants use sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts using chlorophyll to capture light energy, producing glucose for energy and growth and releasing oxygen as a by-product.", "correctAnswer": "photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1770097159677_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Chlorophyll", "Carotene", "Hemoglobin", "Melanin"], "question": "Which pigment in leaves is primarily responsible for their green color and for capturing light energy during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Chlorophyll (especially chlorophyll a and b) absorbs light for photosynthesis and reflects green light, making leaves appear green. Carotene is an accessory pigment (orange), hemoglobin carries oxygen in blood, and melanin is a pigment in skin and hair.", "correctAnswer": "Chlorophyll"}]	none	2026-02-03 05:39:19.691353	\N
7ab38be7-f6da-400b-873f-fb2072769ad0	7533b1ad-6515-47e0-a8c6-d49d13fc840d	geography - Medium	geography	medium	[{"id": "q_1770097374626_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cold offshore ocean currents and descending air under subtropical highs", "Orographic rain shadows from coastal mountain ranges", "Seasonal monsoon wind reversal bringing winter dryness", "Low sun angle at high latitudes reducing evaporation"], "question": "Which factor most directly explains why many deserts occur on the western coasts of continents between about 20° and 30° latitude?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At roughly 20–30° latitude, the descending limb of the Hadley cell creates subtropical high pressure with sinking, warming air that suppresses precipitation. Where this coincides with cold offshore currents (e.g., the Peru, Benguela, or California Currents), the cool, stable marine layer further inhibits convection. This combination fosters aridity along western continental margins, producing deserts like the Atacama and Namib. The other options can create dryness locally but do not systematically explain the broad west-coast desert belt.", "correctAnswer": "Cold offshore ocean currents and descending air under subtropical highs"}, {"id": "q_1770097374626_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "When flying eastward from Fiji to Hawaii across the International Date Line, you set your calendar one day earlier.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Crossing the International Date Line eastward means you subtract a day; crossing westward means you add a day. Fiji sits just west of the line (ahead in date), while Hawaii is east of it (behind in date). So an eastbound traveler moves to the previous calendar day—even though the clock time changes mainly due to time zones, the date resets because of the convention that keeps the global calendar consistent.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770097374626_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The dry region on the leeward side of a mountain, created as air loses moisture on the windward slope and warms as it descends, is called the ________.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "As moist air ascends a mountain, it cools, condenses, and precipitates on the windward side (orographic rainfall). The now drier air descends on the leeward side, warming adiabatically and inhibiting cloud formation, creating a rain shadow. Classic examples include the dry Great Basin east of California’s Sierra Nevada and Patagonia east of the Andes.", "correctAnswer": "rain shadow"}, {"id": "q_1770097374626_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Two countries have similar populations and total land areas, but Country A has 12% arable land while Country B has 45%. Which population density metric better assesses pressure on farmland, and how would you expect it to compare between the two countries?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Arithmetic density (people per total land area) ignores how much land can actually support agriculture. Physiological density (people per unit of arable land) captures pressure on cultivable land. With less arable land, Country A would have more people per hectare of farmland than Country B, indicating greater potential stress on food-producing resources.", "correctAnswer": "Physiological density; it would be higher in Country A because the same population is supported by much less arable land."}, {"id": "q_1770097374626_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Azimuthal equidistant projection centered on the city", "Mercator projection", "Robinson projection", "Gall–Peters projection"], "question": "Which map projection is most suitable for plotting the shortest flight paths from a specific city to other destinations, because great-circle routes radiating from that city appear as straight lines and distances from that city are accurate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "An azimuthal equidistant map preserves true distances from the center to any other point and shows great-circle paths from that center as straight lines, making it ideal for planning routes from a specific origin. The Mercator preserves angles and makes rhumb lines straight (useful for constant compass bearings) but distorts distance and area, especially at high latitudes. Robinson and Gall–Peters are compromises that do not preserve distances or directions from a point for route planning.", "correctAnswer": "Azimuthal equidistant projection centered on the city"}]	none	2026-02-03 05:42:54.720355	\N
37cb3156-abc8-4c7a-91fd-012ede2d1ad2	4a02fd82-3adb-4ee5-9526-e081e6840749	MATHS - Medium	MATHS	medium	[{"id": "q_1770097385149_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(-1, 4)", "[-1, 4]", "x < -1 or x > 4", "(-4, 1)"], "question": "Solve the inequality |2x − 3| < 5. Which interval represents the solution set?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For |2x − 3| < 5, rewrite as a double inequality: -5 < 2x − 3 < 5. Add 3 to all parts to get -2 < 2x < 8. Divide by 2: -1 < x < 4. This is the open interval (-1, 4). The other options either include endpoints (which are not allowed for a strict inequality) or represent incorrect intervals.", "correctAnswer": "(-1, 4)"}, {"id": "q_1770097385149_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "For x > 0, the derivative of ln(x^2) is 2/x.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When x > 0, ln(x^2) = 2 ln(x). Differentiating gives d/dx[2 ln(x)] = 2 · (1/x) = 2/x. Alternatively, using the chain rule directly: d/dx[ln(u)] = (1/u)·du/dx with u = x^2, so (1/x^2)·(2x) = 2/x.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770097385149_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for x in exact form: 2^x = 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Take natural logs: ln(2^x) = ln(5) ⇒ x·ln(2) = ln(5). Solving for x gives x = ln(5)/ln(2). This is also log base 2 of 5 (log₂5).", "correctAnswer": "ln(5)/ln(2)"}, {"id": "q_1770097385149_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices (1, 2), (4, 6), and (6, 3). Give your answer in exact form.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the shoelace formula: Arrange points (1,2), (4,6), (6,3). Compute downward products: 1·6 + 4·3 + 6·2 = 30. Upward products: 2·4 + 6·6 + 3·1 = 47. Area = (1/2)|30 − 47| = (1/2)·17 = 17/2.", "correctAnswer": "17/2"}, {"id": "q_1770097385149_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/6", "5/36", "1/12", "7/36"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is at least 10?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sums ≥ 10 are 10, 11, and 12. Count outcomes: sum 10 has 3 ((4,6), (5,5), (6,4)), sum 11 has 2 ((5,6), (6,5)), sum 12 has 1 ((6,6)). Total favorable = 6. Total outcomes = 36. Probability = 6/36 = 1/6.", "correctAnswer": "1/6"}]	none	2026-02-03 05:43:05.269406	\N
d7cfeb3e-1083-4d81-81a3-fb14e24f805e	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	eletric coductors stage 6 - Easy	eletric coductors stage 6	easy	[{"id": "q_1773853098441_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Wood", "Rubber", "Copper", "Plastic"], "question": "Which of the following materials is a good electrical conductor?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Copper is widely recognized as an excellent conductor of electricity due to its high electrical conductivity. Materials like wood, rubber, and plastic are insulators and do not conduct electricity well.", "correctAnswer": "Copper"}, {"id": "q_1773853098441_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "All metals are good electrical conductors.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "While many metals are good conductors, not all metals have the same level of conductivity. Some metals, like lead, are much less conductive compared to others like copper and silver.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1773853098441_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "______ is the term used to describe materials that allow electricity to flow through them easily.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A conductor is a material that permits the flow of electric charge. Conductors are essential in electrical circuits for enabling the movement of electrons.", "correctAnswer": "Conductor"}, {"id": "q_1773853098441_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common use of electrical conductors.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Electrical conductors, such as copper wires, are commonly used in electrical wiring to transmit electricity from one point to another in homes, buildings, and devices.", "correctAnswer": "Electrical wiring"}, {"id": "q_1773853098441_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Low resistance", "High conductivity", "Insulation", "Ability to carry current"], "question": "Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of good electrical conductors?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Insulation is a property that prevents the flow of electricity, while good conductors are characterized by low resistance, high conductivity, and the ability to carry electric current.", "correctAnswer": "Insulation"}]	none	2026-03-18 16:58:18.52379	\N
d223b1be-1300-444c-9d99-d9e6996c3cda	4961ee9c-3263-43b3-a734-abdc2cc8ca2e	Jujutsu kaisen - Easy	Jujutsu kaisen	easy	[{"id": "q_1770104792079_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Tokyo Jujutsu High", "Kyoto Jujutsu High", "Karasuno High", "U.A. High"], "question": "Which school do Yuji and his friends attend to learn jujutsu sorcery?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Yuji Itadori, Megumi Fushiguro, and Nobara Kugisaki are first-year students at Tokyo Jujutsu High, a specialized school for training jujutsu sorcerers. Kyoto Jujutsu High exists in the series but is the sister school, while Karasuno and U.A. are from other anime.", "correctAnswer": "Tokyo Jujutsu High"}, {"id": "q_1770104792079_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Cursed Energy in Jujutsu Kaisen is generated from negative emotions.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the series, Cursed Energy is the power source born from negative emotions such as fear, anger, and sorrow. Sorcerers learn to control and refine this energy to fight curses.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770104792079_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Yuji ______ is the main protagonist of Jujutsu Kaisen.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The full name of the main character is Yuji Itadori. He becomes a jujutsu sorcerer after encountering curses and ingesting one of Sukuna’s fingers.", "correctAnswer": "Itadori"}, {"id": "q_1770104792079_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Curses", "Sorcerers", "Monsters", "Dragons"], "question": "Ryomen Sukuna is known as the \\"King of ______.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ryomen Sukuna is feared as the King of Curses. His immense power and the 20 cursed fingers associated with him make him central to the story’s conflict.", "correctAnswer": "Curses"}, {"id": "q_1770104792079_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Who uses the Straw Doll Technique to exorcise curses among the first-year students?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nobara Kugisaki wields a hammer, nails, and a straw doll as part of her Straw Doll Technique, allowing her to attack curses from a distance and perform resonant damage.", "correctAnswer": "Nobara Kugisaki"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:46:32.092736	\N
431cbb5c-e078-408b-be4f-eecfc13e3b2a	ccda7dad-d9b6-49a6-940c-f590944ad9a7	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104899637_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "-5", "3", "0"], "question": "Consider the quadratic function f(x) = -2(x - 3)^2 + 5. What is the maximum value of f(x)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is a parabola in vertex form. The vertex is at (3, 5), and since the coefficient of the squared term is negative, the parabola opens downward. Therefore, the maximum value is the y-coordinate of the vertex, which is 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If u and v are perpendicular vectors in the plane, then |u + v|^2 = |u|^2 + |v|^2.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By the parallelogram law and dot product properties, |u + v|^2 = |u|^2 + |v|^2 + 2u·v. If u and v are perpendicular, u·v = 0, so |u + v|^2 = |u|^2 + |v|^2.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The slope of a line perpendicular to the line y = -3x + 2 is ___.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals. The given slope is -3, so the perpendicular slope is -1/(-3) = 1/3.", "correctAnswer": "1/3"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic sequence with first term a1 = 7 and common difference d = 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the sum formula for an arithmetic sequence: S_n = n/2 [2a1 + (n−1)d]. Here, S_20 = 20/2 [2(7) + 19(3)] = 10 [14 + 57] = 10 × 71 = 710.", "correctAnswer": "710"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/9", "1/12", "5/36", "2/9"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is 9?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sums of 9 occur with (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3), which is 4 outcomes out of 36 total. So the probability is 4/36 = 1/9.", "correctAnswer": "1/9"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4", "-2", "2", "8"], "question": "Solve for x: log2(x) + log2(x − 2) = 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine logs: log2[x(x − 2)] = 3, so x(x − 2) = 2^3 = 8. This gives x^2 − 2x − 8 = 0 → (x − 4)(x + 2) = 0 → x = 4 or x = −2. The domain requires x > 2 and x − 2 > 0, so x = 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The function g(x) = |x − 1| is differentiable at x = 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Absolute value functions have a sharp corner at the point where the expression inside equals zero. At x = 1, the left-hand derivative is −1 and the right-hand derivative is 1, so the derivative does not exist.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Find the area of a sector of a circle with radius 6 and central angle 60 degrees: ___.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sector area with angle in degrees is (θ/360)πr^2. Here, (60/360) × π × 6^2 = (1/6) × π × 36 = 6π.", "correctAnswer": "6π"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the system: 2x + 3y = 13 and x − y = 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x − y = 1, get x = y + 1. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 13 → 2(y + 1) + 3y = 13 → 5y + 2 = 13 → 5y = 11 → y = 11/5. Then x = y + 1 = 11/5 + 5/5 = 16/5.", "correctAnswer": "x = 16/5, y = 11/5"}, {"id": "q_1770104899637_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-4", "4", "2i", "-2i"], "question": "Compute (1 + i)^4.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "(1 + i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 2i. Then (1 + i)^4 = (2i)^2 = 4i^2 = 4(−1) = −4.", "correctAnswer": "-4"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:48:19.651985	\N
62eaaad8-1bd6-43c4-9e32-b57c05145fa4	a243f241-0bc9-4ac1-84d9-1eb6b9c40943	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104907490_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(11/3, 0)", "(0, 11/3)", "(0, -11/3)", "(3, 0)"], "question": "The line passing through (2, 5) with slope -3 has which x-intercept?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Using point-slope form: y - 5 = -3(x - 2) → y = -3x + 11. The x-intercept occurs when y = 0, so solve -3x + 11 = 0 → x = 11/3. Thus the x-intercept is (11/3, 0). The choice (0, 11/3) is the y-intercept, not the x-intercept.", "correctAnswer": "(11/3, 0)"}, {"id": "q_1770104907490_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If a function is differentiable at x = a, then it must be continuous at x = a.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Differentiability at a point implies the limit of the difference quotient exists, which requires the function’s limit to exist and equal its value there. Therefore, differentiability at x = a guarantees continuity at x = a. The converse is not necessarily true.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770104907490_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A bag contains 6 red, 4 blue, and 5 green marbles. Two marbles are drawn without replacement. The probability that both are red is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The probability is (6/15) × (5/14) = 30/210 = 1/7. Equivalently, combinations give C(6,2)/C(15,2) = 15/105 = 1/7.", "correctAnswer": "1/7"}, {"id": "q_1770104907490_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the system of equations: 2x + 3y = 13 and x − y = 1. Give your answer as an ordered pair (x, y).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x − y = 1, get x = 1 + y. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 13: 2(1 + y) + 3y = 13 → 2 + 2y + 3y = 13 → 5y = 11 → y = 11/5. Then x = 1 + 11/5 = 16/5.", "correctAnswer": "(16/5, 11/5)"}, {"id": "q_1770104907490_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["20*sqrt(3)", "40*sqrt(3)", "80", "20"], "question": "Find the area of a triangle with sides of lengths 8 and 10 and an included angle of 60 degrees.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use the formula A = (1/2)ab sin(C) for two sides and the included angle. Here, A = (1/2)(8)(10)sin(60°) = 40 × (√3/2) = 20√3.", "correctAnswer": "20*sqrt(3)"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:48:27.501406	\N
f4fe8c91-47f3-46bb-8a98-2ad4af21a608	7e8264ae-ef01-4386-8fd8-95dc8fbb28e5	MATH - Medium	MATH	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104915755_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["x = -1 and x = 5", "x = -3 and x = 3", "x = 2 only", "No real x-intercepts"], "question": "Find the x-intercepts of the quadratic function f(x) = (x - 2)^2 - 9.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "To find x-intercepts, set f(x) = 0: (x - 2)^2 - 9 = 0 ⇒ (x - 2)^2 = 9 ⇒ x - 2 = ±3 ⇒ x = -1 or x = 5. Thus the graph crosses the x-axis at x = -1 and x = 5.", "correctAnswer": "x = -1 and x = 5"}, {"id": "q_1770104915755_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: In a dataset, the median is more affected by extreme outliers than the mean.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The mean is sensitive to extreme values because it uses all data values directly. The median is resistant to outliers since it depends only on the middle position, not the magnitude of extreme observations.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770104915755_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The line L has equation 3x - 4y = 8. The slope of any line perpendicular to L is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite 3x - 4y = 8 as y = (3/4)x - 2, so the slope of L is 3/4. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals, so the perpendicular slope is -4/3.", "correctAnswer": "-4/3"}, {"id": "q_1770104915755_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A theater sold 150 tickets to a show. Adult tickets cost $12 and student tickets cost $8. If total ticket revenue was $1520, how many adult tickets were sold?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let a be adult tickets and s be student tickets. Then a + s = 150 and 12a + 8s = 1520. Substitute s = 150 - a into revenue: 12a + 8(150 - a) = 1520 ⇒ 12a + 1200 - 8a = 1520 ⇒ 4a = 320 ⇒ a = 80.", "correctAnswer": "80"}, {"id": "q_1770104915755_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/15", "1/10", "1/5", "2/15"], "question": "A bag contains 5 red, 3 blue, and 2 green marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are blue?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The probability the first marble is blue is 3/10. Without replacement, the probability the second is blue is 2/9. Multiply: (3/10) × (2/9) = 6/90 = 1/15.", "correctAnswer": "1/15"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:48:35.768459	\N
2ff02a5a-8c7a-4b00-914f-b96de6d33fb3	4961ee9c-3263-43b3-a734-abdc2cc8ca2e	SCIENCE - Medium	SCIENCE	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104932742_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It doubles", "It halves", "It stays the same", "It increases by 1 atm"], "question": "A sealed syringe traps 10 mL of air at room temperature. You pull the plunger until the volume of the trapped air is 20 mL, with temperature remaining constant. What happens to the pressure of the gas inside the syringe?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At constant temperature, pressure and volume are inversely related (Boyle's law: P1V1 = P2V2). Doubling the volume from 10 mL to 20 mL causes the pressure to decrease to half its original value.", "correctAnswer": "It halves"}, {"id": "q_1770104932742_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In aerobic cellular respiration, the majority of ATP is produced during glycolysis.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Glycolysis nets only 2 ATP per glucose. Most ATP in aerobic respiration is produced by oxidative phosphorylation (electron transport chain and chemiosmosis) in the mitochondria, which yields the bulk of ATP.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770104932742_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When plants close their stomata to conserve water on a hot, dry day, the uptake of _____ is directly reduced, which can slow photosynthesis.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Stomata regulate gas exchange. Closing them reduces CO2 diffusion into the leaf, limiting the Calvin cycle and thus photosynthesis, even though water loss is reduced.", "correctAnswer": "carbon dioxide (CO2)"}, {"id": "q_1770104932742_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why is oceanic crust generally younger than continental crust?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Seafloor spreading forms new oceanic crust at ridges, and its higher density leads it to subduct and be destroyed relatively quickly. Continental crust is less dense and rarely subducts, so it accumulates great age.", "correctAnswer": "It is continually created at mid-ocean ridges and recycled at subduction zones, while buoyant continental crust resists subduction and persists much longer."}, {"id": "q_1770104932742_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Water on the skin conducts heat away into the air", "Evaporation of sweat absorbs a large amount of heat from the skin", "Sweating increases blood flow, which radiates more heat", "The high specific heat of water stores excess body heat on the skin"], "question": "Sweating cools your body most effectively because...", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Evaporation requires energy (latent heat of vaporization). The phase change from liquid to vapor draws heat from the skin, providing the primary cooling effect. Conduction, convection, and radiation play smaller roles under most conditions.", "correctAnswer": "Evaporation of sweat absorbs a large amount of heat from the skin"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:48:52.847473	\N
83498f80-0857-4df3-ba10-62a23f3a652a	4961ee9c-3263-43b3-a734-abdc2cc8ca2e	english  - Medium	english 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104935412_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The committee values honesty, punctuality, and efficiency in its members.", "The committee values honesty, to be punctual, and members working efficiently.", "The committee values that members are honest, punctual, and working efficiently.", "The committee values honesty, being punctual, and that members work efficiently."], "question": "Which revision best improves parallel structure and clarity in the sentence: \\"The committee values honesty, to be punctual, and that members work efficiently\\"?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires items in a list to share the same grammatical form. \\"Honesty, punctuality, and efficiency\\" are all nouns, creating a balanced series. The original mixes a noun (honesty), an infinitive (to be punctual), and a clause (that members work efficiently), which disrupts clarity and rhythm.", "correctAnswer": "The committee values honesty, punctuality, and efficiency in its members."}, {"id": "q_1770104935412_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A restrictive relative clause providing essential information about which students receive priority.", "A nonrestrictive relative clause adding extra information about all students.", "An appositive phrase renaming \\"students.\\"", "An absolute phrase modifying the whole sentence."], "question": "In the sentence \\"Students who submit the form early receive priority,\\" what is the function of the clause \\"who submit the form early\\"?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The clause \\"who submit the form early\\" restricts the noun \\"students\\" to a specific subset—only those who submit early receive priority. Its lack of commas signals that the information is essential (restrictive), not merely additional.", "correctAnswer": "A restrictive relative clause providing essential information about which students receive priority."}, {"id": "q_1770104935412_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "In formal writing, \\"less\\" should be used with countable nouns and \\"fewer\\" with uncountable nouns.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "\\"Fewer\\" is used with countable nouns (fewer apples, fewer books), while \\"less\\" is used with uncountable nouns (less water, less time). A common exception is with measurements treated as amounts (money, time, distance), where \\"less\\" is acceptable (less than 10 dollars, less than two hours).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770104935412_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence with correct subject–verb agreement: \\"Either the teacher or the students ___ bringing their laptops to the workshop.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "With \\"either/or\\" constructions, the verb agrees with the noun closest to it. Since \\"students\\" is plural and closest to the verb, the correct form is \\"are.\\"", "correctAnswer": "are"}, {"id": "q_1770104935412_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence to eliminate the dangling modifier: \\"Walking down the street, the skyscrapers towered over me.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A dangling modifier occurs when the modifier (\\"Walking down the street\\") lacks a clear subject to modify. By adding the explicit subject (\\"I\\"), the modifier correctly attaches to the person walking, resolving the ambiguity.", "correctAnswer": "As I walked down the street, the skyscrapers towered over me."}]	none	2026-02-03 07:48:55.425604	\N
4488417f-2fde-4082-8c78-e6514fbf4e71	c06c34b8-da52-4c7c-a10e-605c55534c81	maths - Medium	maths	medium	[{"id": "q_1770104997665_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["6", "9", "12", "15"], "question": "An investment grows according to A(t) = 500(1.08)^t, where t is in years. Approximately how many years (to the nearest year) will it take for the investment to double?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "We want 500(1.08)^t = 1000, so (1.08)^t = 2. Taking natural logs: t = ln(2) / ln(1.08) ≈ 0.6931 / 0.07696 ≈ 9.0 years. A quick check using the Rule of 72 (72/8 ≈ 9) also supports this answer.", "correctAnswer": "9"}, {"id": "q_1770104997665_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If events A and B are independent with P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.5, then P(A ∪ B) = 0.9.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For any events, P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). Independence gives P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2. Thus P(A ∪ B) = 0.4 + 0.5 − 0.2 = 0.7, not 0.9.", "correctAnswer": "false"}, {"id": "q_1770104997665_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for the positive solution: 3/x + 2/(x + 1) = 1. x = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Combine using a common denominator: 3(x+1) + 2x = x(x+1). This gives 5x + 3 = x^2 + x, or x^2 − 4x − 3 = 0. Solving the quadratic: x = [4 ± sqrt(16 + 12)]/2 = [4 ± sqrt(28)]/2 = 2 ± sqrt(7). The positive solution is x = 2 + sqrt(7). Note x ≠ 0 and x ≠ −1 are excluded by the original denominators.", "correctAnswer": "2 + sqrt(7)"}, {"id": "q_1770104997665_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the equation of the line that passes through (2, −3) and is perpendicular to 4x − 2y = 8.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Rewrite 4x − 2y = 8 as y = 2x − 4, so its slope is 2. A perpendicular line has slope −1/2. Using point-slope form with (2, −3): y + 3 = (−1/2)(x − 2) ⇒ y = (−1/2)x + 1 − 3 = (−1/2)x − 2.", "correctAnswer": "y = (-1/2)x - 2"}, {"id": "q_1770104997665_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "4", "7", "13"], "question": "What is the minimum value of the quadratic function f(x) = x^2 − 6x + 13?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Complete the square: f(x) = (x^2 − 6x + 9) + 4 = (x − 3)^2 + 4. Since (x − 3)^2 ≥ 0, the minimum value occurs at x = 3 and equals 4.", "correctAnswer": "4"}]	none	2026-02-03 07:49:57.678926	\N
d2726d16-fafb-4f20-b2ac-4ea2162321d6	9e86bcc0-ec8b-43d4-8b57-0f8268475110	Algebra - Easy	Algebra	easy	[{"id": "q_1770111814837_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5x", "6x", "3x^2", "5"], "question": "Simplify the expression: 3x + 2x", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "3x and 2x are like terms because they both involve x to the first power. Add the coefficients: 3 + 2 = 5, so the result is 5x.", "correctAnswer": "5x"}, {"id": "q_1770111814837_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: The solution to the equation 2 + x = 7 is x = 5.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtract 2 from both sides: x = 7 - 2 = 5. Therefore, the statement is true.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770111814837_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Evaluate 2x + 1 when x = 3. ______", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Substitute x = 3 into the expression: 2(3) + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1770111814837_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve for x: x/4 = 3", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiply both sides by 4 to undo the division: x = 3 × 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770111814837_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "x", "-5", "1"], "question": "What is the coefficient of x in the expression 7x − 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable. In 7x − 5, the coefficient of x is 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}]	none	2026-02-03 09:43:34.951853	\N
52c5970d-71ba-4e99-906c-0ac75285888d	9e86bcc0-ec8b-43d4-8b57-0f8268475110	English - Medium	English	medium	[{"id": "q_1770112465900_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["If I was you, I'd ask for help.", "If I were you, I'd ask for help.", "If I am you, I'd ask for help.", "If I would be you, I'd ask for help."], "question": "Which sentence correctly uses the subjunctive mood for a hypothetical situation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The subjunctive mood uses 'were' with the verb 'to be' in if-clauses that are contrary to fact. 'If I were you' expresses a hypothetical, not reality. The other options use indicative ('was'/'am') or an ungrammatical conditional form ('would be').", "correctAnswer": "If I were you, I'd ask for help."}, {"id": "q_1770112465900_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In the sentence 'Each of the students have their books,' the verb agrees correctly with the subject.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The subject is the singular indefinite pronoun 'Each,' so the verb should be singular: 'Each of the students has their book(s).' Agreement is with 'Each,' not 'students.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770112465900_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the most appropriate conjunctive adverb to show contrast: 'She practiced daily; _____, her performance showed little improvement.'", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Conjunctive adverbs like 'however,' 'nevertheless,' and 'still' signal contrast. 'Nevertheless' fits well and pairs naturally with a semicolon and comma, emphasizing that the second clause contrasts with the first.", "correctAnswer": "nevertheless"}, {"id": "q_1770112465900_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence in active voice: 'The decision was finalized by the committee.'", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Active voice places the agent (the doer) as the subject. Here, 'the committee' performs the action, so 'The committee finalized the decision' is the active equivalent of the passive construction.", "correctAnswer": "The committee finalized the decision."}, {"id": "q_1770112465900_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["She likes reading, to jog, and cooking.", "She likes to read, jogging, and to cook.", "She likes reading, jogging, and cooking.", "She likes to read, to jog, and cooking."], "question": "Which sentence uses parallel structure correctly?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires matching grammatical forms in a series. Option C uses three gerunds ('reading, jogging, cooking'). The other options mix infinitives and gerunds, which breaks parallelism.", "correctAnswer": "She likes reading, jogging, and cooking."}]	none	2026-02-03 09:54:25.988139	\N
dcf7e685-b939-418d-979e-b5542caca31f	578fe8af-8576-422c-b2de-4ea3d55d5294	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1770376666968_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer. A keyboard lets you enter text and commands, while a monitor, printer, and speaker are output devices that display, print, or play information from the computer.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1770376666968_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The CPU is often called the 'brain' of the computer.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Central Processing Unit (CPU) performs the majority of calculations and controls the execution of instructions, coordinating what other parts of the computer do—hence it is commonly called the computer’s 'brain.'", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770376666968_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The software that manages computer hardware and provides common services for programs is called the ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (e.g., Windows, macOS, Linux, Android) manages hardware resources and offers services like file management, memory handling, and user interfaces for applications.", "correctAnswer": "operating system"}, {"id": "q_1770376666968_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".zip", ".docx", ".png", ".mp3"], "question": "Which file extension is commonly used for a compressed archive?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A .zip file groups one or more files into a compressed archive to save space and simplify sharing. .docx is a Word document, .png is an image, and .mp3 is an audio file.", "correctAnswer": ".zip"}, {"id": "q_1770376666968_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In web addresses, what does the acronym 'URL' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the address used to locate and access resources on the internet, such as web pages, images, or files.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:17:46.980538	\N
7f106062-eea9-4caf-b288-008b8ead72b1	9e86bcc0-ec8b-43d4-8b57-0f8268475110	algebra  - Medium	algebra 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770113560927_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(29/7, 11/7)", "(11/7, 29/7)", "(3, 1)", "(2, 3)"], "question": "Solve the system of equations: 2x + 3y = 13 and x − 2y = 1. What is the ordered pair (x, y)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x − 2y = 1, we have x = 1 + 2y. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 13: 2(1 + 2y) + 3y = 13 ⇒ 2 + 4y + 3y = 13 ⇒ 7y = 11 ⇒ y = 11/7. Then x = 1 + 2(11/7) = 1 + 22/7 = 29/7.", "correctAnswer": "(29/7, 11/7)"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(1, 5)", "(-1, 5)", "(1, -5)", "(2, 3)"], "question": "Find the vertex of the quadratic function f(x) = −2x^2 + 4x + 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c, the x-coordinate of the vertex is −b/(2a) = −4/(2·(−2)) = 1. Then f(1) = −2(1)^2 + 4(1) + 3 = −2 + 4 + 3 = 5, so the vertex is (1, 5).", "correctAnswer": "(1, 5)"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(x + 3) / x", "(x − 3) / x", "(x + 3) / (x − 3)", "(x − 3) / (x + 3)"], "question": "Simplify the rational expression: (x^2 − 9) / (x^2 − 3x), assuming x ≠ 0 and x ≠ 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor numerator and denominator: x^2 − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3) and x^2 − 3x = x(x − 3). Cancel (x − 3) (for x ≠ 3) to get (x + 3)/x (also x ≠ 0).", "correctAnswer": "(x + 3) / x"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "4", "-10", "2"], "question": "Let f(x) = 3x − 2 and g(x) = x^2. Find (f ∘ g)(−2).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "(f ∘ g)(−2) = f(g(−2)) = f(4) = 3·4 − 2 = 12 − 2 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The equation |2x − 5| = −3 has no real solution.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Absolute value outputs are always nonnegative. Since −3 is negative, there is no x for which |2x − 5| equals −3.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The solution to the inequality x^2 < 9 is x < −3 or x > 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "x^2 < 9 means the distance of x from 0 is less than 3, so −3 < x < 3. The statement x < −3 or x > 3 corresponds to x^2 > 9, not x^2 < 9.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve for t: 5(t − 2) + 3 = 2t + 9. t = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Distribute: 5t − 10 + 3 = 2t + 9 ⇒ 5t − 7 = 2t + 9 ⇒ 3t = 16 ⇒ t = 16/3.", "correctAnswer": "16/3"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Simplify: x^3 · x^4 ÷ x^2 = ____", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use exponent rules: x^a · x^b = x^(a+b) and x^a ÷ x^b = x^(a−b). Here, x^(3+4−2) = x^5.", "correctAnswer": "x^5"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic sequence with first term 7 and common difference 3.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use S_n = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d]. With a = 7, d = 3, n = 20: S_20 = 20/2[14 + 19·3] = 10[14 + 57] = 10·71 = 710.", "correctAnswer": "710"}, {"id": "q_1770113560927_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the quadratic equation x^2 − 5x + 6 = 0.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor: x^2 − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0. Set each factor to zero: x − 2 = 0 ⇒ x = 2; x − 3 = 0 ⇒ x = 3.", "correctAnswer": "2, 3"}]	none	2026-02-03 10:12:41.018679	\N
952df597-1945-4fa9-8959-dd03cc94611b	5a3f23e3-425b-40a7-bda3-6fd43f8e331e	Biology - Easy	Biology	easy	[{"id": "q_1770130970948_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mitochondrion", "Ribosome", "Nucleus", "Chloroplast"], "question": "Which organelle is known as the \\"powerhouse of the cell\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mitochondria generate most of the cell’s ATP through cellular respiration, providing energy for cellular processes. Ribosomes make proteins, the nucleus stores genetic material, and chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis in plant cells.", "correctAnswer": "Mitochondrion"}, {"id": "q_1770130970948_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In plants and algae, chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and the enzymes needed to convert light energy into chemical energy, producing glucose and oxygen from carbon dioxide and water.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770130970948_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The basic unit of life is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "All living organisms are made of cells. Cells are the smallest structures that can carry out all the processes necessary for life.", "correctAnswer": "cell"}, {"id": "q_1770130970948_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the molecule that carries genetic information in most living organisms.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) stores hereditary information in its sequence of nucleotides and is organized into genes and chromosomes.", "correctAnswer": "DNA"}, {"id": "q_1770130970948_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Carbohydrate", "Lipid", "Protein", "Nucleic acid"], "question": "Which type of biomolecule is made of amino acid building blocks?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Proteins are polymers of amino acids linked by peptide bonds. Carbohydrates are made of sugars, lipids of fatty acids, and nucleic acids of nucleotides.", "correctAnswer": "Protein"}]	none	2026-02-03 15:02:51.078599	\N
4879c1bb-0928-430e-b317-4e2d2dac59c3	258764d5-9b9c-4159-b109-a245f042ad13	diffusion - Medium	diffusion	medium	[{"id": "q_1770138450580_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Increase alveolar surface area", "Thicken the alveolar-capillary membrane", "Lower body temperature", "Reduce the oxygen partial pressure gradient"], "question": "In gas exchange across the alveolar-capillary membrane, which single change would most increase the diffusion rate of oxygen, assuming all other factors remain constant?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Fick's law, diffusion rate increases with surface area and concentration (partial pressure) gradient, and decreases with membrane thickness. Of the options given, increasing surface area directly increases the rate. Thickening the membrane, cooling (lowering temperature reduces molecular motion), and reducing the gradient all decrease the rate.", "correctAnswer": "Increase alveolar surface area"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It moves solutes against their gradient using ATP hydrolysis", "It requires a membrane carrier or channel but does not consume ATP", "It relies on bulk flow driven by pressure differences", "Solutes cross directly through the lipid bilayer without proteins"], "question": "Which statement best describes facilitated diffusion across a cell membrane?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Facilitated diffusion uses specific membrane proteins (channels or carriers) to move substances down their electrochemical gradients without direct energy input. Active transport uses ATP to move against gradients. Bulk flow is a separate process, and simple diffusion through the bilayer does not require proteins.", "correctAnswer": "It requires a membrane carrier or channel but does not consume ATP"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It doubles", "It quadruples", "It halves", "It remains the same"], "question": "If the diffusion distance for a solute increases from 5 µm to 10 µm in a tissue, how does the expected time for diffusion change, assuming diffusion coefficient and gradient are unchanged?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Diffusion time scales with the square of distance (t ∝ x^2/D). Doubling the distance from 5 to 10 µm increases time by a factor of (10/5)^2 = 4, making diffusion much slower over longer distances.", "correctAnswer": "It quadruples"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["O2", "Na+", "Glucose", "Water"], "question": "Which molecule would diffuse most rapidly through a pure phospholipid bilayer (no channels present)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Small, nonpolar molecules like O2 cross lipid bilayers fastest by simple diffusion. Charged ions (Na+) have very low permeability without channels. Glucose is large and polar, and water, while small, is polar and diffuses slower than nonpolar gases unless aquaporins are present.", "correctAnswer": "O2"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "At diffusion equilibrium across a membrane, individual molecules stop moving across the membrane.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At equilibrium, net flux is zero, but molecules continue to move randomly in both directions. The rates of movement in and out are equal, so there is no net change, but microscopic motion persists.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Increasing temperature generally increases the diffusion coefficient and thus the rate of diffusion for a solute in a fluid.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Higher temperature increases molecular kinetic energy and reduces solvent viscosity, typically increasing the diffusion coefficient (D) and speeding diffusion, all else equal.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "According to Fick's first law, the net diffusive flux of a solute is proportional to the ______ gradient across a membrane.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Fick's first law states that flux is proportional to the concentration gradient (and negatively directed from high to low). In gases, partial pressure is analogous, but the underlying driver is the concentration (or chemical potential) difference.", "correctAnswer": "concentration"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "During osmosis across a semipermeable membrane that is impermeable to solute, water moves toward the side with higher ______ concentration.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water moves from lower solute concentration (higher water potential) to higher solute concentration (lower water potential) to equalize chemical potentials, resulting in net movement toward the side with more solute.", "correctAnswer": "solute"}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A cell is placed in a solution with 140 mM Na+ outside and 10 mM Na+ inside. The membrane is permeable to Na+ and there is no electrical potential difference. Predict the net direction of Na+ diffusion and what happens over time.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The concentration gradient favors Na+ moving from 140 mM outside to 10 mM inside. Without an opposing electrical gradient or active transport, Na+ diffuses into the cell, reducing the gradient until inside and outside concentrations approach equality (assuming equal volumes or until other constraints intervene).", "correctAnswer": "Net diffusion into the cell (from outside to inside) until concentrations equalize."}, {"id": "q_1770138450580_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You are tasked with increasing the diffusion rate of a small neutral solute across an epithelial membrane without changing the concentration gradient. Name two specific modifications you could make and why they would help.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Diffusion rate increases with a higher diffusion coefficient (raised by increasing temperature) and decreases with diffusion distance/membrane thickness. Other valid options include increasing membrane surface area (e.g., microvilli), increasing membrane permeability (e.g., adding more channels/carriers for facilitated diffusion), or reducing the unstirred boundary layer.", "correctAnswer": "Increase temperature and decrease membrane thickness."}]	none	2026-02-03 17:07:30.674223	\N
831039b6-e812-45e5-9c8d-d9cd9362eff1	258764d5-9b9c-4159-b109-a245f042ad13	population - Easy	population	easy	[{"id": "q_1770138842635_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A group of individuals of the same species living in a specific area", "All species living on Earth", "All the plants and animals in an ecosystem", "A list of different communities"], "question": "What is a population?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In biology and human geography, a population is defined as all individuals of the same species (or people) living in a particular area at a given time.", "correctAnswer": "A group of individuals of the same species living in a specific area"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["An official count of people in a country", "A measure of birth rates", "A survey of weather", "A political election"], "question": "What is a census?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A census is a systematic, official count and description of the population, often including information like age, sex, and housing.", "correctAnswer": "An official count of people in a country"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Population density is the number of people living per unit area (e.g., per square kilometer).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Population density measures how crowded a place is by dividing the number of people by the land area.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The movement of people into a country is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Immigration increases the population of a country because people arrive to live there.", "correctAnswer": "immigration"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_4", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The movement of people out of a country is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Emigration decreases a country’s population because people move away to live elsewhere.", "correctAnswer": "emigration"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The number of births per 1,000 people per year", "The number of births per square kilometer", "The total babies born in history", "The number of hospitals per 1,000 people"], "question": "Birth rate usually expresses:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Birth rate is commonly reported as births per 1,000 population per year to standardize comparisons between places.", "correctAnswer": "The number of births per 1,000 people per year"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The number of deaths per 1,000 people per year", "The number of deaths per square kilometer", "The total deaths in history", "The average age of the population"], "question": "Death rate usually expresses:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Death rate (mortality rate) is commonly reported as deaths per 1,000 population per year to make comparisons meaningful.", "correctAnswer": "The number of deaths per 1,000 people per year"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is urbanization?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Urbanization describes the shift of population from rural areas to towns and cities, increasing the share of people in urban places.", "correctAnswer": "The increase in the proportion of people living in urban areas (growth of cities)"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Life expectancy is the average number of years a person is expected to live.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Life expectancy summarizes overall mortality patterns and indicates the typical lifespan in a population.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Immigration", "Emigration", "Higher death rate", "Increased disease"], "question": "Which factor directly increases the size of a population?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Immigration adds people to a population. Emigration and higher death rates remove people, lowering population size.", "correctAnswer": "Immigration"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Population growth is affected by births, deaths, immigration, and emigration.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "These four components determine how a population changes over time: births and immigration add people; deaths and emigration remove people.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The study of human populations, including size, distribution, and trends, is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Demography is the statistical study of human populations, examining aspects like age structure, fertility, mortality, and migration.", "correctAnswer": "demography"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["How people are spread across space", "The total number of people in a country", "The percentage of males to females", "The average income of households"], "question": "Population distribution refers to:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Distribution shows where people live and how they are dispersed within a region, not the total number of people.", "correctAnswer": "How people are spread across space"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give one common reason people migrate from rural to urban areas.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "People often move to cities for jobs; other reasons include access to education, healthcare, and services.", "correctAnswer": "Employment opportunities"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_14", "type": "true_false", "question": "Carrying capacity is the maximum number of individuals an environment can support sustainably.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Carrying capacity depends on available resources and limits long-term population size without degrading the environment.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_15", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Children and adolescents", "Elderly (65+)", "Middle-aged adults", "Infants only"], "question": "A population with a large proportion of which age group often indicates potential future growth?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A youthful age structure means many individuals will soon reach reproductive age, which can lead to future population growth.", "correctAnswer": "Children and adolescents"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_16", "type": "true_false", "question": "A country's population can decline even when births still occur.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "If deaths and emigration exceed births and immigration, the total population can decrease despite ongoing births.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_17", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Disease", "Food availability", "Natural disasters", "Competition for resources"], "question": "Which is NOT typically a density-dependent factor affecting population size?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Density-dependent factors (like disease and competition) intensify as population density rises. Natural disasters affect populations regardless of density.", "correctAnswer": "Natural disasters"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_18", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A graph that shows the age structure and sex of a population is called a population _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A population pyramid displays the number of males and females in different age groups, often forming a pyramid shape.", "correctAnswer": "pyramid"}, {"id": "q_1770138842635_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What term describes the percentage of people living in towns and cities compared to rural areas?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The urbanization rate measures the share of the population living in urban areas, indicating how urbanized a country is.", "correctAnswer": "Urbanization rate"}]	none	2026-02-03 17:14:02.718623	\N
6ca37be9-e978-43d2-b072-03a368da876c	bc51b33c-2d25-4884-b39b-b25e8a12cf31	Biology the 5 kingdoms - Medium	Biology the 5 kingdoms	medium	[{"id": "q_1770149395860_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Monera", "Protista", "Fungi", "Plantae"], "question": "In the five-kingdom classification, which kingdom consists of organisms that lack a membrane-bound nucleus?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Monera includes prokaryotes (bacteria and archaea) that lack a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia are eukaryotic and have membrane-bound nuclei.", "correctAnswer": "Monera"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Fungi are absorptive heterotrophs", "Fungi use chlorophyll for photosynthesis", "Plants digest food externally and absorb it", "Plants are unicellular heterotrophs"], "question": "Which statement best explains a key nutritional difference between Fungi and Plantae?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Fungi secrete enzymes to digest organic matter externally and absorb nutrients, while plants are autotrophs that use chlorophyll to photosynthesize. This nutritional mode is a defining distinction.", "correctAnswer": "Fungi are absorptive heterotrophs"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Protists are eukaryotic and may be unicellular or simple multicellular organisms.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Protista encompasses diverse eukaryotes, including unicellular protozoa and algae, as well as some simple multicellular forms like certain seaweeds.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Monera", "Protista", "Fungi", "Animalia"], "question": "A unicellular organism without membrane-bound organelles that thrives in extreme environments is most likely classified in which kingdom?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Organisms like archaea and bacteria are prokaryotic and belong to Monera in the five-kingdom system. Many archaea inhabit extreme environments, and they lack membrane-bound organelles.", "correctAnswer": "Monera"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_4", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The primary structural polymer in fungal cell walls is _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Fungal cell walls are rich in chitin, a nitrogen-containing polysaccharide, whereas plant cell walls are primarily composed of cellulose.", "correctAnswer": "chitin"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why viruses are not placed within any of the five kingdoms.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The five kingdoms classify cellular life. Viruses do not have cells or cellular processes like ATP-based metabolism and rely entirely on host machinery, placing them outside this system.", "correctAnswer": "Viruses are acellular, lack metabolism, and cannot reproduce independently; they require host cells, so they do not meet the criteria for living cellular organisms in the five kingdoms."}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Protista", "Monera", "Fungi", "Animalia"], "question": "Which kingdom includes organisms such as Amoeba and Paramecium?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Amoeba and Paramecium are unicellular eukaryotes with complex organelles and behaviors, fitting the kingdom Protista.", "correctAnswer": "Protista"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mycorrhizae", "Lichens", "Rust infections", "Saprophytism"], "question": "Which of the following represents a mutualistic relationship involving fungi and plant roots?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Mycorrhizae are mutualistic associations between fungal hyphae and plant roots, enhancing nutrient and water uptake for the plant and providing carbohydrates to the fungus. Lichens involve fungi and algae/cyanobacteria.", "correctAnswer": "Mycorrhizae"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "All members of the kingdom Animalia are heterotrophic and lack cell walls.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Animals ingest food (heterotrophy) and their cells do not have rigid cell walls, distinguishing them from plants and fungi.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Eukaryotic cells", "Prokaryotic cells", "Chitin cell walls", "Obligate motility using cilia"], "question": "Which feature is shared by both Plantae and Protista?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Both kingdoms consist of eukaryotic organisms with membrane-bound nuclei. Prokaryotes are in Monera; chitin walls are typical of fungi; not all members use cilia.", "correctAnswer": "Eukaryotic cells"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the five-kingdom system, blue-green algae are placed in the kingdom _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Blue-green algae are cyanobacteria, which are prokaryotic and thus classified under Monera in the five-kingdom scheme.", "correctAnswer": "Monera"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_11", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Describe one ecological role of protists in aquatic environments.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Many protists perform photosynthesis, fixing carbon and supporting higher trophic levels. Their productivity influences nutrient cycles and ecosystem stability.", "correctAnswer": "Photosynthetic protists (phytoplankton) act as primary producers, forming the base of aquatic food webs and contributing significantly to global oxygen production."}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Binary fission", "Budding", "Fragmentation", "Sexual reproduction with gametes"], "question": "Which reproductive strategy is most commonly observed in bacteria?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Bacteria typically reproduce asexually by binary fission, where a single cell divides into two genetically identical daughter cells.", "correctAnswer": "Binary fission"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_13", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Plantae", "Fungi", "Animalia", "Protista"], "question": "A multicellular organism that is non-motile, photosynthetic, and has cellulose cell walls belongs to which kingdom?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Plantae includes multicellular, primarily non-motile autotrophs with cellulose cell walls and chloroplasts for photosynthesis.", "correctAnswer": "Plantae"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_14", "type": "true_false", "question": "Fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Molecular phylogenetics shows fungi and animals share a more recent common ancestor than either does with plants; both are heterotrophs and lack chlorophyll.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_15", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Yeast", "Euglena", "Moss", "Hydra"], "question": "Which organism is correctly classified under the kingdom Fungi?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Yeast are unicellular fungi. Euglena is a protist, moss is a plant, and Hydra is an animal.", "correctAnswer": "Yeast"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The kingdom that includes organisms that are mostly multicellular, ingestive heterotrophs is _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Animals ingest food and are typically multicellular with specialized tissues, placing them in Animalia.", "correctAnswer": "Animalia"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one cellular characteristic that distinguishes Monera from Protista.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The prokaryotic vs. eukaryotic distinction is primary: Monera are prokaryotes; Protista are eukaryotes.", "correctAnswer": "Monera lack a membrane-bound nucleus and organelles, whereas Protista are eukaryotic with a true nucleus and organelles."}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_18", "type": "true_false", "question": "Lichens are classified under Plantae because they photosynthesize.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Lichens are symbiotic associations dominated by fungi, typically paired with algae or cyanobacteria. They are classified with fungi despite photosynthesis by the partner.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770149395860_19", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Protista", "Monera", "Fungi", "Animalia"], "question": "Which kingdom is most diverse in forms and nutrition, including algae, protozoans, and slime molds?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Protista is a broad, diverse group of eukaryotes that do not fit neatly into the other kingdoms, encompassing autotrophs (algae), heterotrophs (protozoans), and organisms like slime molds.", "correctAnswer": "Protista"}]	none	2026-02-03 20:09:58.337788	\N
e383b80d-f983-4d3c-9403-a78314c3c8b9	bc51b33c-2d25-4884-b39b-b25e8a12cf31	Mathematics  - Medium	Mathematics 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770232740601_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-8", "-2", "8", "16"], "question": "Let A be the 2x2 matrix [[1, 2], [3, 4]]. What is det(2A)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For an n×n matrix, det(kA) = k^n det(A). Here n = 2, so det(2A) = 2^2 det(A) = 4 det(A). Compute det(A) = (1)(4) - (2)(3) = 4 - 6 = -2. Thus det(2A) = 4 × (-2) = -8.", "correctAnswer": "-8"}, {"id": "q_1770232740601_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5, and P(A ∩ B) = 0.3, then events A and B are independent.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A and B are independent if P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). Here, P(A)P(B) = 0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3, which matches P(A ∩ B), so the events are independent.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770232740601_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Evaluate the sum S = 2*(0.5)^0 + 2*(0.5)^1 + 2*(0.5)^2 + 2*(0.5)^3 + 2*(0.5)^4.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is a geometric series with first term a = 2, ratio r = 0.5, and 5 terms. The sum is S = a(1 - r^n)/(1 - r) = 2(1 - 0.5^5)/(1 - 0.5) = 2(1 - 1/32)/0.5 = 4 × (31/32) = 31/8.", "correctAnswer": "31/8"}, {"id": "q_1770232740601_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the inequality |2x − 5| < 3. Provide the solution set for x.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Translate |2x − 5| < 3 into −3 < 2x − 5 < 3. Add 5: 2 < 2x < 8. Divide by 2: 1 < x < 4.", "correctAnswer": "1 < x < 4"}, {"id": "q_1770232740601_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A right triangle has legs of lengths 6 and 8. What is the radius of its inscribed circle (inradius)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The hypotenuse is c = √(6^2 + 8^2) = 10. The area is (1/2)(6)(8) = 24, and the semiperimeter is s = (6 + 8 + 10)/2 = 12. The inradius r = Area/s = 24/12 = 2.", "correctAnswer": "2"}]	none	2026-02-04 19:19:00.687457	\N
7606d28d-d0b2-4801-ad77-69de58a1bbb9	2f385e50-9848-47b3-9b1c-353d794b5445	Photosynthesis - Medium	Photosynthesis	medium	[{"id": "q_1770317739165_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Reduced ATP and NADPH supply from the light reactions.", "Immediate decline in atmospheric CO2 concentration around the leaf.", "Permanent loss of chlorophyll pigments.", "Increased Rubisco abundance."], "question": "A potted C3 plant is moved from bright light to deep shade. Which change most directly limits the Calvin cycle under these conditions?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Calvin cycle relies on ATP and NADPH generated by the light reactions to fix carbon and reduce 3-PGA to G3P. In deep shade, photon flux drops, lowering photophosphorylation and NADPH formation. This directly restricts RuBP regeneration and the reduction steps of the Calvin cycle, thereby reducing the rate of sugar production.", "correctAnswer": "Reduced ATP and NADPH supply from the light reactions."}, {"id": "q_1770317739165_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The oxygen released during photosynthesis originates from carbon dioxide.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Oxygen released in photosynthesis comes from water, not CO2. During the light reactions at photosystem II, water is split (photolysis), producing electrons, protons, and O2. CO2 is reduced in the Calvin cycle to form carbohydrates but does not generate O2.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770317739165_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In C4 plants, CO2 is initially fixed by ______ in mesophyll cells to form oxaloacetate, which is later decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 (as bicarbonate) to phosphoenolpyruvate, forming oxaloacetate. It has a high affinity for CO2 and does not bind O2, allowing C4 plants to concentrate CO2 around Rubisco in bundle sheath cells and minimize photorespiration.", "correctAnswer": "PEP carboxylase"}, {"id": "q_1770317739165_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why the photosynthetic rate of a C3 plant may decline at very high temperatures even when light and CO2 are abundant.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At high temperatures, stomata often close to reduce water loss, elevating the internal O2/CO2 ratio. Rubisco more frequently acts as an oxygenase, triggering photorespiration, which consumes ATP and NADPH without producing sugars. Heat stress can also impair enzyme function and membrane integrity, further slowing carbon fixation.", "correctAnswer": "High temperatures promote stomatal closure and increase Rubisco’s oxygenase activity, raising photorespiration; Calvin cycle enzymes can lose efficiency, so net photosynthesis drops."}, {"id": "q_1770317739165_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["O2 evolution ceases; NADPH production drops severely; ATP may continue via cyclic photophosphorylation.", "O2 evolution increases; NADPH rises; ATP synthesis stops completely.", "O2 evolution is unchanged; NADPH production increases due to PSI.", "Only Rubisco activity changes; light reactions are unaffected."], "question": "A researcher applies a herbicide (DCMU) that blocks electron transfer from photosystem II to plastoquinone. What immediate changes would you expect in the chloroplast?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "DCMU inhibits electron flow from PSII, preventing reoxidation of PSII acceptors and halting water splitting turnover—so O2 evolution stops. Electrons cannot reach PSI for linear electron flow, so NADPH formation collapses. However, PSI can still cycle electrons in cyclic photophosphorylation, allowing some ATP production without NADPH or O2 release.", "correctAnswer": "O2 evolution ceases; NADPH production drops severely; ATP may continue via cyclic photophosphorylation."}]	none	2026-02-05 18:55:39.251518	\N
35edfc33-bdb3-41d4-a301-a617a0a6bebf	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science diseases - Easy	Science diseases	easy	[{"id": "q_1770317927321_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Bacterium", "Virus", "Fungus", "Protozoan"], "question": "Which type of microorganism causes tuberculosis (TB)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which is a bacterium. Viruses cause illnesses like influenza, fungi can cause conditions like athlete’s foot, and protozoa cause diseases such as malaria.", "correctAnswer": "Bacterium"}, {"id": "q_1770317927321_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Vaccines help the body build immunity to a disease before you get sick.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Vaccines safely expose the immune system to parts of a germ (like proteins), training it to recognize and fight the disease in the future without causing the illness itself.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770317927321_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Malaria is transmitted to humans through the bite of an infected ______ mosquito.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit malaria by carrying Plasmodium parasites. Other mosquitoes (like Aedes) spread different diseases such as dengue and Zika.", "correctAnswer": "Anopheles"}, {"id": "q_1770317927321_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does it mean when a disease is described as \\"contagious\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A contagious disease is communicable, meaning it can pass between people through routes like direct contact, respiratory droplets, or contaminated surfaces.", "correctAnswer": "It can spread from person to person."}, {"id": "q_1770317927321_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Bacterial infections", "Viral infections", "Genetic disorders", "Allergies"], "question": "Antibiotics are designed to treat which of the following?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Antibiotics target bacteria and are effective against bacterial infections. They do not work on viruses, and they do not treat genetic disorders or allergies.", "correctAnswer": "Bacterial infections"}]	none	2026-02-05 18:58:47.406779	\N
a0a9feb1-743d-4f7d-8aae-1275e549435f	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Setswana/tlhogo and thito - Easy	Setswana/tlhogo and thito	easy	[{"id": "q_1770650558996_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Karolo ya polelo e e supang motho/seo re buang ka sone (subject).", "Karolo ya polelo e e bolelang tiro e e dirwang (predicate).", "Lereo le le supang tiro (lediri).", "Karolo e e amogelang tiro mo polelong (seamogedi/objekte)."], "question": "Tlhogo ke eng mo polelong ya Setswana?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tlhogo ke karolo ya polelo e e bontshang motho kgotsa sengwe se polelo e ka sone; ke “subject.” Sekai: Mo ‘Bana ba a tshameka,’ ‘Bana’ ke tlhogo.", "correctAnswer": "Karolo ya polelo e e supang motho/seo re buang ka sone (subject)."}, {"id": "q_1770650558996_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Bana", "ba a tshameka", "tshameka", "Bana ba"], "question": "Mo polelong 'Bana ba a tshameka', ke efe thito?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Thito ke karolo e e bolelang tiro kgotsa se se dirwang ke tlhogo; mo go ‘Bana ba a tshameka,’ thito ke ‘ba a tshameka’ ka gonne e bontsha tiro ya go tshameka.", "correctAnswer": "ba a tshameka"}, {"id": "q_1770650558996_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "Mo polelong 'Morutabana o ruta bana', 'bana' ke tlhogo.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Morutabana' ke tlhogo ka gonne ke motho yo polelo e buang ka ene. ‘o ruta bana’ ke thito, mme ‘bana’ ke karolo ya thito e e amogelang tiro (objekte).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770650558996_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "_____ ke karolo ya polelo e e bolelang se tlhogo e se dirang kgotsa e leng sona.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Thito e akaretsa lediri le mafoko a a amanang le lone, e bolele tiro kgotsa boemo jwa tlhogo mo polelong.", "correctAnswer": "Thito"}, {"id": "q_1770650558996_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Mo polelong 'Mme o a opela', ngola tlhogo.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tlhogo ke se re buang ka sone; mo sekai seno ke ‘Mme.’ Karolo e e setseng, ‘o a opela,’ ke thito e e bolelang tiro.", "correctAnswer": "Mme"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:22:41.081296	\N
0e669d04-f5e5-4015-bc0c-b0665db323ef	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science diseases - Easy	Science diseases	easy	[{"id": "q_1770318005346_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A virus", "A bacterium", "A protozoan parasite (Plasmodium)", "A fungus"], "question": "Malaria is caused by which type of organism?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Malaria is caused by Plasmodium species, which are protozoan parasites transmitted to humans through the bites of infected Anopheles mosquitoes. It is not caused by viruses, bacteria, or fungi.", "correctAnswer": "A protozoan parasite (Plasmodium)"}, {"id": "q_1770318005346_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Vaccines help prevent many infectious diseases by training the immune system to recognize pathogens.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Vaccines safely expose the immune system to parts of a pathogen (like proteins or weakened/killed forms), allowing the body to build memory so it can respond rapidly if exposed in the future.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770318005346_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The disease characterized by high blood sugar due to problems with insulin is called ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Diabetes (most commonly Type 1 or Type 2) involves issues with insulin production or use, leading to elevated blood glucose levels.", "correctAnswer": "diabetes"}, {"id": "q_1770318005346_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which organ is primarily affected by hepatitis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hepatitis literally means inflammation of the liver. It can be caused by viruses (like hepatitis A, B, or C), toxins, or autoimmune conditions.", "correctAnswer": "liver"}, {"id": "q_1770318005346_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Escherichia coli", "Mycobacterium tuberculosis"], "question": "Which bacterium most commonly causes strep throat?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Strep throat is an infection of the throat and tonsils most often caused by Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Streptococcus). The other listed bacteria are associated with different diseases.", "correctAnswer": "Steptococcus pyogenes"}]	none	2026-02-05 19:00:30.265263	\N
bad757ba-10c6-42ed-848e-0694bead63b1	3566b1de-3607-4759-aa95-75efdf6b52c5	MATH - Easy	MATH	easy	[{"id": "q_1770376082800_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. One way to see this is 7 + 3 = 10, then 10 + 2 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770376082800_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square has four sides of equal length and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770376082800_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 × __ = 45", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "9 times 5 equals 45 because 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 45. So the missing number is 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770376082800_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the place value of the digit 4 in the number 348?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 348, the digits are: 3 in the hundreds place, 4 in the tens place, and 8 in the ones place. So the digit 4 is in the tens place (its value is 40).", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1770376082800_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7 cm", "12 cm", "14 cm", "24 cm"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 4 cm and width 3 cm?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width). So 2 × (4 + 3) = 2 × 7 = 14 cm.", "correctAnswer": "14 cm"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:08:02.814139	\N
86e18c38-66a5-4b3d-8421-f200a0c58d41	578fe8af-8576-422c-b2de-4ea3d55d5294	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1770376205824_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An input device sends data to the computer (e.g., a keyboard). The monitor, printer, and speaker are output devices that display or produce results.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The CPU is often called the 'brain' of the computer.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The CPU (Central Processing Unit) performs most of the processing and controls other components, so it's commonly referred to as the computer's brain.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The operating system that comes pre-installed on most smartphones made by Apple is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Apple’s iPhone uses iOS, while Android is used by many other manufacturers.", "correctAnswer": "iOS"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".docx", ".mp3", ".jpg", ".exe"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for a Microsoft Word document?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".docx is the default Word document format. .mp3 is audio, .jpg is an image, and .exe is an executable program.", "correctAnswer": ".docx"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the abbreviation 'URL' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A URL specifies the address of a resource on the internet, such as a web page.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Google Drive", "DVD", "USB flash drive", "External hard disk"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of cloud storage?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Google Drive stores files on remote servers accessed via the internet (the cloud). DVDs, USB flash drives, and external hard disks are local, physical storage.", "correctAnswer": "Google Drive"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Phishing emails often try to trick you into sharing passwords by pretending to be from a trusted source.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Phishing is a social engineering attack that imitates legitimate organizations to steal information like passwords and credit card numbers.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["LAN", "WAN", "PAN", "MAN"], "question": "Which type of network typically covers a small area like a home, office, or classroom?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A Local Area Network (LAN) connects devices in a limited area like a home or classroom. WANs cover large distances, MANs cover a city, and PANs connect personal devices within a few meters.", "correctAnswer": "LAN"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The hardware component that temporarily stores data for quick access while a computer is running is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Random Access Memory (RAM) holds data and programs that the CPU needs right now, making processing faster.", "correctAnswer": "RAM"}, {"id": "q_1770376205824_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A web browser is the same as a search engine.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A web browser (like Chrome or Firefox) is the software used to access the web. A search engine (like Google or Bing) is a website or service that helps you find information on the web.", "correctAnswer": "False"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:10:05.9323	\N
2076091f-2932-4b45-91a1-8e1db9ec74b9	7c09660a-f7d3-47dc-9c9f-ae65a239b57a	chemistry - Medium	chemistry	medium	[{"id": "q_1770376296241_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["K increases", "K decreases", "K remains unchanged", "A catalyst would increase K"], "question": "For the equilibrium N2(g) + 3 H2(g) ⇌ 2 NH3(g), ΔH° = −92 kJ/mol (exothermic), what happens to the equilibrium constant K when the temperature is increased?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For an exothermic reaction, heat can be viewed as a product. Increasing temperature adds heat, shifting the equilibrium toward reactants (Le Chatelier’s principle). The van ’t Hoff equation shows that for exothermic reactions, increasing temperature decreases K, because the equilibrium favors the side that absorbs heat.", "correctAnswer": "K decreases"}, {"id": "q_1770376296241_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If doubling the concentration of reactant A doubles the initial rate, the reaction must be second order in A.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Doubling [A] doubling the rate indicates the rate is proportional to [A]^1, which is first order in A, not second order. A second-order dependence would cause the rate to increase by a factor of four when [A] is doubled (rate ∝ [A]^2).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770376296241_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A buffer is made by dissolving 0.10 mol NH3 and 0.20 mol NH4Cl in water to make 1.00 L of solution. Given pKb(NH3) = 4.75, the pH of the buffer is ____ (to two decimal places).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For the conjugate acid NH4+, pKa = 14 − pKb = 14 − 4.75 = 9.25. Using Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]) = 9.25 + log(0.10/0.20) = 9.25 + log(0.5) = 9.25 − 0.301 ≈ 8.95.", "correctAnswer": "8.95"}, {"id": "q_1770376296241_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In terms of colligative properties, why is CaCl2 often more effective than NaCl at de-icing roads?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Colligative properties depend on the number of dissolved particles, not their identity. CaCl2 dissociates into three ions (Ca2+ and 2 Cl−), whereas NaCl dissociates into two (Na+ and Cl−). The higher van ’t Hoff factor for CaCl2 yields a larger freezing-point depression, making it more effective at lowering ice’s melting point.", "correctAnswer": "It produces more ions (higher van ’t Hoff factor), causing greater freezing-point depression."}, {"id": "q_1770376296241_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1.10 V", "-1.10 V", "0.34 V", "0.76 V"], "question": "Given standard reduction potentials: Cu2+ + 2 e− → Cu (E° = +0.34 V) and Zn2+ + 2 e− → Zn (E° = −0.76 V), what is E°cell for the galvanic cell Zn(s) | Zn2+(aq) || Cu2+(aq) | Cu(s)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In the galvanic cell, Cu2+ is reduced at the cathode (E°cathode = +0.34 V) and Zn is oxidized at the anode; use the reduction potential for Zn2+/Zn as the anode’s reverse process. E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V, indicating a spontaneous cell.", "correctAnswer": "1.10 V"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:11:36.329885	\N
3047e5b8-3d4a-4fcd-bcf9-9c28d78a6f80	7c09660a-f7d3-47dc-9c9f-ae65a239b57a	chemistry - Easy	chemistry	easy	[{"id": "q_1770376573849_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Atom", "Molecule", "Proton", "Cell"], "question": "What is the smallest particle of an element that retains the properties of that element?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An atom is the basic unit of an element that retains its chemical properties. Molecules are combinations of atoms, protons are subatomic particles within atoms, and cells are biological structures.", "correctAnswer": "Atom"}, {"id": "q_1770376573849_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Water freezing into ice is a chemical change.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Freezing is a physical change because water (H2O) remains chemically the same; only its state changes from liquid to solid.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770376573849_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The chemical symbol for sodium is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sodium’s symbol is Na, derived from its Latin name ‘Natrium.’ Chemical symbols are one or two letters, often based on Latin names.", "correctAnswer": "Na"}, {"id": "q_1770376573849_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the three common states of matter found at everyday temperatures.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The three common states of matter are solid (fixed shape), liquid (fixed volume but variable shape), and gas (no fixed shape or volume). Plasma exists at very high energies and is less common in everyday conditions.", "correctAnswer": "Solid, liquid, gas"}, {"id": "q_1770376573849_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Neutron", "Proton", "Electron", "Nucleus"], "question": "Which subatomic particle has a negative charge?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Electrons carry a negative charge, protons are positively charged, and neutrons are neutral. The nucleus contains protons and neutrons and has an overall positive charge due to protons.", "correctAnswer": "Electron"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:16:13.863338	\N
e93dd18e-0fdf-45bc-9f12-9edd64a45abd	fed3cd47-f85d-4803-bd82-53f87d73c2dc	MATHS - Medium	MATHS	medium	[{"id": "q_1770376604095_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "6", "7", "8"], "question": "For the function f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x + 1, what is the average rate of change from x = 1 to x = 4?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The average rate of change on [a, b] is [f(b) − f(a)] / (b − a). Compute f(4) = 2(16) − 3(4) + 1 = 32 − 12 + 1 = 21 and f(1) = 2(1) − 3(1) + 1 = 0. Then (21 − 0)/(4 − 1) = 21/3 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "7"}, {"id": "q_1770376604095_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: The lines 3x − 4y = 12 and 6x − 8y = 24 are distinct and parallel.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Multiplying the first equation by 2 gives 6x − 8y = 24, which is exactly the second equation. They are the same line, not distinct parallel lines.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770376604095_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A right triangle has legs of lengths x and x + 1, and a hypotenuse of length x + 2. Find the positive value of x.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By the Pythagorean theorem: x^2 + (x + 1)^2 = (x + 2)^2. Simplifying gives x^2 + x^2 + 2x + 1 = x^2 + 4x + 4 ⇒ x^2 − 2x − 3 = 0 ⇒ (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0. The positive solution is x = 3.", "correctAnswer": "3"}, {"id": "q_1770376604095_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A data set has n numbers with mean 50. After adding one new value 80, the new mean becomes 52. What is n?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Original sum is 50n. After adding 80, the sum is 50n + 80 and the count is n + 1. Set (50n + 80)/(n + 1) = 52 ⇒ 50n + 80 = 52n + 52 ⇒ 28 = 2n ⇒ n = 14.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1770376604095_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/3", "5/12", "1/2", "7/18"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is a prime number?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Prime sums from two dice (2–12) are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11. Favorable outcomes: 2 (1 way), 3 (2), 5 (4), 7 (6), 11 (2) for a total of 15. There are 36 total outcomes, so the probability is 15/36 = 5/12.", "correctAnswer": "5/12"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:16:44.109117	\N
7a053c9b-6d88-45cc-95e5-e14d577257c3	7c09660a-f7d3-47dc-9c9f-ae65a239b57a	maths - Easy	maths	easy	[{"id": "q_1770376663815_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "12", "13", "11"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. You can count up from 7: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 (five steps).", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770376663815_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770376663815_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 − 4 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting means taking away. If you start at 9 and take away 4, you land on 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770376663815_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the value of the digit 3 in the number 348?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 348, the 3 is in the hundreds place, which means it represents 3 hundreds, or 300.", "correctAnswer": "300"}, {"id": "q_1770376663815_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "2/3", "3/5", "3/4"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "2/4 simplifies to 1/2 because both numerator and denominator can be divided by 2, giving 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:17:43.899471	\N
7f983905-45ee-4cf8-99b7-10d1fe1ecc0c	3566b1de-3607-4759-aa95-75efdf6b52c5	english - Medium	english	medium	[{"id": "q_1770376679970_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The program helps students to think critically, communicate clearly, and problem-solving effectively.", "The program helps students think critically, communicate clearly, and solve problems effectively.", "The program helps students with thinking critically, to communicate clearly, and solving problems effectively.", "The program helps students to think critically, communicating clearly, and solve problems effectively."], "question": "Which sentence maintains correct parallel structure?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires items in a series to share the same grammatical form. After \\"helps students,\\" the verbs should match. Option B uses balanced bare verb forms: think, communicate, solve. The other options mix gerunds, infinitives, or participles, breaking parallelism.", "correctAnswer": "The program helps students think critically, communicate clearly, and solve problems effectively."}, {"id": "q_1770376679970_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["outweighed", "outweigh", "were outweighing", "had outweigh"], "question": "Choose the option that best completes the sentence: \\"Despite her initial reluctance, she ultimately acquiesced to the plan, recognizing that the benefits ______ the risks.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The sentence is narrated in the past (\\"acquiesced\\"). To maintain tense consistency, use the simple past: \\"benefits outweighed the risks.\\" Additionally, \\"benefits\\" is plural, so the verb form must agree. The other options are grammatically or syntactically incorrect here.", "correctAnswer": "outweighed"}, {"id": "q_1770376679970_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: A complex sentence must contain at least two independent clauses.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "False. A complex sentence contains one independent clause and at least one dependent (subordinate) clause. A sentence with two or more independent clauses (and no dependent clause required) is a compound sentence.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770376679970_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb form: \\"The scientist, as well as her colleagues, ____ presenting their findings at the conference.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "\\"As well as\\" introduces a parenthetical phrase and does not make the subject plural. The head subject is \\"The scientist\\" (singular), so the correct verb is \\"is,\\" not \\"are.\\"", "correctAnswer": "is"}, {"id": "q_1770376679970_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence in active voice: \\"The proposal was rejected by the committee owing to budget constraints.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Active voice places the subject (the doer) before the verb. Here, \\"the committee\\" performs the action, so the active version is \\"The committee rejected the proposal...\\" This construction is more direct and concise.", "correctAnswer": "The committee rejected the proposal owing to budget constraints."}]	none	2026-02-06 11:18:00.05442	\N
0e3bfdbf-20f9-4c55-8e58-1a967af5c291	bc5894e7-b809-4057-9e30-cc0322b62334	photosynthesis - Medium	photosynthesis	medium	[{"id": "q_1770378061429_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It decreases because chlorophyll reflects much of the green light", "It increases because green light penetrates deeper into the leaf", "It stays approximately the same because intensity is unchanged", "It stops entirely because green light cannot drive photosynthesis"], "question": "A healthy leaf is illuminated with blue light and shows a high rate of oxygen release. If the light is switched to predominantly green light of the same intensity, what is the most likely immediate effect on the rate of photosynthesis (oxygen evolution)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The action spectrum of photosynthesis mirrors pigment absorption. Chlorophylls absorb blue and red light efficiently but absorb green poorly, reflecting much of it. Switching to green light reduces the effective energy absorbed by photosystems, so the rate of electron flow and oxygen evolution declines even if total light intensity is unchanged.", "correctAnswer": "It decreases because chlorophyll reflects much of the green light"}, {"id": "q_1770378061429_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "During photosynthesis, the oxygen gas released by plants originates from the splitting of water molecules in the light reactions, not from carbon dioxide.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Isotope-tracing experiments (using H2^18O) show that the oxygen released by photosynthetic organisms comes from water, via photolysis at photosystem II. CO2 contributes carbon and oxygen atoms to carbohydrates, but the O2 gas produced is derived from H2O.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770378061429_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In C3 plants, the enzyme ______ catalyzes the first step of carbon fixation by adding CO2 to RuBP, but it can also react with O2, leading to photorespiration.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "RuBisCO (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) carboxylates RuBP in the Calvin cycle. Because it also has oxygenase activity, high O2 or low CO2 favors oxygenation, producing 2-phosphoglycolate and triggering photorespiration, which reduces net carbon gain.", "correctAnswer": "RuBisCO"}, {"id": "q_1770378061429_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A grower raises CO2 concentration in a greenhouse from 400 ppm to 800 ppm and initially sees faster growth, but the photosynthetic rate soon plateaus despite further CO2 increases. Explain what factor is likely limiting now and why.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is controlled by the most limiting factor (law of limiting factors). Once CO2 is sufficient, another resource—commonly light intensity (or temperature, or nutrient availability)—becomes limiting. If light is not increased, the electron transport rate and ATP/NADPH production cannot rise, so carboxylation in the Calvin cycle cannot accelerate further despite elevated CO2.", "correctAnswer": "Light intensity is now limiting, so the rate cannot increase further even with more CO2."}, {"id": "q_1770378061429_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Stroma", "Thylakoid lumen", "Cytosol", "Chloroplast intermembrane space"], "question": "During the light reactions in chloroplasts, where are protons primarily accumulated to generate the proton motive force that drives ATP synthesis?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Electron flow through photosystem II and the cytochrome b6f complex pumps protons from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen. Water splitting at PSII also adds protons to the lumen. The resulting proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane powers ATP synthase, which faces the stroma and synthesizes ATP there.", "correctAnswer": "Thylakoid lumen"}]	none	2026-02-06 11:41:01.531469	\N
9d40986f-bb83-4b31-9cc3-0aebb645c07c	97b2f3da-267a-42e7-a13e-a5f7660376cd	maths - Medium	maths	medium	[{"id": "q_1770385493473_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["y = -0.5x - 1.5", "y = 2x + 4", "y = -2x + 4", "y = 0.5x - 1.5"], "question": "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to 2y - 4x = 8 that passes through the point (1, -2). Give your answer in slope-intercept form.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "First rewrite 2y - 4x = 8 as y = 2x + 4, so its slope is 2. A line perpendicular to this has slope -1/2. Using point-slope form with point (1, -2): y + 2 = (-1/2)(x - 1). Simplify to y = (-1/2)x + 1/2 - 2 = (-1/2)x - 3/2, which is y = -0.5x - 1.5.", "correctAnswer": "y = -0.5x - 1.5"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The sum of interior angles of an n-sided polygon is (n - 2) × 180°. For a hexagon (n = 6), sum = (6 - 2) × 180° = 4 × 180° = 720°.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Simplify the product: (3x^2y)(-2xy^3) = ______", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Multiply coefficients: 3 × (-2) = -6. Add exponents on like bases: x^2 × x^1 = x^(2+1) = x^3 and y^1 × y^3 = y^(1+3) = y^4. Result: -6x^3y^4.", "correctAnswer": "-6x^3y^4"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Solve the system of equations: 2x + y = 7 and x - y = 1. Give x and y.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x - y = 1, get y = x - 1. Substitute into 2x + y = 7: 2x + (x - 1) = 7 ⇒ 3x - 1 = 7 ⇒ 3x = 8 ⇒ x = 8/3. Then y = x - 1 = 8/3 - 3/3 = 5/3.", "correctAnswer": "x = 8/3, y = 5/3"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(8, 9)", "(6, 12)", "(7, 8)", "(9, 10)"], "question": "Which pair of positive consecutive integers has a product of 72?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let the integers be n and n+1, so n(n+1) = 72 ⇒ n^2 + n - 72 = 0. Solve: Discriminant D = 1 + 288 = 289 ⇒ sqrt(D) = 17. Then n = (-1 + 17)/2 = 8 (reject negative for positive integers). So the pair is (8, 9).", "correctAnswer": "(8, 9)"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_5", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Evaluate: 5^3 × 5^(-2) = ______", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "For the same base, add exponents: 5^(3 + (-2)) = 5^1 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/9", "1/8", "5/36", "1/12"], "question": "Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is 9?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Outcomes that sum to 9 are (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3): 4 outcomes. Total outcomes are 36. Probability = 4/36 = 1/9.", "correctAnswer": "1/9"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "The function g(x) = |x - 4| attains its minimum value at x = 4, and that minimum value is 0.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The absolute value |x - 4| measures distance from x to 4. The smallest distance is zero at x = 4, so g(4) = 0 is the minimum.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices (1, 2), (4, 6), and (7, 2).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The points (1, 2) and (7, 2) form a horizontal base of length 6. The height is the vertical distance from (4, 6) to y = 2, which is 4. Area = (1/2) × base × height = (1/2) × 6 × 4 = 12 square units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770385493473_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["(x + 1)/x", "(x - 1)/x", "x/(x + 1)", "(x + 1)/(x - 1)"], "question": "Simplify the expression: (x^2 - 1)/(x^2 - x). Assume x is not equal to 0 or 1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Factor numerator and denominator: x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1); x^2 - x = x(x - 1). Cancel (x - 1) to get (x + 1)/x, valid for x ≠ 0, 1.", "correctAnswer": "(x + 1)/x"}]	none	2026-02-06 13:44:53.56274	\N
068f59cc-b26e-440f-aacb-f19789edb52a	97b2f3da-267a-42e7-a13e-a5f7660376cd	any - Easy	any	easy	[{"id": "q_1770385786231_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which device is primarily used to input text into a computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A keyboard is an input device used to type letters, numbers, and symbols into a computer. Monitors and speakers are output devices, and printers produce physical copies of digital documents.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["CPU", "Hard drive", "Monitor", "Mouse"], "question": "Which component is often called the 'brain' of the computer?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The CPU (Central Processing Unit) performs calculations and executes instructions, making it the core component that runs programs. Hard drives store data, monitors display information, and mice are input devices.", "correctAnswer": "CPU"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".txt", ".jpg", ".mp3", ".pdf"], "question": "Which file extension is commonly used for a plain text document created with Notepad?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plain text files created in basic editors like Notepad are saved with the .txt extension. .jpg is for images, .mp3 for audio, and .pdf for Portable Document Format documents.", "correctAnswer": ".txt"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Google Chrome", "Windows", "Linux", "USB"], "question": "Which of the following is a web browser?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Google Chrome is a web browser used to access websites. Windows and Linux are operating systems, and USB refers to a connection standard for devices and storage.", "correctAnswer": "Google Chrome"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is permanent storage that keeps data even when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) is volatile; it temporarily stores data for quick access and loses its contents when power is off. Permanent storage is provided by devices like hard drives and SSDs.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Internet and the World Wide Web are not the same thing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is a global network of interconnected computers, while the World Wide Web is a collection of websites and pages accessed via browsers using the Internet. The Web is one service that runs on the Internet.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The bar at the top of a web browser where you type website addresses is called the ______ bar.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 'address bar' (also called the URL bar) is where you enter web addresses, or URLs, to navigate to websites.", "correctAnswer": "address bar"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Copying a file to another folder creates a second ______ of that file.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When you copy a file, you create another copy of the original in a new location. The original remains unchanged, and the copy is a duplicate.", "correctAnswer": "copy"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym 'URL' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "URL stands for Uniform Resource Locator, which is the address used to find and access resources on the World Wide Web.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1770385786231_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one example of an output device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A monitor is an output device that displays visual information. Other examples include printers and speakers, which output printed documents and sound, respectively.", "correctAnswer": "Monitor"}]	none	2026-02-06 13:49:46.340375	\N
29dedc69-1dd8-41cd-9b3d-f819732e9999	4fac5831-4d3e-492c-a3fb-bc9f59bb6f94	English  - Easy	English 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770528028162_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["an", "a", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article: I have ___ apple.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We use 'an' before words that begin with a vowel sound. 'Apple' begins with the vowel sound /a/, so 'an apple' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["cat", "sleeps", "the", "sleeping"], "question": "Which word is a noun in the sentence: The cat sleeps.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun is a person, place, thing, or idea. 'Cat' is a thing. 'Sleeps' is a verb and 'the' is an article.", "correctAnswer": "cat"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["period", "comma", "exclamation mark", "question mark"], "question": "Which punctuation mark ends a question?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Questions end with a question mark (?). It signals that the sentence is asking something.", "correctAnswer": "question mark"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["on", "at", "in", "for"], "question": "Choose the correct preposition: The book is ___ the table.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We use 'on' to show that something is resting on a surface. A book can be on a table.", "correctAnswer": "on"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["sad", "joyful", "angry", "tired"], "question": "Pick the synonym for 'happy'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. 'Joyful' means the same as 'happy'.", "correctAnswer": "joyful"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["boxs", "boxes", "boxies", "box"], "question": "Choose the correct plural: One box, two ___.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Nouns ending in -x often take -es in the plural: box → boxes.", "correctAnswer": "boxes"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["go", "goes", "going", "went"], "question": "Subject-verb agreement: He ___ to school every day.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present, third-person singular subjects (he/she/it) take the -s form: he goes.", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["she", "has", "red", "bag"], "question": "Which word is an adjective in the sentence: She has a red bag.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An adjective describes a noun. 'Red' describes the noun 'bag'.", "correctAnswer": "red"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "In English, sentences begin with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Standard English writing conventions require the first word of a sentence to start with a capital letter.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sentence 'They is my friends' is correct grammar.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With the plural subject 'they', the correct verb is 'are': 'They are my friends.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Pronouns are words that replace nouns.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pronouns like he, she, it, they, and we stand in for nouns to avoid repetition.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The past tense of 'eat' is 'eated'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The correct past tense of 'eat' is the irregular form 'ate'.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence: I ___ 10 years old.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With 'I', we use the verb 'am' in the present tense: I am 10 years old.", "correctAnswer": "am"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence: We ___ soccer on weekends.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present with 'we', the base form is used: We play soccer.", "correctAnswer": "play"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence: Please pass ___ salt.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We use 'the' for specific, known items. 'The salt' refers to the salt on the table.", "correctAnswer": "the"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the sentence: She is taller ___ her brother.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In comparisons, we use 'than': taller than her brother.", "correctAnswer": "than"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the opposite (antonym) of 'hot'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An antonym has the opposite meaning. The opposite of 'hot' is 'cold'.", "correctAnswer": "cold"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the plural form of 'child'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Some nouns have irregular plurals. 'Child' becomes 'children', not 'childs'.", "correctAnswer": "children"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call a word that describes an action?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A verb is a word that shows action or state of being, like run, eat, or is.", "correctAnswer": "verb"}, {"id": "q_1770528028162_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which punctuation mark shows strong feeling or excitement?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An exclamation mark (!) is used to express strong emotion or emphasis.", "correctAnswer": "exclamation mark"}]	none	2026-02-08 05:20:28.279211	\N
ab8d5f79-8231-4f6b-95f8-8acc70fc9112	e1e541bd-59ab-4a5d-a00f-a0599b1be97d	Humanities - Medium	Humanities	medium	[{"id": "q_1770537581043_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Baroque", "High Renaissance", "Neoclassicism", "Impressionism"], "question": "A painting features strong contrasts of light and shadow, dynamic diagonal lines, and heightened emotional intensity in its figures. Which art-historical period does it most likely belong to?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Baroque art (17th century) is characterized by dramatic chiaroscuro, energetic compositions with diagonals, and emotionally charged scenes. High Renaissance favors balanced harmony and calm, Neoclassicism emphasizes clarity and moral restraint, and Impressionism focuses on light effects and loose brushwork rather than theatrical drama.", "correctAnswer": "Baroque"}, {"id": "q_1770537581043_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A medieval monk’s eyewitness chronicle of local events is considered a secondary source for those events.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "An eyewitness chronicle is a primary source because it provides firsthand testimony from the time of the events. Secondary sources interpret or analyze primary sources after the fact.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770537581043_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In Sophocles’ Oedipus Rex, the audience understands Oedipus’s true identity long before he does, an example of __________.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows information that a character does not, producing tension and deeper meaning as actions unfold under misunderstood circumstances.", "correctAnswer": "dramatic irony"}, {"id": "q_1770537581043_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Briefly explain one key difference between Hobbes’s and Locke’s views of the social contract regarding the state of nature and the purpose of government.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Hobbes (Leviathan) argues that fear and insecurity drive individuals to cede broad power to a sovereign for peace. Locke (Second Treatise) posits pre-political natural rights and consent-based, limited government, with the right to revolt if rights are violated.", "correctAnswer": "Hobbes viewed the state of nature as a violent war of all against all, requiring an absolute sovereign to ensure security; Locke saw it as generally free and equal with natural rights, justifying a limited government that protects life, liberty, and property and can be resisted if it fails."}, {"id": "q_1770537581043_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ethos", "Pathos", "Logos", "Kairos"], "question": "A public health campaign that cites peer-reviewed studies, statistics, and causal reasoning is primarily appealing to which rhetorical mode?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Logos appeals to reason and evidence, using data and logical arguments. Ethos emphasizes the speaker’s credibility, Pathos targets emotions, and Kairos focuses on timeliness and the opportune moment.", "correctAnswer": "Logos"}]	none	2026-02-08 07:59:41.143897	\N
61ea735a-46e2-4032-a378-d1a8ac821749	e1e541bd-59ab-4a5d-a00f-a0599b1be97d	French  - Easy	French 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770544668433_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["merci", "bonjour", "s'il vous plaît", "au revoir"], "question": "Which French word means \\"thank you\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Merci means \\"thank you.\\" Bonjour means \\"hello/good morning,\\" s'il vous plaît means \\"please,\\" and au revoir means \\"goodbye.\\"", "correctAnswer": "merci"}, {"id": "q_1770544668433_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In French, all nouns have a grammatical gender (masculine or feminine).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Every French noun is either masculine or feminine. This gender affects articles and adjective agreement, e.g., le livre (masc.), la table (fem.).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770544668433_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete with the correct form of the verb \\"être\\" (to be): Je ____ étudiant.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The verb être in the present tense is: je suis, tu es, il/elle est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils/elles sont. With je, use suis.", "correctAnswer": "suis"}, {"id": "q_1770544668433_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How do you say \\"please\\" in French in a formal context?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "S'il vous plaît is the formal/polite form of \\"please.\\" The informal version is s'il te plaît. Note the accent on plaît.", "correctAnswer": "S'il vous plaît"}, {"id": "q_1770544668433_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["le", "la", "l'", "les"], "question": "Choose the correct definite article before the word \\"école\\" (school).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "École is feminine, but before a vowel sound French uses the elided form l' (l'école). Le is masculine singular, la is feminine singular without elision, and les is plural.", "correctAnswer": "l'"}]	none	2026-02-08 09:57:48.552232	\N
9633f11e-4acb-4e57-b1b6-a1d1b824e1bb	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	Mathematics - Medium	Mathematics	medium	[{"id": "q_1770564002159_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["19", "21", "23", "25"], "question": "A linear function passes through the points (2, 5) and (6, 13). What is f(10)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The slope m = (13 − 5) / (6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2. Using y = mx + b and point (2,5): 5 = 2(2) + b, so b = 1. Thus f(x) = 2x + 1, and f(10) = 21.", "correctAnswer": "21"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45", "50", "55", "60"], "question": "Find the area of a trapezoid with bases 8 and 14 and height 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Area of a trapezoid is A = (1/2)(b1 + b2)h. Substituting: (1/2)(8 + 14)*5 = (1/2)(22)*5 = 11*5 = 55.", "correctAnswer": "55"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/16", "1/17", "1/13", "1/26"], "question": "Two cards are drawn without replacement from a standard 52-card deck. What is the probability that both are hearts?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "There are 13 hearts. P(first heart) = 13/52 = 1/4. Then P(second heart | first heart) = 12/51. Multiply: (1/4)*(12/51) = 12/204 = 1/17.", "correctAnswer": "1/17"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["11 and 12", "12 and 13", "13 and 14", "10 and 15"], "question": "The product of two consecutive integers is 156. Which pair are they?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Let the integers be n and n+1. Then n(n+1) = 156 → n^2 + n − 156 = 0. Discriminant Δ = 625, so n = (−1 ± 25)/2, giving n = 12 or n = −13. The consecutive positive pair is 12 and 13.", "correctAnswer": "12 and 13"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "If a function is increasing on an interval, then its derivative must be positive at every point of that interval.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "An increasing function has f′(x) ≥ 0 where the derivative exists, but it can be zero at points or on flat segments (e.g., f(x) = x^3 has f′(0) = 0 yet is increasing).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The medians of a triangle always intersect at a point that divides each median in a 2:1 ratio (measured from the vertex).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The intersection point of the medians is the centroid, which divides each median in a 2:1 ratio from the vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Simplify: log base 3 of (27x) minus log base 3 of (3) equals ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use log subtraction: log_3(27x) − log_3(3) = log_3((27x)/3) = log_3(9x). Equivalently, since 9 = 3^2, this is 2 + log_3(x).", "correctAnswer": "log_3(9x)"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic sequence with a1 = 4 and common difference d = 3 is S_n = ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use S_n = n/2 [2a1 + (n−1)d] = n/2 [8 + 3(n−1)] = n/2 (3n + 5) = n(3n+5)/2.", "correctAnswer": "n(3n+5)/2"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Given A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]], compute det(A^{-1}).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "det(A) = 1*4 − 2*3 = −2. For an invertible matrix, det(A^{-1}) = 1/det(A) = −1/2.", "correctAnswer": "-1/2"}, {"id": "q_1770564002159_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Find the vertex of the parabola y = 2x^2 − 8x + 5.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The vertex x-coordinate is −b/(2a) = 8/4 = 2. Substitute x = 2: y = 2(4) − 8(2) + 5 = 8 − 16 + 5 = −3. So the vertex is (2, −3).", "correctAnswer": "(2, -3)"}]	none	2026-02-08 15:20:02.738507	\N
61140800-ff9c-4cad-8841-b6c3e5a87c1d	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	English  - Medium	English 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770564189762_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["She enjoys reading, swimming, and biking.", "She enjoys to read, to swim, and biking.", "She enjoys reading, to swim, and to bike.", "She enjoys to reading, swimming, and biking."], "question": "Choose the best revision to correct the faulty parallelism in the sentence: \\"She enjoys reading, to swim, and biking.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Items in a list should be parallel in form. Using three gerunds (reading, swimming, biking) creates a consistent structure and improves clarity.", "correctAnswer": "She enjoys reading, swimming, and biking."}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The committee which met on Tuesday has decided to postpone the vote.", "The committee, which met on Tuesday, has decided to postpone the vote.", "The committee that met on Tuesday, has decided to postpone the vote.", "The committee that met on Tuesday has decided, to postpone the vote."], "question": "Choose the sentence that correctly uses commas to set off a nonessential clause.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Nonessential (nonrestrictive) clauses are set off with commas and are often introduced by \\"which\\". The sentence with commas around \\"which met on Tuesday\\" correctly indicates that the clause is additional, not defining.", "correctAnswer": "The committee, which met on Tuesday, has decided to postpone the vote."}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Concise", "Enthusiastic", "Rambling", "Inarticulate"], "question": "In the sentence, \\"Despite his effusive praise in public, his comments in private were notably terse,\\" which word is the best synonym for \\"terse\\" as used here?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In contrast to \\"effusive,\\" \\"terse\\" means brief and to the point. \\"Concise\\" matches that meaning. \\"Rambling\\" is the opposite, and \\"enthusiastic\\" and \\"inarticulate\\" do not fit the context.", "correctAnswer": "Concise"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Walking down the street, the sun came out.", "Walking down the street, I saw the sun come out.", "While walking down, the sun came out.", "Walking down the street, the weather improved."], "question": "Which sentence correctly avoids a dangling modifier?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The introductory modifier \\"Walking down the street\\" should logically describe the subject that follows. Only the sentence with \\"I\\" as the subject correctly links the action to a person, avoiding a dangling modifier.", "correctAnswer": "Walking down the street, I saw the sun come out."}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sentence \\"Neither the coach nor the players are willing to change their strategy\\" is grammatically correct.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "With \\"neither…nor,\\" the verb agrees with the subject closer to it. Since \\"players\\" is plural and closest to the verb, \\"are\\" is correct. The pronoun \\"their\\" also agrees with the plural \\"players.\\"", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The passive voice always obscures the agent and should be avoided in formal writing.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Passive voice can include the agent (e.g., \\"by the committee\\") and is useful when the receiver of the action is the focus or when the agent is unknown or irrelevant. It is not inherently wrong and can be appropriate in formal contexts.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb: \\"Not only ____ the results inconclusive, but they also contradicted earlier findings.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "After negative adverbials like \\"Not only,\\" subject–verb inversion occurs: \\"were the results.\\" The subject is plural (results), so the plural verb \\"were\\" is required.", "correctAnswer": "were"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct word: \\"There were ____ than ten errors in the report.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use \\"fewer\\" with countable nouns (errors). \\"Less\\" is generally used with uncountable quantities (time, money, water).", "correctAnswer": "fewer"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the rhetorical device used in the sentence: \\"The committee demanded not answers, not excuses, but action.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Anaphora is the repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses or phrases. Repeating \\"not\\" at the start of each item creates emphasis and rhythm.", "correctAnswer": "anaphora"}, {"id": "q_1770564189762_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In the sentence \\"The bouquet of roses was beautiful, and it filled the room with fragrance,\\" what does the pronoun \\"it\\" refer to?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The singular pronoun \\"it\\" refers to the singular antecedent \\"bouquet (of roses),\\" not to \\"roses,\\" which is plural within the prepositional phrase.", "correctAnswer": "the bouquet of roses"}]	none	2026-02-08 15:23:09.776518	\N
ef607704-bd86-4f3a-b27c-e7cdac3f27b5	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	Science  - Medium	Science 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770564241165_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["6 Ω", "12 Ω", "24 Ω", "0.04 Ω"], "question": "A 12 V battery drives a current of 0.50 A through a resistor. What is the resistance of the resistor?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By Ohm’s law, V = IR. Rearranging gives R = V/I = 12 V / 0.50 A = 24 Ω. The other options result from common mistakes like dividing incorrectly or inverting the ratio.", "correctAnswer": "24 Ω"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Enzymes speed up chemical reactions by increasing the activation energy required for the reaction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzymes lower the activation energy, not increase it. By stabilizing the transition state, they reduce the energy barrier, allowing more reactant molecules to convert to products at a given temperature.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The primary cause of Earth's seasons is its ____ relative to its orbital plane.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Earth’s approximately 23.5° axial tilt causes changes in the angle of sunlight and day length over the year. Distance from the Sun changes only slightly and is not the main driver of seasons.", "correctAnswer": "axial tilt"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which cellular organelle modifies, sorts, and packages proteins for secretion or delivery to other organelles?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Golgi apparatus (Golgi complex) processes proteins from the rough ER by modifying them (e.g., glycosylation), sorting them, and packaging them into vesicles for targeted delivery or secretion.", "correctAnswer": "Golgi apparatus"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It increases significantly.", "It decreases slightly.", "It stays exactly the same.", "It decreases drastically."], "question": "An acetic acid/acetate buffer is prepared at pH 4.8. A small amount of strong acid (HCl) is added. What happens to the pH?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Buffers resist pH changes by converting added H+ into the weak acid form. In an acetate buffer, added H+ shifts acetate to acetic acid, causing only a small pH decrease within the buffer capacity.", "correctAnswer": "It decreases slightly."}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10,000 J", "1,000 J", "100 J", "10 J"], "question": "If a grassland ecosystem has about 10,000 J of energy at the producer level, approximately how much energy is available to tertiary consumers (assuming a 10% transfer efficiency between trophic levels)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Applying the 10% rule: producers (10,000 J) → primary consumers (1,000 J) → secondary consumers (100 J) → tertiary consumers (10 J). Energy diminishes with each transfer due to metabolic losses as heat.", "correctAnswer": "10 J"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Astronauts in low Earth orbit are weightless because there is essentially no gravity at that altitude.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Gravity is still strong in low Earth orbit (about 90% of surface gravity). Astronauts are weightless because they are in continuous free fall around Earth, experiencing microgravity due to orbital motion providing centripetal acceleration.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The law that states the total pressure exerted by a mixture of gases equals the sum of the partial pressures of each gas is known as ____ law.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dalton’s law of partial pressures applies to ideal gases and many real gas mixtures at moderate conditions. Each gas exerts pressure independently as if it occupied the volume alone; the total is the sum.", "correctAnswer": "Dalton's"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In a monohybrid cross between a heterozygous individual (Aa) and a homozygous recessive individual (aa), what percentage of offspring are expected to show the recessive phenotype?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The cross Aa × aa yields genotypes: Aa and aa in a 1:1 ratio. Only aa expresses the recessive phenotype, so 50% of the offspring show it.", "correctAnswer": "50%"}, {"id": "q_1770564241165_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Transform boundary", "Divergent boundary (mid-ocean ridge)", "Convergent boundary: oceanic–oceanic subduction", "Convergent boundary: continental–continental collision"], "question": "Which type of plate boundary is most commonly associated with the formation of volcanic island arcs?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When one oceanic plate subducts beneath another, water is released into the mantle wedge, lowering the melting point and generating magma. This leads to chains of volcanoes that form island arcs (e.g., the Marianas).", "correctAnswer": "Convergent boundary: oceanic–oceanic subduction"}]	none	2026-02-08 15:24:01.280045	\N
50754465-3694-4fe3-8201-44ecb6e14b12	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	Mathematics  - Easy	Mathematics 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770565422996_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["12", "11", "13", "10"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 combines to make 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A triangle has three sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a triangle is a polygon with exactly three sides and three angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the number 3,582, the digit in the hundreds place is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Place value from right to left is ones (2), tens (8), hundreds (5), thousands (3). So the hundreds digit is 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 6 units and width 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a rectangle is 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (6 + 3) = 2 × 9 = 18 units.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3/6", "2/5", "4/7", "5/8"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying the numerator and denominator of 1/2 by 3 gives 3/6, which is the same value.", "correctAnswer": "3/6"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is neither a positive nor a negative number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Zero is neutral; it is the boundary between positive and negative numbers and has no sign.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The product of 9 and 0 is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Any number multiplied by 0 equals 0.", "correctAnswer": "0"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the next number in the sequence: 2, 4, 6, 8, ___?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is counting by twos (even numbers). After 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Square", "Rectangle", "Rhombus", "Trapezoid"], "question": "Which shape has exactly four equal sides and four right angles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A square has four equal sides and four right angles. A rectangle has right angles but not necessarily equal sides, and a rhombus has equal sides but not necessarily right angles.", "correctAnswer": "Square"}, {"id": "q_1770565422997_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "5", "6", "9"], "question": "What is 15 − 9?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 9 from 15 leaves 6 because 15 − 9 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}]	none	2026-02-08 15:43:43.105598	\N
394f696d-d5b3-4722-85f7-327bfb546289	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	Joints - Easy	Joints	easy	[{"id": "q_1770650866367_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ball-and-socket", "Hinge", "Pivot", "Gliding (plane)"], "question": "Which type of joint allows the greatest range of motion, including rotation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ball-and-socket joints, such as the shoulder and hip, allow movement in multiple planes and rotation. Hinge joints mainly permit bending and straightening, pivot joints allow rotation around one axis, and gliding joints permit limited sliding movements.", "correctAnswer": "Ball-and-socket"}, {"id": "q_1770650866367_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Ligaments connect muscles to bones.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ligaments connect bone to bone and help stabilize joints. Tendons connect muscle to bone, transmitting the force that moves the joint.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770650866367_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The fluid that lubricates and nourishes a synovial joint is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Synovial fluid is produced by the synovial membrane and reduces friction between articular cartilages while providing nutrients to them.", "correctAnswer": "synovial fluid"}, {"id": "q_1770650866367_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is a joint in the human body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A joint is the meeting point of bones and can be movable or immovable. Joints provide stability and allow various types of movement depending on their structure.", "correctAnswer": "A place where two or more bones meet."}, {"id": "q_1770650866367_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Suture", "Hinge", "Ball-and-socket", "Cartilaginous"], "question": "Which type of joint is found between the bones of the skull?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sutures are fibrous joints that tightly connect skull bones and are essentially immovable, protecting the brain. Hinge and ball-and-socket joints allow more movement and are found in places like the elbow and shoulder.", "correctAnswer": "Suture"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:27:46.452211	\N
1eeffb39-4a0f-49db-8311-c9e49ccf077d	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	English  - Easy	English 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770567400325_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["dog", "runs", "fast", "the"], "question": "Which word is the noun in the sentence: 'The dog runs fast'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. In the sentence, 'dog' is the thing being talked about. 'Runs' is a verb, 'fast' is an adverb, and 'the' is an article.", "correctAnswer": "dog"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "no article"], "question": "Choose the correct article: 'I saw ___ apple on the table.'", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'an' before words that begin with a vowel sound. 'Apple' begins with the vowel sound /a/, so 'an apple' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "A complete sentence must have a subject and a verb.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A sentence needs a subject (who or what the sentence is about) and a verb (the action or state) to express a complete thought.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb form: 'She ____ to school every day.'", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present tense, third person singular (he, she, it) adds -s or -es to the verb. 'Go' becomes 'goes.'", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["on", "in", "at", "by"], "question": "Choose the correct preposition: 'The cat is ____ the box.'", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Prepositions show relationships of place or time. 'In' indicates that the cat is inside the box, which fits this sentence best.", "correctAnswer": "in"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb 'walk'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed. Therefore, 'walk' becomes 'walked.'", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The word 'their' is the contraction of 'they are.'", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Their' is a possessive adjective (showing ownership). The contraction of 'they are' is 'they're.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct pronoun: 'Maria and Carlos are here. ____ are friends.'", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'They' is the correct subject pronoun to refer to two people (Maria and Carlos).", "correctAnswer": "They"}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["i like english.", "I like english.", "I like English.", "i Like English."], "question": "Which sentence is correctly capitalized?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The first word of a sentence and proper nouns should be capitalized. 'I' is capitalized, and 'English' is a proper noun.", "correctAnswer": "I like English."}, {"id": "q_1770567400325_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is an adjective?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adjectives give more information about nouns or pronouns (for example, 'blue car,' 'happy child') by describing qualities or characteristics.", "correctAnswer": "A word that describes a noun or pronoun."}]	none	2026-02-08 16:16:40.419268	\N
f8470002-58d1-47c7-9e0f-4368eb911b57	e1e541bd-59ab-4a5d-a00f-a0599b1be97d	FRENCH - Easy	FRENCH	easy	[{"id": "q_1770574395442_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Bonjour", "Salut", "Au revoir", "Merci"], "question": "How do you say \\"hello\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bonjour is the standard way to say hello, especially in daytime and in more formal contexts. Salut also means hi but is informal. Au revoir means goodbye, and Merci means thank you.", "correctAnswer": "Bonjour"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Merci", "Pardon", "De rien", "S'il vous plaît"], "question": "Which word means \\"thank you\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Merci means thank you. Pardon means excuse me/sorry, De rien means you're welcome, and S'il vous plaît means please.", "correctAnswer": "Merci"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["la", "le", "les", "un"], "question": "Which is the feminine definite article in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "La is the feminine singular definite article. Le is masculine singular, les is plural for both genders, and un is the masculine indefinite article.", "correctAnswer": "la"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["cinq", "un", "trois", "neuf"], "question": "What is the French word for the number 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cinq is 5 in French. Un is 1, trois is 3, and neuf is 9.", "correctAnswer": "cinq"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["bleu", "rouge", "vert", "noir"], "question": "Which word means \\"blue\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bleu means blue. Rouge means red, vert means green, and noir means black.", "correctAnswer": "bleu"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Au revoir", "Salut", "Bonsoir", "À bientôt"], "question": "How do you say \\"goodbye\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Au revoir is the standard way to say goodbye. Salut can be used informally for hi/bye, Bonsoir means good evening, and À bientôt means see you soon.", "correctAnswer": "Au revoir"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["je suis", "je es", "je est", "j'ai"], "question": "Choose the correct form of the verb \\"être\\" (to be) for \\"je\\":", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Je suis means I am. The forms \\"je es\\" and \\"je est\\" are incorrect; \\"tu es\\" and \\"il/elle est\\" are correct for you (singular) and he/she. J'ai means I have (from the verb avoir).", "correctAnswer": "je suis"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["S'il vous plaît", "S'il te plaît", "Excusez-moi", "Bonjour"], "question": "Which phrase is the formal way to say \\"please\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "S'il vous plaît is the formal/plural form of please. S'il te plaît is informal (used with friends). Excusez-moi means excuse me, and Bonjour means hello.", "correctAnswer": "S'il vous plaît"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "In French, all nouns have gender (masculine or feminine).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Every French noun is either masculine or feminine, which affects the choice of articles and adjective agreement.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Bon nuit is the correct way to say good night.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The correct phrase is Bonne nuit. Nuit (night) is feminine, so the adjective bonne is used instead of bon.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "The letter \\"h\\" in French is usually silent.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "French h is generally silent; words like homme and habitat are pronounced without the h sound.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Je m'appelle means \\"I am hungry.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Je m'appelle means my name is. I am hungry is J'ai faim.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "_____ means \\"yes\\" in French.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Oui is the French word for yes. The opposite is Non, which means no.", "correctAnswer": "Oui"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "To ask \\"How are you?\\" informally, you say \\"Ça _____ ?\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ça va? literally means \\"It goes?\\" and is used to ask how someone is doing informally. The typical reply is \\"Ça va\\" (I'm fine).", "correctAnswer": "va"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The plural definite article in French is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Les is the plural definite article for both masculine and feminine nouns (e.g., les livres, les maisons).", "correctAnswer": "les"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Translate \\"cat\\" into French: _____ (masculine).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Chat is the masculine word for cat. The feminine form is chatte when specifying a female cat.", "correctAnswer": "chat"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the French word for \\"water\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Eau means water. It is pronounced like \\"oh\\" and the final letters are silent.", "correctAnswer": "eau"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the French expression for \\"My name is\\" (include the subject pronoun).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Je m'appelle literally means \\"I call myself\\" and is the standard way to introduce your name in French.", "correctAnswer": "Je m'appelle"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the French word for the number 12.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Douze is 12 in French. It follows onze (11) and precedes treize (13).", "correctAnswer": "douze"}, {"id": "q_1770574395442_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How do you say \\"See you tomorrow\\" in French?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "À demain means see you tomorrow. The accent on À indicates the preposition \\"to/at\\".", "correctAnswer": "À demain"}]	none	2026-02-08 18:13:15.541525	\N
060de947-3ecc-4ec2-85e8-08edcf086d29	9b6febd7-a114-402e-8eb0-b9c76b21900b	Grammar  - Medium	Grammar 	medium	[{"id": "q_1770575055171_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The committee are meeting at noon.", "The committee is meeting at noon.", "The committee have met at noon.", "The committee were meeting at noon."], "question": "Which sentence has correct subject–verb agreement when using the collective noun as a single unit?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When a collective noun (like committee) is acting as a single entity, it takes a singular verb: is meeting. Plural verbs (are, have, were) would be used if emphasizing members acting individually, which the sentence does not indicate.", "correctAnswer": "The committee is meeting at noon."}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In the sentence \\"Each of the players have a unique style,\\" the verb \\"have\\" is correct.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Each is singular, so the verb should be singular: \\"Each of the players has a unique style.\\" The prepositional phrase \\"of the players\\" does not change the number of the subject.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct pronoun: \\"To ____ did you send the invitation that she mentioned?\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Whom is the object form and is correct after the preposition to. In this question structure, whom functions as the object of the verb send (and the preposition to), so \\"To whom\\" is required.", "correctAnswer": "whom"}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Correct the dangling modifier in this sentence: \\"Running down the street, the backpack fell off Jake.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The modifier \\"Running down the street\\" must logically modify the subject that follows. The original sentence suggests the backpack was running. Rewriting to make Jake the subject fixes the dangling modifier.", "correctAnswer": "As Jake was running down the street, his backpack fell off."}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["She likes hiking, swimming, and to ride a bike.", "She likes to hike, swimming, and riding a bike.", "She likes hiking, swimming, and riding a bike.", "She likes to hike, to swim, and riding a bike."], "question": "Which sentence maintains parallel structure?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Parallel structure requires consistent grammatical forms. Using three gerunds (hiking, swimming, riding) maintains parallelism. The other choices mix infinitives and gerunds, breaking the pattern.", "correctAnswer": "She likes hiking, swimming, and riding a bike."}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Passive voice is always incorrect in formal writing.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Passive voice is acceptable and often preferable when the actor is unknown, irrelevant, or should be de-emphasized (e.g., \\"The report was completed yesterday\\"). It’s a stylistic choice, not an error by default.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete with the correct verb form for tense consistency: \\"By the time the train ____ (arrive), we had already eaten.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Past perfect (had eaten) typically pairs with a simple past event that occurs later as the time marker. Thus, \\"arrived\\" (simple past) is consistent: By the time the train arrived, we had already eaten.", "correctAnswer": "arrived"}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite the sentence to correct pronoun–antecedent agreement while keeping the meaning: \\"Everyone should take out their notebooks.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Everyone is a singular indefinite pronoun, so use a singular pronoun: his or her. Note that singular they (\\"Everyone should take out their notebook\\") is widely accepted in modern usage, but traditional grammar prefers \\"his or her.\\"", "correctAnswer": "Everyone should take out his or her notebook."}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["My brother who lives in Boston is visiting next week.", "My brother, who lives in Boston is visiting next week.", "My brother who lives in Boston, is visiting next week.", "My brother, who lives in Boston, is visiting next week."], "question": "Assuming the speaker has only one brother, which sentence punctuates the nonessential clause correctly?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "If there is only one brother, \\"who lives in Boston\\" is nonessential and should be set off by a pair of commas. Option D correctly uses two commas; A lacks commas, B is missing the second comma, and C misplaces a comma before the verb.", "correctAnswer": "My brother, who lives in Boston, is visiting next week."}, {"id": "q_1770575055172_9", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct comparative: \\"There are ____ cars on the road today than yesterday.\\"", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Use fewer with countable nouns (cars) and less with noncountable quantities (traffic, water, time). Therefore, \\"fewer cars\\" is correct.", "correctAnswer": "fewer"}]	none	2026-02-08 18:24:15.257112	\N
77878140-5d8f-475d-bcf3-954d045f10cd	2698cd5e-715f-494a-bfd7-0eda6a1ad356	Science - Medium	Science	medium	[{"id": "q_1770632779505_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It shrinks due to water leaving the cell", "It bursts because too much water enters", "It becomes turgid as water enters", "There is no net movement of water across the membrane"], "question": "What most likely happens to a plant cell when it is placed in a hypotonic solution?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A hypotonic solution has a lower solute concentration outside the cell than inside, so water moves into the cell by osmosis. Plant cells do not burst because their rigid cell wall limits expansion; instead, they become turgid as internal pressure (turgor pressure) increases.", "correctAnswer": "It becomes turgid as water enters"}, {"id": "q_1770632779505_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster in air than in steel.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sound travels faster in steel than in air because solids have higher stiffness (elastic modulus) and transmit vibrations more efficiently. The speed of sound depends on both the medium’s elasticity and density; in metals like steel, the high stiffness dominates, giving much higher speeds than in gases.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770632779505_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In cellular respiration, oxygen serves as the final electron ______ in the mitochondrial electron transport chain.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain. It combines with electrons and protons to form water, allowing the chain to continue operating and enabling ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation.", "correctAnswer": "acceptor"}, {"id": "q_1770632779505_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A car’s velocity decreases uniformly from 20 m/s to 8 m/s over 6 seconds. What is its acceleration?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity: a = (v_final − v_initial)/time = (8 − 20)/6 = −12/6 = −2 m/s^2. The negative sign indicates the car is slowing down.", "correctAnswer": "-2 m/s^2"}, {"id": "q_1770632779505_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why does adding salt to ice water lower its temperature below 0°C?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dissolved salt lowers water’s freezing point (a colligative property), so ice can melt at temperatures below 0°C. Melting requires energy (latent heat of fusion), which is drawn from the surroundings, reducing the temperature of the ice–salt–water mixture.", "correctAnswer": "Salt causes freezing point depression, making ice melt while absorbing heat, which cools the mixture."}]	none	2026-02-09 10:26:19.635065	\N
2789ae30-2ab3-4250-a20b-e37bf8695eb4	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Setswana/ Maina - Easy	Setswana/ Maina	easy	[{"id": "q_1770650228514_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["nouns", "verbs", "adjectives", "tenses"], "question": "In Setswana grammar, what does the term \\"maina\\" refer to?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "\\"Maina\\" is the plural of \\"leina,\\" which means \\"noun.\\" Maina are naming words used to refer to people, places, things, or ideas.", "correctAnswer": "nouns"}, {"id": "q_1770650228514_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Setswana singular prefix \\"se-\\" usually changes to \\"di-\\" in the plural (for example, sekolo -> dikolo).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In Setswana, many nouns in class 7 use the singular prefix \\"se-\\" and form their plural in class 8 with \\"di-\\" (e.g., sekolo/dikolo, selepe/dilepe).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770650228514_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the plural form: legapu -> ______", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Legapu (watermelon) is a class 5 noun with the singular prefix \\"le-\\". Its plural is class 6 with the prefix \\"ma-\\": legapu -> magapu.", "correctAnswer": "magapu"}, {"id": "q_1770650228514_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["batho", "dimotho", "bomotho", "megatho"], "question": "What is the correct plural of motho (person)?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Motho is class 1 with the singular prefix \\"mo-\\". Its class 2 plural uses \\"ba-\\": motho -> batho.", "correctAnswer": "batho"}, {"id": "q_1770650228514_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the singular form of \\"maina\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Maina is the plural form meaning \\"nouns.\\" The singular is \\"leina\\" (noun), following the le- (singular) to ma- (plural) pattern: leina -> maina.", "correctAnswer": "leina"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:17:08.602111	\N
e9611e90-63ac-4aa6-a12d-f8843d2170e2	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Setswana/tlhogo and thito - Easy	Setswana/tlhogo and thito	easy	[{"id": "q_1770650511083_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Karolo e e arabelang potso “mang? kgotsa eng?” (subject)", "Karolo e e bolelang tiro e e dirwang (verb/predicate)", "Lefoko le le bontshang nako (tense marker)", "Karolo e e oketsang tlhaloso ya leina (adjective)"], "question": "Ke eng “tlhogo” mo polelong ya Setswana?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tlhogo ke karolo ya polelo e e bontshang gore polelo e bua ka mang kgotsa eng (subject). E tlwaelega go tlhaga pele ga tiro, mme thito yona e bolela se se buiwang ka tlhogo—tiro kgotsa tiragalo.", "correctAnswer": "Karolo e e arabelang potso “mang? kgotsa eng?” (subject)"}, {"id": "q_1770650511083_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Polelo: “Ngwana o a bala.” Tshwaelo: Tlhogo ke “o a bala.”", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Seno ga se nnete. Mo polelong “Ngwana o a bala,” “Ngwana” ke tlhogo (who/what), mme “o a bala” ke thito ka gonne e bontsha tiro e tlhogo e e dirang (predicate/verb phrase).", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770650511083_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Mo polelong “Ntša e a loma,” the ______ is “Ntša.”", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "“Ntša” e arabela potso ya “mang?” ka jalo ke tlhogo. Karolo e e salang, “e a loma,” ke thito e e bontshang tiro e e dirwang.", "correctAnswer": "tlhogo"}, {"id": "q_1770650511083_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Tlhalosa “thito” mo tlhaloganyong ya ditemana tsa Setswana.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mo Setswana, polelo hangata e arogana ka tlhogo (subject) le thito (predicate). Thito e akaretsa lemodimo la tiro (verb) le mafoko a a e tlhomamisang, e re bolelela gore tlhogo e dira eng kgotsa go diragalang mo go yone.", "correctAnswer": "Thito ke karolo ya polelo e e bolelang se se buiwang ka tlhogo—tiro kgotsa tiragalo (predicate/verb phrase)."}, {"id": "q_1770650511083_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Morutabana", "Bana", "O ruta bana", "Morutabana bana"], "question": "Mo polelong “Morutabana o ruta bana,” ke eng thito?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "“Morutabana” ke tlhogo ka gore e araba “mang?”. “O ruta bana” ke thito ka gonne e tlhalosa se se buiwang ka tlhogo—tiro ya gagwe (go ruta) le ba a ba rutang (bana).", "correctAnswer": "O ruta bana"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:21:53.54672	\N
56a630a8-d30c-4e7a-8543-1d1dfd242469	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	Maths - Medium	Maths	medium	[{"id": "q_1770650957503_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-3", "1", "3", "-5"], "question": "What is the minimum value of the quadratic function f(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + 5?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The parabola opens upward since a = 2 > 0, so its minimum occurs at the vertex. The x-coordinate of the vertex is -b/(2a) = 8/4 = 2. Evaluate f(2): 2(2^2) - 8(2) + 5 = 8 - 16 + 5 = -3. Therefore, the minimum value is -3.", "correctAnswer": "-3"}, {"id": "q_1770650957503_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If events A and B are independent with P(A) = 0.6 and P(B) = 0.5, then P(A ∪ B) = 1.1.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By inclusion-exclusion, P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A)P(B) for independent events. That is 0.6 + 0.5 - (0.6)(0.5) = 1.1 - 0.3 = 0.8. Probabilities cannot exceed 1, so the statement is false.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770650957503_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Solve the system 2x + 3y = 13 and x - y = 1. The value of y is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "From x - y = 1, we get x = y + 1. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 13: 2(y + 1) + 3y = 13 → 2y + 2 + 3y = 13 → 5y = 11 → y = 11/5.", "correctAnswer": "11/5"}, {"id": "q_1770650957503_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A geometric series has first term 5 and common ratio 3. What is the smallest integer n such that the sum of the first n terms exceeds 500?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The sum of the first n terms is S_n = 5(3^n - 1)/(3 - 1) = (5/2)(3^n - 1). We need (5/2)(3^n - 1) > 500 ⇒ 3^n - 1 > 200 ⇒ 3^n > 201. Since 3^4 = 81 and 3^5 = 243, the smallest n is 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770650957503_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3x - 4y = 10", "3x - 4y = 12", "4x + 3y = 10", "4x - 3y = 10"], "question": "Find the equation of the line that passes through (2, -1) and is parallel to 3x - 4y = 12.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Lines parallel to 3x - 4y = 12 have the form 3x - 4y = c. Substitute (2, -1): 3(2) - 4(-1) = 6 + 4 = 10, so c = 10. Therefore, the equation is 3x - 4y = 10.", "correctAnswer": "3x - 4y = 10"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:29:17.514538	\N
bf4d46e5-d5dd-470c-b040-f19a2049a6b2	5bc34fa2-61ce-4d62-9340-f2ff7985216b	Setswana /Maina  - Easy	Setswana /Maina 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770651104522_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Maina", "Mafoko", "Madiri", "Maele"], "question": "Ke lefoko lefe la Setswana le le kayang \\"noun\\" mo dithutong tsa puo?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mo Setswana, maina ke mafoko a a supang dilo, batho, dibopiwa, le maikutlo. Singular ke leina, plural ke maina. Mafoko ke \\"words\\" ka kakaretso, madiri ke \\"verbs\\", mme maele ke metlae kgotsa dinanalo (proverbs).", "correctAnswer": "Maina"}, {"id": "q_1770651104522_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Mo Setswana, maina a a mo sehlopheng sa 1 a bontshiwa ka \\"mo-\\", mme bontsi jwa one (sehlopha sa 2) bo dirisa \\"ba-\\".", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sehlopha sa 1/2 se dirisa mo-/ba-: mo- mo bongwe (singular), ba- mo bongwe jo bontsi (plural). Ditema tse di tlwaelegileng ke motho/batho, ngwana/bana. Se se thusa go bona tumalano ya maina le dithaloganyo (noun class agreement).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770651104522_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Tlatsa phatla: Bontsi jwa \\"sefate\\" ke __________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sefate se le mo sehlopheng sa 7 se dirisa se-, mme bontsi jwa sone mo sehlopheng sa 8 bo dirisa di-. Ka jalo, sefate (singular) bo fetoga difate (plural).", "correctAnswer": "difate"}, {"id": "q_1770651104522_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Fa plural e le \\"maina\\", kwana le singular ya yone.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Leina/maina ke sehlopha sa 5/6 mo Setswana (le-/ma-). Leina ke singular, maina ke plural. Ke sekai se se tlwaelegileng sa kamano ya diprefegetsi tsa sehlopha.", "correctAnswer": "leina"}, {"id": "q_1770651104522_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["motho/batho", "sefate/difate", "ntša/maina", "leina/dileina"], "question": "Ke kgetho efe e e bontshang kago e e siameng ya singular le plural ya motho?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Motho/batho e supa kago ya sehlopha sa 1/2 (mo-/ba-) bakeng sa batho. Sefate/difate ke sehlopha sa 7/8 (se-/di-), e seng motho. Ntša/maina ga go tshwane (ntša e dirisana le dintša). Leina ga le dire bontsi ka di-, bontsi jwa lone ke maina.", "correctAnswer": "motho/batho"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:31:44.603036	\N
952f7060-16ec-42fb-9f95-773439b80a02	5bc34fa2-61ce-4d62-9340-f2ff7985216b	English  - Easy	English 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770651248400_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["cat", "sleeps", "on", "the"], "question": "In the sentence \\"The cat sleeps on the sofa.\\", which word is a noun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, or thing. In the options, \\"cat\\" is a thing (an animal), so it is a noun. \\"sleeps\\" is a verb (an action), \\"on\\" is a preposition, and \\"the\\" is an article.", "correctAnswer": "cat"}, {"id": "q_1770651248400_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "A sentence in English should begin with a capital letter.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Standard English capitalization rules say that the first word of every sentence starts with a capital letter. This helps readers see where each sentence begins.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770651248400_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"I have ____ umbrella.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use \\"an\\" before words that begin with a vowel sound. \\"Umbrella\\" starts with the vowel sound /ʌ/, so the correct article is \\"an.\\"", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1770651248400_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Most regular verbs in English form the past tense by adding -ed to the base form. \\"Walk\\" becomes \\"walked.\\"", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1770651248400_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["sad", "joyful", "angry", "tired"], "question": "Which word is a synonym for \\"happy\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. \\"Joyful\\" means very happy, so it is a synonym of \\"happy.\\" The other options describe different feelings.", "correctAnswer": "joyful"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:34:08.500597	\N
1ff11009-f839-474e-a5f2-de6ab02e84e8	b88315bc-b21a-4843-94ef-3d596f111814	Maths /angels - Easy	Maths /angels	easy	[{"id": "q_1770651922713_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45°", "90°", "180°", "360°"], "question": "How many degrees are in a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle measures exactly 90°. It is the angle formed by perpendicular lines and is often marked with a small square at the vertex.", "correctAnswer": "90°"}, {"id": "q_1770651922713_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Complementary angles add up to 180°.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles add up to 90°. Angles that add up to 180° are called supplementary angles.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770651922713_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A straight line forms an angle of ___ degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A straight angle measures 180° because it represents a half-turn or two right angles placed back-to-back.", "correctAnswer": "180"}, {"id": "q_1770651922713_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call an angle that is greater than 90° but less than 180°?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An obtuse angle is any angle between 90° and 180°. It is wider than a right angle but not a straight angle.", "correctAnswer": "Obtuse angle"}, {"id": "q_1770651922713_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Adjacent angles", "Vertical angles", "Complementary angles", "Acute angles"], "question": "Angles that are opposite each other when two lines cross are called what?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Vertical (opposite) angles are formed by two intersecting lines and are directly across from each other. They are always equal in measure.", "correctAnswer": "Vertical angles"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:45:25.222614	\N
729b789b-92b7-4d9a-bf12-3540e3e847bc	ac2c39a1-139b-4776-b6e9-c6f02f05bdd5	Mathematics - Easy	Mathematics	easy	[{"id": "q_1770732640646_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["9", "12", "15", "21"], "question": "Which number is even?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2. 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so 12 is even.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "14", "16"], "question": "What is the value of 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition: 3 × 4 means 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Square", "Triangle", "Rectangle", "Circle"], "question": "Which shape has 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides. A square and rectangle have 4 sides, and a circle has no straight sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "0 is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An even number is any integer divisible by 2. 0 ÷ 2 = 0 with no remainder, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "A rectangle has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has opposite sides equal and parallel, but all four sides are equal only in a square.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: 15 − 9 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 9 from 15 leaves 6 because 15 − 9 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "6"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the number 452, the digit 5 is in the ___ place.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In 452, 4 is hundreds, 5 is tens, and 2 is ones. The 5 represents 50, which is the tens place.", "correctAnswer": "tens"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What fraction of a pizza is one out of four equal slices?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "One out of four equal parts is one-fourth, written as 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "1/4"}, {"id": "q_1770732640647_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How many minutes are in 1 hour?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 60 minutes in an hour by definition of time measurement.", "correctAnswer": "60"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:10:40.749618	\N
3fbbe8fc-a944-4336-a61e-1b631e46bb26	b88315bc-b21a-4843-94ef-3d596f111814	Maths /angels - Easy	Maths /angels	easy	[{"id": "q_1770651954821_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45 degrees", "60 degrees", "90 degrees", "180 degrees"], "question": "What is the measure of a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle is exactly 90 degrees, which is a quarter turn. Angles smaller than 90 degrees are acute, and those larger than 90 but less than 180 are obtuse.", "correctAnswer": "90 degrees"}, {"id": "q_1770651954821_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: An acute angle measures less than 90 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, an acute angle is any angle that is greater than 0 degrees but less than 90 degrees. A 90-degree angle is called a right angle.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770651954821_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Two angles that add up to 180 degrees are called ______ angles.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Angles that sum to 180 degrees are supplementary because together they form a straight angle. In contrast, complementary angles add up to 90 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "supplementary"}, {"id": "q_1770651954821_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call the point where the two sides (rays) of an angle meet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The common endpoint of the two rays that form an angle is called the vertex. The rays are the sides of the angle.", "correctAnswer": "vertex"}, {"id": "q_1770651954821_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["30 degrees", "75 degrees", "120 degrees", "90 degrees"], "question": "Which of the following is an obtuse angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Obtuse angles measure more than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees. Among the options, 120 degrees fits this definition.", "correctAnswer": "120 degrees"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:45:56.911233	\N
9e6fc213-35bb-42d5-b7cd-5b95fa7e4518	f01819a1-02ae-4c5b-b324-e5ab89669584	Maths / angle - Easy	Maths / angle	easy	[{"id": "q_1770652485314_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45°", "90°", "180°", "360°"], "question": "How many degrees are in a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle is defined as an angle that measures exactly 90 degrees. It is the angle found in the corner of a square or rectangle.", "correctAnswer": "90°"}, {"id": "q_1770652485314_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "An acute angle measures less than 90°.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Acute angles are those with measures greater than 0° and less than 90°. Right angles are exactly 90°, and obtuse angles are greater than 90° but less than 180°.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770652485314_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A straight angle measures ____ degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A straight angle forms a straight line. It is half of a full turn (360°), so it measures 180°.", "correctAnswer": "180°"}, {"id": "q_1770652485314_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do we call two angles whose measures add up to 90°?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles are two angles whose measures sum to 90°. For example, 30° and 60° are complementary.", "correctAnswer": "Complementary angles"}, {"id": "q_1770652485314_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Protractor", "Ruler", "Compass", "Thermometer"], "question": "Which tool is commonly used to measure angles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A protractor is specifically designed to measure and draw angles in degrees. Rulers measure length, compasses draw arcs and circles, and thermometers measure temperature.", "correctAnswer": "Protractor"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:54:45.412396	\N
57f2b81c-60fa-415e-a035-67cd08a215e5	f01819a1-02ae-4c5b-b324-e5ab89669584	Maths / angle - Easy	Maths / angle	easy	[{"id": "q_1770652508480_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45°", "60°", "90°", "180°"], "question": "What is the measure of a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle measures exactly 90 degrees. It is the angle formed by perpendicular lines.", "correctAnswer": "90°"}, {"id": "q_1770652508480_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: Complementary angles are two angles whose measures add up to 180°.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles add up to 90°. Angles that add up to 180° are called supplementary angles.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770652508480_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "An angle that measures less than 90° is called an ______ angle.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Angles less than 90° are acute angles. Right angles are exactly 90°, and obtuse angles are greater than 90° but less than 180°.", "correctAnswer": "acute"}, {"id": "q_1770652508480_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0°", "90°", "180°", "360°"], "question": "What is the measure of a straight angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A straight angle forms a straight line and measures exactly 180 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "180°"}, {"id": "q_1770652508480_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the complement of a 35° angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles add to 90°. So 90° − 35° = 55°.", "correctAnswer": "55°"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:55:08.568128	\N
418879f7-5313-43d8-a2b8-d1080cc35e62	35808bd3-6ed2-4d5b-8a6a-5dc2ad9824b9	Maths/angles - Easy	Maths/angles	easy	[{"id": "q_1770652548071_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["45", "90", "180", "360"], "question": "How many degrees are in a right angle?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A right angle is exactly one quarter of a full turn. A full turn is 360°, so one quarter is 90°, which defines a right angle.", "correctAnswer": "90"}, {"id": "q_1770652548071_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Complementary angles are two angles whose measures add up to 90 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Complementary angles sum to 90°. In contrast, supplementary angles sum to 180°. For example, 30° and 60° are complementary.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770652548071_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The measure of a straight angle is ____ degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A straight angle forms a straight line. A straight line represents half of a full turn, which is 180°.", "correctAnswer": "180"}, {"id": "q_1770652548071_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What do you call an angle that measures less than 90 degrees?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Angles less than 90° are called acute angles. Exactly 90° is a right angle, and between 90° and 180° is obtuse.", "correctAnswer": "Acute angle"}, {"id": "q_1770652548071_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["100° and 60°", "120° and 40°", "90° and 45°", "110° and 70°"], "question": "Which pair of angles is supplementary?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Supplementary angles add up to 180°. Only 110° and 70° sum to 180°. The others total 160°, 160°, and 135° respectively.", "correctAnswer": "110° and 70°"}]	none	2026-02-09 15:55:48.201182	\N
c21897bf-0e4e-48d9-80dc-68a63473266e	e1e541bd-59ab-4a5d-a00f-a0599b1be97d	Economics Management Science - Easy	Economics Management Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1770654226276_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The value of the next best alternative forgone", "The money you earn from an activity", "A sunk cost already paid", "Accounting profit after taxes"], "question": "What is opportunity cost?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Opportunity cost is the benefit you give up by choosing one option over the next best alternative. It highlights trade-offs in decision-making.", "correctAnswer": "The value of the next best alternative forgone"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a perfectly competitive market, firms are price takers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perfect competition assumes many small firms selling identical products, so no single firm can influence the market price; they accept the market price.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The law of demand states that as price increases, quantity demanded _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Demand and price have an inverse relationship: higher prices lead to lower quantity demanded, all else equal.", "correctAnswer": "decreases"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Define Gross Domestic Product (GDP).", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "GDP measures the economic output of a nation, counting only final goods and services to avoid double counting.", "correctAnswer": "The total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period."}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Raw materials", "Factory rent", "Hourly wages", "Shipping fees"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of a fixed cost?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Fixed costs do not change with output levels. Rent remains the same regardless of how much is produced.", "correctAnswer": "Factory rent"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Inflation measures the rate at which the general price level of goods and services is falling.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Inflation is the rate at which the general price level is rising. A falling price level is called deflation.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Total revenue equals total cost", "Profit is maximized", "Marginal cost equals marginal revenue", "Variable cost equals fixed cost"], "question": "The break-even point occurs when:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Break-even is where a business covers all costs without profit or loss, meaning total revenue equals total cost.", "correctAnswer": "Total revenue equals total cost"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The slope of a typical linear demand curve is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A downward-sloping demand curve reflects the inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded.", "correctAnswer": "negative"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does EOQ stand for, and what is it used for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "EOQ balances ordering and holding costs to find the most cost-effective order quantity.", "correctAnswer": "Economic Order Quantity; it determines the order size that minimizes total inventory costs."}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Vertex (corner point)", "Mid-point", "Interior random point", "Curved boundary"], "question": "In linear programming, the optimal solution typically occurs at a _____ of the feasible region.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "For linear objectives and constraints, optimal solutions are found at extreme points (vertices) of the feasible region.", "correctAnswer": "Vertex (corner point)"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Marginal cost is the cost of producing one additional unit of output.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Marginal cost measures the change in total cost when output increases by one unit.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_11", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["PERT/CPM", "SWOT analysis", "Pareto chart", "Fishbone diagram"], "question": "Which project management technique identifies the critical path by mapping activities and durations?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Technique) and CPM (Critical Path Method) model tasks and durations to find the critical path.", "correctAnswer": "PERT/CPM"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The amount of a good that producers are willing and able to sell at a given price is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Supply describes the quantity producers offer for sale at various prices.", "correctAnswer": "supply"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "Price elasticity of demand measures how quantity demanded responds to changes in price.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Elasticity captures the sensitivity of demand to price changes, helping firms and policymakers anticipate impacts.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_14", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Monopoly", "Monopolistic competition", "Oligopoly", "Perfect competition"], "question": "Which market structure features a single seller and no close substitutes?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In a monopoly, one firm dominates the market and offers a unique product with no close substitutes.", "correctAnswer": "Monopoly"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_15", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the break-even formula in units.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This formula divides fixed costs by unit contribution margin (price minus variable cost) to find the quantity needed to break even.", "correctAnswer": "Break-even units = Fixed Costs / (Price per unit − Variable Cost per unit)."}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_16", "type": "true_false", "question": "In queuing theory, Little’s Law states L = λW.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Little’s Law links average number in the system (L), arrival rate (λ), and average time in the system (W).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_17", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Moving average", "Exponential smoothing", "Delphi method", "Linear regression"], "question": "A simple time-series forecast using the average of the most recent observations is called:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Moving averages smooth out short-term fluctuations by averaging recent data points to forecast the next value.", "correctAnswer": "Moving average"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_18", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sunk costs should influence future economic decisions.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sunk costs cannot be recovered; decisions should be based on future costs and benefits, not past expenditures.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770654226276_19", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Key Performance Indicator", "Knowledge Process Integration", "Known Profit Index", "Key Planning Input"], "question": "In management, KPI stands for:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "KPIs are measurable values that indicate how effectively objectives are being achieved.", "correctAnswer": "Key Performance Indicator"}]	none	2026-02-09 16:23:46.806743	\N
ced776c7-f0df-406f-a6eb-54aaee82029c	a1976679-96f1-4fd5-99ae-86f7e09fbc52	EMS - Easy	EMS	easy	[{"id": "q_1770656942747_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Begin CPR immediately", "Check the patient's pulse", "Ensure the scene is safe", "Perform a secondary assessment"], "question": "What is the first priority when you arrive at an emergency scene?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Scene safety comes first to protect the responder, the patient, and bystanders. You cannot help effectively if the environment is dangerous, so you assess hazards before providing care.", "correctAnswer": "Ensure the scene is safe"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The primary survey focuses on identifying and treating immediate life threats.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The primary survey addresses airway, breathing, circulation, disability, and exposure to quickly find and manage life-threatening problems before moving to detailed assessments.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the ABCs of assessment, the A stands for ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ABCs stand for Airway, Breathing, and Circulation. Ensuring a patent airway is the first step in managing a patient.", "correctAnswer": "Airway"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does PPE stand for in EMS practice?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "PPE includes items such as gloves, masks, and eye protection that help prevent exposure to blood, body fluids, and other hazards.", "correctAnswer": "Personal Protective Equipment"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["High fever", "Pale, cool, clammy skin", "Itchy rash", "Localized muscle cramp"], "question": "Which of the following is a common sign of shock?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Shock often presents with pale, cool, clammy skin due to poor perfusion. Other signs include rapid pulse, rapid breathing, and weakness.", "correctAnswer": "Pale, cool, clammy skin"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Medical history", "Medications", "Mechanism of injury", "Meals"], "question": "In the SAMPLE history, what does the M stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SAMPLE is a quick patient history: Signs and symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past medical history, Last oral intake, Events leading up to the illness or injury.", "correctAnswer": "Medications"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "An AED can be used by lay rescuers following simple prompts.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Automated external defibrillators are designed to be user friendly, guiding the rescuer with voice and visual prompts to analyze rhythm and deliver a shock if indicated.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The emergency phone number in the United States is ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dialing 911 connects callers to emergency dispatchers who can send appropriate EMS, fire, or police resources and provide pre-arrival instructions.", "correctAnswer": "911"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one action you should take before approaching a patient at a traffic collision.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ensuring scene safety includes checking for traffic hazards, turning on hazard lights, and using cones or flares if available to prevent further injury.", "correctAnswer": "Ensure scene safety"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Radial artery at the wrist", "Carotid artery in the neck", "Femoral artery in the groin", "Brachial artery in the upper arm"], "question": "Where is the best place to check a pulse in an unresponsive adult?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The carotid pulse is strong and accessible, making it the preferred site for checking circulation in an unresponsive adult.", "correctAnswer": "Carotid artery in the neck"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "Implied consent allows EMS to treat an unconscious adult who cannot refuse care.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When a patient is unconscious or otherwise unable to consent and needs emergency care, consent is legally implied to allow necessary treatment.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_11", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To assess pain characteristics", "To assess the severity of bleeding", "To determine patient allergies", "To measure blood pressure"], "question": "What is the main purpose of the OPQRST mnemonic?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "OPQRST stands for Onset, Provocation, Quality, Region and radiation, Severity, and Time, and helps structure a pain assessment.", "correctAnswer": "To assess pain characteristics"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "Impaled objects should generally be left in place until advanced care arrives.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Removing an impaled object can worsen bleeding and tissue damage. Stabilize the object and leave removal to surgical care unless it directly obstructs the airway.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The position used for an unconscious, breathing patient without suspected spine injury is the ________ position.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The recovery position helps keep the airway open and allows fluids to drain, reducing the risk of aspiration in an unconscious, breathing patient.", "correctAnswer": "recovery"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "The FAST acronym helps identify which medical emergency?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "FAST stands for Face droop, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, and Time to call emergency services, and is used to recognize signs of stroke.", "correctAnswer": "Stroke"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_15", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Gloves", "Earplugs", "Sunscreen", "Scarf"], "question": "Which piece of PPE is most essential when there is a risk of blood exposure?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gloves protect the rescuer from blood and other body fluids, reducing the risk of infection during patient care.", "correctAnswer": "Gloves"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_16", "type": "true_false", "question": "Oxygen should be given to every patient regardless of their condition.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Oxygen is a medication and should be given based on indications such as signs of hypoxia, respiratory distress, or per local protocols, not universally.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_17", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Gasping breaths fewer than 8 per minute", "Breathing 12–20 per minute with regular chest rise", "Blue or cyanotic lips", "Breathing 30 per minute with accessory muscle use"], "question": "Which finding suggests adequate breathing in a calm adult?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Normal adult respiratory rate is about 12–20 breaths per minute with steady effort and good chest rise. Gasping, cyanosis, or very rapid breathing suggests distress.", "correctAnswer": "Breathing 12–20 per minute with regular chest rise"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does EMS stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "EMS encompasses dispatch, first responders, EMTs and paramedics, and transport systems that provide emergency medical care.", "correctAnswer": "Emergency Medical Services"}, {"id": "q_1770656942747_19", "type": "true_false", "question": "Before delivering a shock with an AED, you must ensure no one is touching the patient.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Clearing the patient prevents accidental shock to rescuers and ensures the AED accurately analyzes the heart rhythm without artifact.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2026-02-09 17:09:02.856047	\N
0c98b2c9-5ee3-459e-ab99-84bf5be68895	a1976679-96f1-4fd5-99ae-86f7e09fbc52	math  - Easy	math 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770657202348_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Square", "Triangle", "Pentagon", "Circle"], "question": "Which shape has exactly 3 sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has 3 sides. A square has 4, a pentagon has 5, and a circle has no sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3 ones", "3 tens", "3 hundreds", "3 thousands"], "question": "In the number 304, what is the value of the digit 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 3 is in the hundreds place, so it represents 300.", "correctAnswer": "3 hundreds"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1/2", "2/4", "3/6", "All of the above"], "question": "Which fraction(s) are equal to one half?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "1/2 is one half. 2/4 simplifies to 1/2 by dividing top and bottom by 2, and 3/6 simplifies to 1/2 by dividing both by 3.", "correctAnswer": "All of the above"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are integers divisible by 2. 0 ÷ 2 = 0 with no remainder, so 0 is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "A rectangle always has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A rectangle has opposite sides equal and four right angles. Only a square has four equal sides.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 − 4 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank: 2, 4, 6, 8, __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time, so after 8 comes 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the perimeter of a square with side length 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Perimeter of a square is 4 × side length: 4 × 3 = 12 units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770657202348_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How many hours are in 60 minutes?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "There are 60 minutes in 1 hour, so 60 minutes equals 1 hour.", "correctAnswer": "1"}]	none	2026-02-09 17:13:22.440966	\N
e7411631-c805-478f-91da-abacc779aaf3	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	ICT input devices, output, hardware, software, ROM, RAM etc - Easy	ICT input devices, output, hardware, software, ROM, RAM etc	easy	[{"id": "q_1770658634017_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer for processing. A keyboard is used to enter text and commands. Monitors and printers are output devices, and speakers output sound.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1770658634017_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is a type of volatile memory that is cleared when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) temporarily stores data for programs while they run. It is volatile, meaning its contents are lost when power is removed. ROM, by contrast, is non-volatile.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770658634017_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The device that displays visual output from the computer is called a ________.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A monitor is an output device that shows images, text, and videos produced by the computer. It converts digital signals into visual display.", "correctAnswer": "monitor"}, {"id": "q_1770658634017_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does ROM stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ROM stands for Read-Only Memory. It is non-volatile storage that typically contains firmware or permanent instructions needed for booting and hardware control.", "correctAnswer": "Read-Only Memory"}, {"id": "q_1770658634017_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Operating system", "Hard disk drive", "Keyboard", "Monitor"], "question": "Which of the following is software rather than hardware?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Software is a set of programs and instructions; an operating system (like Windows, macOS, or Linux) manages hardware and software resources. A hard disk drive, keyboard, and monitor are physical hardware components.", "correctAnswer": "Operating system"}]	none	2026-02-09 17:37:14.122958	\N
ddbc27b8-d1d9-438d-bce2-61ca49978e16	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	Maths BIDMAS - Medium	Maths BIDMAS	medium	[{"id": "q_1770660020447_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["32", "20", "12", "8"], "question": "Evaluate: 12 ÷ 3 × (5 − 2)^2 − 4", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Apply BIDMAS: brackets first (5 − 2 = 3), indices next (3^2 = 9), then division and multiplication left to right (12 ÷ 3 = 4, 4 × 9 = 36), and finally subtraction (36 − 4 = 32). Choosing multiplication before division or doing subtraction earlier leads to incorrect results.", "correctAnswer": "32"}, {"id": "q_1770660020447_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In the expression 8 − 2 × 3, calculating (8 − 2) × 3 gives the correct BIDMAS result.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By BIDMAS, multiplication precedes subtraction. Compute 2 × 3 = 6 first, then 8 − 6 = 2. Doing (8 − 2) × 3 evaluates subtraction before multiplication, giving 6 × 3 = 18, which is incorrect.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770660020447_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The value of 5 + [12 ÷ (2^2)] × 3 is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Inside the brackets, do the index first: 2^2 = 4. Then 12 ÷ 4 = 3. Next multiply: 3 × 3 = 9. Finally add: 5 + 9 = 14. This sequence follows BIDMAS strictly.", "correctAnswer": "14"}, {"id": "q_1770660020447_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Evaluate: -3^2 + (6 ÷ 3) × 4", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Indices apply before the leading negative: -3^2 means −(3^2) = −9, not (−3)^2. Then compute the bracket: 6 ÷ 3 = 2, and multiply: 2 × 4 = 8. Finally add: −9 + 8 = −1.", "correctAnswer": "-1"}, {"id": "q_1770660020447_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["8", "0", "24", "-8"], "question": "Evaluate: 2(3 + 5) − 4^2 ÷ 2", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Brackets first: (3 + 5) = 8, then multiply: 2 × 8 = 16. Next index: 4^2 = 16, then division: 16 ÷ 2 = 8. Finally subtract: 16 − 8 = 8. Multiplication and division share precedence and are handled left to right.", "correctAnswer": "8"}]	none	2026-02-09 18:00:20.550446	\N
954b0e89-03e8-40b9-b83f-5b4765e46e79	a1976679-96f1-4fd5-99ae-86f7e09fbc52	sicence - Medium	sicence	medium	[{"id": "q_1770660289298_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Wire the bulbs in series across the 6 V battery.", "Wire the bulbs in parallel across the 6 V battery.", "Connect only one bulb to the battery at a time.", "No safe configuration; both will be too bright."], "question": "You have two identical 3 V light bulbs and a 6 V battery. Which wiring allows both bulbs to glow at normal brightness without damage?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Two identical bulbs in series share the total voltage roughly equally. With a 6 V battery and equal resistances, each bulb gets about 3 V—its rated voltage—so both operate at normal brightness without overheating. In parallel, each bulb would receive the full 6 V and likely be damaged.", "correctAnswer": "Wire the bulbs in series across the 6 V battery."}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["pH 1.5", "pH 2", "pH 3", "pH 11"], "question": "A solution of a strong monoprotic acid has pH 1. If you dilute it by a factor of 100 (at constant temperature), what is the new pH?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A 100-fold dilution decreases the hydrogen ion concentration by a factor of 100. Since pH = −log10[H+], decreasing [H+] by 100 increases the pH by 2 units. Thus, pH 1 becomes pH 3.", "correctAnswer": "pH 3"}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Enzymes lower activation energy and shift the equilibrium toward products.", "Enzymes provide energy to drive endergonic reactions.", "Enzymes lower activation energy but do not change the reaction's equilibrium constant.", "Enzymes increase the reaction rate by raising the transition state energy."], "question": "Which statement about enzymes is correct?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Enzymes speed up reactions by lowering the activation energy, making it easier to reach the transition state. They accelerate both forward and reverse reactions equally, so the equilibrium position (and K_eq) does not change.", "correctAnswer": "Enzymes lower activation energy but do not change the reaction's equilibrium constant."}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The sea warms faster than the land, creating low pressure over the sea.", "The land warms faster than the sea, creating rising air over land and surface flow from sea to land.", "Earth’s rotation directly pushes air from sea to land.", "Tidal forces drag air toward the shore."], "question": "On a sunny afternoon near a coastline, a sea breeze develops with wind blowing from the sea toward the land. What primarily drives this pattern?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Land heats up more quickly than water, warming the air above it and lowering surface pressure over land. Air rises over land and cooler, higher-pressure air from over the sea moves in at the surface to replace it, creating a sea breeze.", "correctAnswer": "The land warms faster than the sea, creating rising air over land and surface flow from sea to land."}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "We always see the same face of the Moon because the Moon does not rotate on its axis.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Moon is tidally locked to Earth; it does rotate once on its axis each time it orbits Earth. Because the rotation period equals the orbital period, the same lunar hemisphere faces Earth.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a perfectly inelastic collision occurring in an isolated system, total momentum is conserved even though kinetic energy is not.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In the absence of external forces, the total momentum of a system is conserved in all types of collisions. In perfectly inelastic collisions, some kinetic energy is transformed into internal energy (heat, deformation), but momentum remains conserved.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a monohybrid cross of two heterozygous plants (Aa × Aa) for a trait with complete dominance, the expected phenotypic ratio among the offspring is ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The genotype ratio from Aa × Aa is 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa. With complete dominance, both AA and Aa display the dominant phenotype, yielding a 3 dominant : 1 recessive phenotypic ratio.", "correctAnswer": "3:1"}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "According to the reaction 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O, reacting 4.0 moles of O2 completely with excess H2 produces ____ moles of H2O.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The balanced equation shows 1 mole of O2 produces 2 moles of H2O. Therefore, 4.0 moles of O2 will produce 4.0 × 2 = 8.0 moles of H2O when hydrogen is in excess.", "correctAnswer": "8.0"}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why do top-level predators often show higher concentrations of fat-soluble pollutants like PCBs than organisms lower in the food chain?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Fat-soluble pollutants accumulate in tissues and are not easily excreted. As predators consume many prey, the pollutants concentrate at higher trophic levels, leading to higher levels in top predators—a process known as biomagnification.", "correctAnswer": "Biomagnification of fat-soluble toxins up the food chain."}, {"id": "q_1770660289298_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why does sweating cool your body even if the sweat itself is warm?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "When sweat evaporates, water molecules require energy to transition to vapor; this latent heat is taken from your skin, removing thermal energy and lowering skin temperature, which cools the body.", "correctAnswer": "Evaporative cooling; evaporation uses latent heat from the skin."}]	none	2026-02-09 18:04:49.788937	\N
272e92fe-b735-445b-9f19-a690dee17c2e	c6991598-421a-4185-8c34-061f5252824e	characteristics of living organisms - Medium	characteristics of living organisms	medium	[{"id": "q_1770660432482_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Response to stimuli (phototropism)", "Reproduction", "Genetic inheritance", "Cellular respiration"], "question": "A potted plant placed by a sunny window gradually bends toward the light over several days. Which characteristic of living organisms is best illustrated by this observation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The plant’s bending toward light is an example of responding to an environmental stimulus. Although this bending involves growth, the key point is that the direction of growth is regulated by the stimulus (light) via hormones like auxins—classic phototropism. Reproduction and genetic inheritance are not being demonstrated, and cellular respiration is a metabolic process not directly indicated by the bending behavior.", "correctAnswer": "Response to stimuli (phototropism)"}, {"id": "q_1770660432482_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A person sweating to lower body temperature on a hot day", "A plant performing photosynthesis to produce glucose", "A flock of birds migrating south for winter", "A teenager’s bones lengthening during growth spurts"], "question": "Which scenario best illustrates homeostasis in a living organism?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment. Sweating helps dissipate heat through evaporation, restoring body temperature to a set range. Photosynthesis is metabolism, migration is a behavioral response to environmental change, and bone growth reflects growth and development rather than immediate internal regulation.", "correctAnswer": "A person sweating to lower body temperature on a hot day"}, {"id": "q_1770660432482_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: A dormant seed that shows no visible movement is not living.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Dormancy is a state of greatly reduced metabolic activity that allows seeds to survive unfavorable conditions. Even though there is no visible movement, the seed is alive and can resume active metabolism and growth (germination) when conditions improve.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770660432482_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "During exercise, muscle cells increase ATP production by breaking down glucose in a process called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Cellular respiration is the set of metabolic reactions that extract energy from nutrients (like glucose) to form ATP. In eukaryotes, much of this occurs in mitochondria via glycolysis, the Krebs cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation, enabling cells to meet increased energy demands.", "correctAnswer": "cellular respiration"}, {"id": "q_1770660432482_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A bacterium swims toward a region with higher sugar concentration and then divides into two identical cells. Name two characteristics of life this behavior demonstrates.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Swimming toward sugar is chemotaxis, a response to an environmental stimulus (the chemical gradient). Dividing into two cells is binary fission, which is reproduction. Together, these show two core characteristics of living organisms.", "correctAnswer": "Response to stimuli and reproduction"}]	none	2026-02-09 18:07:12.581021	\N
42637cdb-59eb-455a-8461-6eb9f5fb052d	c6991598-421a-4185-8c34-061f5252824e	photosynthesis - Easy	photosynthesis	easy	[{"id": "q_1770660623452_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants take in from the air during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plants use carbon dioxide from the air, along with water and light energy, to make glucose. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1770660623452_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Photosynthesis uses light energy to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the basic summary of photosynthesis: light energy drives the conversion of carbon dioxide and water into glucose (a sugar) and oxygen.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770660623452_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The green pigment in plant leaves that absorbs light energy for photosynthesis is called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Chlorophyll is the green pigment in chloroplasts that captures light energy, giving plants their green color and enabling photosynthesis.", "correctAnswer": "chlorophyll"}, {"id": "q_1770660623452_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the cell organelle where photosynthesis mainly occurs in plant cells.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis takes place in chloroplasts, which contain chlorophyll. Light reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes, and the Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma.", "correctAnswer": "Chloroplast"}, {"id": "q_1770660623452_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Glucose", "Sucrose", "Fructose", "Lactose"], "question": "What is the main sugar produced by photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Glucose is the primary sugar made during photosynthesis. It stores chemical energy that the plant can use or convert into other carbohydrates like sucrose or starch.", "correctAnswer": "Glucose"}]	none	2026-02-09 18:10:25.554387	\N
28ede35e-5862-42e3-9226-d890dc5aef7f	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	Mathematics  - Easy	Mathematics 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770732367243_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["11", "12", "13", "10"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12. One way to see this is 7 + 3 = 10, then add the remaining 2 to get 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Triangle", "Square", "Pentagon", "Circle"], "question": "Which shape has exactly three sides?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A triangle has three sides. A square has four, a pentagon has five, and a circle has no straight sides.", "correctAnswer": "Triangle"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5 ones", "5 tens", "5 hundreds", "5 thousands"], "question": "In the number 452, what is the place value of the digit 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "452 means 4 hundreds, 5 tens, and 2 ones. So the 5 represents 5 tens.", "correctAnswer": "5 tens"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "12", "15", "19"], "question": "Which of these numbers is even?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are divisible by 2 with no remainder. 12 is even; 7, 15, and 19 are odd.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are multiples of 2. Since 0 = 2 × 0, zero is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is 180 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In plane (Euclidean) geometry, the three angles of a triangle always add up to 180 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "A square has four equal sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a square has four equal sides and four right angles.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "9 - __ = 4", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 5 from 9 gives 4 because 9 − 5 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "2, 4, 6, 8, __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The pattern increases by 2 each time (even numbers), so the next number is 10.", "correctAnswer": "10"}, {"id": "q_1770732367243_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is 3 × 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication as repeated addition: 3 × 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:06:07.350843	\N
c5042123-d706-42cc-ae74-3cc59d5a7de2	ac2c39a1-139b-4776-b6e9-c6f02f05bdd5	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1770733012449_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["cat", "quickly", "beautiful", "run"], "question": "Which word is a noun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. 'Cat' is a thing (an animal), so it is a noun. 'Quickly' is an adverb, 'beautiful' is an adjective, and 'run' is typically a verb.", "correctAnswer": "cat"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["a", "an", "the", "some"], "question": "Choose the correct article: \\"I have ___ apple.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We use 'an' before words that start with a vowel sound. 'Apple' begins with the vowel sound /a/, so 'an apple' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "an"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["go", "goes", "going", "gone"], "question": "Select the correct verb form: \\"She ___ to school every day.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In the simple present tense, verbs add -s for third person singular (he/she/it). Therefore, 'She goes' is correct.", "correctAnswer": "goes"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["joyful", "sad", "angry", "bored"], "question": "Which word is a synonym for 'happy'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A synonym is a word with a similar meaning. 'Joyful' means very happy. 'Sad,' 'angry,' and 'bored' do not mean happy.", "correctAnswer": "joyful"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The word 'they' is a pronoun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'They' takes the place of a noun or nouns (people or things), so it is a pronoun.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sentence \\"i live in london.\\" is correctly capitalized.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sentences begin with a capital letter, the pronoun 'I' is always capitalized, and proper nouns like 'London' are capitalized. It should be: \\"I live in London.\\"", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct verb: \\"My friends _____ from Spain.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Friends' is plural, so we use the plural form of the verb 'to be': 'are.'", "correctAnswer": "are"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Choose the correct preposition: \\"The book is _____ the table.\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "We use 'on' to show that something is resting on the surface of something else: 'on the table.'", "correctAnswer": "on"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Rewrite with correct capitalization and punctuation: \\"what is your name\\"", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Capitalize the first word of the sentence and add a question mark at the end of a direct question.", "correctAnswer": "What is your name?"}, {"id": "q_1770733012449_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of the verb \\"walk\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed. 'Walk' becomes 'walked.'", "correctAnswer": "walked"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:16:52.923599	\N
9d19347a-c19d-4e78-817d-0fa9cb5d52af	ac2c39a1-139b-4776-b6e9-c6f02f05bdd5	English - Easy	English	easy	[{"id": "q_1770733068478_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["cat", "sleeps", "the", "very"], "question": "In the sentence 'The cat sleeps.', which word is a noun?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea. 'Cat' is a thing (an animal), while 'sleeps' is a verb and 'the' is an article.", "correctAnswer": "cat"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "An adjective describes a noun.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adjectives give more information about nouns, such as size, color, or quality (e.g., 'blue car').", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct article: ___ apple is red.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'an' before words that begin with a vowel sound, such as 'apple'. At the start of a sentence, it is capitalized as 'An'.", "correctAnswer": "An"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["babys", "babies", "babyes", "babyes"], "question": "Choose the correct plural form of 'baby'.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "When a noun ends in a consonant + 'y', change 'y' to 'i' and add 'es' to make it plural: baby → babies.", "correctAnswer": "babies"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the past tense of 'walk'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Regular verbs form the past tense by adding '-ed': walk → walked.", "correctAnswer": "walked"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sentence 'I has a book' is grammatically correct.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "With the subject 'I', the correct verb form is 'have': 'I have a book.'", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["in", "on", "at", "by"], "question": "Choose the correct preposition: We will meet ___ 3 PM.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use 'at' for specific times: at 3 PM, at noon, at midnight.", "correctAnswer": "at"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct pronoun: Maria and I are friends. ___ like to study together.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Use the subject pronoun 'we' when referring to yourself together with others as the subject of a sentence.", "correctAnswer": "We"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the opposite of 'hot'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The antonym (opposite) of 'hot' in temperature is 'cold'.", "correctAnswer": "cold"}, {"id": "q_1770733068478_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["where is the bus stop", "Where is the bus stop", "Where is the bus stop?", "where is the bus stop?"], "question": "Which sentence is correctly capitalized and punctuated?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A sentence should start with a capital letter and a question should end with a question mark.", "correctAnswer": "Where is the bus stop?"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:17:50.592219	\N
706d599c-f30c-4fec-b6a6-9358d7f807a2	69257e0f-68d8-4fec-8991-75829ca320c0	Mathematics  - Easy	Mathematics 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770733099325_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["10", "11", "12", "13"], "question": "What is 7 + 5?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Adding 7 and 5 gives 12 because 7 + 5 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "question": "What is 9 − 4?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Subtracting 4 from 9 leaves 5 because 9 − 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["12", "16", "18", "20"], "question": "What is 3 × 6?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplication is repeated addition. 3 × 6 means 6 + 6 + 6 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "18"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["2/4", "3/7", "4/10", "5/8"], "question": "Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A fraction equivalent to 1/2 has the same ratio of numerator to denominator. 2/4 simplifies to 1/2 by dividing both by 2.", "correctAnswer": "2/4"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["6", "9", "12", "15"], "question": "What is the perimeter of a square with side length 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A square has four equal sides. Perimeter = 4 × side = 4 × 3 = 12 units.", "correctAnswer": "12"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "30", "300", "3000"], "question": "In the number 5,342, what is the value of the digit 3?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The 3 is in the hundreds place, so its value is 3 × 100 = 300.", "correctAnswer": "300"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5", "10", "15", "20"], "question": "What is 10% of 50?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ten percent means one-tenth. One-tenth of 50 is 50 ÷ 10 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["7", "8", "9", "10"], "question": "What is the next number in the sequence 2, 4, 6, ?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The sequence increases by 2 each time: 2, 4, 6, 8.", "correctAnswer": "8"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Zero is an even number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Even numbers are integers divisible by 2 with no remainder. 0 ÷ 2 = 0, so zero is even.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "A triangle has four sides.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "By definition, a triangle has three sides.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "The sum of the interior angles in a triangle is 180 degrees.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In Euclidean geometry, the three angles of any triangle add up to 180 degrees.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Multiplying any number by 1 changes its value.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Multiplying by 1 leaves a number unchanged. This is called the multiplicative identity.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "The decimal 0.75 is equal to the fraction 3/4.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.75 means 75 hundredths, which is 75/100. Simplifying by dividing numerator and denominator by 25 gives 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "15 ÷ 3 = __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Division asks how many groups of 3 fit into 15. 15 ÷ 3 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The square root of 16 is __", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The square root is the number that, when multiplied by itself, gives 16. 4 × 4 = 16.", "correctAnswer": "4"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "__ is the smallest prime number.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A prime number has exactly two distinct positive divisors: 1 and itself. The smallest such number is 2.", "correctAnswer": "2"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "1 kilometer equals __ meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The metric system defines 1 kilometer as 1000 meters.", "correctAnswer": "1000"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Convert the decimal 0.5 to a fraction in simplest form.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "0.5 is 5/10. Dividing numerator and denominator by 5 gives 1/2.", "correctAnswer": "1/2"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the area of a rectangle with length 5 units and width 3 units?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Area of a rectangle is length × width. So 5 × 3 = 15 square units.", "correctAnswer": "15 square units"}, {"id": "q_1770733099325_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Write the next two odd numbers after 7.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Odd numbers increase by 2: after 7 come 9 and 11.", "correctAnswer": "9, 11"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:18:19.426643	\N
dfeff3e2-dac4-4ff8-b6c9-dc0e8ed2d7e4	ac2c39a1-139b-4776-b6e9-c6f02f05bdd5	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1770733796440_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cell", "Tissue", "Organ", "Molecule"], "question": "What is the smallest unit of life?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms. Tissues and organs are made of cells.", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas do plants absorb during photosynthesis?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Plants take in carbon dioxide and water and, using sunlight, produce glucose and release oxygen.", "correctAnswer": "Carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a definite shape and a definite volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids keep their shape and volume, while liquids have a definite volume but change shape, and gases have neither fixed shape nor volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Magnetism", "Friction", "Gravity", "Electricity"], "question": "Which force pulls objects toward Earth’s center?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity is the attractive force between masses, responsible for keeping us on the ground and causing objects to fall.", "correctAnswer": "Gravity"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Sun is a star.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Sun is a medium-sized star that produces energy through nuclear fusion, just like other stars.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound can travel through a vacuum.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound needs a medium like air, water, or solids to travel. In a vacuum, there are no particles to carry sound waves.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In the water cycle, evaporation turns liquid water into _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evaporation is the process where liquid water gains energy and becomes water vapor, a gas in the atmosphere.", "correctAnswer": "water vapor"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The chemical symbol for sodium is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sodium’s symbol is Na, derived from the Latin name 'Natrium' used historically for the element.", "correctAnswer": "Na"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What instrument is used to measure temperature?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A thermometer measures temperature using materials that change in response to heat, such as mercury, alcohol, or electronic sensors.", "correctAnswer": "Thermometer"}, {"id": "q_1770733796440_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which organ in the human body is primarily responsible for pumping blood?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood through the circulatory system, delivering oxygen and nutrients to the body.", "correctAnswer": "Heart"}]	none	2026-02-10 14:29:56.547667	\N
fbcdd9c8-e49d-4860-9a27-6da516b0f743	258764d5-9b9c-4159-b109-a245f042ad13	accounting - Easy	accounting	easy	[{"id": "q_1770735787773_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Accounts Receivable", "Accounts Payable", "Owner's Equity", "Service Revenue"], "question": "Which of the following is classified as an asset on the balance sheet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Assets are resources owned by a business that have future economic benefit. Accounts Receivable represents amounts customers owe the company, so it is an asset. Accounts Payable is a liability, Owner's Equity is the residual interest in assets after liabilities, and Service Revenue is an income (not an asset).", "correctAnswer": "Accounts Receivable"}, {"id": "q_1770735787773_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The accounting equation states: Assets = Liabilities + Owner's Equity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "This is the foundational accounting equation. It shows that what the company owns (assets) is financed by what it owes (liabilities) and the owners’ claims (equity). Every transaction keeps this equation in balance.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770735787773_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In double-entry accounting, every transaction is recorded in at least ____ accounts.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Double-entry means each transaction affects at least two accounts—one debit and one credit—so that total debits always equal total credits, keeping the accounting equation in balance.", "correctAnswer": "two"}, {"id": "q_1770735787773_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which financial statement reports a company's revenues and expenses for a specific period?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The income statement (also called the statement of profit and loss) summarizes revenues and expenses over a period to show net income or loss, unlike the balance sheet which reports financial position at a point in time.", "correctAnswer": "Income statement"}, {"id": "q_1770735787773_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cash", "Revenue", "Supplies", "Prepaid Insurance"], "question": "Which account normally carries a credit balance?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Revenue accounts typically have credit balances because credits increase revenues. Asset accounts like Cash, Supplies, and Prepaid Insurance normally have debit balances.", "correctAnswer": "Revenue"}]	none	2026-02-10 15:03:07.861136	\N
2bc3fece-4572-47a3-b3a7-1de5e01edfbe	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	Humaties: Communities, Geographical , cultural, religious, economic,add challenges, stengths, characteristics, world veiw, beliefs system, values and ethics, cultural and social context - Medium	Humaties: Communities, Geographical , cultural, religious, economic,add challenges, stengths, characteristics, world veiw, beliefs system, values and ethics, cultural and social context	medium	[{"id": "q_1770740969505_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Co-designing a marine spatial plan that sets seasonal no-build zones for key fishing areas, includes revenue-sharing, and funds gear transition training", "Imposing a temporary fishing ban until turbines are installed to speed construction", "Relocating the community to a government-built inland housing estate", "Approving the project as proposed, with jobs offered on a first-come basis"], "question": "A coastal fishing community with strong ancestral ties to its fishing grounds is approached to host an offshore wind farm promising jobs and municipal revenue. Which engagement strategy best balances economic development with cultural values and livelihood security?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Participatory co-management protects culturally significant livelihoods while allowing development. Seasonal zoning mitigates ecological and cultural impacts, revenue-sharing offsets costs, and training supports adaptation. Top-down bans, relocation, or unmodified approval ignore community values and increase conflict.", "correctAnswer": "Co-designing a marine spatial plan that sets seasonal no-build zones for key fishing areas, includes revenue-sharing, and funds gear transition training"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Cultural relativism means all cultural practices are automatically ethical and should never be critiqued.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Cultural relativism asks us to understand practices within their cultural context and avoid ethnocentrism. It does not prohibit ethical evaluation; rather, it encourages careful, informed critique grounded in universal human rights and situated understanding.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process through which a community blends elements of multiple religious traditions to create a new, locally meaningful practice is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Syncretism describes the combining of different religious or cultural traditions into new forms. It is common in contexts of migration, colonization, or intercultural contact, reflecting communities’ adaptive strengths.", "correctAnswer": "syncretism"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Organizing a community land trust to acquire property and preserve affordable housing", "Prohibiting all new businesses from opening in the area", "Encouraging short-term rentals to increase tourism revenue", "Replacing informal support networks with app-based delivery services"], "question": "An inner-city neighborhood is experiencing rapid gentrification and displacement. Which community-led strategy most directly leverages local strengths to preserve social cohesion and affordability?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Community land trusts leverage collective ownership and governance to stabilize housing, retain residents, and maintain social networks. Blanket bans on businesses, promoting short-term rentals, or eroding informal support systems undermine cohesion and often accelerate displacement.", "correctAnswer": "Organizing a community land trust to acquire property and preserve affordable housing"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give two concrete ways a diasporic community can maintain cultural identity while integrating economically in a host society.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Cultural institutions transmit language and traditions across generations, and economic niches provide livelihoods and visibility. Civic engagement fosters inclusion and rights. Together these strategies balance identity maintenance with integration.", "correctAnswer": "By sustaining cultural institutions (e.g., language schools and cultural centers) and building economic niches (e.g., ethnic businesses that serve both the diaspora and wider markets) while engaging in local civic life."}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_5", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When analyzing how an Indigenous community understands land as a living relative rather than property, you are examining its ______ about what exists and how it exists.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Ontology concerns the nature of reality and categories of being. Understanding a community’s ontological views—such as land as kin—clarifies values, ethics, and stewardship practices that guide decision-making.", "correctAnswer": "ontology"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Average travel time to the nearest secondary school and the out-of-pocket transport costs per student", "Average standardized test scores in the district", "Municipal population density", "Average mobile internet speed in the region"], "question": "Which indicator best captures how geography and household economics jointly affect educational attainment in rural communities?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Travel time and transport costs directly link spatial distance to economic burden, shaping attendance and completion. Test scores, density, or internet speed can matter but do not directly measure the combined geographic-economic barrier to access.", "correctAnswer": "Average travel time to the nearest secondary school and the out-of-pocket transport costs per student"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Secularization necessarily erodes a society’s ethical frameworks.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Ethical frameworks can be grounded in religious or secular philosophies. Secularization shifts authority from religious institutions but can coexist with robust ethics based on human rights, civic norms, and philosophical traditions.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Reduced herd health due to overgrazing and loss of mobility", "Increased resilience to drought through mobility", "Stronger ritual cycles linked to seasonal movement", "Improved market access without trade-offs"], "question": "A pastoralist community that migrates seasonally is settled permanently to ease service delivery. Which unintended consequence is most likely?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sedentarization can concentrate herds on limited pasture, causing overgrazing and disease, while disrupting cultural practices tied to mobility. Market access may improve, but trade-offs often include ecological degradation and cultural loss.", "correctAnswer": "Reduced herd health due to overgrazing and loss of mobility"}, {"id": "q_1770740969505_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Define ethnocentrism and explain one risk it poses when designing community development projects.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Ethnocentrism can lead to misdiagnosing problems and overlooking strengths, resulting in low adoption, cultural harm, or ethical breaches. Culturally responsive, participatory approaches mitigate this risk.", "correctAnswer": "Ethnocentrism is judging other cultures by one’s own cultural standards; it risks imposing inappropriate solutions that ignore local knowledge and values, reducing effectiveness and legitimacy."}]	none	2026-02-10 16:29:29.520039	\N
a129361c-f982-4b72-80ea-cdc926b926f1	a1976679-96f1-4fd5-99ae-86f7e09fbc52	PE - Easy	PE	easy	[{"id": "q_1770745564044_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To prepare your body and increase blood flow", "To make you tired before exercising", "To cool your body down", "To build muscle quickly"], "question": "What is the main purpose of a warm-up in Physical Education?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A warm-up gently raises heart rate and increases blood flow to muscles, improving mobility and readiness. This helps your body transition safely from rest to activity and reduces the risk of injury.", "correctAnswer": "To prepare your body and increase blood flow"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Stretching should only be done before exercise.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Stretching is useful both before and after exercise. Dynamic stretches are best before activity to prepare movement, while static stretches are best after activity to improve flexibility and help muscles relax.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The type of exercise that uses oxygen for energy over time is called ______ exercise.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Aerobic exercise relies on oxygen to produce energy and can be sustained for longer periods. Examples include brisk walking, jogging, swimming, and cycling.", "correctAnswer": "aerobic"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Plank", "Bicep curl", "Calf raise", "Bench press"], "question": "Which exercise primarily strengthens your core muscles?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The plank engages the muscles of the abdomen, lower back, and hips, which make up your core. Strong core muscles support posture and balance in many activities.", "correctAnswer": "Plank"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Drinking water is important before, during, and after physical activity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hydration helps maintain performance and prevents cramps, dizziness, and overheating. Drinking water before, during, and after activity replaces fluids lost through sweat.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What should you do before starting vigorous exercise to help prevent injury?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Warming up gradually increases heart rate and loosens joints and muscles. This prepares your body for harder work and lowers the chance of strains and sprains.", "correctAnswer": "Warm up"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["5–10 minutes", "20–30 minutes", "0 minutes", "1 minute"], "question": "How long should a basic cool-down last after moderate exercise?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A 5–10 minute cool-down with light movement and gentle stretching helps heart rate return to normal, reduces dizziness, and can aid recovery.", "correctAnswer": "5–10 minutes"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "Balance can be improved by practicing standing on one foot or using a balance beam.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Balance skills improve with practice. Simple drills like standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking, and using a balance beam train your body to control posture and stability.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_8", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "When you hold a stretch without moving, it is called ______ stretching.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Static stretching involves holding a position for 10–30 seconds to gently lengthen muscles. It is most effective after exercise when muscles are warm.", "correctAnswer": "static"}, {"id": "q_1770745564045_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Brisk walking", "Sprinting", "Heavy weight lifting", "Sitting still"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of moderate-intensity aerobic activity?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Brisk walking keeps your heart rate elevated for an extended period without being all-out. Sprinting is high intensity, heavy lifting is anaerobic and focuses on strength, and sitting still is not exercise.", "correctAnswer": "Brisk walking"}]	none	2026-02-10 17:46:04.151923	\N
a14dfe37-2a0a-4317-aedc-7fc0d30e3cee	258764d5-9b9c-4159-b109-a245f042ad13	the accounting equation - Easy	the accounting equation	easy	[{"id": "q_1770748383244_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Assets = Liabilities + Equity", "Assets = Revenue - Expenses", "Equity = Assets + Liabilities", "Liabilities = Assets + Equity"], "question": "What is the basic accounting equation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The accounting equation states that a company's resources (assets) are funded by claims against those resources: liabilities (debts) and equity (owner's claim). This is the foundation of the balance sheet.", "correctAnswer": "Assets = Liabilities + Equity"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "If a company buys equipment with cash, the total assets stay the same.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cash (an asset) decreases and equipment (another asset) increases by the same amount. The composition of assets changes, but the total assets remain unchanged. The equation still balances.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Complete the equation: Assets = _____ + Equity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Assets are financed by two sources: liabilities (creditors' claims) and equity (owners' claims). Therefore, Assets = Liabilities + Equity.", "correctAnswer": "Liabilities"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["$30,000", "$70,000", "$20,000", "$50,000"], "question": "If assets are $50,000 and liabilities are $20,000, what is equity?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Using the equation Equity = Assets − Liabilities: $50,000 − $20,000 = $30,000.", "correctAnswer": "$30,000"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the three components of the accounting equation.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The accounting equation is Assets = Liabilities + Equity. These three categories make up the balance sheet and must always balance.", "correctAnswer": "Assets, Liabilities, Equity"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "The accounting equation must balance after every transaction.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Every transaction affects at least two accounts so that Assets = Liabilities + Equity stays equal. This balance is the core of double-entry accounting.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770748383244_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Assets increase $5,000 and liabilities increase $5,000", "Assets decrease $5,000 and liabilities increase $5,000", "Equity increases $5,000 and liabilities decrease $5,000", "Assets increase $5,000 and equity increases $5,000"], "question": "A business receives a $5,000 bank loan. According to the accounting equation, what changes?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cash (an asset) increases by $5,000 from the loan, and Loan Payable (a liability) also increases by $5,000. Equity does not change at the time of borrowing.", "correctAnswer": "Assets increase $5,000 and liabilities increase $5,000"}, {"id": "q_1770748383245_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Fill in: Equity = Assets − _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Rearranging the accounting equation (Assets = Liabilities + Equity) gives Equity = Assets − Liabilities.", "correctAnswer": "Liabilities"}, {"id": "q_1770748383245_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Paying an expense with cash reduces equity.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Expenses reduce profits and therefore retained earnings, which are part of equity. Paying cash also reduces assets, keeping the equation balanced.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770748383245_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Accounts Payable", "Cash", "Equipment", "Owner's Capital"], "question": "Which of the following is a liability?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Accounts Payable is an obligation to pay suppliers, so it is a liability. Cash and Equipment are assets, and Owner's Capital is equity.", "correctAnswer": "Accounts Payable"}]	none	2026-02-10 18:33:06.621854	\N
3f3c4959-2a95-4718-a46c-bfe94f2fdeab	ae18d83d-749c-4733-b5ef-0811a50dc58a	Science - Medium	Science	medium	[{"id": "q_1770824218006_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["-4 m/s^2", "-2 m/s^2", "-5 m/s^2", "4 m/s^2"], "question": "A car traveling at 20 m/s uniformly slows to a stop in 5 seconds. What is its acceleration?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Acceleration is the change in velocity over time. The car's velocity changes from 20 m/s to 0 m/s in 5 s, so a = (0 − 20)/5 = −4 m/s^2. The negative sign indicates deceleration (acceleration opposite to motion).", "correctAnswer": "-4 m/s^2"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Increasing temperature to 60°C", "Increasing enzyme concentration slightly", "Adjusting pH slightly near its optimum", "Lowering temperature to 20°C"], "question": "A human enzyme functions best at about 37°C. Which condition will most likely drastically reduce its activity by denaturing the enzyme?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "High temperatures disrupt the weak interactions that maintain an enzyme’s 3D structure, causing denaturation and loss of activity. Moderate cooling slows reactions but usually does not denature; small changes in pH near the optimum or enzyme concentration changes do not cause denaturation.", "correctAnswer": "Increasing temperature to 60°C"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["3", "5", "7", "9"], "question": "A solution has a hydrogen ion concentration [H+] = 1 × 10^-5 M. What is the pH of the solution?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "pH = −log10[H+]. With [H+] = 10^-5 M, pH = 5. This is slightly acidic, since a neutral solution at 25°C has pH ≈ 7.", "correctAnswer": "5"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Divergent boundary", "Convergent boundary", "Transform boundary", "Intraplate hotspot"], "question": "Which plate boundary is most associated with deep ocean trenches and powerful earthquakes?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Deep ocean trenches form where one plate subducts beneath another at convergent boundaries. Subduction also generates powerful earthquakes due to the immense stresses and friction as plates interact.", "correctAnswer": "Convergent boundary"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The galaxy is moving away rapidly", "The galaxy is very hot", "The galaxy is very old", "The galaxy is rotating faster"], "question": "An astronomer measures a strong redshift in a distant galaxy’s light. What is the best conclusion?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Redshift indicates that light wavelengths are stretched, typically due to the Doppler effect and the expansion of the universe. A strong redshift means the source is receding quickly relative to the observer.", "correctAnswer": "The galaxy is moving away rapidly"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["100 units", "10 units", "900 units", "500 units"], "question": "If producers capture 1000 units of energy, approximately how much energy is available to secondary consumers?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient, averaging about 10%. Primary consumers receive ~100 units from producers, and secondary consumers receive ~10 units from primary consumers.", "correctAnswer": "10 units"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["It decreases hemoglobin’s affinity, releasing more O2 to tissues", "It increases hemoglobin’s affinity, retaining more O2", "It has no significant effect", "It converts hemoglobin into myoglobin"], "question": "During vigorous exercise, how does increased CO2 in tissues affect hemoglobin’s oxygen delivery?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Bohr effect: higher CO2 (and lower pH) shifts the hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, reducing affinity and promoting oxygen unloading where it’s needed.", "correctAnswer": "It decreases hemoglobin’s affinity, releasing more O2 to tissues"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["0%", "25%", "50%", "75%"], "question": "In a cross of two heterozygous individuals (Aa × Aa), what is the probability of an offspring being homozygous recessive (aa)?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Punnett square yields genotypes AA, Aa, Aa, and aa. One out of four is aa, giving a 25% probability.", "correctAnswer": "25%"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound travels faster in water than in air.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sound speed depends on the medium’s density and elasticity. Water’s higher elasticity and continuous structure allow sound to propagate faster than in air.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Carbon-14 dating is effective for determining the age of rocks that are millions of years old.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Carbon-14 dating applies to once-living materials up to about 50,000 years. Rocks millions of years old are dated using other isotopes (e.g., uranium-lead) with much longer half-lives.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a closed system, the total mass remains constant during a chemical reaction.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By the law of conservation of mass, matter is neither created nor destroyed in ordinary chemical reactions. Measured mass stays constant if no matter enters or leaves the system.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "An object moving in uniform circular motion experiences zero net acceleration.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing direction, resulting in centripetal acceleration toward the center of the circle.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which plants release water vapor through their leaves is called ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Transpiration is water loss through stomata, driving the movement of water from roots to leaves and influencing plant cooling and local humidity.", "correctAnswer": "transpiration"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_13", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The SI unit of force is the ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The newton (N) is defined as the force needed to accelerate 1 kg of mass at 1 m/s^2 (1 N = 1 kg·m/s^2).", "correctAnswer": "newton"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The smallest functional unit of the kidney that filters blood is the ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Each kidney contains about a million nephrons. They filter plasma, reabsorb needed substances, and secrete wastes to form urine.", "correctAnswer": "nephron"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In eukaryotic cells, most ATP produced during aerobic respiration is generated in the ____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The mitochondrion houses the electron transport chain and ATP synthase, where oxidative phosphorylation produces the bulk of ATP.", "correctAnswer": "mitochondrion"}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_16", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Why does adding salt to ice water lower its freezing point?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Solute particles interfere with the orderly arrangement of water molecules needed for freezing. This effect depends on the number of dissolved particles (colligative properties), so salt lowers the temperature at which liquid and solid phases are in equilibrium.", "correctAnswer": "Dissolved salt disrupts ice crystal formation, lowering water’s chemical potential and causing freezing-point depression, a colligative property proportional to solute concentration."}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_17", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A balloon rubbed on hair sticks to a wall. Explain the mechanism.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Frictional charging (triboelectric effect) causes the balloon to become negatively charged. Nearby wall molecules polarize, with positive charges closer to the balloon, resulting in an attractive electrostatic force.", "correctAnswer": "Rubbing transfers electrons to the balloon, giving it a negative charge that polarizes the wall’s surface; opposite charges attract, causing electrostatic adhesion."}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What happens to a light ray entering a medium with a higher refractive index at an angle, and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Snell’s law dictates refraction toward the normal when n increases. The light speed is lower in higher-index media. Frequency is set by the source, so wavelength decreases (λ = v/f) as speed decreases.", "correctAnswer": "It slows down and bends toward the normal; its wavelength decreases while frequency remains unchanged due to the medium’s optical properties."}, {"id": "q_1770824218006_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How does vaccination lead to long-term immunity?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "By safely mimicking infection, vaccines stimulate adaptive immunity. Memory B and T cells persist, enabling rapid recognition and elimination of the pathogen if encountered again.", "correctAnswer": "Vaccines present antigens that activate B and T cells, generating specific antibodies and memory cells, which produce a faster, stronger response upon future exposure."}]	none	2026-02-11 15:37:00.116745	\N
7a9710fb-a0c5-46a2-a9d4-2be34027b4df	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science /circulatory  - Easy	Science /circulatory 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770829887270_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To carry oxygen", "To fight infections", "To produce hormones", "To form blood clots"], "question": "What is the main function of red blood cells in the circulatory system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, a protein that binds oxygen in the lungs and transports it to tissues throughout the body. They do not primarily fight infections (white blood cells), make hormones (endocrine glands), or form clots (platelets).", "correctAnswer": "To carry oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1770829887270_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The human heart has four chambers.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is divided into four chambers: two upper atria (right and left) and two lower ventricles (right and left). This structure helps separate oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood for efficient circulation.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770829887270_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The largest artery in the human body is the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The aorta is the main artery that carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle of the heart to the rest of the body through its branches.", "correctAnswer": "aorta"}, {"id": "q_1770829887270_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Arteries", "Veins", "Capillaries", "Lymphatic vessels"], "question": "Which type of blood vessel primarily carries blood back to the heart?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Veins return blood to the heart, often carrying oxygen-poor blood from the body back to the right atrium. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, capillaries are sites of exchange, and lymphatic vessels move lymph, not blood.", "correctAnswer": "Veins"}, {"id": "q_1770829887270_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the valve that prevents backflow of blood from the left ventricle into the left atrium.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The mitral valve, also called the bicuspid valve, lies between the left atrium and left ventricle. It opens to let blood flow into the ventricle and closes during ventricular contraction to prevent backflow.", "correctAnswer": "mitral valve"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:11:29.763628	\N
162147cc-b175-4363-a1f8-7bba2ae58342	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science heartbeat and pulse - Easy	Science heartbeat and pulse	easy	[{"id": "q_1770830035163_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The rhythmic expansion and recoil of an artery as the heart pumps blood", "The electrical signal that controls breathing", "The pressure of blood inside veins only", "The movement of air in and out of the lungs"], "question": "What is a pulse in the context of human biology?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Each heartbeat pushes blood into the arteries, causing them to expand and recoil. This rhythmic throbbing can be felt as the pulse at certain points on the body.", "correctAnswer": "The rhythmic expansion and recoil of an artery as the heart pumps blood"}, {"id": "q_1770830035163_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "An average adult's resting heart rate is typically between 60 and 100 beats per minute.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "For most healthy adults at rest, heart rate commonly falls between 60–100 bpm. Factors like fitness, stress, hydration, and medications can shift it slightly; trained athletes often have resting rates below 60 bpm.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770830035163_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The instrument doctors use to listen to heart sounds is called a ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A stethoscope amplifies internal body sounds, allowing clinicians to hear heartbeats and rhythms. It differs from a sphygmomanometer, which measures blood pressure.", "correctAnswer": "stethoscope"}, {"id": "q_1770830035163_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common body site where the pulse is easily felt using your fingertips.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The radial artery at the wrist is a common site to feel the pulse. Other easy locations include the neck (carotid artery) and the inside of the elbow (brachial artery). Use gentle pressure with two or three fingertips.", "correctAnswer": "wrist (radial artery)"}, {"id": "q_1770830035163_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Arteries", "Veins", "Capillaries", "Lymphatic vessels"], "question": "Which type of blood vessel carries the pulse wave and is typically used to check your pulse?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Pulse is the pressure wave generated by each heartbeat and travels through arteries. Veins return blood to the heart at lower pressure, so a pulse is not typically felt in them.", "correctAnswer": "Arteries"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:13:55.2542	\N
0e986aa9-ca2f-4b9f-af48-2363bec4627c	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	 Science Respirotory  - Easy	 Science Respirotory 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770830197222_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Trachea", "Esophagus", "Aorta", "Spinal cord"], "question": "Which structure is commonly called the windpipe and carries air from the throat to the lungs?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The trachea is the windpipe that transports air to the lungs. The esophagus carries food to the stomach, the aorta is a major artery, and the spinal cord is part of the nervous system.", "correctAnswer": "Trachea"}, {"id": "q_1770830197222_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves the blood in the alveoli of the lungs.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Alveoli are tiny air sacs with thin walls where gases are exchanged: oxygen diffuses into the blood and carbon dioxide diffuses out to be exhaled.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770830197222_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Tiny air sacs at the ends of the bronchioles where gas exchange occurs are called ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Alveoli are clusters of microscopic sacs that greatly increase lung surface area, enabling efficient exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide with the blood.", "correctAnswer": "alveoli"}, {"id": "q_1770830197222_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What sheet-like muscle contracts to help pull air into the lungs during inhalation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The diaphragm is a dome-shaped muscle separating the chest and abdomen. When it contracts and moves downward, chest volume increases and air flows into the lungs.", "correctAnswer": "diaphragm"}, {"id": "q_1770830197222_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To exchange gases (bring in oxygen and remove carbon dioxide)", "To break down food into nutrients", "To pump blood through the body", "To produce urine and remove wastes from blood"], "question": "What is the main purpose of the respiratory system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The respiratory system’s primary function is gas exchange: supplying oxygen to the body and removing carbon dioxide produced by cells.", "correctAnswer": "To exchange gases (bring in oxygen and remove carbon dioxide)"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:16:37.3501	\N
e3274277-9d8d-43ad-9b51-6f223b5e416d	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science reproducation  - Easy	Science reproducation 	easy	[{"id": "q_1770830308115_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Fertilization", "Pollination", "Mitosis", "Budding"], "question": "What is the process called when a male and female gamete fuse to form a zygote?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Fertilization is the fusion of male and female gametes (such as sperm and egg) to form a zygote. Pollination is the transfer of pollen in plants, mitosis is cell division for growth, and budding is a type of asexual reproduction.", "correctAnswer": "Fertilization"}, {"id": "q_1770830308115_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Asexual reproduction produces offspring that are genetically identical to the parent.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In asexual reproduction, only one parent is involved, and the offspring are produced by processes like binary fission or budding without mixing genetic material. This results in clones (genetically identical offspring).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770830308115_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In humans, the female gamete is called an ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The female gamete is called an egg, also known as an ovum. Eggs carry half the genetic information and combine with sperm during fertilization to form a zygote.", "correctAnswer": "egg"}, {"id": "q_1770830308115_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Budding", "Fertilization", "Pollination", "Meiosis"], "question": "Which of the following is a type of asexual reproduction?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Budding is an asexual process where a new organism grows from a part of the parent (seen in yeast and hydra). Fertilization and pollination are related to sexual reproduction, and meiosis is the cell division that produces gametes.", "correctAnswer": "Budding"}, {"id": "q_1770830308115_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the type of cell division that reduces the chromosome number by half to form gametes.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Meiosis is a specialized cell division that produces haploid gametes (sperm and eggs) from diploid cells. It involves two rounds of division, ensuring offspring receive the correct amount of genetic material.", "correctAnswer": "Meiosis"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:18:28.205072	\N
53784956-a7f3-44f3-a1a7-7aa6e2a43c76	92772ca5-4b2b-40df-af53-9fc98191b3ca	Science/ diseases - Easy	Science/ diseases	easy	[{"id": "q_1770830834012_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Bacterium", "Virus", "Fungus", "Parasite"], "question": "Tuberculosis (TB) is primarily caused by which type of microorganism?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tuberculosis is caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Bacteria are single-celled organisms; in TB, they infect the lungs most commonly but can affect other organs.", "correctAnswer": "Bacterium"}, {"id": "q_1770830834012_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Vaccines help prevent infectious diseases by training the immune system to recognize and fight specific germs.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Vaccines expose the immune system to a harmless form or part of a pathogen, prompting the body to produce memory cells and antibodies. This prepares the immune system to respond quickly if the real pathogen is encountered.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770830834012_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A deficiency of vitamin C can lead to the disease called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C (ascorbic acid), which is essential for collagen formation. Symptoms include fatigue, gum disease, and easy bruising.", "correctAnswer": "scurvy"}, {"id": "q_1770830834012_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the insect vector that transmits malaria to humans.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Malaria is transmitted by the bite of infected female Anopheles mosquitoes, which carry Plasmodium parasites that enter the bloodstream and infect the liver and red blood cells.", "correctAnswer": "female Anopheles mosquito"}, {"id": "q_1770830834012_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Liver", "Heart", "Lungs", "Kidneys"], "question": "Hepatitis primarily affects which organ of the body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hepatitis refers to inflammation of the liver. It can be caused by viruses (like hepatitis A, B, and C), toxins, alcohol, or autoimmune conditions.", "correctAnswer": "Liver"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:27:14.112286	\N
ada90789-ae08-41c3-b4cf-91b73e97301f	c49bc2c0-a181-47f4-b27e-de620f591748	Science - Easy	Science	easy	[{"id": "q_1770831132370_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Plasma"], "question": "Which state of matter has a fixed shape and a fixed volume?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solids have both a definite shape and a definite volume because their particles are tightly packed and only vibrate in place. Liquids have a definite volume but take the shape of their container, and gases have neither fixed shape nor fixed volume.", "correctAnswer": "Solid"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Mars", "Venus", "Jupiter", "Mercury"], "question": "Which planet is known as the 'Red Planet'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Mars is called the Red Planet because its surface is covered with iron oxide (rust), which gives it a reddish appearance when viewed from Earth.", "correctAnswer": "Mars"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which gas is essential for human respiration?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Humans need oxygen for cellular respiration, the process that releases energy from food. Although air is mostly nitrogen, oxygen is the gas the body uses to produce energy.", "correctAnswer": "Oxygen"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["80°C", "90°C", "100°C", "110°C"], "question": "At sea level, what is the boiling point of pure water?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Under standard atmospheric pressure (at sea level), pure water boils at 100 degrees Celsius. Changes in altitude and pressure can shift the boiling point.", "correctAnswer": "100°C"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Sun is a star.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Sun is a medium-sized star that produces energy through nuclear fusion. It is the closest star to Earth and the center of our solar system.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sound can travel through a vacuum.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sound needs a material medium (like air, water, or solids) to travel. In a vacuum, there are no particles to carry the vibrations, so sound cannot propagate.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which green plants use sunlight to make their own food is called ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Photosynthesis is the process where plants convert light energy into chemical energy, using carbon dioxide and water to produce glucose and oxygen.", "correctAnswer": "photosynthesis"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Water freezes at ____ degrees Celsius.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water changes from liquid to solid (ice) at 0 degrees Celsius.", "correctAnswer": "0°C"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the largest planet in our solar system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Jupiter is the largest planet, with a diameter of about 143,000 km. Its massive size and strong gravity influence other bodies in the solar system.", "correctAnswer": "Jupiter"}, {"id": "q_1770831132370_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Which organ in the human body pumps blood throughout the body?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The heart is a muscular organ that contracts rhythmically to pump blood, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues and removing waste products.", "correctAnswer": "The heart"}]	none	2026-02-11 17:32:12.391051	\N
42b2111d-d1b5-454f-b1f3-2c983cd081da	0a954735-5aa9-4a4e-8660-832b568156a3	ems - Easy	ems	easy	[{"id": "q_1772556973507_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Emergency Medical Services", "Emergency Management System", "Environmental Monitoring Service", "Emergency Medicine Specialists"], "question": "What does EMS stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "EMS stands for Emergency Medical Services, the system of trained personnel and resources that provide prehospital care and transport for the sick and injured.", "correctAnswer": "Emergency Medical Services"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ensure scene safety", "Check airway", "Assess circulation", "Begin CPR"], "question": "What is the first step before approaching any patient in an EMS scene?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Scene safety is always the first priority to protect responders, patients, and bystanders. You cannot help the patient if you become a victim yourself.", "correctAnswer": "Ensure scene safety"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Professional Provider Education", "Prehospital Patient Evaluation", "Personal Protective Equipment", "Primary Prevention Efforts"], "question": "What does PPE stand for in EMS practice?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "PPE means Personal Protective Equipment, such as gloves, masks, eye protection, and gowns, which reduce exposure to infectious and hazardous materials.", "correctAnswer": "Personal Protective Equipment"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["6-10 breaths per minute", "12-20 breaths per minute", "24-30 breaths per minute", "30-40 breaths per minute"], "question": "What is a normal resting respiratory rate for a healthy adult?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A normal adult breathes about 12-20 times per minute at rest. Rates below or above this range can indicate respiratory problems or other conditions.", "correctAnswer": "12-20 breaths per minute"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["AED (Automated External Defibrillator)", "Glucometer", "Pulse oximeter", "Stethoscope"], "question": "Which device analyzes heart rhythm and may deliver a shock during cardiac arrest?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An AED analyzes the heart rhythm and advises or delivers a shock if needed to treat certain lethal arrhythmias during cardiac arrest.", "correctAnswer": "AED (Automated External Defibrillator)"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Recovery position (on the side)", "Trendelenburg position (feet elevated)", "High Fowler's position (sitting upright)", "Prone position (face down)"], "question": "Which position is recommended for an unconscious, breathing patient without suspected spinal injury?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The recovery position helps maintain an open airway and allows fluids to drain, reducing the risk of aspiration in an unconscious, breathing patient.", "correctAnswer": "Recovery position (on the side)"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "The 'A' in the ABCs of assessment stands for Airway.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ABCs stand for Airway, Breathing, and Circulation—the foundational priorities in patient assessment and resuscitation.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "A tourniquet should be placed directly over a joint.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Tourniquets should be placed 2-3 inches above the bleeding site, never over a joint, to effectively compress the arteries and control severe bleeding.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772556973507_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "In the SAMPLE history, the 'A' stands for Allergies.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SAMPLE stands for Signs/Symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past medical history, Last oral intake, and Events leading up to the illness/injury.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The FAST acronym (Face, Arm, Speech, Time) is used to recognize signs of stroke.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "FAST helps quickly identify stroke: facial droop, arm weakness, slurred speech, and the importance of calling for help immediately (Time).", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The number to call for emergency help in the United States is ____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Dialing 911 connects you to emergency dispatch who can send EMS, fire, and police. Early activation of EMS saves lives.", "correctAnswer": "911"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "To open the airway of an unresponsive patient without suspected spinal injury, use the ____-____ maneuver.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The head-tilt chin-lift moves the tongue away from the back of the throat, helping keep the airway open when there is no spinal trauma concern.", "correctAnswer": "head-tilt chin-lift"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "If direct pressure fails to control severe limb bleeding, a ____ may be applied proximal to the wound.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A properly applied tourniquet compresses arterial flow to control life-threatening extremity hemorrhage when direct pressure is insufficient.", "correctAnswer": "tourniquet"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Give one common sign of shock in a patient.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Shock often presents with pale, cool, clammy skin due to poor perfusion. Other signs include rapid pulse, low blood pressure, and altered mental status.", "correctAnswer": "pale, cool, clammy skin"}, {"id": "q_1772556973508_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the OPQRST mnemonic help you evaluate?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "OPQRST guides a focused assessment of pain (Onset, Provocation/Palliation, Quality, Region/Radiation, Severity, Time), clarifying the patient's symptoms.", "correctAnswer": "pain"}]	none	2026-03-03 16:56:13.652288	\N
03f5ed5a-5c06-43ab-ad3a-7e0165abf122	0a954735-5aa9-4a4e-8660-832b568156a3	unstable smp - Easy	unstable smp	easy	[{"id": "q_1772557017984_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Single-Player Mode", "Survival Multiplayer", "Super Mod Pack", "Server Map Project"], "question": "In the term \\"Unstable SMP,\\" what does SMP stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SMP commonly stands for Survival Multiplayer, a mode where multiple players survive and build together on the same world. \\"Unstable\\" often indicates experimental or frequently changing gameplay.", "correctAnswer": "Survival Multiplayer"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Unstable SMPs may include unpredictable events or bugs because they use experimental mods or frequent updates.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unstable SMPs often test new features or mod combinations. This can introduce unexpected behavior, making the experience more chaotic and experimental.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A common safety tip in an Unstable SMP is to keep a recent ______ to protect your progress if problems occur.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Backups protect progress from crashes or rollbacks. Admins usually handle server backups, but knowing why backups matter helps players understand how progress is preserved.", "correctAnswer": "backup"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In an SMP, what is the \\"spawn\\" area?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Spawn is the location where new players join the world and where players may return after death. It is often protected and used as a hub.", "correctAnswer": "The place where players first appear or respawn."}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keep using the item to see if it fixes itself", "Report the bug to admins with steps to reproduce", "Blame other players in chat", "Delete your entire game folder immediately"], "question": "If a new item from a mod crashes the game on an Unstable SMP, what should you do first?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Reporting the issue with details helps admins and mod developers find and fix the problem. Randomly continuing or deleting files can make things worse or lose data.", "correctAnswer": "Report the bug to admins with steps to reproduce"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "In an Unstable SMP, the term \\"unstable\\" means the server is always offline and cannot be played.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unstable refers to experimental features or frequent changes that may cause bugs or balance issues, not permanent downtime. The server is intended to be played.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Most SMPs have rules against ______, which means destroying or stealing from others’ builds without permission.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Griefing undermines collaboration and is usually prohibited to keep the server fair and friendly, especially when chaos from instability is already present.", "correctAnswer": "griefing"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Lower your render distance", "Increase shader resolution", "Install higher-resolution texture packs", "Run multiple game instances at once"], "question": "Which client setting can help reduce lag for beginners on an Unstable SMP?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Lowering render distance reduces how much the game tries to display, improving performance. High-res shaders and textures add load and usually increase lag.", "correctAnswer": "Lower your render distance"}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does it mean if an SMP is \\"whitelisted\\"?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A whitelist restricts access to specific usernames, improving security and community moderation.", "correctAnswer": "Only approved player accounts are allowed to join."}, {"id": "q_1772557017984_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To coordinate during random events or bugs", "To cheat game mechanics", "To avoid all exploration", "To remove the need for server rules"], "question": "Why is communication with teammates especially important on an Unstable SMP?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unpredictable changes require quick coordination—sharing information about issues, dangers, and plans helps the team adapt and stay safe.", "correctAnswer": "To coordinate during random events or bugs"}]	none	2026-03-03 16:56:58.085679	\N
96bef1ce-2aaf-43eb-a592-c1704f8b9e42	1194fc07-997a-42cd-ba4a-ce5bc3ea7ccc	unstable smp - Easy	unstable smp	easy	[{"id": "q_1772557274171_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Survival Multiplayer", "Singleplayer Mode Plus", "Super Mod Pack", "Server Map Project"], "question": "In Minecraft, what does the acronym SMP stand for, as in an 'unstable SMP'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SMP commonly means Survival Multiplayer, a mode where multiple players share a survival world. Calling it 'unstable' usually hints at unpredictable gameplay or frequent changes.", "correctAnswer": "Survival Multiplayer"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: An 'unstable' SMP often features unpredictable events, rapid changes, or experimental settings.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "'Unstable' typically signals that rules, features, or the social environment may change quickly, making gameplay less predictable.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Unpredictable PvP encounters", "Guaranteed peaceful mode at all times", "No rule changes ever", "Single-player only access"], "question": "Which of the following is a common challenge players face on an unstable SMP?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unstable SMPs often have shifting dynamics that can lead to sudden PvP conflicts. The other options contradict the idea of a multiplayer, changeable environment.", "correctAnswer": "Unpredictable PvP encounters"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Forming ______ with other players can help you survive on an unstable SMP.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Alliances provide protection, shared resources, and information—especially useful when server conditions or player relations are unpredictable.", "correctAnswer": "alliances"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does PvP stand for, and why might it matter on an unstable SMP?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "PvP means Player versus Player. On unstable SMPs, conflicts can arise suddenly, so knowing PvP is allowed or likely helps you prepare defenses and strategies.", "correctAnswer": "Player versus Player; combat between players is common or can happen unpredictably."}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Strictly enforced no-PvP rules at all times", "Frequent pranks and raids", "Rapid rule or feature changes", "Experimental mods or datapacks"], "question": "Which is LEAST likely to describe an unstable SMP?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "While policies vary by server, unstable SMPs typically imply fewer restrictions or shifting rules, making a permanently strict no-PvP stance less typical.", "correctAnswer": "Strictly enforced no-PvP rules at all times"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: Keeping multiple hidden stashes or making backups of important coordinates can reduce risk on an unstable SMP.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Spreading valuables and tracking safe locations helps limit losses during raids, resets, or sudden conflicts common on unstable servers.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In an unstable SMP, server rules and features are often ______ and may change without much notice.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Calling a server 'unstable' often implies experimental or provisional rules and features that can change quickly.", "correctAnswer": "experimental"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Testing new updates and features early", "Locking the server to one version forever", "Disabling PvP entirely for all players", "Guaranteeing there will be no world resets"], "question": "If a server is described as 'unstable,' which admin practice might you reasonably expect?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Unstable servers often experiment with new versions or plugins/datapacks. The other options suggest rigid stability, which contradicts the 'unstable' label.", "correctAnswer": "Testing new updates and features early"}, {"id": "q_1772557274171_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one simple strategy to protect your base on an unstable SMP.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Concealment—like building far away, underground, or camouflaged—reduces chances of discovery and raids in a volatile environment.", "correctAnswer": "Hide your base (e.g., build underground or far from spawn)."}]	none	2026-03-03 17:01:16.875926	\N
f699ad96-7e69-4d1f-a1c2-751ba8c79602	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1772702785121_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Projector"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer. A keyboard lets users enter text and commands, while monitors and projectors display output and printers produce hard copies.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Microsoft Word", "Hard disk drive", "Mouse", "Monitor"], "question": "Which of these is an example of software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Software is a set of programs and instructions. Microsoft Word is an application program, whereas a hard disk drive, mouse, and monitor are hardware components.", "correctAnswer": "Microsoft Word"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Personal Unit", "Central Program Utility", "Control Processing Unit"], "question": "What does CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The CPU (Central Processing Unit) is the brain of the computer that performs calculations and executes instructions.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".jpg", ".txt", ".exe", ".pptx"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for a digital photo?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The .jpg (or .jpeg) format is a common compressed image file type. .txt is a text file, .exe is an executable, and .pptx is a presentation file.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["LAN", "WAN", "MAN", "VPN"], "question": "Which type of network commonly connects computers within a single building or small area?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A LAN (Local Area Network) connects devices in a small area like a home, school, or office. WANs span large areas; MANs cover cities; VPN is a private encrypted connection over a public network.", "correctAnswer": "LAN"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Manage hardware and software resources", "Create presentations", "Browse the web", "Scan for viruses"], "question": "What is the main function of an operating system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (e.g., Windows, macOS, Linux) coordinates hardware, manages files, and provides a platform for applications to run.", "correctAnswer": "Manage hardware and software resources"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["HyperText Transfer Protocol", "High Transfer Text Protocol", "Hyperlink Transfer Process", "Home Tool Transfer Program"], "question": "In a website address, what does 'http' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "HTTP is the protocol used for transferring web pages on the internet. It defines how messages are formatted and transmitted.", "correctAnswer": "HyperText Transfer Protocol"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ctrl+X", "Ctrl+V", "Ctrl+C", "Ctrl+Z"], "question": "Which key combination is commonly used to copy selected text on Windows?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ctrl+C copies the selection. Ctrl+X cuts, Ctrl+V pastes, and Ctrl+Z undoes the last action. On macOS, the equivalent for copy is Command+C.", "correctAnswer": "Ctrl+C"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hard disk drive", "RAM", "Cache memory", "CPU register"], "question": "Which of the following is non-volatile storage?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Non-volatile storage retains data when power is off. Hard drives (and SSDs) are non-volatile. RAM, cache, and registers lose data when power is off.", "correctAnswer": "Hard disk drive"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Byte", "Bit", "Kilobyte", "Pixel"], "question": "What is the smallest unit of information in computing?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A bit (binary digit) represents a 0 or 1. Eight bits make a byte. Kilobytes are larger units of storage, and pixels are picture elements, not data units.", "correctAnswer": "Bit"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "A firewall helps protect a computer by monitoring network traffic.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Firewalls filter incoming and outgoing network traffic based on security rules, helping block unauthorized access.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "The cloud means your data is stored only on your local computer.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cloud storage keeps data on remote servers accessed via the internet, not solely on your local device.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "Using 'CC' in an email sends a hidden copy to recipients.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CC (carbon copy) sends a visible copy to recipients. BCC (blind carbon copy) sends a hidden copy that other recipients cannot see.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "A spreadsheet program is best suited for organizing numbers and performing calculations.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Spreadsheets like Microsoft Excel or Google Sheets are designed for numeric data, formulas, and analysis in rows and columns.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_14", "type": "true_false", "question": "Bluetooth is a wired communication technology.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bluetooth is a short-range wireless technology used to connect devices like headphones, keyboards, and phones.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The physical parts of a computer that you can touch are called _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hardware includes tangible components like the keyboard, monitor, and motherboard as opposed to software, which is the programs and instructions.", "correctAnswer": "hardware"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The program used to view web pages is called a _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A web browser (e.g., Chrome, Firefox, Safari) retrieves and displays web pages. It interprets HTML and other web technologies.", "correctAnswer": "web browser"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_17", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The keyboard shortcut Ctrl+Z is commonly used to _____ the last action.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ctrl+Z reverses the most recent action in many applications, helping recover from mistakes quickly.", "correctAnswer": "undo"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one example of an output device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Output devices present information from the computer. Examples include monitors, printers, speakers, and projectors. Any one is acceptable.", "correctAnswer": "Monitor"}, {"id": "q_1772702785121_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym 'URL' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the web address that specifies the location of a resource on the internet.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:26:25.135211	\N
4efc46bd-d9aa-4f27-83c3-b81a4cf55e1f	fb202835-8d89-481d-bd02-4376f89bedff	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1772702851215_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Windows 10", "Google Chrome", "Intel Core i5", "USB flash drive"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of an operating system?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (OS) manages a computer’s hardware and software resources. Windows 10 is an OS. Google Chrome is a web browser, Intel Core i5 is a CPU model, and a USB flash drive is a storage device.", "correctAnswer": "Windows 10"}, {"id": "q_1772702851215_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "RAM is a type of temporary memory that is cleared when the computer is turned off.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) is volatile memory. It stores data only while the computer is powered on, allowing fast access for active programs. When power is lost, the data in RAM is cleared.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702851215_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The ______ is the physical part of a computer system that you can touch.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Hardware refers to the tangible components of a computer (like the keyboard, monitor, and CPU). Software refers to programs and instructions that run on the hardware.", "correctAnswer": "hardware"}, {"id": "q_1772702851215_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the abbreviation 'URL' stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the address used to access resources on the web, such as https://example.com, telling the browser where to find a specific page or file.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1772702851215_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Monitor", "Mouse", "Scanner", "Microphone"], "question": "Which device is primarily an output device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An output device shows or plays the results of computer processing to the user. A monitor displays visual output. A mouse and microphone are input devices, and a scanner is an input device for images/documents.", "correctAnswer": "Monitor"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:27:31.227458	\N
4b5667a9-b394-46c5-a332-8cc41d9c065f	6e3f17a8-c6a6-4244-bac4-1687c820a140	COMPUTER - Medium	COMPUTER	medium	[{"id": "q_1772702931882_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["UDP (User Datagram Protocol)", "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)", "HTTP/2 over TLS", "FTP"], "question": "You’re building a real-time multiplayer game where low latency is more important than guaranteed delivery order. You can implement your own lightweight retransmission for critical events at the application layer. Which transport protocol is the better default choice?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "UDP avoids connection setup and head-of-line blocking, minimizing latency. It allows the application to tolerate some loss or implement custom reliability only where needed. TCP ensures reliable, ordered delivery but can introduce delays due to congestion control and retransmissions, making it less suitable for fast-paced, real-time gameplay. HTTP/2 and FTP are application-layer protocols built atop TCP and are not appropriate for real-time transport needs.", "correctAnswer": "UDP (User Datagram Protocol)"}, {"id": "q_1772702931882_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Replace the linked list with a dynamic array/vector to store elements contiguously", "Unroll the summation loop by a factor of 2", "Randomize the order of fields in the struct", "Replace the linked list with a hash table keyed by index"], "question": "A program repeatedly sums values stored in a very large linked list of small structs. On a modern CPU with a deep cache hierarchy, which change is most likely to improve performance without changing the algorithmic complexity?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Linked lists scatter nodes in memory, causing frequent cache misses due to poor spatial locality. Storing elements contiguously in an array/vector lets the CPU prefetch sequential cache lines and greatly reduces cache miss penalties. Loop unrolling offers limited benefit if the bottleneck is memory latency. Randomizing field order is unlikely to help and may hurt, and a hash table increases overhead without addressing memory locality.", "correctAnswer": "Replace the linked list with a dynamic array/vector to store elements contiguously"}, {"id": "q_1772702931882_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "On a 64-bit operating system and CPU, the C/C++ 'int' type is always 64 bits wide.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The size of 'int' is implementation-defined and depends on the compiler and data model (e.g., LP64, LLP64). On most mainstream 64-bit systems using the LP64 model (Linux/macOS), 'int' remains 32 bits while 'long' is 64 bits. On Windows (LLP64), 'int' is also typically 32 bits while 'long long' is 64 bits. Therefore, 'int' is not guaranteed to be 64 bits just because the platform is 64-bit.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702931882_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The CPU technique that guesses the outcome of conditional jumps to keep pipelines busy and reduce stalls is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Branch prediction attempts to forecast whether a branch will be taken so that the CPU can speculatively execute the likely path and keep the pipeline full. Accurate prediction improves instruction throughput; mispredictions cause pipeline flushes and performance penalties.", "correctAnswer": "branch prediction"}, {"id": "q_1772702931882_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You have a CPU-bound Python program and want to utilize multiple CPU cores. Which concurrency approach is more appropriate, and why?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "CPython’s Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) prevents multiple native threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously, limiting CPU-bound threads to a single core. Using the multiprocessing module creates separate processes with independent interpreters, allowing true parallelism across cores at the cost of higher inter-process communication overhead.", "correctAnswer": "Multiprocessing (separate processes bypass the GIL and can run truly in parallel on multiple cores)."}]	none	2026-03-05 09:28:51.895637	\N
