difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (or JPEG) is a common image file format for photos. .mp3 is audio, .docx is a Word document, and .zip is a compressed archive.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Uniform Resource Locator", "Universal Resource Link", "Unified Routing Locator", "Uniform Reference Link"], "question": "What does URL stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "URL stands for Uniform Resource Locator, which is the address used to access resources (like web pages) on the internet.", "correctAnswer": "Uniform Resource Locator"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["LAN", "WAN", "MAN", "PAN"], "question": "Which type of network typically connects computers within a single building?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A LAN (Local Area Network) connects devices in a limited area such as a room, office, or building. WANs cover very large areas, MANs cover cities, and PANs connect personal devices around an individual.", "correctAnswer": "LAN"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["password", "john1990", "123456", "Giraffe123!"], "question": "Which of the following is the strongest password?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Strong passwords are longer and include a mix of letters, numbers, and symbols. “Giraffe123!” is stronger than simple or common passwords like “password,” “john1990,” or “123456.”", "correctAnswer": "Giraffe123!"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Storing files on remote internet servers", "Storing files on a USB drive", "Storing files on your desktop", "Storing files on a CD"], "question": "What is 'cloud storage'?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cloud storage saves data on remote servers accessed over the internet, allowing access from multiple devices and locations.", "correctAnswer": "Storing files on remote internet servers"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Monitor", "Keyboard", "Mouse", "Scanner"], "question": "Which of the following is an output device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Output devices display or produce information from the computer; a monitor shows visual output. A keyboard and mouse are input devices, and a scanner captures input.", "correctAnswer": "Monitor"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_10", "type": "true_false", "question": "A byte is made up of 8 bits.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A bit is the smallest unit of data (0 or 1). Eight bits grouped together form one byte, a standard unit for measuring data size.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_11", "type": "true_false", "question": "Malware is software designed to protect your computer from threats.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Malware stands for malicious software, such as viruses or spyware, designed to harm or exploit computers. Antivirus software is used to protect against malware.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "Wi-Fi allows devices to connect to a network without physical cables.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wi‑Fi uses radio waves to connect devices wirelessly to a network or the internet, eliminating the need for ethernet cables.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "Saving a file and copying a file are the same thing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Saving writes changes to the current file, while copying creates a duplicate file in the same or another location.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_14", "type": "true_false", "question": "HTTPS is more secure than HTTP because it encrypts data during transmission.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "HTTPS adds encryption (using SSL/TLS) to protect data between your browser and the website, making it more secure than HTTP.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_15", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In Microsoft Windows, the bar at the bottom of the screen that shows open applications is called the _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The taskbar shows open apps, the Start menu, pinned programs, and the system tray, helping users switch between tasks easily.", "correctAnswer": "taskbar"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_16", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In email, the field used to send a copy to recipients without others seeing them is _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "BCC stands for Blind Carbon Copy. Recipients listed in BCC are hidden from other recipients to protect privacy.", "correctAnswer": "BCC"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_17", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The keyboard shortcut Ctrl+C is used to _____ selected text or files.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Ctrl+C is a common shortcut for copying selected content to the clipboard. It pairs with Ctrl+V to paste.", "correctAnswer": "copy"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_18", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym ICT stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "ICT stands for Information and Communication Technology, which covers technologies for processing information and enabling communication (computers, networks, the internet, etc.).", "correctAnswer": "Information and Communication Technology"}, {"id": "q_1772702953951_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name an application software used for word processing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Microsoft Word is a word processing application used to create and edit documents. Other examples include Google Docs and LibreOffice Writer.", "correctAnswer": "Microsoft Word"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:29:13.963359	\N
