ebsites on the internet.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Browsers like Chrome, Firefox, or Edge retrieve and display web pages, making them the primary tools for viewing content on the internet.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703127534_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".exe", ".jpg", ".txt", ".mp3"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for a plain text file?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The .txt extension indicates a plain text file. .exe is an executable program, .jpg is an image file, and .mp3 is an audio file.", "correctAnswer": ".txt"}, {"id": "q_1772703127534_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A small network within a home, classroom, or office is called a _____.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "LAN stands for Local Area Network, which connects devices in a limited area such as a building or campus for sharing resources.", "correctAnswer": "LAN"}, {"id": "q_1772703127534_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the main purpose of antivirus software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Antivirus software detects, prevents, and removes malicious software (malware), helping keep systems safe from infection and data loss.", "correctAnswer": "To protect a computer from viruses and malware"}, {"id": "q_1772703127534_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Google Drive", "USB flash drive", "Internal hard disk", "CD-ROM"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of a cloud storage service?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cloud storage services, like Google Drive, store files on remote servers accessible via the internet. USB drives, hard disks, and CDs are local storage.", "correctAnswer": "Google Drive"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:32:07.54842	\N
b95743ce-20e2-40ad-998f-98ba5505570b	2d3b9109-7c7d-4042-99ed-b2734a682dd4	ict - Medium	ict	medium	[{"id": "q_1772703127746_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Star topology", "Bus topology", "Ring topology", "Mesh topology"], "question": "An office network is wired so that each workstation connects to a central switch. One workstation’s cable is damaged; only that workstation loses connectivity. Which topology best describes this network and explains the fault isolation?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In a star topology, each device connects to a central switch or hub with its own cable. If a single cable fails, only that device is affected, while the rest of the network remains operational. In a bus or ring topology, a single break can disrupt many or all devices.", "correctAnswer": "Star topology"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["192.168.1.128", "192.168.1.0", "192.168.1.64", "192.168.1.192"], "question": "You are assigned the IP address 192.168.1.130 with subnet mask 255.255.255.192 (/26). Which of the following is the correct network address for your subnet?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A /26 mask has block sizes of 64 addresses (0, 64, 128, 192). The address 192.168.1.130 falls in the 128–191 range, so the subnet network address is 192.168.1.128 and the broadcast is 192.168.1.191.", "correctAnswer": "192.168.1.128"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["First Normal Form (1NF)", "Second Normal Form (2NF)", "Third Normal Form (3NF)", "Boyce–Codd Normal Form (BCNF)"], "question": "A table has a composite primary key and contains attributes that depend only on part of that key, causing update anomalies. Which normal form specifically addresses this issue?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "2NF eliminates partial dependency by ensuring all non-key attributes depend on the whole of a composite primary key, not just part of it. 1NF deals with atomicity; 3NF and BCNF address transitive and more general dependency issues.", "correctAnswer": "Second Normal Form (2NF)"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Click the link and log in using a test account", "Reply to ask the sender to prove their identity", "Report the email via the company’s phishing reporting process and delete it", "Forward the email to colleagues to warn them"], "question": "You receive an email that appears to be from your IT department asking you to verify your login via a link. The sender address looks suspicious. What is the most appropriate action?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Phishing emails should be reported through official channels and not interacted with. Clicking links or replying increases risk, and forwarding spreads potential harm. Security teams can analyze the message safely.", "correctAnswer": "Report the email via the company’s phishing reporting process and delete it"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_4", "type": "true_false", "question": "Solid-state drives (SSDs) have no moving parts and are generally not affected by magnets in the same way as hard disk drives (HDDs).", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "SSDs use flash memory and have no magnetic platters, so magnets do not affect them like they can affect HDDs. However, SSDs can still fail for other reasons such as wear on flash cells or controller issues.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "Transport Layer Security (TLS) is considered a Network layer protocol in the OSI model.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "TLS operates above the Transport layer, often described as part of the Presentation layer (handling encryption) or sitting between Application and Transport. It is not a Network layer (Layer 3) protocol.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "On Windows, to view the sequence of routers that a packet traverses to a destination, you would use the ______ command.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The Windows command is tracert (trace route). On Linux/macOS, the equivalent is traceroute. This tool helps diagnose routing issues by showing each hop and its response time.", "correctAnswer": "tracert"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process of converting readable data into an unreadable form to protect it from unauthorized access is called ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Encryption transforms plaintext into ciphertext using an algorithm and key so that only authorized parties can decrypt and read the data. It is fundamental for securing data at rest and in transit.", "correctAnswer": "encryption"}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In the context of the computer boot process, explain one key functional difference between RAM and ROM.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "At power-on, the CPU executes firmware instructions stored in non-volatile ROM (or flash) to perform POST and bootstrap the OS. Once started, the OS and applications load into RAM because it is fast, temporary memory that supports active computation.", "correctAnswer": "ROM is non-volatile firmware that stores the boot/BIOS/UEFI code used to initialize hardware, while RAM is volatile working memory used to load and run the operating system and programs and is cleared when power is removed."}, {"id": "q_1772703127746_9", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A relational database table with millions of rows has slow SELECT queries that filter on a specific column. Without changing the query logic, what practical step can you take to improve performance and why will it help?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Indexes organize column values for fast lookup (e.g., B-trees or hashes). With an appropriate index, the optimizer can use index seeks to quickly locate qualifying rows, reducing I/O and query time significantly on large tables.", "correctAnswer": "Create an index on the column used in the WHERE clause (or a composite index matching the query) so the database can find matching rows via the index instead of scanning the entire table."}]	none	2026-03-05 09:32:07.760877	\N
ef23f24a-c2af-4bc9-9dfc-9ff6885e2e13	d7b5f9b3-4892-4549-9b8b-ea87b495889d	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1772703435126_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["TCP", "UDP", "SMTP", "ICMP"], "question": "A video conferencing app experiences occasional packet loss. To minimize delay and maintain smooth real-time communication, which transport protocol is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "UDP is preferred for real-time media because it avoids retransmission and head-of-line blocking, keeping latency low even when some packets are lost. TCP guarantees delivery by retransmitting lost packets, which introduces delay and jitter—undesirable in live audio/video. SMTP and ICMP are not transport protocols for application data delivery.", "correctAnswer": "UDP"}, {"id": "q_1772703435126_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Enabling NAT on a router allows multiple devices on a private network to share a single public IP address.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Network Address Translation maps many private IP addresses to a single public IP using port/address translation. This conserves public IPv4 space and permits outbound connectivity for many hosts behind one public IP, though it can complicate unsolicited inbound connections without port forwarding.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703435126_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "A backup strategy that captures only the files changed since the last backup of any type is called an ______ backup.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Incremental backups include only changes since the most recent backup (full or incremental), reducing time and storage. In contrast, differential backups include changes since the last full backup, growing larger over time until the next full backup.", "correctAnswer": "incremental"}, {"id": "q_1772703435126_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "In cloud computing, give one scenario where PaaS is preferable to IaaS and identify a specific advantage it provides.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "PaaS abstracts infrastructure, providing managed runtimes, databases, and deployment pipelines. It suits developers who need to focus on code and features rather than OS, middleware, and scaling. IaaS offers raw VMs and full control but requires more ops effort for configuration, patching, and scaling.", "correctAnswer": "Building and deploying a web application using a managed runtime (e.g., a Node.js or Python app) on PaaS to avoid managing servers/OS; advantage: the platform handles scaling, patching, and runtime dependencies, speeding development and deployment."}, {"id": "q_1772703435126_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["302 Found", "301 Moved Permanently", "404 Not Found", "500 Internal Server Error"], "question": "A company reorganized its website and wants old URLs to permanently redirect to new ones so search engines update their indexes. Which HTTP status code should be returned?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A 301 status indicates a permanent redirect, signaling clients and search engines to update bookmarks and indexes to the new URL. A 302 is temporary, 404 indicates the resource is missing without a redirect, and 500 signals a server error.", "correctAnswer": "301 Moved Permanently"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:37:15.139506	\N
8e38558a-fae3-4492-8828-3146ed060bcc	f88fcb05-8321-433d-8068-5bde317fad1c	ict - Medium	ict	medium	[{"id": "q_1772703448858_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Router", "Unmanaged switch", "Hub", "Repeater"], "question": "You need to reduce broadcast traffic between two departments by placing them on different IP subnets while still allowing controlled communication between them. Which device should you deploy between the two network segments?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Routers separate broadcast domains and forward traffic between different IP subnets using routing. Unmanaged switches and hubs extend the same broadcast domain, and repeaters simply regenerate signals without segmentation. A router (or a Layer 3 switch configured for inter-VLAN routing) is the correct device for this task.", "correctAnswer": "Router"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["IaaS", "PaaS", "SaaS", "Colocation"], "question": "Your team wants to deploy a custom web application without managing operating system patches or servers, while retaining control over the application code and runtime dependencies. Which cloud service model is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides a managed platform (runtime, middleware, and OS) so developers can focus on code and dependencies. IaaS still requires managing VMs and OS patches, SaaS provides a complete application with little to no code control, and colocation is a physical hosting arrangement rather than a cloud service model.", "correctAnswer": "PaaS"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_2", "type": "true_false", "question": "HTTPS (TLS) both encrypts data in transit and authenticates the server’s identity to the client.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "HTTPS uses TLS to encrypt the connection, protecting confidentiality and integrity of data in transit. It also authenticates the server via X.509 certificates issued by trusted certificate authorities, helping ensure the client is talking to the intended server. Note that if TLS is terminated by a proxy/CDN, the trust and encryption apply to that endpoint.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_3", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a spreadsheet, to ensure a formula always refers to cell B2 when copied across rows and columns, you would use an ______ cell reference like $B$2.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "An absolute reference uses dollar signs to lock both the column and row (e.g., $B$2), so when the formula is copied, it still points to the same cell. Relative references adjust when copied, and mixed references lock only the row or the column.", "correctAnswer": "absolute"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "You have the network 192.168.1.0/24 and need at least 6 separate subnets of equal size. What subnet mask (CIDR and dotted decimal) should you use, and how many usable host addresses per subnet will it provide?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Splitting a /24 into /27s creates 8 subnets (2^(27−24) = 8), which satisfies the requirement for at least 6. A /27 has 32 addresses, with 30 usable hosts per subnet (32 total minus network and broadcast). The dotted decimal mask is 255.255.255.224.", "correctAnswer": "/27 (255.255.255.224), 30 usable hosts per subnet"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["SHA-256", "AES-256", "RSA-2048", "GZIP"], "question": "You downloaded a large ISO file and want to verify it hasn’t been altered in transit by comparing a digest posted by the vendor. Which algorithm should you use?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "SHA-256 is a cryptographic hash function used to compute a fixed-length digest that can be compared to a trusted value for integrity verification. AES-256 is an encryption algorithm, RSA-2048 is an asymmetric encryption/signature algorithm, and GZIP is a compression format; none of these are used for simple digest checking in this context.", "correctAnswer": "SHA-256"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "In a relational database, a foreign key in a child table may contain duplicate values if multiple rows relate to the same parent row, provided referential integrity is enforced.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Foreign keys reference primary keys in another table. It is common for many child rows to reference the same parent row, resulting in duplicate foreign key values. Referential integrity ensures that each foreign key value exists in the parent table or is NULL if allowed.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_7", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The internet protocol that translates human‑readable domain names into numeric IP addresses is ______.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "DNS (Domain Name System) resolves domain names like example.com to IP addresses such as 93.184.216.34, allowing users to use readable names instead of numeric addresses.", "correctAnswer": "DNS"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_8", "type": "short_answer", "question": "An employee receives an email from \\"HR Updates\\" urging them to \\"confirm your payroll details in the next 30 minutes\\" via a shortened link. The sender’s domain is external and the link leads to a look‑alike login page. What is this attack, and what is the safest immediate action the employee should take?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "This is a phishing attempt that uses urgency and a fake login page to steal credentials. The safest action is to avoid interacting with the link or attachments, report the message to the security team or via the company’s phishing reporting tool, and then delete it.", "correctAnswer": "Phishing; do not click the link—report it to IT/security and delete the email"}, {"id": "q_1772703448858_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["200 OK", "201 Created", "204 No Content", "302 Found"], "question": "A REST API receives a POST request that successfully creates a new resource and includes a Location header with the URL of the new resource. Which HTTP status code is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "HTTP 201 Created indicates that the request succeeded and a new resource was created, typically accompanied by a Location header pointing to the resource’s URL. 200 OK is generic success, 204 No Content indicates success with no body but not necessarily creation, and 302 Found is a redirection.", "correctAnswer": "201 Created"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:37:28.871602	\N
4fd823fe-6f1d-432a-b19f-e594c01fac9e	a457ad32-0855-4806-9db5-6beb17121662	world war 2 - Easy	world war 2	easy	[{"id": "q_1772703533371_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Germany's invasion of Poland (1939)", "The bombing of Pearl Harbor (1941)", "The Battle of Stalingrad (1942–1943)", "The D-Day landings in Normandy (1944)"], "question": "Which event is commonly considered the start of World War II in Europe?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War II in Europe began when Germany invaded Poland on September 1, 1939. Britain and France declared war on Germany shortly afterward. Pearl Harbor led to U.S. entry in 1941, while Stalingrad and D-Day were later turning points.", "correctAnswer": "Germany's invasion of Poland (1939)"}, {"id": "q_1772703533371_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Axis Powers primarily included Germany, Italy, and Japan.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Germany, Italy, and Japan formed the core of the Axis Powers, coordinating military efforts and treaties against the Allied nations during World War II.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703533371_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The Allied invasion of Normandy on June 6, 1944, is commonly known as ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "D-Day refers to the Allied landings in Normandy, France, on June 6, 1944—part of Operation Overlord—which opened a Western front against Nazi Germany.", "correctAnswer": "D-Day"}, {"id": "q_1772703533371_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name the British Prime Minister who led the United Kingdom through most of World War II.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in May 1940 and led Britain through much of the war, rallying the nation during its darkest hours with his speeches and leadership.", "correctAnswer": "Winston Churchill"}, {"id": "q_1772703533371_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Hiroshima", "Nagasaki", "Tokyo", "Kyoto"], "question": "Which Japanese city was the site of the first atomic bombing on August 6, 1945?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The United States dropped the first atomic bomb on Hiroshima on August 6, 1945. A second bomb was dropped on Nagasaki on August 9, contributing to Japan’s surrender.", "correctAnswer": "Hiroshima"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:38:53.462291	\N
d5254eae-a13b-4480-8c07-3a09c79465c4	a457ad32-0855-4806-9db5-6beb17121662	ICT - Easy	ICT	easy	[{"id": "q_1772703571322_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An input device sends data to the computer for processing. A keyboard is used to enter text and commands. Monitors and speakers are output devices, and a printer is also an output device.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1772703571322_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Internet and the World Wide Web are the same thing.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Internet is the global network of connected computers and infrastructure. The World Wide Web is a collection of web pages and resources that run on top of the Internet using HTTP. They are related but not the same.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772703571322_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The software that manages computer hardware and provides common services for applications is called the ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "An operating system (OS) controls hardware resources (like CPU, memory, and storage) and provides services that allow applications to run. Examples include Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, and iOS.", "correctAnswer": "operating system"}, {"id": "q_1772703571322_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It performs most of the processing inside a computer, executing instructions and managing tasks.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1772703571322_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".zip", ".docx", ".jpg", ".pptx"], "question": "Which file extension is typically used for a compressed archive?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The .zip extension indicates a compressed archive that can contain one or more files. .docx is a Word document, .jpg is an image file, and .pptx is a PowerPoint presentation.", "correctAnswer": ".zip"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:39:31.421602	\N
54a5e9d6-c950-4840-96ed-fcbc2dc81f5d	a457ad32-0855-4806-9db5-6beb17121662	ww1 - Easy	ww1	easy	[{"id": "q_1772703602191_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["1912", "1914", "1916", "1918"], "question": "In which year did World War I begin?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War I began in 1914 after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in June, which triggered a chain of alliances and declarations of war through July and August of that year.", "correctAnswer": "1914"}, {"id": "q_1772703602191_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "The Treaty of Versailles formally ended World War I in 1919.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Treaty of Versailles was signed on June 28, 1919, officially ending the state of war between Germany and the Allied Powers. Other treaties addressed Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703602191_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The Western Front was characterized by ______ warfare, with soldiers living and fighting from long dug-out lines.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Trench warfare dominated the Western Front, creating a stalemate with elaborate trench systems, barbed wire, and a deadly no-man’s-land between opposing lines.", "correctAnswer": "trench"}, {"id": "q_1772703602191_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Ottoman Empire", "United States", "British Empire", "Japan"], "question": "Which of the following was a member of the Central Powers during World War I?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Central Powers were primarily Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria. The United States, British Empire, and Japan fought with the Allies.", "correctAnswer": "Ottoman Empire"}, {"id": "q_1772703602191_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What was the name of the British passenger ship sunk by a German U-boat in 1915, influencing U.S. public opinion?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The RMS Lusitania was torpedoed by a German U-boat in May 1915, killing nearly 1,200 people, including Americans. The incident intensified anti-German sentiment in the United States.", "correctAnswer": "Lusitania"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:40:02.288615	\N
f2c6dd1f-1994-4c60-bd42-acaa9a61b0a1	f88fcb05-8321-433d-8068-5bde317fad1c	WORLD WAR 2 - Easy	WORLD WAR 2	easy	[{"id": "q_1772703724130_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Germany's invasion of Poland (September 1939)", "Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor (December 1941)", "The D-Day landings in Normandy (June 1944)", "The Battle of Britain (1940)"], "question": "Which event is widely considered the start of World War II in Europe?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "World War II in Europe is generally considered to have begun when Germany invaded Poland on September 1, 1939. Britain and France declared war on Germany shortly afterward.", "correctAnswer": "Germany's invasion of Poland (September 1939)"}, {"id": "q_1772703724130_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "True or False: The Axis Powers during World War II were led by Germany, Italy, and Japan.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Germany, Italy, and Japan formed the core of the Axis alliance, coordinating military and political strategies during the war.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772703724130_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The Allied invasion of Nazi-occupied France on June 6, 1944, is known as ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "June 6, 1944, is commonly called D-Day, the start of Operation Overlord, when Allied forces landed on the beaches of Normandy to liberate Western Europe.", "correctAnswer": "D-Day"}, {"id": "q_1772703724130_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Who was the U.S. President when the United States entered World War II after the attack on Pearl Harbor?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "After the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor on December 7, 1941, President Franklin D. Roosevelt asked Congress to declare war, bringing the United States into World War II.", "correctAnswer": "Franklin D. Roosevelt"}, {"id": "q_1772703724130_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Franklin D. Roosevelt", "Harry S. Truman", "Dwight D. Eisenhower", "Woodrow Wilson"], "question": "Which U.S. president authorized the use of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Harry S. Truman became president in April 1945 after Franklin D. Roosevelt’s death and authorized the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945.", "correctAnswer": "Harry S. Truman"}]	none	2026-03-05 09:42:04.215389	\N
64404ee1-204f-400c-a0fd-0e58af32de2c	2142b8ed-3a28-4a77-bfdf-2b66aafcd926	hentai - Medium	hentai	medium	[{"id": "q_1772703910231_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Perverted/abnormal (a colloquial descriptor), not a formal genre label", "Explicit animated content as an official industry category", "Lightly suggestive material (fanservice) without explicit content", "Romance manga aimed at teens"], "question": "In contemporary Japanese usage, what does the word “hentai” most commonly denote?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "In Japanese, “hentai” is a colloquial word meaning perverted/abnormal and is not typically used by publishers as a formal category label. In English-language fandoms, however, the term is often used to refer broadly to explicit adult anime or manga. Japanese retailers and publishers more commonly use labels like “ero,” “R18,” or “成人向け” (for adults).", "correctAnswer": "Perverted/abnormal (a colloquial descriptor), not a formal genre label"}, {"id": "q_1772703910231_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "In Japan, publishers and retailers typically label adult-oriented comics and animation as “hentai” on covers and store shelves.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "False. Japanese industry practice generally uses terms such as “ero,” “18+,” “R18,” and “成人向け” (for adults) rather than “hentai.” The English-language use of “hentai” as a genre label is a fandom and localization convention, not a standard Japanese marketing category.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772703910231_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The legal basis in Japan that has historically prompted mosaic pixelation in adult visual media is Article ___ of the Penal Code.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Article 175 of the Japanese Penal Code restricts the distribution of “obscene” materials. Industry self-regulation to comply with this law has typically involved pixelation or mosaics over genitalia in adult videos and some animated content. While enforcement and standards have evolved, Article 175 remains the foundational statute shaping these practices.", "correctAnswer": "175"}, {"id": "q_1772703910231_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "A Western licensing company plans to localize an adult animated title. Identify two practical factors they must evaluate to comply with local laws and platform policies, and explain how those factors could change editing or distribution decisions.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Different jurisdictions have varying thresholds for what is legally permissible in adult animation, and platforms impose their own rules beyond the law. Evaluating both sets of constraints helps determine whether content must be altered, how it is rated and marketed, and where it can be offered (e.g., adult-only storefronts versus mainstream streaming).", "correctAnswer": "Two key factors are (1) legal standards in the target market (e.g., obscenity tests, bans on certain depictions, rules regarding youthful-looking characters) and (2) platform/publisher policies (content guidelines, age-gating, and advertising restrictions). If the work includes elements that violate local laws or platform policies, the distributor might edit or remove scenes, add stronger age verification and content warnings, limit release to adult-only channels, or forgo certain platforms altogether to remain compliant."}, {"id": "q_1772703910231_4", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain why the OVA (Original Video Animation) market facilitated the growth of explicit adult animation in Japan during the 1980s–1990s, and analyze one implication this had for production or audiences.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The OVA market’s direct retail model circumvented broadcaster oversight, enabling content that would not pass TV standards. This supported niche adult genres and allowed producers to align content with a paying, segmented audience. However, reliance on direct sales shaped production economics—encouraging shorter formats and careful budgeting—while still expanding thematic diversity.", "correctAnswer": "OVAs were sold direct-to-video and thus were not subject to broadcast TV standards, making it easier to release explicit or niche content. As a result, producers could target smaller, dedicated audiences without fitting broadcast schedules or content restrictions. One implication was more experimentation with short, self-contained releases and niche storytelling, but with tighter budgets and runtimes that sometimes constrained animation quality compared to theatrical projects."}]	none	2026-03-05 09:45:10.329605	\N
af69cf67-5922-43d2-bf14-1386c3607ec0	6d4fec84-a568-4114-b932-66fee4d15d30	GLOBAL PERSPECTIVES - Easy	GLOBAL PERSPECTIVES	easy	[{"id": "q_1772733777956_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["The process of countries becoming more connected through trade, communication, and culture", "The isolation of countries from international affairs", "Only the spread of fast food chains around the world", "The legal union of two countries into one state"], "question": "What is globalization?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Globalization refers to the increasing interconnectedness of people and places through trade, technology, travel, and cultural exchange. It is broader than any single product or merger and the opposite of isolation.", "correctAnswer": "The process of countries becoming more connected through trade, communication, and culture"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Millennium Development Targets", "Sustainable Development Goals", "Paris Climate Goals", "Universal Human Rights Charter"], "question": "Which term refers to the UN’s 17 goals adopted in 2015 to end poverty, protect the planet, and promote prosperity?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are 17 global targets set by the United Nations in 2015, covering areas such as poverty, health, education, climate action, and inequality.", "correctAnswer": "Sustainable Development Goals"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A person who owns stock in a company", "Anyone with an interest in or affected by the issue", "Only government officials involved in policy", "Journalists who report on the issue"], "question": "Which best describes a stakeholder in a global issue?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "In global perspectives, a stakeholder is any individual or group that is affected by or has an interest in an issue, including communities, businesses, governments, and NGOs—not just shareholders or officials.", "correctAnswer": "Anyone with an interest in or affected by the issue"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_3", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A textbook summary about protests", "An eyewitness interview recorded the same day", "An opinion column analyzing the protest weeks later", "A Wikipedia article about civil movements"], "question": "Which is a primary source about a recent protest?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Primary sources provide direct, first-hand evidence from the time of the event. An interview with an eyewitness recorded the same day is primary; textbooks, opinion columns, and encyclopedias are secondary or tertiary.", "correctAnswer": "An eyewitness interview recorded the same day"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A city launching a community recycling program", "A national law regulating vehicle emissions", "A regional trade agreement between continents", "A global treaty on ozone-depleting substances"], "question": "Which is a local response to a global environmental issue?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Actions are often taken at different scales. A city’s recycling program is local, while national laws, regional pacts, and global treaties operate at broader levels.", "correctAnswer": "A city launching a community recycling program"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_5", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Soil erosion", "Increased demand for timber", "Expanding agriculture into forests", "Population growth in rural areas"], "question": "Which is mainly a consequence of deforestation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Deforestation often leads to soil erosion because tree roots that hold soil in place are removed. Increased timber demand, agricultural expansion, and demographic changes are typically causes or pressures.", "correctAnswer": "Soil erosion"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_6", "type": "true_false", "question": "Bias is a tendency to favor one perspective that can affect how information is presented.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Bias involves leaning toward a particular viewpoint, which can shape the selection and framing of information. Recognizing bias helps assess the reliability of sources.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_7", "type": "true_false", "question": "All global issues can be solved by one country acting alone.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Most global issues—such as climate change, pandemics, and migration—cross borders and require international cooperation, not action by a single nation alone.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Cultural diversity refers to the variety of cultural expressions and practices within a society.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Cultural diversity encompasses the range of languages, traditions, beliefs, and arts present in a community or nation, contributing to social richness and innovation.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "Evidence and opinion mean the same thing in research.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Evidence consists of facts, data, or direct observations that support claims, while opinions are personal judgments or beliefs that may not be backed by data.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The 2015 international climate accord to limit global warming is the ______ Agreement.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015 under the UNFCCC to limit global temperature rise, with countries setting and updating their own emissions targets.", "correctAnswer": "Paris"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_11", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The movement of people from one country to another to live or work is called ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Migration refers to people moving across borders. Entering a country is immigration; leaving a country is emigration. Both are forms of migration.", "correctAnswer": "migration"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_12", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations is known as ______ development.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sustainable development balances environmental protection, social well-being, and economic growth so future generations can meet their needs too.", "correctAnswer": "sustainable"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_13", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one renewable source of energy used to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Solar is renewable because it uses energy from the sun. Other valid examples include wind, hydro (water), geothermal, and biomass when sustainably managed.", "correctAnswer": "Solar energy"}, {"id": "q_1772733777956_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What does the acronym SDG stand for in global development?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "SDG stands for Sustainable Development Goals—the UN’s 17 goals covering areas like poverty, health, education, climate action, and equality.", "correctAnswer": "Sustainable Development Goals"}]	none	2026-03-05 18:02:58.461355	\N
0940900f-fe9b-4f85-9a83-6b285d9d8d53	c46c2c13-34b3-4129-9003-a7648bc1d93b	I.C.T - Easy	I.C.T	easy	[{"id": "q_1772877344877_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Printer", "Speaker"], "question": "Which of the following is an input device?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Input devices send data to the computer. A keyboard is used to input text and commands, while monitors, printers, and speakers are output devices.", "correctAnswer": "Keyboard"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_1", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["RAM", "Hard drive", "DVD", "USB flash drive"], "question": "Which type of memory stores data temporarily while the computer is running?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "RAM (Random Access Memory) holds data and programs that the CPU is currently using. It is volatile, meaning it loses data when power is off.", "correctAnswer": "RAM"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_2", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Personal Unit", "Central Program Utility", "Control Processing Utility"], "question": "What does CPU stand for?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The CPU (Central Processing Unit) is the 'brain' of the computer that performs calculations and runs instructions.", "correctAnswer": "Central Processing Unit"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_3", "type": "true_false", "question": "An operating system is system software that manages hardware and software resources.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Operating systems like Windows, macOS, and Linux manage files, memory, devices, and provide a user interface for running applications.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_4", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The __________ is the main circuit board of a computer that holds the CPU, memory, and other components.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "The motherboard connects all the components of a computer and allows them to communicate with each other.", "correctAnswer": "motherboard"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_5", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one example of an output device.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Output devices display or produce information from the computer. Examples include a monitor, printer, and speakers.", "correctAnswer": "Monitor"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_6", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": [".jpg", ".docx", ".xlsx", ".pptx"], "question": "Which file extension is commonly used for an image?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": ".jpg (or .jpeg) is a common image format. .docx is for Word documents, .xlsx for Excel spreadsheets, and .pptx for PowerPoint presentations.", "correctAnswer": ".jpg"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_7", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["LAN", "WAN", "VPN", "DNS"], "question": "Which type of network typically covers a small area like a home, office, or school?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A LAN (Local Area Network) connects devices in a limited area such as a home or office. WANs cover larger areas; VPN and DNS are not network size categories.", "correctAnswer": "LAN"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_8", "type": "true_false", "question": "Wi‑Fi uses cables to connect devices to the internet.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Wi‑Fi is a wireless networking technology that uses radio waves, not cables, to connect devices.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_9", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Google Chrome", "Microsoft Windows", "Google Drive", "Bluetooth"], "question": "Which of the following is a web browser?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Google Chrome is a web browser. Microsoft Windows is an operating system, Google Drive is cloud storage, and Bluetooth is a wireless protocol.", "correctAnswer": "Google Chrome"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The address you type into a web browser to visit a website is called a _____ .", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "URL stands for Uniform Resource Locator and specifies the location of a resource on the internet.", "correctAnswer": "URL"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_11", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Use a mix of letters, numbers, and symbols", "Use your first name", "Use '123456' for easy recall", "Share it with friends in case you forget"], "question": "Which is a best practice for creating a strong password?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Strong passwords are long and include a mix of characters that are hard to guess. Personal information and common sequences are weak choices.", "correctAnswer": "Use a mix of letters, numbers, and symbols"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_12", "type": "true_false", "question": "Phishing is a technique used to trick people into giving away personal information such as passwords.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Phishing attempts often come via email or messages that look legitimate but aim to steal sensitive information.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_13", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Cell", "Sheet", "Chart", "Range"], "question": "In a spreadsheet, the intersection of a row and a column is called a:", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Each box in a spreadsheet is a cell, identified by its column letter and row number (e.g., A1).", "correctAnswer": "Cell"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_14", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is a binary digit called?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A binary digit, or bit, is the smallest unit of data in computing and can have a value of 0 or 1.", "correctAnswer": "bit"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_15", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Router", "Scanner", "Projector", "Keyboard"], "question": "Which device connects a local network to the internet?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "A router directs network traffic and connects local networks to external networks like the internet.", "correctAnswer": "Router"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_16", "type": "true_false", "question": "Software refers to the physical parts of a computer, such as the monitor and keyboard.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Software is the set of programs and instructions. The physical components are called hardware.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_17", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Saving a copy of your important files to the cloud or another device is called making a _____ .", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Backups protect your data by keeping extra copies in case of loss, damage, or malware.", "correctAnswer": "backup"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_18", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Microsoft Word", "BIOS", "Device driver", "Firmware"], "question": "Which of the following is an example of application software?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Microsoft Word is an application used by end users. BIOS, drivers, and firmware are system-level software.", "correctAnswer": "Microsoft Word"}, {"id": "q_1772877344877_19", "type": "true_false", "question": "Email attachments allow you to send files along with your message.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Attachments are files (like documents or images) added to an email so recipients can download and view them.", "correctAnswer": "True"}]	none	2026-03-07 09:55:44.983236	\N
064daaba-fdfa-49a8-acbd-5baa5c7b6476	fed3cd47-f85d-4803-bd82-53f87d73c2dc	ICT - Medium	ICT	medium	[{"id": "q_1773040846574_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Deploy a Content Delivery Network (CDN) to cache static assets at edge locations", "Increase the database server’s RAM", "Switch the site to IPv6 only", "Run daily full backups to a cloud storage bucket"], "question": "A global e-commerce site experiences high page load times for users far from its data center. Which approach most directly reduces latency for those users without changing application code?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A CDN places frequently requested content closer to users on geographically distributed edge servers, reducing round-trip time and improving load speed without code changes. Adding RAM may improve backend performance but doesn’t address geographic latency. IPv6 adoption alone does not materially reduce latency. Backups are about data protection, not delivery speed.", "correctAnswer": "Deploy a Content Delivery Network (CDN) to cache static assets at edge locations"}, {"id": "q_1773040846574_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Because RAID 1 mirrors data across disks, it can be used as a substitute for regular backups.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "RAID 1 provides redundancy for hardware failure and improves availability, but it mirrors deletions, corruption, ransomware encryption, and configuration errors instantly across disks. Backups create independent, point-in-time copies that can be restored after logical mistakes, malware, or site disasters.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1773040846574_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a relational database, a __________ key in one table that references a primary key in another helps enforce referential integrity and prevents orphan records.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A foreign key links a column (or set of columns) in one table to a primary key in another. Enforcing foreign key constraints ensures that references are valid, preventing orphaned rows and maintaining consistent relationships between tables.", "correctAnswer": "foreign"}, {"id": "q_1773040846574_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "An employee receives an email from it-support@company-security.com urging an immediate password reset via a link. Identify two red flags that suggest phishing and state the immediate action they should take.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Phishing often uses domain look-alikes and urgency to provoke quick clicks. A legitimate IT notice will come from the official domain and direct users to known portals. The safest response is to avoid the link, report the message, and independently verify via trusted channels.", "correctAnswer": "Red flags: the sender domain doesn’t match the company’s official domain and the urgent, pressure-inducing language with an unsolicited reset link. Action: do not click the link; report the email to IT/security and verify through official channels (e.g., the corporate portal)."}, {"id": "q_1773040846574_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Use spot/preemptible instances with autoscaling and graceful shutdown handling", "Purchase 3-year reserved instances for fixed capacity", "Migrate to dedicated hosts for exclusive hardware", "Upgrade to the largest instance type to avoid scale-out"], "question": "A startup runs stateless web workers with spiky, unpredictable traffic and wants to minimize cloud compute cost without long-term commitments. Which option best fits?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Spot/preemptible instances offer steep discounts in exchange for potential interruptions. Stateless workers can handle interruptions with autoscaling and graceful termination. Reserved instances reduce cost only with long-term commitments and predictable usage; dedicated hosts are pricier and unnecessary here; scaling up to the largest instance is cost-inefficient and not resilient to spikes.", "correctAnswer": "Use spot/preemptible instances with autoscaling and graceful shutdown handling"}]	none	2026-03-09 07:20:46.683452	\N
