rimarily involved in regulating blood sugar levels?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Insulin, produced by the pancreas, is key in lowering blood glucose levels by facilitating the uptake of glucose into cells, playing a crucial role in glucose metabolism.", "correctAnswer": "Insulin"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The liver is the only organ capable of regenerating itself.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "While the liver has remarkable regenerative capabilities, other organs such as the skin and intestines also possess regenerative properties, although to varying extents.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The __________ is the functional unit of the kidney that filters blood and forms urine.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Nephrons are the basic structural and functional units of the kidney, responsible for filtering blood, reabsorbing essential substances, and excreting waste as urine.", "correctAnswer": "nephron"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_11", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the primary role of the immune system?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "The immune system plays a critical role in protecting the body from infections and diseases, adapting its response to different pathogens to ensure effective defense.", "correctAnswer": "The immune system defends the body against pathogens, such as bacteria and viruses, through a complex network of cells, tissues, and organs that work together to recognize and eliminate foreign invaders."}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Skeletal muscle", "Cardiac muscle", "Smooth muscle", "Striated muscle"], "question": "Which type of muscle tissue is involuntary and found in the walls of internal organs?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Smooth muscle is involuntary and responsible for movements within internal organs such as the digestive tract and blood vessels, distinguishing it from voluntary skeletal muscle.", "correctAnswer": "Smooth muscle"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "DNA replication occurs during the S phase of the cell cycle.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "During the S phase (synthesis phase) of the cell cycle, DNA is replicated to ensure that each new cell has an identical copy of the genetic material.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The __________ is responsible for producing hormones that regulate metabolism, growth, and development.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "The thyroid gland produces hormones such as thyroxine, which play critical roles in regulating metabolism, growth, and development, impacting overall health.", "correctAnswer": "thyroid gland"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_15", "type": "short_answer", "question": "How do vaccines work to protect the body from diseases?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Vaccines introduce a harmless component of a pathogen, prompting the immune system to develop a defense mechanism without causing the disease, ensuring protection against future infections.", "correctAnswer": "Vaccines stimulate the immune system to recognize and remember specific pathogens, enabling a quicker and stronger response upon future exposure, thus providing immunity against diseases."}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_16", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Axon", "Dendrite", "Soma", "Synapse"], "question": "Which part of the neuron is responsible for receiving signals from other neurons?", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Dendrites are the branched extensions of neurons that receive signals from other neurons and conduct these impulses towards the cell body.", "correctAnswer": "Dendrite"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_17", "type": "true_false", "question": "The skin is the largest organ in the human body.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "The skin is indeed the largest organ in the human body, serving as a protective barrier and playing key roles in thermoregulation and sensory perception.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_18", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The __________ system is responsible for the exchange of gases between the body and the environment.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "The respiratory system facilitates gas exchange, allowing oxygen to enter the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to be expelled, which is vital for cellular respiration.", "correctAnswer": "respiratory"}, {"id": "q_1773380421257_19", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Discuss the importance of homeostasis in human physiology.", "difficulty": "expert", "explanation": "Maintaining homeostasis involves regulating variables such as temperature, pH, and electrolyte balance, crucial for proper physiological function and overall well-being.", "correctAnswer": "Homeostasis is crucial as it maintains a stable internal environment, allowing the body to function optimally despite external changes, ensuring the survival and health of the organism."}]	35a2c104-9f25-443e-8823-e44692b88212	2026-03-13 05:40:21.269112	\N
