ble future, unlike fossil fuels which can deplete.", "correctAnswer": "Solar Energy"}]	none	2026-03-18 12:55:09.639797	\N
a1866e39-2490-4db4-88e6-2993ce636b1a	192d9737-8017-48ec-abf3-ff823bad0d7f	sdiematation - Easy	sdiematation	easy	[{"id": "q_1773838681913_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["To separate solids from liquids", "To mix different liquids", "To increase temperature", "To dissolve solids"], "question": "What is the primary purpose of sedimentation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sedimentation is a process used to separate suspended solids from liquids, allowing for clear liquid to be obtained after the solids settle at the bottom.", "correctAnswer": "To separate solids from liquids"}, {"id": "q_1773838681913_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Sedimentation can occur naturally in bodies of water such as rivers and lakes.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sedimentation occurs naturally as particles settle due to gravity in water bodies, leading to the formation of sediment layers over time.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773838681913_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In sedimentation, the process is usually accelerated by the force of ______.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Gravity plays a crucial role in sedimentation as it pulls the denser solid particles down, allowing them to settle out of the liquid.", "correctAnswer": "gravity"}, {"id": "q_1773838681913_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Name one common application of sedimentation in water treatment.", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "Sedimentation is commonly used in water treatment processes to remove suspended solids and impurities, allowing for cleaner water to be produced.", "correctAnswer": "Clarification or removal of impurities"}, {"id": "q_1773838681913_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Particle size", "Temperature of the liquid", "Viscosity of the liquid", "All of the above"], "question": "Which of the following factors can affect the rate of sedimentation?", "difficulty": "easy", "explanation": "All these factors influence the rate at which particles settle; larger particles settle faster, while higher temperatures and lower viscosity can enhance sedimentation.", "correctAnswer": "All of the above"}]	none	2026-03-18 12:58:01.924938	\N
a5998222-7219-4b90-964a-d99d6a819048	e43866cd-4d5d-400a-81ee-69fff884ea8a	Rock cycle - Medium	Rock cycle	medium	[{"id": "q_1773840400754_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Erosion", "Melting and solidification", "Compaction and cementation", "Weathering"], "question": "Which of the following processes is primarily responsible for the formation of igneous rocks?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava. This process occurs when molten rock material cools down, either beneath the Earth's surface (intrusive igneous rocks) or on the surface after a volcanic eruption (extrusive igneous rocks).", "correctAnswer": "Melting and solidification"}, {"id": "q_1773840400754_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Metamorphic rocks can be formed from both igneous and sedimentary rocks.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "True. Metamorphic rocks can indeed form from both igneous and sedimentary rocks through the process of metamorphism, which involves heat, pressure, and chemically active fluids altering the original rock.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773840400754_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process by which rocks are broken down into smaller pieces is known as __________.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Weathering is the process that breaks down rocks into smaller particles through physical, chemical, or biological means. This is a key step in the rock cycle that leads to the formation of sedimentary rocks.", "correctAnswer": "weathering"}, {"id": "q_1773840400754_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "Explain how sedimentary rocks are formed in the rock cycle.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Sedimentary rocks are created when particles from other rocks or organic materials accumulate in layers. Over time, these layers are compacted under pressure and cemented together, forming solid rock. This process highlights the cyclical nature of rock transformation in the rock cycle.", "correctAnswer": "Sedimentary rocks are formed through the processes of sedimentation, compaction, and cementation of mineral and organic particles over time."}, {"id": "q_1773840400754_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Limestone", "Sandstone", "Basalt", "Shale"], "question": "Which of the following is NOT a type of sedimentary rock?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Basalt is an igneous rock that forms from the rapid cooling of lava. In contrast, limestone, sandstone, and shale are all types of sedimentary rocks formed from the accumulation of sediments or organic materials.", "correctAnswer": "Basalt"}]	none	2026-03-18 13:26:40.764969	\N
ba89f216-8e1c-4188-8e31-3521cadb84ec	af671e0c-534f-4cae-ace2-ed45be503aac	Chemistry - Medium	Chemistry	medium	[{"id": "q_1773842422576_0", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Helium", "Hydrogen"], "question": "Which of the following elements is a noble gas?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Helium is a noble gas, which means it is chemically inert and does not readily form compounds under standard conditions. This property is due to its full valence shell of electrons.", "correctAnswer": "Helium"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_1", "type": "true_false", "question": "Acids have a pH greater than 7.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Acids have a pH less than 7. The pH scale ranges from 0 to 14, with 7 being neutral, below 7 indicating acidity, and above 7 indicating alkalinity.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_2", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The chemical formula for table salt is _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Table salt is composed of sodium (Na) and chlorine (Cl). When these elements combine, they form sodium chloride (NaCl), which is commonly known as table salt.", "correctAnswer": "NaCl"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_3", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the primary type of bond that holds water molecules together?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Water molecules are held together primarily by hydrogen bonds, which are weak attractions between the positively charged hydrogen atoms of one water molecule and the negatively charged oxygen atom of another. This property contributes to water's unique characteristics.", "correctAnswer": "Hydrogen bonds"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Good conductors of heat", "Malleable", "Brittle", "Luster"], "question": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of metals?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Metals are typically not brittle; they are malleable and ductile, meaning they can be shaped without breaking. Brittleness is more characteristic of nonmetals.", "correctAnswer": "Brittle"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_5", "type": "true_false", "question": "All isotopes of an element have the same number of protons.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Isotopes of an element have the same number of protons, which defines the element, but they differ in the number of neutrons.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_6", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The process of a liquid changing into a gas is called _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Evaporation is the process where liquid molecules gain enough energy to enter the gas phase, typically occurring at the surface of the liquid.", "correctAnswer": "evaporation"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_7", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the term for the amount of substance in a given volume, often expressed in g/L?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Concentration refers to how much solute is present in a given volume of solution. It is a crucial concept in chemistry for understanding reactions and solutions.", "correctAnswer": "Concentration"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_8", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["NaCl", "C6H12O6", "H2O", "CO2"], "question": "Which of the following compounds is an example of an organic compound?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "C6H12O6, also known as glucose, is an organic compound because it contains carbon and is part of the study of organic chemistry, which focuses on carbon-containing compounds.", "correctAnswer": "C6H12O6"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_9", "type": "true_false", "question": "The atomic number of an element is determined by the number of neutrons in its nucleus.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "The atomic number of an element is determined by the number of protons in its nucleus, not neutrons. Neutrons contribute to the atomic mass but not to the atomic number.", "correctAnswer": "False"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_10", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "The main gas responsible for the greenhouse effect is _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a significant greenhouse gas that traps heat in the atmosphere, contributing to global warming and climate change.", "correctAnswer": "carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_11", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What is the term for the energy required to break a bond between two atoms?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Bond dissociation energy is the amount of energy required to break a bond between two atoms in a molecule. It's an important concept in understanding chemical reactions and stability.", "correctAnswer": "Bond dissociation energy"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_12", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["C + O2 -> CO2", "Na + Cl -> NaCl", "H2 + O2 -> H2O", "Ca + H2O -> Ca(OH)2"], "question": "Which of the following reactions is an example of a combustion reaction?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A combustion reaction involves a substance reacting with oxygen to produce heat and light, typically producing carbon dioxide and water as products. The reaction C + O2 -> CO2 is a classic example.", "correctAnswer": "C + O2 -> CO2"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_13", "type": "true_false", "question": "Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Hydrogen is indeed the most abundant element in the universe, making up about 75% of its elemental mass. It plays a crucial role in the formation of stars and galaxies.", "correctAnswer": "True"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_14", "type": "fill_blank", "question": "In a chemical reaction, the substances present before the reaction are called _____.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Reactants are the starting materials in a chemical reaction that undergo transformation to form products.", "correctAnswer": "reactants"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_15", "type": "short_answer", "question": "What type of reaction involves the transfer of electrons between species?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "A redox reaction, or reduction-oxidation reaction, involves the transfer of electrons between two species, leading to changes in their oxidation states.", "correctAnswer": "Redox reaction"}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_16", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["They are consumed in a reaction.", "They slow down reactions.", "They lower the activation energy.", "They change the equilibrium position."], "question": "Which of the following statements is true regarding catalysts?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Catalysts are substances that increase the rate of a reaction by lowering the activation energy required for the reaction to proceed, without being consumed in the process.", "correctAnswer": "They lower the activation energy."}, {"id": "q_1773842422576_17", "type": "true_false", "question": "All chemical reactions are reversible.", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Not all chemical reactions are reversible; some reactions go to completion and cannot be reversed. This is especially true for certain reactions involving the formation of precipitates or gases.", "correctAnswer": "False"}]	none	2026-03-18 14:00:22.587329	\N
