first law of motion, also known as the law of inertia, describes how an object will maintain its state of motion unless a net external force acts on it. Inertia is the measure of an object's resistance to change in its state of motion, illustrating the fundamental relationship between force and motion.", "correctAnswer": "Inertia is the property of an object to remain at rest or in uniform motion unless acted upon by an external force, which is essentially what Newton's first law states."}, {"id": "q_1773850430332_4", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["A. Newton's third law", "B. The principle of conservation of momentum", "C. Newton's first law", "D. The law of universal gravitation"], "question": "When a car brakes suddenly, passengers lurch forward due to which principle?", "difficulty": "medium", "explanation": "Passengers lurch forward when a car brakes suddenly because of Newton's first law of motion, which states that an object in motion will stay in motion unless acted upon by a net external force. The car decelerates, but the passengers' bodies tend to continue moving forward at the same speed until they are acted upon by the seatbelt or other restraints.", "correctAnswer": "C. Newton's first law"}]	none	2026-03-18 16:13:50.342025	\N
